window.DEEP_STORIES["printing-press"] = {"slug":"printing-press","title":"The Printing Press","emoji":"🖨️","tagline":"From clay marks to glowing screens: how words learned to multiply","intro":"Imagine a world where every single book had to be copied by hand, letter by letter, taking months of work. For most of human history, that was real life. In this Deep Dive we follow one of the biggest adventures in all of history: how people learned to record words and then copy them fast. We start with clay tablets in ancient Sumer, travel to China where paper and woodblock printing were born, visit Korea where metal type was cast long before Europe, and arrive in Mainz, Germany, where Johannes Gutenberg built a printing system that changed the world. Then we watch printing spread like wildfire, sparking arguments, science, newspapers and revolutions, before steam engines, giant rotary presses, photocopiers, laser printers and the internet take over. Along the way you will learn exactly how the machines work, who deserves the credit, what went wrong, and where printing might go next, even into 3D.","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"Before Books Were Easy","when":"about 40,000 BCE – 3000 BCE"},{"n":2,"title":"Alphabets, Scrolls and the First Books","when":"about 3000 BCE – 400 CE"},{"n":3,"title":"The Paper Revolution in China","when":"about 200 BCE – 700 CE"},{"n":4,"title":"Paper Travels the World","when":"about 700 – 1400"},{"n":5,"title":"Woodblocks: Printing a Whole Page at Once","when":"about 600 – 1100"},{"n":6,"title":"Movable Type Is Born in Asia","when":"about 1040 – 1450"},{"n":7,"title":"Europe's Hand-Copied Books","when":"about 500 – 1450"},{"n":8,"title":"Gutenberg's Secret Project","when":"about 1400 – 1450"},{"n":9,"title":"How Gutenberg's System Worked","when":"about 1440 – 1455"},{"n":10,"title":"The Gutenberg Bible and a Broken Partnership","when":"about 1452 – 1468"},{"n":11,"title":"Inside an Early Print Shop","when":"about 1455 – 1800"},{"n":12,"title":"Printing Spreads Like Wildfire","when":"1462 – 1500s"},{"n":13,"title":"Ideas Unleashed: Reformation and Science","when":"1517 – 1600s"},{"n":14,"title":"News, Spelling and Print Around the World","when":"1500s – 1700s"},{"n":15,"title":"Steam, Iron and Endless Paper","when":"1800 – 1880"},{"n":16,"title":"Typesetting Machines and Printed Pictures","when":"1796 – 1950"},{"n":17,"title":"How Modern Printing Works","when":"1900 – today"},{"n":18,"title":"The Dark Side of Print","when":"1500s – today"},{"n":19,"title":"From Desktop to Digital Publishing","when":"1950s – today"},{"n":20,"title":"The Future of Printing — and the Big Picture","when":"today and tomorrow"}],"people":[["Cai Lun (about 50s–121 CE)","Han dynasty court official who in 105 CE reported an improved papermaking method; improved, did not first invent, paper"],["Empress Shōtoku of Japan (718–770)","ordered the one million printed Hyakumanto Darani charms"],["Wang Jie (lived around 868)","person named in the Diamond Sutra's note as having it made for his parents"],["Bi Sheng (about 970–1051)","Chinese commoner who invented clay (ceramic) movable type in the 1040s"],["Shen Kuo (1031–1095)","Chinese scholar-official whose Dream Pool Essays described Bi Sheng's type"],["Wang Zhen (active about 1290–1333)","Chinese official who made wooden movable type and revolving type tables"],["King Sejong the Great of Korea (1397–1450)","created the Hangul alphabet and supported printing"],["Wang Yuanlu (about 1849–1931)","Taoist monk who found the Dunhuang library cave in 1900"],["Aurel Stein (1862–1943)","Hungarian-British explorer who took the Diamond Sutra to Britain in 1907"],["Johannes Gutenberg (about 1400–1468)","Mainz goldsmith and inventor of Europe's movable-type printing system"],["Johann Fust (about 1400–1466)","Mainz businessman who financed Gutenberg, then sued him in 1455"],["Peter Schoeffer (about 1425–1503)","scribe turned printer who worked with Fust on the 1457 Mainz Psalter"],["Andreas Dritzehn (died 1438)","Gutenberg's Strasbourg partner whose death led to the 1439 lawsuit"],["Enea Silvio Piccolomini, later Pope Pius II (1405–1464)","wrote in 1455 about seeing Gutenberg Bible pages"],["Laurens Janszoon Coster (about 1370–1440)","Haarlem man said by Dutch legend to have printed before Gutenberg; no solid evidence"],["Konrad Sweynheym and Arnold Pannartz (active 1465–1470s)","set up Italy's first press at Subiaco"],["Nicolas Jenson (about 1420–1480)","French printer in Venice famed for roman type"],["Aldus Manutius (about 1450–1515)","Venetian publisher of pocket-size books and the first italic type"],["Francesco Griffo (about 1450–1518)","punchcutter who cut the first italic type for Aldus"],["William Caxton (about 1422–1491)","first printer of a book in English and of England's first press"],["Martin Luther (1483–1546)","German reformer whose printed writings spread rapidly"],["William Tyndale (about 1494–1536)","translated and printed the New Testament in English; executed for heresy"],["Nicolaus Copernicus (1473–1543)","astronomer whose sun-centered book was printed in 1543"],["Andreas Vesalius (1514–1564)","anatomist whose illustrated Fabrica was printed in 1543"],["Johann Carolus (1575–1634)","Strasbourg publisher of the 'Relation', the first newspaper (1605)"],["Juan Pablos (died 1560)","first printer in the Americas, in Mexico City from 1539"],["Stephen Daye (about 1594–1668)","ran the first press in British North America; printed the Bay Psalm Book"],["İbrahim Müteferrika (1674–1745)","founded the first Ottoman Turkish-language press"],["Benjamin Franklin (1706–1790)","American printer, publisher, scientist and statesman"],["Samuel Johnson (1709–1784)","compiled the influential English Dictionary of 1755"],["Alois Senefelder (1771–1834)","invented lithography"],["Charles Stanhope, 3rd Earl Stanhope (1753–1816)","built the iron Stanhope press"],["Louis-Nicolas Robert (1761–1828)","invented the continuous papermaking machine"],["Henry and Sealy Fourdrinier (1766–1854; 1773–1847)","English brothers who financed and developed Robert's paper machine"],["Bryan Donkin (1768–1855)","English engineer who built the Fourdrinier machines"],["Friedrich Koenig (1774–1833)","invented the steam-powered cylinder press"],["Friedrich Gottlob Keller (1816–1895)","invented making paper from ground wood pulp"],["Richard March Hoe (1812–1886)","American inventor of the rotary printing press"],["William Bullock (1813–1867)","invented the web rotary press using rolls of paper"],["Ottmar Mergenthaler (1854–1899)","German-American inventor of the Linotype"],["Tolbert Lanston (1844–1913)","American inventor of the Monotype"],["Ira Washington Rubel (died 1908)","American printer credited with discovering offset printing on paper"],["Chester Carlson (1906–1968)","inventor of xerography (photocopying)"],["Gary Starkweather (1938–2019)","inventor of the laser printer at Xerox"],["John Warnock (1940–2023) and Charles Geschke (1939–2021)","Adobe founders who created PostScript"],["Michael Hart (1947–2011)","founder of Project Gutenberg"],["Tim Berners-Lee (born 1955)","inventor of the World Wide Web"],["Chuck Hull (born 1939)","inventor of stereolithography 3D printing"],["Elizabeth Eisenstein (1923–2016)","historian who argued print was an 'agent of change'"]],"glossary":[["cuneiform","wedge-shaped writing pressed into clay, first used in Sumer"],["papyrus","a writing material made from pressed strips of a reed plant; not true paper"],["parchment / vellum","writing material made from specially prepared animal skin (vellum is fine calfskin)"],["codex","a book with pages bound on one side, like books today"],["paper","a thin sheet made from plant fibers beaten in water, scooped onto a screen and dried"],["woodblock printing","carving a whole page in reverse on wood, inking it and pressing paper on it"],["movable type","separate pieces for each character that can be arranged, printed and reused"],["punch","a steel rod with a letter carved backwards on its tip, used to make a matrix"],["matrix","a piece of copper with a letter-shaped dent, used as the mould for casting type"],["type metal","an alloy of lead, tin and antimony used to cast metal type"],["compositor","the worker who sets type into lines and pages"],["forme","the locked-up pages of type ready to be inked and printed"],["platen","the flat plate in a press that pushes paper against the inked type"],["incunabula","books printed in Europe before 1501 ('cradle books')"],["lithography","printing from a flat surface using the fact that oil and water repel each other"],["offset printing","ink goes from a plate to a rubber blanket and then onto paper"],["halftone","a picture made of tiny dots of different sizes that the eye blends into shades"],["CMYK","cyan, magenta, yellow and black, the four inks used for full-color printing"],["xerography","copying with static electricity and powdered toner (how photocopiers and laser printers work)"],["3D printing","making a solid object by adding material one thin layer at a time"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"Before Books Were Easy","when":"about 40,000 BCE – 3000 BCE","summary":"Long ago there were no books, no paper and no letters at all. People kept their stories in their heads and painted animals on cave walls. Then, in a busy city called Uruk, clever accountants began pressing little wedge marks into wet clay, and writing was born.","sections":[{"heading":"A World That Lived in Memory","paragraphs":["Close your eyes and imagine a world with no writing at all. No books, no signs, no shopping lists, no birthday cards, no labels on cereal boxes. If you wanted to remember something important, such as which plants were safe to eat, where the river flooded, or who your great-great-grandmother was, you had exactly one place to keep it: inside your head. For almost all of the time that modern humans have existed, about 300,000 years, that was simply how life worked.","This does not mean people were forgetful or simple. In fact, people without writing often trained their memories to a level that would amaze us today. Knowledge was passed from older people to younger people by speaking, singing and showing. Historians call this oral tradition, from the Latin word for mouth. A grandmother might teach a child a long song that listed every family member back seven generations. A hunter might learn a story that was secretly a map, describing which hills, rocks and waterholes came one after another along a route.","We can still see how powerful oral tradition is in cultures that kept it alive. In West Africa, professional storytellers and musicians called griots have for centuries memorized the histories of kingdoms and families, performing them with music. The epic of Sundiata, about the founder of the Mali Empire in the 1200s, was carried for hundreds of years mainly by griots before scholars wrote it down. In Australia, Aboriginal peoples have songlines: long sung journeys that describe the land, its water sources and its sacred places, linking stories to the landscape itself.","The ancient Greek poems the Iliad and the Odyssey, said to be by Homer, were probably shaped by generations of singers who performed them aloud long before they were written down. In the 1930s an American scholar named Milman Parry studied singers in the Balkans who could perform poems thousands of lines long without any written text. He noticed that they used repeated phrases and patterns, like building blocks, to help them remember and improvise. The same kinds of repeated phrases appear in Homer, such as the famous 'rosy-fingered Dawn'.","Oral tradition had real strengths. It was free, it travelled wherever people travelled, and it could change to fit a new audience. But it also had weaknesses. If the only person who knew a story died suddenly, the story could vanish. Details could drift over time like a giant game of telephone. And there was one job that memory found especially hard: keeping track of lots of exact numbers. How many jars of oil did the temple receive last spring? How many sheep does a farmer owe? For questions like those, people needed something outside their heads. They needed marks."]},{"heading":"Paint, Notches and Secret Signs","paragraphs":["Long before anyone wrote a word, people were already storing information in pictures and marks. Some of the most breathtaking examples are hidden deep underground in the caves of Europe.","On 12 September 1940, an 18-year-old named Marcel Ravidat was exploring the hills near the village of Montignac in southwestern France. His dog, Robot, sniffed at a hole left where a tree had been uprooted. Ravidat came back with friends, squeezed down into the darkness, and found himself in a cave whose walls were covered with animals: horses, deer, wild cattle called aurochs, bison and even a mysterious creature that looks a bit like a unicorn. This was Lascaux, one of the most famous painted caves in the world.","Lascaux's paintings are usually dated to roughly 17,000 to 22,000 years ago, although scientists still debate the exact age. The cave holds around 6,000 figures in total, including more than 600 paintings and many hundreds of engravings scratched into the rock. Horses are the most common animal, with more than 360 of them. There is only one clear human figure, a strange stick-like man near a wounded bison, deep in a part of the cave called the Well.","The artists mixed their paints from natural minerals. Red and yellow came from iron-rich earth called ochre, and black came from charcoal or manganese. They blew paint through hollow bones, dabbed it with pads of fur or moss, and worked by the light of small stone lamps that burned animal fat. Some paintings are high up on the ceiling, which means the artists probably built wooden scaffolding.","Were these pictures a kind of writing? Not exactly. Nobody knows for sure what they meant. They may have been about hunting, religion, stories, seasons or something we cannot guess. But next to the animals, the artists also painted rows of dots, grids, lines and other repeated signs. Some researchers think these signs carried agreed meanings, perhaps counting days or marking events. That idea is still debated, but it shows that people were already experimenting with marks that stood for something.","Even older are tally marks: notches cut into bone or wood to keep count. The Lebombo bone, a baboon leg bone from the mountains between South Africa and Eswatini, carries a row of notches (often counted as 29) and is dated to roughly 42,000 to 43,000 years ago. The Ishango bone from what is now the Democratic Republic of the Congo, about 20,000 years old, has groups of notches that some people think show early arithmetic, though others say the patterns may be accidental. Tallies were simple but powerful: one notch, one thing.","Visitors loved Lascaux almost to death. After it opened to the public in 1948, the breath, body heat and moisture of more than a thousand visitors a day caused mould and damage, so the cave was closed in 1963. Today people visit careful copies instead, including Lascaux IV, which reproduces the whole cave and opened in 2016. It is a reminder that information stored on a surface can be fragile, a problem that will keep showing up all through the history of writing and printing."]},{"heading":"Counting Goods in Uruk","paragraphs":["Let us travel to a place where the story of writing truly begins: southern Mesopotamia, the flat land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers in what is now Iraq. Around 5,000 years ago, a region called Sumer was home to some of the first true cities on Earth. The greatest of them was Uruk.","Imagine walking into Uruk around 3200 BCE. Mud-brick walls rise around you. Farmers lead donkeys loaded with barley along dusty streets. Canals shimmer with water carried from the river to the fields. On a raised platform stands a huge temple, the heart of the city, where priests and officials collect and store grain, oil, wool, beer and animals. Historians estimate that Uruk may have had tens of thousands of residents, making it one of the largest cities in the world at the time.","A city this big had a problem that no single memory could solve. Thousands of people were bringing goods in, taking rations out, paying what they owed and borrowing what they needed. Somebody had to keep accurate records. And the solution grew, step by step, out of something very humble: little pieces of clay.","For thousands of years before Uruk, people across the Near East had used small clay tokens to keep count. Archaeologists have found them at sites going back to roughly 8000 BCE, around the time farming began. The tokens came in different shapes: cones, spheres, discs, cylinders and little tetrahedrons. The archaeologist Denise Schmandt-Besserat, who studied thousands of them, argued that each shape stood for a type of goods. A certain cone might mean a small measure of grain, a disc might mean a sheep. Three sheep would be three discs.","To keep tokens safe and honest, people sealed them inside hollow clay balls called envelopes, or bullae. Think of a clay piggy bank holding a receipt. If two people disagreed about a deal, they could break the envelope and count the tokens inside. But that destroyed the envelope. So people began pressing the tokens into the soft outside of the envelope before sealing them inside, leaving a picture of what was hidden within.","Then someone realised something brilliant: if the marks on the outside already showed the information, why bother with the tokens at all? A flat piece of clay with marks pressed into it could do the same job. According to Schmandt-Besserat's theory, this is how the first clay tablets with numbers and signs appeared. Not every scholar agrees with every step of her explanation, but nearly all agree on the big picture: writing in Mesopotamia grew out of accounting. The first writers were not poets. They were bookkeepers.","The earliest tablets from Uruk, from around 3300 to 3200 BCE, are mostly lists: so many measures of barley, so many workers, so many jars of beer. Archaeologists call this early stage proto-cuneiform. It used number signs plus little pictures for goods, such as a sheaf of grain or the head of an ox. It was not yet a way to write down spoken sentences. But it was the seed from which a full writing system would grow."]},{"heading":"Wedges in Wet Clay: How Cuneiform Worked","paragraphs":["Over the next few centuries, the simple counting marks of Uruk grew into a complete writing system. We call it cuneiform, from the Latin word cuneus, meaning wedge, because every sign is built from little wedge-shaped marks. It appeared in Uruk around 3300 to 3200 BCE and stayed in use for about 3,000 years, longer than the Latin alphabet has been used for English.","Here is how a scribe actually wrote. First, he (most scribes were men, though some women scribes are known) took a lump of fine, damp clay from the riverbank and shaped it in his palm into a tablet. Many tablets were small enough to hold in one hand, about the size of a phone or a bar of soap, though some were much larger. The clay had to be just right: wet enough to take a mark, but firm enough not to smear.","Next he picked up a stylus, a pen cut from a reed that grew along the rivers. The end of the stylus was trimmed to a sharp corner. Instead of dragging it across the clay like a pencil, the scribe pressed it in. Each press left a mark with a triangular head and a thin tail: a wedge. By turning the stylus to different angles and pressing harder or softer, he could make horizontal wedges, vertical wedges and slanting ones. Groups of wedges formed signs.","Pressing worked much better than drawing. Curved lines tend to drag up little ridges of clay, but quick straight presses stay clean and sharp. Over time the early picture-signs became more and more abstract, until the picture of an ox head no longer looked like an ox at all, just a neat cluster of wedges. This made writing faster. It also turned it into a code that had to be learned.","The biggest breakthrough was the idea of using signs for sounds, not just things. Scribes discovered the rebus principle, the same trick as drawing an eye and a saw to mean 'I saw'. A sign that meant one word could be borrowed for another word that sounded the same, or for one syllable of a longer word. With sound-signs, scribes could finally write names, grammar, and full spoken sentences. Early cuneiform used well over 1,000 different signs; later this was reduced to about 600 in common use, which was still a huge amount to memorise.","When the tablet was finished, it was usually left to dry in the hot Mesopotamian sun, which turned it hard like leather. Unfired tablets could be softened with water and reused, a little like a whiteboard. Important tablets that needed to last were sometimes baked in a kiln, turning them into something close to pottery. Some contracts were even sealed inside a clay envelope, with a copy of the text written on the outside, so nobody could secretly change the version inside.","Cuneiform turned out to be extremely adaptable. It was invented for the Sumerian language, but later peoples used it for Akkadian, Babylonian and Assyrian, and also for Elamite, Hittite, Hurrian and others, a bit like how the Latin alphabet is now used for English, Spanish, Vietnamese and Swahili. People wrote letters, laws, recipes, medical texts, school exercises, maths problems, star charts and epic poems on clay. One of the world's oldest great stories, the Epic of Gilgamesh, survives on clay tablets.","Learning cuneiform took years. Boys, mostly from well-off families, went to a school the Sumerians called the 'tablet house'. Teachers wrote a model text on one side of a tablet, and students copied it on the other side, over and over. Archaeologists have found thousands of these practice tablets, complete with mistakes. Some even record students grumbling about being punished by their teachers, a reminder that school has always been school.","Eventually, cheaper alphabets written with ink took over, and cuneiform faded away. The latest known cuneiform tablets, mostly astronomical records, date to around the first century CE. Then the script was forgotten for more than 1,500 years, until scholars in the 1800s, including Georg Friedrich Grotefend and Henry Rawlinson, worked out how to read it again."]},{"heading":"Rolling Pictures: The Cylinder Seal","paragraphs":["Now we come to an object that looks like a tiny rolling pin and contains one of the oldest seeds of the idea of printing. It is called a cylinder seal.","A cylinder seal is a small cylinder, often only a few centimetres long and about as thick as an adult's finger, carved from stone such as lapis lazuli, marble, obsidian or other hard rocks, and sometimes from shell or metal. Into its curved surface a skilled craftsman carved a design in reverse, cutting into the stone so the picture was sunk below the surface. This kind of carving is called intaglio. A hole was usually drilled through the middle so the seal could hang on a cord around the owner's neck or wrist, or be pinned to clothing.","To use it, you rolled the seal across a patch of soft, damp clay. Wherever the stone was carved away, clay pushed up into the hollows. The result was a raised picture, the right way round, that could be as long as you liked. Roll the seal once and you got the whole design. Roll it twice and the design repeated, like a pattern on wallpaper.","Cylinder seals were in common use in Mesopotamia by about 3500 BCE, around the same time the first cities and the first writing were developing, and they stayed in use there for about 3,000 years. Some scholars trace stamp seals and seal-like objects back even earlier. The designs could show gods, heroes fighting lions, rows of animals, banquets, and later the owner's name and title written in cuneiform.","Seals worked as signatures and as locks. A merchant might close a jar with a clay stopper and roll his seal across it. If the seal impression was broken, everyone would know someone had opened the jar. Doors of storerooms were tied shut with string, and lumps of sealed clay were pressed over the knot. On tablets, rolling a seal across the clay showed who had agreed to a sale, a loan or a marriage contract. Seals were so important that if someone lost one, they reported it to officials, so that anyone who used the stolen seal later could be caught out. People also believed seals could act as lucky charms that protected their owners.","Here is why the cylinder seal matters so much to our story. Think about what it actually does. A craftsman makes one carved original, in mirror image. Then that single original can create the same picture again and again and again, quickly and perfectly, on as many surfaces as you like. That is the central idea of printing: make a master once, then copy it many times. Mesopotamians were not printing books, and they did not use ink. But they were already using a reversed carved surface to reproduce an image, more than 4,000 years before Gutenberg."]},{"heading":"Hieroglyphs and the Scribes of Egypt","paragraphs":["While Sumerians were pressing wedges into clay, another great civilisation along the Nile River was developing its own writing. Egyptian hieroglyphs appeared around 3200 BCE. Some of the earliest known examples are small bone and ivory labels found in a royal tomb at Abydos in southern Egypt, dated to roughly that time. Whether the Egyptians got the idea of writing from Mesopotamia or invented it independently is still debated by historians, but their signs look completely different.","Hieroglyphs are beautiful little pictures: owls, feet, water ripples, reeds, snakes, hands, loaves of bread. The name comes from Greek words meaning 'sacred carvings', because the Greeks saw them on temples and tombs. But like cuneiform, hieroglyphs were not just pictures of things. Some signs stood for sounds, some for whole words, and some were silent helpers that showed what kind of word you were reading. By the Middle Kingdom, around 2000 BCE, the system used about 900 different signs, and in much later times the number grew into the thousands.","Carving hieroglyphs in stone was slow, so for everyday writing Egyptians developed faster, flowing forms. Hieratic was a joined-up script written with a brush. Much later, from around the 600s BCE, an even quicker script called demotic was used for business and legal papers. The beautiful carved hieroglyphs were kept for temples, monuments and tombs, a bit like the way we might use fancy lettering on a certificate but scribble a note in quick handwriting.","The people who could write all of this were called scribes, and they were rare and highly respected. Many historians estimate that only a small fraction of ancient Egyptians, perhaps around one percent, could read and write well, though such numbers are hard to know for sure. Becoming a scribe meant years of training, starting as a boy. Students practised by copying classic texts onto broken pieces of pottery and flakes of limestone called ostraca, which were free, before being trusted with more expensive materials.","A scribe's tool kit was simple but clever. He carried a long, flat palette, usually of wood, with hollows for two cakes of ink: black ink made from soot, and red ink made from ochre. Red was used for headings and important words, which is where the idea of a 'rubric' (from the Latin for red) in later books comes from. He wrote with a thin reed whose end was chewed or cut into a fine brush. A small pot of water let him wet the ink.","Being a scribe was a very good job. Scribes kept records of taxes and harvests, wrote letters for officials, copied religious texts and managed building projects. An old Egyptian text known as the Satire of the Trades, which students copied in school, tries to persuade boys to study hard by describing how tiring and dirty every other job is, from metalworking to fishing, and how comfortable life is for a scribe. Egyptians even believed writing was a gift from Thoth, the ibis-headed god of wisdom.","Hieroglyphs were used for well over 3,000 years. The last known hieroglyphic inscription was carved in 394 CE at the temple of Philae. Afterwards, the knowledge was lost until the Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, carried the same decree in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek. Using it, the French scholar Jean-François Champollion announced in 1822 that he had cracked the code."]},{"heading":"The Clay Library of Nineveh","paragraphs":["To finish our first journey, let us visit one of the greatest libraries of the ancient world, built entirely of clay. In the 600s BCE, the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal ruled a vast empire from his capital city, Nineveh, near today's city of Mosul in northern Iraq. Unusually for a king of his time, Ashurbanipal boasted that he could read and write cuneiform himself.","Ashurbanipal wanted to own every important text he could find. He sent officials across his empire to collect and copy tablets on medicine, magic, astronomy, omens, prayers, history and literature. His scribes made fresh copies, often adding notes at the end about which original they had copied from and stating that the tablet belonged to the king. The result was a collection that today numbers more than 30,000 clay tablets and fragments. Many of them are actually everyday letters, reports and legal documents, but others are priceless works of literature, including the best-known version of the Epic of Gilgamesh.","Then disaster struck. In 612 BCE, armies of the Babylonians and Medes attacked and destroyed Nineveh. The palaces burned and collapsed. But here is the amazing twist: the fire that destroyed the city actually helped save the library. Many of the sun-dried tablets were baked hard in the flames, almost like pottery, which made them far tougher. Buried under rubble, they waited for more than 2,400 years.","In the middle of the 1800s, archaeologists including Austen Henry Layard and his assistant Hormuzd Rassam dug into the ruins of Nineveh and found huge piles of tablets in the palaces. Tens of thousands of them were shipped to the British Museum in London, where most of the collection is still kept. In 1872 a young British Museum worker named George Smith, studying one of the tablets, realised he was reading a flood story from the Epic of Gilgamesh that resembled the story of Noah. The news caused a sensation around the world.","Clay, then, had done an incredible job. It was cheap, it was everywhere, and when baked it could survive for thousands of years. Writing on it had let people keep accounts, make laws, send letters and save stories. And cylinder seals had shown that a single carved original could make copy after copy. But clay had serious drawbacks. A library of tablets weighed as much as a mountain of bricks. A long letter might need several heavy tablets. You could not roll it up and tuck it under your arm. And learning hundreds of complicated signs meant that only a small, highly trained group of scribes could read and write at all.","Clay was tough but heavy, so people began searching for lighter things to write on and simpler ways to write."]}],"deepDive":"Did writing appear once and spread, or was it invented several times? Most historians now think writing was invented independently at least three or four times: in Mesopotamia (around 3300 to 3200 BCE), in China (by about 1200 BCE, seen in oracle bone inscriptions from the Shang dynasty), and in Mesoamerica (by the first millennium BCE). Egypt is the tricky case. Its hieroglyphs appear around 3200 BCE, almost at the same moment as cuneiform, and the two regions were in contact, so some scholars argue the idea of writing travelled from Mesopotamia to Egypt, while others point out that the signs, materials and system look entirely Egyptian, suggesting an independent invention. The early Abydos labels have made the question harder, because they are roughly as old as the earliest Uruk tablets. The origin of cuneiform is also debated. Denise Schmandt-Besserat's token theory, published mainly in the 1970s to 1990s, is famous and widely taught: tokens, then envelopes with impressions, then tablets. Critics accept that tokens and envelopes existed, but question whether every token shape had a fixed meaning and whether the path to writing was really so direct, noting that many token types never became signs. What scholars largely agree on is that the earliest Mesopotamian writing was administrative. The large majority of the earliest Uruk tablets, often estimated at around 85 to 90 percent, are economic records, while literature appears centuries later. Another important idea is the difference between proto-writing and true writing. Tally marks, cave signs and token systems store information, but they cannot record any sentence of a spoken language. True writing arrives when signs systematically represent the sounds and words of a language, which in cuneiform happened through the rebus principle. Finally, it is worth noticing how much our knowledge depends on durable materials. Clay tablets survive in enormous numbers, so we know a lot about Mesopotamia, while writing on wood, leaves or cloth in other places may simply have rotted away, which can make some societies look less literate than they were.","funFact":"The fire that destroyed the Assyrian city of Nineveh in 612 BCE accidentally baked many of King Ashurbanipal's clay tablets, helping thousands of them survive for more than 2,500 years.","check":{"q":"What tool did Sumerian scribes use to make wedge-shaped cuneiform marks in wet clay?","a":"A reed stylus","wrong":["A goose-feather quill","A metal printing stamp","A paintbrush made of fur"]},"sources":[{"title":"Cuneiform","publisher":"World History Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.worldhistory.org/cuneiform/"},{"title":"Cylinder Seal","publisher":"World History Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.worldhistory.org/Cylinder_Seal/"},{"title":"Egyptian Hieroglyphs","publisher":"World History Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.worldhistory.org/Egyptian_Hieroglyphs/"},{"title":"Lascaux","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lascaux"},{"title":"Library of Ashurbanipal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Ashurbanipal"},{"title":"Bulla (seal)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulla_(seal)"},{"title":"Cuneiform","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuneiform"},{"title":"Lebombo bone","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebombo_bone"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/01-1.jpg","alt":"A clay tablet from about 3300–3100 BCE, Uruk period, keeping a record of barley and emmer wheat with early proto-cuneiform signs.","file":"Clay tablet, record of barley and emmer. Late Uruk period, 3300-3100 BCE. Purchased via Christie's, no provenance.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_tablet,_record_of_barley_and_emmer._Late_Uruk_period,_3300-3100_BCE._Purchased_via_Christie%27s,_no_provenance.jpg","author":"Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/01-2.jpg","alt":"A small stone cylinder seal from Nippur (about 2100 BCE) next to the picture it makes when rolled across soft clay.","file":"Cylinder seal and modern impression. Worshiper pouring a libation before a seated god,ca. 2112–2004 B.C. Neo-Sumarian Mesopotamia Nippur.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god,ca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg","author":"Metropolitan Museum of Art","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/01-3.jpg","alt":"Aurochs, horses and deer painted on the wall of Lascaux cave in France about 17,000 years ago.","file":"Lascaux painting.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lascaux_painting.jpg","author":"EU","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4013},{"n":2,"title":"Alphabets, Scrolls and the First Books","when":"about 3000 BCE – 400 CE","summary":"Egyptians made long rolls from a river reed called papyrus, and miners in the desert invented a short list of letters called an alphabet. Later, people wrote on animal skins, filled a giant library in Alexandria, and finally folded pages together into the very first books with covers.","sections":[{"heading":"A Reed That Became a Roll","paragraphs":["Picture the banks of the Nile River in ancient Egypt about 5,000 years ago. Thick green stands of a tall water plant sway in the breeze, their stems taller than a grown-up, each topped with a fluffy, firework-shaped head. This plant is papyrus, and Egyptians found a use for almost every part of it. They ate its roots, twisted its fibres into rope, wove it into mats, baskets and sandals, and tied bundles of stems together to make light boats. But its most famous use was as a writing surface.","Here is how papyrus sheets were made. Workers cut the triangular green stems and peeled off the tough outer skin, revealing a soft, white, spongy core called the pith. They sliced the pith into long thin strips. Then they laid a row of strips side by side, all running the same direction, and placed a second layer on top running crosswise, like a lattice. The sheet was pressed or beaten with a mallet and left to dry in the sun. The plant's own sticky juices helped glue the layers together, and the result was a light, smooth, cream-coloured sheet that could be polished with a stone or shell.","It is important to know that papyrus is not true paper, even though the English word paper comes from the word papyrus. True paper, which we will meet in China in the next part of our story, is made by breaking plant fibres apart in water until they become a mushy pulp and then letting them dry into a sheet. Papyrus is different: it is made from whole strips of plant laid on top of each other, more like a very thin plywood made of reed.","Single sheets were useful, but Egyptians wanted longer surfaces. So they glued sheets edge to edge to make a roll, often around twenty sheets long as a standard size. Rolls could be joined to make even longer ones. Writers usually wrote first on the side where the fibres ran horizontally, because the pen moved more smoothly along the grain. A famous Egyptian medical text called the Ebers Papyrus, from around 1550 BCE, fills a roll about 20 metres long, longer than a tennis court.","Papyrus was in use in Egypt by about 3000 BCE. One of the oldest rolls ever found, discovered in the tomb of an official named Hemaka at Saqqara and dating from around that time, is said to have been completely blank, perhaps as if it had been packed for writing in the afterlife. Scribes wrote on it with reed brushes and black and red ink. Over the centuries Egypt became the world's supplier of papyrus, exporting it across the Mediterranean. The Greeks and then the Romans used it for letters, contracts, poems and history books. A Greek book was really a roll, and long works were divided into several rolls, which is why ancient texts are still split into 'books' today.","Papyrus was a giant step up from clay. It was light, easy to carry, and could be rolled up and stored on a shelf or in a jar. But it had weaknesses too. In damp climates it slowly rots and crumbles, which is why most surviving ancient papyri come from the hot, dry sands of Egypt. It could tear along the fibres, it was hard to fold without cracking, and because it grew mainly in Egypt, everyone else depended on the Egyptian supply. Papyrus was too expensive for many uses, so even Egyptian students did their homework on broken pottery."]},{"heading":"Miners, Ox Heads and the First Letters","paragraphs":["Papyrus made writing lighter. The next great invention made writing simpler. To understand why it mattered, remember the problem from Part 1: cuneiform needed hundreds of signs, and Egyptian hieroglyphs needed hundreds more. Learning them took years, so only a small group of professional scribes could read. What if you could write every word in a language using only a couple of dozen signs?","That is exactly what an alphabet does. An alphabet uses a small set of letters, each standing for one basic sound. Mix and match those letters and you can spell any word, even a word nobody has ever written before. Today the English alphabet has 26 letters, and a five-year-old can learn them all.","The story of the alphabet begins, surprisingly, in Egypt, among people who were not Egyptian. In the winter of 1904 to 1905 the British archaeologist Flinders Petrie and his wife Hilda were exploring Serabit el-Khadim, a remote spot in the mountains of the Sinai Peninsula where ancient Egyptians mined turquoise. Near a temple to the goddess Hathor, they found short inscriptions scratched into rocks and small statues. The signs looked a bit like hieroglyphs, but there were only a few dozen of them, and they were used in an unfamiliar way.","In 1916 the Egyptologist Alan Gardiner worked out the trick. The writers had borrowed pictures from Egyptian writing but used each picture for the first sound of its name in their own Semitic language. The picture of an ox head was called alp, meaning ox, so it stood for the sound at the start of that word. The picture of a house was called bayt, meaning house, so it stood for the sound b. This idea, where a letter gets its sound from the first sound of the thing it pictures, is called the acrophonic principle. Gardiner read one repeated group of signs as 'to the Lady', probably meaning the goddess worshipped at the mine.","Scholars call this early alphabet Proto-Sinaitic. Many of the Sinai inscriptions are thought to date to roughly the 1700s or 1600s BCE, though some researchers argue for around 1800 BCE. Then, in the 1990s, two short inscriptions were found carved on a cliff at Wadi el-Hol, a desert valley in Middle Egypt, dated to roughly 1900 to 1800 BCE. These suggest that the alphabet was born among Semitic-speaking workers, soldiers or traders living in or around Egypt, who adapted Egyptian picture-signs into a far simpler system.","The beautiful part of this story is that the alphabet was probably not invented by elite scribes in a palace, but by ordinary people who needed a practical way to write. They kept just one sign per consonant sound, so the whole system needed only around two dozen signs.","Over the following centuries, these letters spread northward along the trade routes of Canaan, the land along the eastern Mediterranean coast. Different groups wrote them in slightly different ways. Slowly, the letters became simpler lines rather than recognisable pictures. The ox head was turned on its side and became a simple shape that, many centuries later, would be flipped again and become our capital A. If you turn an A upside down, you can still imagine the horns of an ox."]},{"heading":"Twenty-Two Letters for the Sea Traders","paragraphs":["By around 1050 BCE, one version of this alphabet had become standard in the busy port cities of Phoenicia, along the coast of what is now Lebanon. The Phoenicians were the great sea traders of the ancient Mediterranean. From cities such as Byblos, Tyre and Sidon, they sailed far and wide, selling cedar wood, glass and a famous purple dye made from sea snails, and they founded colonies such as Carthage in North Africa.","The Phoenician alphabet had 22 letters, all of them consonants. There were no letters for vowels at all, so readers had to fill in the vowel sounds from context, a bit like reading 'txt msgs' where you can usually guess 'text messages'. It was written from right to left. One of the most famous early Phoenician inscriptions is carved on the stone coffin of a king named Ahiram of Byblos, dated to around 1000 BCE, warning anyone who disturbs his tomb that they will be punished.","For merchants, this alphabet was perfect. A trader did not need years of scribal school. He could learn 22 letters quickly and use them to keep accounts, write contracts and send messages to partners in faraway ports. Because Phoenician ships visited so many places, their alphabet travelled too. It became the ancestor of an astonishing number of scripts. Through Aramaic, a related script used across the Persian Empire, it led to the Hebrew and Arabic scripts. And when it reached the Greeks, it began the line that leads to the letters you are reading right now."]},{"heading":"The Greeks Add Vowels","paragraphs":["Sometime around 800 BCE, Greeks who traded with the Phoenicians borrowed their alphabet. The Greeks even admitted the borrowing: some ancient Greek writers called letters 'Phoenician things'. But Greek had a problem. The Greek language depends heavily on vowel sounds, and a script with no vowels made Greek words very hard to read.","So the Greeks made a small change with enormous consequences. Some Phoenician letters stood for consonant sounds that Greek did not use. Instead of throwing those letters away, the Greeks recycled them as vowels. The Phoenician aleph, the old ox-head letter, became alpha, the sound a. The letter he became epsilon, the sound e. Others became the vowels i, o and later u. For the first time, an alphabet had separate letters for both consonants and vowels, so a reader could sound out almost any word exactly. Many historians call this the first full alphabet.","Even the word alphabet comes from this moment: it joins the names of the first two Greek letters, alpha and beta, which came from the Phoenician aleph and bet, ox and house. So when you say 'alphabet', you are really saying 'ox-house', a little fossil of the miners of Sinai hiding inside an everyday word.","Early Greek writing went in different directions. Some inscriptions ran right to left like Phoenician. Some ran in a back-and-forth zigzag called boustrophedon, which means 'as the ox turns', like an ox ploughing a field: one line left to right, the next line right to left. Eventually Greeks settled on writing left to right, the direction used in Europe ever since.","Greek colonists carried their alphabet to southern Italy, including the city of Cumae near Naples. There the Etruscans, a powerful people of central Italy, adopted it, and the Romans learned it in turn. The Latin alphabet of classical Rome had 23 letters. Letters such as J, U and W were added much later, in the Middle Ages and after. As the Roman Empire grew, the Latin alphabet spread across western Europe. Meanwhile, more than a thousand years later, a version of the Greek alphabet became the basis of Cyrillic, used today for Russian and other languages.","Why does all of this matter for the story of printing? Because an alphabet with about two dozen letters is perfect for movable type. Much later, a European printer would need to cast many copies of only a few dozen letters, plus capitals, numbers and punctuation, rather than many thousands of different characters. The simplicity of the alphabet, invented by desert workers and improved by Greek traders, quietly prepared the ground for Gutenberg nearly 3,000 years later."]},{"heading":"Writing on Skins: Parchment and Vellum","paragraphs":["Papyrus grew in Egypt, but people elsewhere needed something they could make at home. One answer came from animals. Parchment is a writing material made from specially prepared animal skin, usually from sheep, goats or calves. The finest kind, made from calfskin, is called vellum. People had written on animal hides for a very long time, but parchment is something special: it is not leather, because it is not tanned. It is stretched and dried skin.","Making parchment was hard, smelly work. First the skin was soaked in water for about a day to clean it. Then it went into a bath of lime, a strong chemical made from burnt limestone, for several days, which loosened the hair. The parchment maker scraped away the hair and fat and stretched the wet skin tight on a wooden frame. While it dried under tension, he scraped it again and again with a curved, half-moon-shaped knife until it was thin, smooth and even. Rubbing it with pumice stone made a surface that ink would grip. The finished sheet was strong, pale and could be written on both sides.","The word parchment comes from the name of Pergamon, a wealthy Greek city in what is now western Turkey, which was a famous centre for making it. There is a well-known story about how this happened, told by the Roman writer Pliny the Elder in the first century CE, who said he got it from an earlier scholar named Varro. According to the tale, the kings of Egypt and the kings of Pergamon were rivals, each trying to build the greatest library. To stop Pergamon, the Egyptian king banned the export of papyrus, and so the people of Pergamon invented parchment instead. It is a great story, but it is a legend. Writing on prepared skins was known long before Pergamon became important. The most likely truth is that Pergamon became famous for making excellent parchment, so its name stuck.","Parchment had big advantages over papyrus. It was tough, it could be folded without cracking, it lasted well even in damper climates, and it could be made anywhere that had farm animals. If a scribe made a mistake, he could gently scrape the ink off with a knife and write again. People sometimes scraped whole books clean and reused the parchment for new texts. A reused page like this is called a palimpsest. Today, scientists use special lighting and imaging to read the faint older writing hidden underneath, recovering lost works, including writings by the ancient Greek mathematician Archimedes.","The big disadvantage was cost. Each animal gave only a few large sheets. A large book needed the skins of dozens or even hundreds of animals. One of the most extreme examples is the Codex Amiatinus, a giant Latin Bible made around 700 CE at the monastery of Wearmouth-Jarrow in northern England. It has 1,040 leaves of vellum, weighs about 34 kilograms, heavier than many 11-year-olds, and is about 49 centimetres tall. The manuscript expert Christopher de Hamel estimated its pages used the skins of around 515 cattle, and the monastery set out to make three of these huge Bibles. Today the Codex Amiatinus is kept in a library in Florence, Italy.","Parchment would remain the main writing material of Europe for more than a thousand years, until paper arrived. In fact, it is still used today for Jewish Torah scrolls, and until 2015 the United Kingdom recorded its new laws on vellum."]},{"heading":"The Great Library of Alexandria","paragraphs":["Now let us visit the most famous library of the ancient world. After Alexander the Great died in 323 BCE, one of his generals, Ptolemy, took control of Egypt and founded a royal family that ruled for nearly 300 years. Their capital was Alexandria, a gleaming city on the Mediterranean coast with a giant lighthouse, the Pharos, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.","Around 300 BCE the first Ptolemy, perhaps encouraged by the Greek scholar Demetrius of Phalerum (according to one ancient account), began planning a great centre of learning. It was called the Mouseion, a temple to the Muses, the goddesses of the arts and sciences. This is where our word museum comes from. His son, Ptolemy II, built up its library. The goal was breathtaking: to collect a copy of every important book in the world.","The Ptolemies were famously determined. According to ancient accounts, when ships docked in Alexandria's harbour, officials searched them for books. Any scrolls found were taken to the library and copied by hand. The library kept the originals, and the owners were given the copies. Agents travelled around the Mediterranean buying texts, and the library is said to have borrowed official copies of the great Greek plays from Athens, paying a huge deposit, and then kept the originals, happily losing its money.","How many scrolls did it hold? Ancient writers give numbers as high as 400,000 or even 700,000 scrolls, while other ancient estimates are far smaller. Modern historians think these numbers are probably exaggerated, and many scrolls held only part of a work, but it was clearly the largest collection of its time. Remember, every one of those scrolls had been copied by hand, letter by letter, with a reed pen on papyrus. Imagine teams of copyists bent over their desks day after day, checking each line.","The library was also a research centre. The scholar Callimachus created a giant catalogue of its holdings, called the Pinakes, which ran to 120 volumes by itself and is often called the first library catalogue. Eratosthenes, one of the library's head librarians, used shadows and geometry to estimate the size of the Earth surprisingly well. Scholars at Alexandria compared different copies of Homer, marked errors and tried to produce the most accurate text. That problem, how to stop mistakes creeping in when books are copied by hand, is one that only printing would truly solve.","You may have heard that the library was destroyed in one great fire, perhaps when Julius Caesar's soldiers set ships ablaze in 48 BCE. Historians now think the real story is slower and sadder. That fire probably burned warehouses near the docks, and the library continued to be mentioned afterwards. Its decline came over centuries, as royal funding dried up, scholars were driven away in 145 BCE, and wars damaged the city in the 200s CE. Knowledge can be lost not only in flames, but simply when nobody pays to look after it."]},{"heading":"From Scroll to Codex","paragraphs":["For most of ancient history, a book was a scroll. Scrolls worked, but they were awkward. You needed both hands to read, unrolling with one hand and rolling up with the other. If you wanted to find a passage near the end, you had to unroll almost the whole thing. Most scrolls had writing on one side only, wasting half the material. And a long work needed many scrolls, which had to be stored in boxes or on shelves with little tags hanging off the ends to show their titles.","Romans already used a different kind of writing object for notes: wooden tablets coated with wax, which they scratched with a pointed stylus and could smooth over to reuse. Two or more tablets were often tied together along one edge with cord, so they opened and closed like a book. At some point, people realised they could do the same thing with sheets of parchment or papyrus: stack them, fold them in half, and stitch them together along one side. The result was the codex, the ancestor of every book on your shelf. The word comes from the Latin caudex, meaning tree trunk or block of wood, a memory of those wooden tablets.","In the first century CE, the Roman poet Martial praised small parchment codices of famous authors, telling readers that such a book was compact enough to carry on a journey and could be held in one hand. To him it was a handy new travel format, almost like a paperback.","The codex had many advantages. Both sides of every page could be used, so it held more text for the same amount of material. Its covers protected the pages. It stacked neatly on a shelf. Most importantly, you could flip straight to any page, which made it much easier to look things up, compare passages and add page references. Early Christians seem to have liked the codex especially for their sacred writings, perhaps because it was practical for carrying and for finding passages, though historians still debate exactly why.","The change happened gradually. By around 300 CE, codices and scrolls were roughly equal in number among surviving books from Egypt, and during the 400s and 500s the codex largely replaced the scroll for books in the Roman world. One of the great surviving codices from this period is the Codex Sinaiticus, a Greek Bible handwritten on parchment in the 300s CE. By the time the Roman Empire in the west was ending, the book had taken the basic shape it still has today: pages, a spine and covers."]},{"heading":"The Mystery of the Phaistos Disc","paragraphs":["Before we leave the ancient world, let us look at one of its strangest puzzles. On 3 July 1908, the Italian archaeologist Luigi Pernier was digging in the ruins of a Minoan palace at Phaistos on the island of Crete. In a storeroom he found a round disc of fired clay about 16 centimetres across, a little bigger than a CD. Both sides were covered with little pictures arranged in a spiral, running from the edge towards the centre.","The pictures include a walking man, a head wearing a plumed headdress, a fish, a bird, a ship, a bow and many other shapes. There are 45 different signs, used 242 times in total, divided into groups by lines. Here is what makes the disc so remarkable: the signs were not drawn or scratched by hand. Each one was pressed into the wet clay using a separate stamp, before the disc was fired. Every time the same sign appears, it looks almost exactly the same, because the same stamp made it.","That means someone, perhaps around 1700 BCE, though the date is uncertain and estimates range across several centuries, had a set of carved stamps, one for each symbol, and used them to 'type' a text into clay. It is a little like an ancient set of rubber stamps. Some people have called it the world's first printed document, or even an early form of movable type.","But we should be careful. Nobody has ever deciphered the Phaistos Disc, so we do not know what language it records, whether it is a prayer, a story or a list, or even whether it was made in Crete at all. It is the only object of its kind ever found. No other text made with the same stamps has turned up, and there is no sign of a workshop producing stamped documents. So the Phaistos Disc was a curiosity, a brilliant one-off experiment, not the start of a printing industry. Today it sits in the Heraklion Archaeological Museum on Crete, still guarding its secret.","By around 400 CE, then, the world had made huge progress. People had alphabets that were easy to learn, light rolls of papyrus, tough sheets of parchment, giant libraries and the codex, the book with pages. But every single book was still copied by hand, and the materials were costly. Scrolls and skins were expensive, but far to the east, someone was about to make a cheap new writing material from plants and rags."]}],"deepDive":"Historians love to argue about why the codex replaced the scroll. The numbers are clear enough: among surviving books from Roman Egypt, codices were a small minority in the first and second centuries CE, roughly matched scrolls by around 300 CE, and by the 400s and 500s far outnumbered them. The classic study by Colin Roberts and T. C. Skeat noticed that Christian texts from Egypt were almost always codices, even when most non-Christian literature was still copied on rolls. Some scholars think Christians chose the codex because it was cheaper, using both sides of the page; others think it was easier to search for passages during worship and debate; others suggest an early, influential Christian book was a codex and set the fashion. The truth may be a mix, and the evidence is limited mostly to what survived in Egypt's dry sands. A second debate concerns the alphabet. Older textbooks often credited the Phoenicians with inventing it, but the Sinai and Wadi el-Hol discoveries show that alphabetic writing existed hundreds of years earlier among Semitic speakers in and around Egypt, with the Phoenician version being one well-organised descendant. Linguists also argue about terms: because Phoenician had no vowel letters, many call it an abjad rather than a true alphabet, reserving alphabet for the Greek system that added vowels. Numbers about ancient libraries deserve skepticism too. Ancient authors often gave enormous rounded figures, such as 400,000 or 700,000 scrolls at Alexandria, perhaps to glorify the Ptolemies. Modern estimates are much smaller and difficult to pin down, since a single work could fill many rolls and one roll could hold several short works. Finally, the Phaistos Disc shows why historians separate an idea from an industry. The idea of stamping repeatable signs appears in seals, brick stamps and this disc, but it only changes the world when combined with cheap materials, enough demand, and a whole system of tools and workers, which is the story of the parts to come.","funFact":"The word alphabet comes from alpha and beta, the first two Greek letters, which go back to old Semitic words meaning ox and house.","check":{"q":"Why is papyrus not considered true paper?","a":"It is made from whole strips of reed pith laid in layers and pressed, not from fibres broken down into pulp","wrong":["It is made from animal skins","It was invented in China","It is made from clay pressed flat"]},"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Papyrus","publisher":"World History Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.worldhistory.org/Egyptian_Papyrus/"},{"title":"Alphabet","publisher":"World History Encyclopedia","url":"https://www.worldhistory.org/alphabet/"},{"title":"Library of Alexandria","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Alexandria"},{"title":"Proto-Sinaitic script","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proto-Sinaitic_script"},{"title":"Phoenician alphabet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoenician_alphabet"},{"title":"Parchment","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parchment"},{"title":"Codex Amiatinus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Codex_Amiatinus"},{"title":"Phaistos Disc","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaistos_Disc"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/02-1.jpg","alt":"Part of the Book of the Dead papyrus made for Nany, a singer of Amun in ancient Egypt, with columns of hieroglyphs.","file":"Book of the Dead for the Singer of Amun, Nany MET LC EG 213987.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Book_of_the_Dead_for_the_Singer_of_Amun,_Nany_MET_LC_EG_213987.jpg","author":"This file was donated to Wikimedia Commons as part of a project by the…","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/02-2.jpg","alt":"Side A of the Phaistos Disc from Crete, its symbols pressed into the clay with stamps in a spiral.","file":"Phaistos Disc - Side A - 6380 - crop1.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaistos_Disc_-_Side_A_-_6380_-_crop1.jpg","author":"C messier, edit by Bammesk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/02-3.jpg","alt":"The stone coffin of King Ahiram of Byblos in the National Museum of Beirut; its lid edge carries an early Phoenician inscription.","file":"Ahiram Sarcophagus 1.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahiram_Sarcophagus_1.JPG","author":"O.Mustafin","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4070},{"n":3,"title":"The Paper Revolution in China","when":"about 200 BCE – 700 CE","summary":"Long ago in China, people wrote on heavy bamboo sticks and costly silk. Then clever workers learned to turn mushy plant fibers and old rags into thin, light sheets: paper! It was cheap, it was smooth, and it soon spread to Korea and Japan.","sections":[{"heading":"Books You Needed a Cart to Carry","paragraphs":["Imagine you are a government clerk in China about 2,100 years ago. Your desk is not covered in sheets of paper. Instead it is stacked with bundles of thin bamboo strips, each one about as long as a ruler and as wide as a finger. You write on them with a brush dipped in black ink, one narrow column of characters per strip, from top to bottom. When a document is finished, the strips are tied together with cords, like a little bamboo fence, and then rolled up into a bundle.","These strips are called jiandu, which means something like 'slips and tablets'. Bamboo grew in huge forests across central and southern China, so it was easy to find. Workers cut the stems into strips, scraped them smooth and dried them over a gentle fire so they would not rot or crack or get eaten by insects. In the dry northwest, where bamboo did not grow, people used thin slips of wood instead. Archaeologists have dug up tens of thousands of these slips from tombs and old army outposts, many of them still readable.","Bamboo worked, but it had one enormous problem: weight. A single long book could fill dozens of bundles. An old Chinese history says that the First Emperor of Qin, who unified China in 221 BCE, set himself a goal of reading a fixed weight of documents every day before he rested, measured on a scale. Whether or not that story is exactly true, it tells us something real: Chinese rulers measured their paperwork in kilograms, because it really was heavy. Moving a library meant loading carts.","Bamboo books had other troubles too. The cords that held the strips together could rot or snap, and then the strips fell out of order. Scholars today spend years puzzling over ancient bamboo texts whose strips got jumbled in the ground, like a jigsaw puzzle with no picture on the box.","There was a lighter choice: silk. Chinese weavers had been making silk for thousands of years, and a piece of silk cloth could be written on, folded, and carried easily. In 1973 archaeologists opening a tomb at Mawangdui, in today's Hunan province, found silk sheets covered with writing and even maps, buried in 168 BCE. But silk was precious. It was one of the most valuable goods in the ancient world, traded all the way to Rome. Writing a long letter on silk was a bit like writing your homework on a fancy silk scarf. Only the rich could afford it often.","So China had two writing surfaces: one cheap but heavy, one light but expensive. What people really needed was something in between, as light as silk and as cheap as bamboo. That something turned out to be paper."]},{"heading":"Paper Before the Famous Inventor","paragraphs":["For a long time, schoolbooks said paper was invented in the year 105 CE by a man named Cai Lun. That date still matters, and we will meet Cai Lun soon. But the ground in China has been telling a different, more interesting story.","In 1986, archaeologists were excavating a group of tombs at a place called Fangmatan, near the city of Tianshui in Gansu province, in northwest China. In Tomb 5, which dates to the early part of the Western Han dynasty, around the 2nd century BCE, they found something remarkable. The man buried there had a map laid on his chest, and the map was drawn on paper. Water had seeped into the tomb over the centuries, so only a small scrap survived, only a few centimeters across, roughly the size of a domino. But on that scrap you can still see thin black lines showing mountains, rivers and roads.","That little scrap is often described as one of the oldest surviving pieces of paper in the world, and it is roughly 250 to 300 years older than Cai Lun's famous report. Historians date it to roughly 179 to 141 BCE.","Fangmatan is not alone. Other finds of early Han paper have turned up in different parts of China. At a site called Xuanquan, a Han dynasty relay station on the road near Dunhuang where messengers changed horses, archaeologists in the 1990s found many fragments of paper, a few of them with writing on them. Another find, from near the old capital city of Xi'an in 1957, is called Baqiao paper. Experts still argue about whether the Baqiao fragments are true paper or just a matted clump of fibers, which shows how careful scientists have to be.","Many of these early papers seem to have been rough and thick. Some were probably used for wrapping things, like medicines or bronze mirrors, rather than for writing. That makes sense: when a new material first appears, people often use it for simple jobs before they discover its best uses.","So paper was not invented in a single flash by one person. It grew slowly, probably over generations, in workshops where people noticed that fibers soaked in water could be laid out to make a thin, flexible mat. That is how many inventions really happen: lots of nameless people improving an idea a little at a time, until someone makes it good enough, and famous enough, to change the world."]},{"heading":"Cai Lun and His Report to the Emperor","paragraphs":["Now let us meet the man whose name is forever tied to paper. Cai Lun was born around the middle of the 1st century CE in Guiyang, a district in what is now Hunan province in southern China. As a young man he went to serve in the palace of the Eastern Han dynasty in the capital, Luoyang. He became a trusted court official and rose to an important job: he was put in charge of the imperial workshops, where skilled craftsmen made weapons, tools and fine objects for the emperor's household. Cai Lun was known for being careful and hardworking, and for paying close attention to how things were made.","In the year 105 CE, according to the official history of the Han dynasty, Cai Lun presented a report to Emperor He about a better way to make paper. The history explains the problem he was solving: bamboo was heavy and silk was expensive, and neither was convenient. Cai Lun's idea was to make paper from cheap things that were easy to find: the inner bark of trees, scraps of hemp, old rags of cloth, and worn-out fishing nets.","Think about how clever that list is. Rags and old fishing nets were trash. Hemp waste was left over after making rope and cloth. Tree bark grew everywhere. By using leftovers, Cai Lun turned rubbish into a valuable product. In a way, it was one of history's earliest recycling ideas.","The emperor was pleased. The history says that from then on, paper came into use everywhere, and people called it 'the paper of Marquis Cai', because Cai Lun was later given the noble title of marquis. Whether paper truly spread 'everywhere' right away is doubtful, since bamboo slips stayed in use for another couple of centuries. But Cai Lun's method, backed by the emperor, gave papermaking an official stamp of approval, better recipes, and a big push.","Cai Lun's own story did not end happily. Palace life in Han China was full of rivalries between powerful families. Years earlier, Cai Lun had been involved in a dispute that harmed the grandmother of the later Emperor An. When Emperor An took full power in 121 CE, he ordered Cai Lun to report to the authorities for punishment. Rather than face disgrace, Cai Lun dressed in his finest robes and ended his own life by taking poison. It is a sad ending for a man who gave the world such a useful gift.","Over the following centuries, Cai Lun was honored more and more. Papermakers in China came to treat him as the patron, or special protector, of their craft, and shrines and temples were built in his memory. In his hometown area, a memorial park still stands today. In 2008, during the opening ceremony of the Beijing Olympic Games, paper was celebrated in front of a worldwide audience as one of ancient China's great inventions.","Remember the fair way to say it: Cai Lun did not invent paper from nothing. Older paper already existed. What he did was improve the method, use better and cheaper materials, and present it to the emperor so it became widely known. That is still a huge achievement. And the official history that tells his story, the Book of the Later Han, was written in the 400s CE, more than 300 years after he lived, so historians read its details with some caution."]},{"heading":"How to Make Paper: The Science of Soggy Fibers","paragraphs":["Paper looks simple, but it is a small miracle of science. Let us follow the steps a papermaker in Han China might have used, and see what is happening at each stage.","Step one: gather fibers. Plants are built from tiny tubes and threads made of a material called cellulose. Cellulose is what makes celery stringy and wood strong. The best papermaking plants have long, tough cellulose fibers. In China, favorites were the inner bark of the paper mulberry tree, hemp, ramie (a kind of nettle) and later bamboo. Old rags and fishing nets were useful because they were already made of hemp or similar fibers.","Step two: soak and cook. The fibers were soaked in water and often boiled with wood ash or lime. Ash mixed with water makes an alkaline liquid that softens the plant material and loosens the gummy substances that hold fibers together in the plant. After cooking, the stuff would be soft and floppy.","Step three: beat it. Workers pounded the softened fibers with wooden mallets or stones, or with foot-powered hammers. Beating does two jobs. It separates the fibers from one another, and it frays their surfaces, so each fiber becomes fuzzy with tiny side threads, called fibrils. Those fuzzy edges are important, as we will see.","Step four: make the pulp. The beaten fibers were stirred into a big vat of water, making a thin, milky soup called pulp. Most of the soup is water; only a small part is fiber.","Step five: form the sheet. Here comes the clever part. The papermaker takes a mould, which is a frame with a screen stretched across it, a little like a sieve. Early Chinese papermakers sometimes used a frame covered with woven cloth, floated on the water, and poured pulp onto it. Later, they used screens made of very thin strips of bamboo that could be dipped straight into the vat and lifted out. As the screen rises, the water drains through the tiny gaps, but the fibers are caught on top, forming a thin, even layer, like a wet spiderweb of fibers crossing in every direction.","Step six: press and dry. The wet sheet is turned out onto a flat surface, pressed to squeeze out more water, and then dried, often by brushing it onto a warm wall or a board in the sun.","Now the big question: when the sheet dries, why does it hold together, with no glue at all? The answer lies in the cellulose molecules. Cellulose is covered with tiny groups of atoms that include oxygen and hydrogen. When the fibers are wet, water molecules sit between them. As the water evaporates, the shrinking film of water pulls the fibers closer and closer together, until their surfaces are almost touching. Then weak attractions between the molecules on neighboring fibers, such as hydrogen bonds and van der Waals forces, snap into place where the fibers cross. Each bond is incredibly weak, but there are trillions of them, and together they lock the whole tangled web into a strong, flexible sheet.","This also explains a fact you can test at home: wet paper tears easily. When you soak paper, water molecules slip back in between the fibers and break apart many of those tiny bonds. The fibers can then slide apart. When it dries again, many bonds re-form, but the sheet is usually wrinkly and weaker. The fuzzy, frayed fibrils made by beating matter because they create far more contact points between fibers, so the paper ends up stronger."]},{"heading":"A Visit to a Papermakers' Workshop","paragraphs":["No one filmed a Han dynasty paper workshop, of course, but we can piece together a good picture from old tools, surviving paper and later Chinese books that describe the craft. Let us imagine stepping inside one on a cool spring morning, somewhere beside a river in central China.","The first thing you notice is the water. Papermaking needs a lot of clean water, which is why workshops were built beside streams and rivers. Bundles of bark sit soaking in a pool, slowly softening. Nearby, a big pot of ash and water bubbles over a fire, and a worker stirs the steaming mass of fibers with a long pole. The air smells of wet wood and smoke.","In one corner, two workers take turns pounding a heap of cooked fibers on a flat stone. Thud, thud, thud. Hour after hour they beat, until the stringy mass becomes a soft, fluffy paste. It is hard, tiring work. Much later, Chinese papermakers used heavy hammers lifted by a foot-pedal or even by a waterwheel, which made beating much faster.","At the center of the room stands the vat, a big wooden or stone tub full of cloudy water. The most skilled worker in the workshop stands here. He dips his screen into the vat at a slight angle, scoops up pulp, lifts the screen level and gives it a gentle shake so the fibers spread evenly. Water rains back into the vat. In just a few seconds, a pale wet sheet has formed on the screen. Making every sheet the same thickness takes years of practice; a beginner's sheets come out lumpy, too thick in some places and full of holes in others.","The sheet is flipped onto a growing stack. When the stack is tall enough, it is pressed under heavy weights to squeeze out water. Then, one by one, the damp sheets are peeled off and brushed onto a smooth heated wall to dry. A few minutes later they can be peeled off, dry, flat and ready to use.","A good team could make hundreds of sheets in a day. Compare that with the time it took to cut, scrape, dry and bind bamboo slips, or to raise silkworms, spin their thread and weave silk cloth, and you can see why paper won.","Centuries later, in 1637, a Chinese scholar named Song Yingxing published an illustrated encyclopedia of crafts and technology called the Tiangong Kaiwu, often translated as 'The Exploitation of the Works of Nature'. Its woodcut pictures show every step of making paper from bamboo: soaking stalks in a pit, cooking them, pounding them, dipping the screen in the vat, pressing the stack and drying sheets on a heated wall. These pictures are some of the best views we have of traditional Chinese papermaking, and the basic steps they show had been worked out many centuries earlier."]},{"heading":"Why Paper Was Such a Big Deal","paragraphs":["Put a bundle of bamboo slips next to a stack of paper holding the same words, and the difference is shocking. The paper might weigh only a small fraction as much and take up far less space. Suddenly a scholar could carry a small library in a box instead of a cart.","Paper was also cheap, especially once workshops used rags, hemp scraps and fast-growing plants. It was smooth, so the soft tip of a writing brush glided across it. And it soaked up ink in just the right way. Chinese ink was made from soot mixed with animal glue and pressed into solid sticks; the writer ground the stick with a little water on a stone to make liquid ink. On good paper, this ink sank into the fibers and stayed sharp and black for centuries.","Paper could be cut to any size, folded, rolled into scrolls, pasted together into long sheets, or stacked. You could write big or small. Painters soon discovered it, and Chinese painting and calligraphy, the art of beautiful writing, grew up alongside paper.","Papermakers kept improving their product. They learned to coat paper with a thin layer of starch or other materials so that ink would not spread and blur; this coating is called sizing. They also dyed paper with a yellow liquid made from the bark of the Amur cork tree, called huangbo. The dye gave paper a golden color and helped keep away insects that liked to eat paper. Many old Buddhist scrolls from the Dunhuang caves are this golden-yellow color.","Change still took time. For two centuries or so after Cai Lun, Chinese officials used both bamboo and paper. By around the 300s CE, paper had become the normal material for writing, and bamboo slips were fading away. A famous step came around 404 CE, when a powerful warlord named Huan Xuan is said to have ordered his officials to use paper instead of bamboo slips for official documents. Some historians see this as the moment paper finally won.","Paper also found jobs nobody had imagined. During the Tang dynasty (618 to 907 CE), Chinese people made paper cups, paper napkins and folded paper bags for storing tea. Paper was used for windows, letting soft light into rooms while blocking wind, and for fans, kites, lanterns and umbrellas. Much later, in the Song dynasty, China printed the world's first government paper money. Few inventions have ever had so many uses.","Most important of all, paper made writing affordable for many more people. Students could practice their characters, merchants could keep records, and poets could jot down verses. More writing surface meant more writing, and more writing meant more copying, more letters and more books. That huge pile of words would one day create a demand for a faster way to copy them: printing."]},{"heading":"Paper Crosses to Korea and Japan","paragraphs":["China's neighbors soon wanted this marvelous material. Ideas and goods moved back and forth between China and the Korean peninsula for centuries, carried by merchants, officials, monks and soldiers. During the Han dynasty, China even governed parts of northern Korea for a time. Historians think papermaking knowledge probably reached Korea somewhere between the 3rd and 6th centuries CE, although no one knows the exact date.","Buddhism played a big part. This religion began in India and spread through Central Asia to China and then to Korea and Japan. Buddhist monks wanted to copy holy texts called sutras, and copying a sutra was considered a good and holy deed. That created an enormous demand for paper and ink. Korean papermakers became famous for their own paper, now called hanji, made from the inner bark of the paper mulberry tree. Hanji is known for being strong and long-lasting, and it is still made by hand in Korea today.","Japan's story has a well-known starting point. An ancient Japanese history called the Nihon Shoki, finished in 720 CE, says that in the year 610 a Buddhist monk named Damjing (called Donchō in Japanese) arrived from the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo. The history says he knew how to make paints, paper and ink, and also built a water-powered mill. For centuries this has been told as the moment papermaking arrived in Japan. But historians point out that the text does not clearly say he introduced papermaking for the first time, and Japan may already have had paper before 610. So it is best to call 610 the traditional date.","Either way, Japanese papermakers made the craft their own. Japanese paper is called washi, and it is often made from kozo, the Japanese name for paper mulberry bark, as well as other plants called mitsumata and gampi. Japanese makers developed a technique of rocking the mould back and forth in the vat so that long fibers crossed in many layers, making paper that is thin yet surprisingly tough. Some very old Japanese documents on paper still survive, kept for over 1,200 years in a treasure house at Nara called the Shōsōin. In 2014 UNESCO added traditional Japanese washi papermaking to its list of the world's Intangible Cultural Heritage.","By around the 600s CE, then, paper was being made across East Asia: in China, in Korea and in Japan. Each region used local plants and invented its own tools and styles, but they all shared the same basic recipe that Chinese workers had pioneered centuries earlier: beat fibers, mix them with water, catch them on a screen and let them dry.","In these same lands, in these same centuries, people were also carving seals and stamping charms and pictures, getting closer and closer to the idea of printing whole pages. That is a story for a later part of this Deep Dive. For now, keep in mind that without cheap paper, printing would never have caught on. You cannot print a million copies on silk, and you certainly cannot press a printing block against a bundle of bamboo strips.","Paper was ready. Over the next few centuries, it would begin its long journey west."]}],"deepDive":"Two debates sit underneath this chapter. The first is about credit. The traditional date of 105 CE comes from the biography of Cai Lun in the Book of the Later Han (Hou Hanshu), compiled by Fan Ye in the 400s CE, more than three centuries after the events. For a long time it was read as the moment paper was invented. Archaeology changed that. The Fangmatan map fragment (Tomb 5, excavated 1986) is generally dated to the early Western Han, around the 2nd century BCE, and Han-era paper has also been found at sites such as Xuanquan near Dunhuang. The Baqiao find from near Xi'an (1957) is more controversial: some specialists argue it is a loose mat of fibers rather than a sheet formed on a screen, which raises a useful question of definition. Most historians therefore describe Cai Lun as an improver and promoter, perhaps a standardizer of good recipes in the imperial workshops, rather than the first inventor. That is not a demotion: making a technology cheap, reliable and officially approved is often what actually changes the world. The second debate is about chemistry. Textbooks usually say paper holds together because hydrogen bonds form between the hydroxyl (OH) groups on neighboring cellulose fibers as the sheet dries, and capillary forces during drying pull the fibers into close contact. That explanation is a good first answer, and it neatly explains why wet paper loses most of its strength. But researchers still argue about the details. In a 2015 study in Scientific Reports, Ulrich Hirn and Robert Schennach of Graz University of Technology estimated the contributions of several mechanisms: hydrogen bonding, van der Waals forces, electrostatic (Coulomb) attraction, capillary bridges, mechanical interlocking of fibrils, and interdiffusion of molecules. Their model suggested that van der Waals forces may contribute more than hydrogen bonds, while all the mechanisms depend heavily on how much area of the fibers comes into true molecular contact. That is why beating, which frays fibers and increases contact area, matters so much for strength. The safe summary: many weak molecular attractions, including van der Waals forces and hydrogen bonds, glue a paper sheet together without any added adhesive.","funFact":"One of the oldest known pieces of paper in the world, from a tomb at Fangmatan in China, is a scrap of a map only a few centimeters across, roughly the size of a domino.","check":{"q":"What cheap materials did Cai Lun suggest for making paper in 105 CE?","a":"Tree bark, hemp waste, old rags and fishing nets","wrong":["Papyrus reeds and river mud","Sheepskin and calfskin","Wood pulp from pine logs"]},"sources":[{"title":"Comprehensive analysis of individual pulp fiber bonds quantifies the mechanisms of fiber bonding in paper","publisher":"Scientific Reports (via PubMed Central, NIH)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5377052/"},{"title":"Paper (1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica)","publisher":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, 11th edition, via Wikisource","url":"https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Paper"},{"title":"Pair of moulds used for making hand made paper","publisher":"Science Museum Group","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co160279/pair-of-moulds-used-for-making-hand-made-paper"},{"title":"Cai Lun","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cai_Lun"},{"title":"Science and technology of the Han dynasty","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_and_technology_of_the_Han_dynasty"},{"title":"Damjing","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damjing"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/03-1.jpg","alt":"A bronze bust of Cai Lun, the Han dynasty official honored for improving papermaking, at China's National Museum of Chinese Writing.","file":"Wzbwgcailun.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wzbwgcailun.png","author":"Caidiyun","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/03-2.jpg","alt":"Han dynasty bamboo slips with ink writing, found at Juyan, shown at the Chinese Writing Museum in Anyang.","file":"Han Bamboo Slips Discovered at Juyan (10195000756).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Han_Bamboo_Slips_Discovered_at_Juyan_(10195000756).jpg","author":"Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/03-3.jpg","alt":"An old Chinese woodcut: a papermaker dips a bamboo screen into a vat of pulp to lift out a new sheet.","file":"Five Steps of Papermaking - Step 3 - Casting the Paper Pulp - As described by Cai Lun in 105 CE.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Steps_of_Papermaking_-_Step_3_-_Casting_the_Paper_Pulp_-_As_described_by_Cai_Lun_in_105_CE.jpg","author":"Unknown author from the time of Ming dynasty","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3863},{"n":4,"title":"Paper Travels the World","when":"about 700 – 1400","summary":"Paper went on a long trip! It traveled along the Silk Road to Samarkand and Baghdad, where it filled giant libraries, then across North Africa to Spain and Italy. Italian papermakers even learned to hide secret pictures called watermarks inside their paper.","sections":[{"heading":"Merchants, Caravans and a Bag of Lost Letters","paragraphs":["In 1907, the explorer Aurel Stein was searching the ruins of an old Chinese watchtower in the desert west of Dunhuang, at the far western edge of China. In the rubble, he found a small bundle of letters, folded and tied, that had never been delivered. They were written on paper, in a language called Sogdian, and they dated to the early 300s CE. Today they are known as the Sogdian Ancient Letters.","The Sogdians were merchants from the region around Samarkand and Bukhara, in what is now Uzbekistan. They were the great traders of the Silk Road, the web of caravan routes that linked China with Central Asia, Persia, India and the Mediterranean. One of the letters was written by a woman named Miwnay, who had been left stranded in Dunhuang with her daughter and was furious with her husband for abandoning them. Another was a merchant's report about trouble in China. More than 1,600 years later, we can still read their worries, because they were written on paper.","Those letters tell us something important: paper was already traveling west in the hands of merchants hundreds of years before the Islamic world started making it. Silk Road towns such as Turfan, in today's western China, have also yielded many early paper documents. Paper moved the way most ideas moved on the Silk Road: slowly, from oasis to oasis, carried by traders, monks and travelers.","But carrying paper is not the same as knowing how to make it. For centuries, Central Asia and the lands further west mostly imported paper, or used other materials: papyrus in Egypt, parchment made from animal skin in the Mediterranean and Middle East, and even palm leaves and birch bark in India. The real turning point came when the skill of papermaking itself took root in the cities of Central Asia and the Islamic world."]},{"heading":"The Battle of Talas and the Story of the Captured Papermakers","paragraphs":["Our next stop is a battlefield. In the year 751, two great empires clashed near the Talas River, in a valley on the border of what are now Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan. On one side was the army of Tang dynasty China, led by the general Gao Xianzhi. On the other side was the army of the Abbasid Caliphate, a vast Muslim empire that had just come to power in the Middle East, together with its allies. During the fighting, some of the Tang army's allies switched sides, and the Chinese army was badly defeated. Many Chinese soldiers were taken prisoner.","Here is where a famous story begins. According to a tradition recorded by later Arab writers, some of these prisoners were papermakers, and they taught their skill in Samarkand. From there, the story says, papermaking spread across the Islamic world. Samarkand did become famous for its paper; a writer in the 11th century, al-Tha'alibi, praised Samarkand paper and repeated the story about the Chinese captives.","It is a great tale, but historians treat it carefully, and you should too. It is a tradition, not a proven fact. Archaeologists have found evidence that paper was already present in Central Asia well before 751, and Sogdian merchants had been using it for centuries. Papermaking may have been practiced in the Samarkand region before the battle. Also, the writers who told the prisoner story lived long after the battle.","Interestingly, one of the Chinese captives, a man named Du Huan, actually wrote about his experiences. He was taken west and lived in the Abbasid lands for about ten years before returning to China by sea. In his account, he mentioned Chinese craftsmen he met there, including weavers, but he did not mention papermakers. That does not prove the story is false, but it is one more reason to be careful.","What we can say for sure is this: by the second half of the 700s, papermaking was well established in Samarkand and the cities of Central Asia, and over the next decades it spread to the heart of the Islamic world. One clever change happened along the way. The paper mulberry trees used in China did not grow well in these dry lands, so papermakers there relied on fibers they had plenty of: linen and hemp, often in the form of old rags and ropes. Rag paper would become the standard paper of the Islamic world and later of Europe for about a thousand years."]},{"heading":"Baghdad: A City Built on Paper","paragraphs":["In 762, the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur founded a new capital on the Tigris River: Baghdad. It was built as a perfectly round city, with the caliph's palace and great mosque at the center, and it grew quickly into one of the largest cities in the world. Merchants, scientists, poets and scholars came from everywhere.","Running an enormous empire, stretching from North Africa to Central Asia, took mountains of paperwork: tax records, letters, orders, maps and accounts. Around 794 to 795, during the reign of the famous caliph Harun al-Rashid, Baghdad began making its own paper. Later writers credited the powerful Barmakid family of government ministers with encouraging paper in government offices.","Why did officials prefer paper to parchment? One reason was cost: parchment had to be made from expensive animal skins, while paper could be made from cheap rags. Another reason, mentioned by later historians, was security. On parchment, a dishonest person could scrape off ink with a knife and write something new, and the change could be hard to spot. On paper, ink soaks into the fibers, so scraping it off leaves an obvious damaged patch. A government document on paper was harder to forge.","Baghdad's paper fed a whole new world of books. The city had a famous Paper Sellers' Market, the Suq al-Warraqin, lined with shops. A warraq was a person who dealt in paper and books: a stationer, copyist and bookseller all in one. Customers could buy fresh paper, order copies of books, or sit and read. A 9th-century geographer, al-Ya'qubi, wrote that Baghdad had more than a hundred booksellers' shops.","One of Baghdad's best-known writers, al-Jahiz, who lived from about 776 to 869, wrote an essay about the drawbacks of parchment, after a patron asked him to produce a book on it. He complained that parchment was heavy, swelled up when damp, smelled bad, cost too much and was easy to tamper with. He clearly preferred paper, which was lighter and easier to carry.","In Baghdad, scholars also worked on translating books from Greek, Persian and Indian languages into Arabic, including works of mathematics, astronomy, medicine and philosophy by writers such as Aristotle, Euclid and Galen. This is often linked to the Bayt al-Hikma, the House of Wisdom, a library supported by the caliphs, especially Harun al-Rashid's son al-Ma'mun. Historians argue about how big or organized the House of Wisdom really was; some think it was mainly a palace library and translation office rather than a grand university. But everyone agrees that Baghdad became a world center of learning, and paper made it far cheaper to copy all those translations."]},{"heading":"Cairo, North Africa and a Treasure Room of Scraps","paragraphs":["From Iraq, papermaking spread west to Syria, where Damascus became an important paper center and trading hub, and then to Egypt. Egypt is a fascinating case, because it was the home of papyrus, the reed-based writing material that people there had used for thousands of years. You might expect Egyptians to stick with papyrus. Instead, paper slowly pushed papyrus aside, and by around the 900s and 1000s papyrus was falling out of use. Paper was simply more flexible, could be made from cheap rags, and did not depend on reeds growing in the Nile marshes.","Cairo grew into one of the great book cities of the medieval world. In the 1040s, a Persian traveler named Nasir-i Khusraw visited Cairo and was amazed to see that shopkeepers in the market wrapped the goods they sold, such as vegetables, spices and hardware, in paper. In most of the world at that time, paper was still too valuable to throw away like that. In Cairo it was common enough to use as wrapping.","Cairo also gives us one of the most astonishing paper treasure troves on Earth. In the Ben Ezra Synagogue in Fustat, the old part of Cairo, the Jewish community had a storeroom called a geniza. Jewish tradition said that writings containing the name of God should not be thrown away, so for many centuries people placed old papers there: letters, contracts, shopping lists, school exercises, prayers, medical prescriptions and much more. The dry Egyptian air preserved them. When scholars studied the Cairo Geniza in the late 1800s, they found hundreds of thousands of fragments, many of them on paper, giving an incredibly detailed picture of daily life around the Mediterranean in the Middle Ages.","Paper also changed mathematics. Earlier, calculations were often done on a dust board, a tray of sand where numbers could be drawn and quickly wiped out. With cheap paper, people could write out their calculations step by step and keep them. The Hindu-Arabic numerals we use today, the digits 0 to 9, spread through the Islamic world partly through books like those of the Baghdad mathematician al-Khwarizmi, whose name gives us the word 'algorithm'. Paper helped these numbers travel.","Across North Africa, paper mills grew up in cities such as Fez in Morocco, which by the 1100s was an important papermaking center. Libraries grew enormous. Tales from the time describe the library of the Umayyad ruler al-Hakam II in Córdoba, Spain, in the 900s as holding hundreds of thousands of volumes. Historians think such numbers may be exaggerated, but even if the real count was smaller, these were some of the largest libraries in the world at the time, filled with books on paper.","One fair question is why the Islamic world, which loved paper and books so much, did not adopt printing for many centuries. Historians have suggested several reasons, including the great respect and beauty of handwritten Arabic calligraphy, especially for the Qur'an, the shapes of Arabic letters that join together in many forms, and the strength of a large, skilled copyist profession. We will meet the first Ottoman Turkish printing press much later in this Deep Dive."]},{"heading":"Into Europe Through Spain and Sicily","paragraphs":["Europe met paper through its southern doorways. Much of Spain, which Muslims called al-Andalus, was under Muslim rule for centuries, and the island of Sicily was ruled by Muslims in the 800s to 1000s before being conquered by Norman knights. Through these lands, paper and papermaking crossed into Europe.","Some of the oldest known documents on paper in Europe come from the Norman rulers of Sicily in the early 1100s. The paper itself was probably imported from the Islamic world, but it shows that European rulers were already using it.","The first big papermaking center on European soil was the town of Xàtiva (in Spanish, Játiva), near Valencia in eastern Spain. Around the middle of the 1100s, the geographer al-Idrisi wrote that Xàtiva made paper so good that it could not be found anywhere else in the civilized world, and that it was sent east and west. In 1244 the Christian king James I of Aragon conquered Xàtiva, but the papermakers, many of them Muslims, kept working, and the king's government used and taxed their paper.","Not everyone in Europe trusted the new material at first. In 1231, the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II, who ruled Sicily and southern Italy, ordered that official public documents should be written on parchment, not paper. He worried that paper would not last as long. For important legal documents that had to survive for centuries, many officials kept using parchment for a long time.","But paper had a huge advantage in price, and as Europe's towns grew, so did the need for cheap writing material. Merchants needed account books, notaries needed registers, students needed notebooks, and governments needed copies of letters. Around the 1100s, a French abbot, Peter the Venerable of Cluny, described books made from scraps of old rags, one of the first European mentions of rag paper. Papermaking spread from Spain into southern France by the late 1100s or 1200s. But the place that truly made European paper famous was a small town in the hills of central Italy."]},{"heading":"Fabriano: The Town That Upgraded Paper","paragraphs":["Fabriano sits in the Marche region of central Italy, in a green valley with fast streams running down from the Apennine Mountains. Papermills were set up there by the 1270s, and by the 1300s Fabriano was the most successful papermaking center in Europe. Its workers did not just copy the Islamic and Spanish methods. They improved them in three important ways.","The first upgrade was power. Beating rags into pulp by hand was slow, exhausting work. Fabriano's papermakers used waterwheels turned by the river to drive rows of heavy wooden hammers, often tipped with iron or nails, that rose and fell into troughs full of wet rags. These were called stampers. Several hammers worked side by side, pounding day and night as long as the water flowed. It was an early example of a machine doing factory work.","The second upgrade was sizing. Remember that paper fibers soak up ink like a sponge, and without sizing, ink spreads and blurs. Islamic papermakers had usually sized their paper with starch, often made from wheat or rice. Fabriano's makers used gelatin instead, a jelly-like glue made by boiling animal hides, hooves and bones. Each dried sheet was dipped into warm gelatin and dried again. Gelatin sizing made the paper stronger and smoother, gave a better surface for quill pens and European inks, and helped protect it from damage over time. Starch also attracted insects and mold, while gelatin did so less.","The third upgrade was the mould itself. Instead of screens made from bamboo or reeds, Italian makers built moulds with a screen of fine metal wires stretched across a wooden frame. Thin wires ran close together in one direction, held in place by thicker wires further apart in the other direction. That stiff wire screen made it possible for two workers to form sheets very quickly: one dipped moulds into the vat, while another flipped each fresh wet sheet onto a piece of felt. Stacks of sheets and felts were then squeezed in a big screw press to remove water.","Fabriano also used high-quality linen and hemp rags, and its river water was very pure. Modern scientists who have studied old Fabriano paper think its high calcium content and clean water help explain why so much of it is still in good condition today, more than 600 years later. Fabriano paper later supplied printers in Venice, Rome, Florence and Naples. Today the town has a Paper and Watermark Museum, where visitors can watch paper being made the old way. And the Fabriano company still makes paper, including some kinds used by artists."]},{"heading":"Watermarks: Secret Pictures Inside the Paper","paragraphs":["Fabriano's most famous invention was a hidden one. Try this: take a sheet of good writing paper or a banknote and hold it up to a bright window. You may see a faint picture or pattern glowing inside the paper. That is a watermark, and the earliest known European watermarks come from Italy, around 1282.","Here is how they work. Remember the wire screen on the mould. A papermaker would bend a piece of wire into a shape, such as a circle, a cross, a letter, a bull's head, a flower or a set of scales, and sew it onto the screen. When the mould was dipped into the vat, the pulp settled all across the screen, but where the wire shape stuck up, a little less pulp could settle. So the finished paper was slightly thinner along the lines of the wire design. Hold the sheet up to light, and those thinner areas let more light through, so the design glows.","If you look closely at old handmade paper against the light, you will also see faint lines across the whole sheet: many close-together lines and a few widely spaced ones. These come from the thin and thick wires of the mould screen. Experts call them laid lines and chain lines. Paper with this pattern is called laid paper.","Why did papermakers want watermarks? Mostly as a trademark, to show which mill made the paper and to advertise its quality. Watermarks could also show the size or grade of a sheet. Some historians point out that in Italy watermarks also helped show that paper was made locally rather than imported, which could matter for trade and church rules.","Watermarks turned out to be a gift to future historians. Each mould's wire design was slightly different, and moulds wore out and were replaced every year or two. So by comparing a watermark in an undated document with watermarks in dated documents, experts can often work out roughly when and where the paper was made. A Swiss scholar named Charles-Moïse Briquet spent decades collecting tracings of thousands of watermarks from European archives, and his great catalog, published in 1907, is still used today to date old papers and books, including early printed ones.","Watermarks can even reveal the secrets of famous books. When experts examined the paper copies of the Gutenberg Bible, which you will meet later in this Deep Dive, they found watermarks that helped show the paper had been imported over the Alps from northern Italy, probably from mills around Caselle in Piedmont. So the story of printing in Germany depended on paper made in Italian river valleys, using skills that had traveled all the way from China.","Watermarks are still with us. Many banknotes have a watermark portrait you can see against the light, because it is very hard to copy with an ordinary printer. A trick invented in medieval Italy is still helping to stop forgers today."]},{"heading":"Mills Across Europe and a Hunger for Rags","paragraphs":["From Italy, papermaking spread across Europe during the 1300s, usually carried by Italian craftsmen who were hired to set up new mills. A mill was established at Padua in 1340, and there were others around Bologna, Genoa and Venice. In France, more and more mills appeared, especially around Troyes in the northeast. In 1390 a Nuremberg merchant named Ulman Stromer founded one of the first paper mills in Germany, bringing in Italian workers to run it. He even made his workers swear to keep the methods secret.","England was slow to make its own paper and mostly bought it from abroad. Paper was in use there by the early 1300s, but the first known English papermaker, John Tate, did not start his mill near Hertford until the late 1400s.","All these mills had one big need: rags. European paper was made mostly from worn-out linen and cotton cloth, and it would stay that way until the 1800s. Rag collectors went from house to house and town to town, buying old shirts, sheets and sacks. The rags were sorted, cut into small pieces and left to soak and rot slightly in damp heaps, which loosened the fibers, before going under the stamping hammers. It was smelly work. In later centuries, rags became so valuable that some governments banned their export.","Linen had become much more common in Europe in the later Middle Ages, as people wore linen underclothes and used linen sheets. That meant more old linen for papermakers, and more paper. Over the 1300s and 1400s, paper became steadily cheaper than parchment. A sheep's skin could make only a few large sheets of parchment, but a mill could turn out thousands of sheets of paper from carts of rags.","Think how far paper had come. It had started as rough wrapping material and maps in Han dynasty China. It had traveled along the Silk Road in merchants' letter bags, been made in Samarkand and Baghdad, filled the libraries of Cairo and Córdoba, crossed into Spain and Sicily and been upgraded in Fabriano with waterwheels, gelatin and watermarks. By 1400, mills in Italy, France, Spain and Germany were turning out paper in large quantities.","Europe now had a cheap, smooth surface that was perfect for writing and, as it would turn out, perfect for printing. But in East Asia, people had already found a way to stamp whole pages onto paper at once."]}],"deepDive":"The westward journey of paper is a good case study in how historians separate stories from evidence. The Talas narrative, in which Chinese prisoners taken by Abbasid forces in 751 teach papermaking in Samarkand, appears in Arabic sources such as al-Tha'alibi (early 11th century), written roughly 250 years after the battle. Against it stands physical evidence: paper letters carried by Sogdian merchants in the early 4th century, early paper documents from Turfan and other Silk Road sites, and paper finds in Central Asia that predate 751. Historians such as Jonathan Bloom, in Paper Before Print (2001), argue that papermaking probably spread gradually through Central Asia, and that the more important event was the decision of the Abbasid state to adopt paper on a large scale in Baghdad from the 790s. In that view, the real driver was demand from a huge bureaucracy and a booming book trade, not a single group of captives. A second debate concerns materials and dating. Medieval European writers called Eastern paper charta bombycina or charta damascena, and for a long time scholars assumed it was cotton paper. Microscopic analysis has shown that most early Islamic and European papers were made mainly from linen and hemp rag fibers; 'damascena' probably reflects Damascus as a trading center. Dating European paper relies heavily on watermarks. The earliest known European watermarks date from about 1282 in Italy. Because a pair of moulds typically lasted only a year or two before wires bent or broke and were repaired, a watermark's exact shape can date paper quite closely. Charles-Moïse Briquet's Les Filigranes (1907) catalogued roughly 16,000 watermark tracings, and digital databases today extend his work. Finally, there is the question of why paper spread faster in Europe than parchment could be replaced in law. Rulers like Frederick II (1231) restricted paper for official documents because of doubts about durability. Ironically, good gelatin-sized rag paper from the medieval period often survives in better condition than the acidic wood-pulp paper made in the 1800s and 1900s, a problem this Deep Dive returns to later.","funFact":"In the 1040s the Persian traveler Nasir-i Khusraw was amazed to see shopkeepers in Cairo wrapping vegetables and spices in paper, at a time when paper was still precious in most of the world.","check":{"q":"How does a watermark become visible in a sheet of paper?","a":"A wire design on the mould makes the paper slightly thinner there, so it shows when held up to light","wrong":["It is painted on with invisible ink after the paper dries","It is pressed in with a hot iron stamp","It is printed with a special white ink"]},"sources":[{"title":"Fabriano: City of Medieval and Renaissance Papermaking (lecture by Sylvia Rodgers Albro)","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://loc.gov/preservation/outreach/tops/albro/index.html"},{"title":"Connected Histories: al-Jahiz and paper","publisher":"Bridging Cultures: Muslim Journeys (National Endowment for the Humanities / American Library Association)","url":"https://bridgingcultures-muslimjourneys.org/items/show/156"},{"title":"Paper (1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica)","publisher":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, 11th edition, via Wikisource","url":"https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Paper"},{"title":"Pair of moulds used for making hand made paper (with watermark)","publisher":"Science Museum Group","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co160279/pair-of-moulds-used-for-making-hand-made-paper"},{"title":"Battle of Talas","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Talas"},{"title":"Cairo Geniza","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cairo_Geniza"},{"title":"Paper mill","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paper_mill"},{"title":"Du Huan","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Du_Huan"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/04-1.jpg","alt":"The papermaker from Jost Amman's Book of Trades (1568): he dips a mould into the vat while a boy carries paper.","file":"Fotothek df tg 0002100 Ständebuch ^ Handwerk ^ Handel ^ Papierer ^ Papierhändler ^ Papiermacher.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fotothek_df_tg_0002100_St%C3%A4ndebuch_%5E_Handwerk_%5E_Handel_%5E_Papierer_%5E_Papierh%C3%A4ndler_%5E_Papiermacher.jpg","author":"Deutsche Fotothek","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/04-2.jpg","alt":"A long letter on paper written in Sogdian, found in the Bezeklik caves near Turpan on the Silk Road.","file":"Sogdian-language Manichaean Letter.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sogdian-language_Manichaean_Letter.jpg","author":"Shahryâr Zâdag","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/04-3.jpg","alt":"A paper mould from Sweden's Klippan mill; the wire crown, shield and name sewn onto it make watermarks in each sheet.","file":"Paper mold with Klippan watermark, Malmohus County coat of arms, and date 1573, Klippan paper mill, 1894-1902, TM10884 - Tekniska museet - Stockholm, Sweden - DSC01573.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paper_mold_with_Klippan_watermark,_Malmohus_County_coat_of_arms,_and_date_1573,_Klippan_paper_mill,_1894-1902,_TM10884_-_Tekniska_museet_-_Stockholm,_Sweden_-_DSC01573.JPG","author":"Daderot","license":"CC0"}],"words":3682},{"n":5,"title":"Woodblocks: Printing a Whole Page at Once","when":"about 600 – 1100","summary":"In China, people learned to carve a whole page of writing backwards into a block of wood, ink it, and press paper on top. Suddenly one carved block could make hundreds of copies! The oldest printed book with a date on it, the Diamond Sutra from the year 868, was found hidden in a secret cave.","sections":[{"heading":"Stamps, Seals and Stone Copies","paragraphs":["Imagine you are a clerk in an office in China about 1,500 years ago. On your desk sits a little square seal carved from jade or bronze. When you dip it in red paste and press it onto a document, your official's name appears, perfectly, every single time. You have done this a thousand times. Have you ever stopped to think that you are printing?","Most of the pieces needed for printing were sitting around in China long before anyone printed a book. Seals were the first piece. Chinese officials, merchants and artists had used carved seals for well over a thousand years. A seal is carved in reverse, so that when it is pressed down the characters come out the right way round. That trick, carving a mirror image, is the very heart of printing. But a seal is small. It can say a name, not a whole page.","The second piece was the stone rubbing. In 175 CE, during the Han dynasty, scholars had the most important Confucian classics carved onto large stone tablets and set up outside the imperial academy in the capital, Luoyang, so that students everywhere would copy the correct, official wording. Later, people discovered a clever way to copy carved stones without writing anything. They laid damp paper over the stone, pressed it gently into the carved grooves, and then dabbed ink over the surface with a pad. The ink touched only the flat parts of the stone, so the carved characters came out as white letters on a black background. A rubbing was a perfect copy of the stone, made in minutes.","The third piece was paper itself, which you met earlier in this story. By the 600s CE, paper was common and cheap in China. And the fourth piece was ink. Chinese ink was made from soot, collected from burning pine wood or oil, mixed with animal glue and pressed into sticks. Ground up with water on an inkstone, it made a rich black liquid that soaked nicely into paper.","Seals carved in reverse, rubbings that copied a whole surface, cheap paper and good ink: put them all together and you get woodblock printing. Nobody knows the name of the person who first did it. It probably was not one person at all, but many carvers, monks and craftspeople trying ideas over many years. Historians think it was happening in China by the 600s or 700s, during the Tang dynasty, one of the richest and most outward-looking periods in Chinese history.","Cloth was printed even earlier. Pieces of silk with flower patterns stamped on them from carved blocks survive from the Han dynasty, before 220 CE. So people knew how to stamp a repeating pattern long before they used blocks to make whole pages of words."]},{"heading":"How to Carve a Page Backwards","paragraphs":["Let's step into a Tang dynasty print workshop and watch how a page is made. It is a quiet place. There are no big machines, no levers or screws. The most important tools are a sharp knife, a few chisels, a brush, a block of wood and a very steady hand.","Step one is the writing. A skilled scribe, chosen for beautiful handwriting, writes the text neatly on a sheet of very thin paper. Usually the page is laid out in columns, read from top to bottom and right to left, with lines ruled to keep everything straight.","Step two is the clever part. The block of wood is smoothed flat and brushed with a thin layer of paste, often made from rice. The carver lays the thin paper on top, face down. Because the paper is so thin, the ink shows through from the back, and now the characters can be seen in mirror image on the wood. The carver rubs the paper gently and may wet it, so that the ink of the characters sticks to the wood. When the paper is peeled or rubbed away, a backwards copy of the page is left on the block.","Step three is the long, slow carving. The carver cuts away every bit of wood that is not part of a character. When the work is done, the characters stand up from the surface like little ridges, raised a few millimeters above the hollowed-out background. Printers call this kind of surface relief, because the image stands up in relief. Carvers usually chose hard woods with a fine, even grain, such as pear or jujube wood, because they could hold tiny details without splitting. A single page might take a skilled carver a few days. Records from much later, in the 1600s, suggest a carver might finish around one hundred to one hundred and fifty characters a day.","Step four is printing, and this is surprisingly fast. The printer brushes water-based ink over the raised characters. A sheet of paper is laid carefully on top. Then the printer rubs the back of the paper with a dry, flat brush or pad, pressing it down onto the inked ridges. No press is needed. The paper is peeled off, and there is the page, with every character the right way round. The printer inks again, lays down another sheet, rubs, and peels. By some accounts, a skilled printer could make well over a thousand prints in a single day.","Notice something important: East Asian paper was thin and soft, so it was usually printed on only one side. To make a book, each printed sheet was folded in half with the blank side inside, and the folded sheets were stacked and sewn along the open edges. That is why old Chinese books have pages that are double-layered, with the fold at the outer edge.","There is also some sneaky science here. Water-based ink works beautifully on wood, because wood soaks up a little of the ink and then releases it evenly when paper presses down. Remember that, because in a later chapter you will see that this same ink did not work well on metal type, and a printer in Europe had to invent a different, oily ink."]},{"heading":"A Charm in a Korean Pagoda","paragraphs":["In October 1966, workers were repairing an old stone pagoda at Bulguksa, a famous Buddhist temple near the city of Gyeongju in South Korea. A pagoda is a tower-like monument, and this one, called Seokgatap, had been built around 751 CE, when the Silla kingdom ruled much of the Korean peninsula. Thieves had damaged the pagoda, and so experts took it apart to repair it.","Inside, in a hollow space in the stone, they found a reliquary, a special box for holy objects. And inside that, rolled up and partly damaged by centuries of damp and insects, was a narrow paper scroll printed with Chinese characters. It was a Buddhist prayer text called the Mugujeonggwang Great Dharani Sutra. A dharani is a kind of sacred chant or spell, which Buddhists believed could protect people and bring blessings. Making copies of such a text and placing them inside pagodas was thought to earn great merit.","The scroll is long and skinny: about 6.2 meters long, but only about 8 centimeters tall, so it would wrap around your arm many times. It was printed from woodblocks, and its characters are small and neat. Because it was sealed inside a pagoda finished around 751, it must have been printed before then. The text itself was translated into Chinese in about 704 or 705, so the print was almost certainly made sometime in the half-century between then and 751.","That makes it one of the oldest printed texts anywhere in the world. Some unusual character shapes in it were officially used in China only during the reign of Empress Wu Zetian, who ruled until 705, which helps support the early date. Historians do argue about one thing: where it was printed. Many Korean scholars believe it was printed in Silla, while some Chinese scholars think it was printed in China and carried to Korea. Either way, it shows that by the first half of the 700s, woodblock printing was being used to spread holy texts across East Asia.","It is a good reminder that the oldest surviving objects are not always the first ones ever made. The Bulguksa scroll survived because it was sealed away in stone. Countless other early prints were simply used, worn out, burned or thrown away."]},{"heading":"One Million Tiny Pagodas","paragraphs":["Now cross the sea to Japan, to the city of Nara in the 760s. Nara was Japan's capital then, full of grand Buddhist temples with sweeping wooden roofs. In 764 a dangerous rebellion broke out, led by a powerful minister called Fujiwara no Nakamaro, who is also known as Emi no Oshikatsu. The rebellion was crushed, and the ruler, Empress Shōtoku, wanted to give thanks and ask the Buddha for protection.","Her plan was enormous. She ordered one million small wooden pagodas to be made, each one about 21 centimeters tall, roughly the height of a school ruler standing on its end. They were turned on a lathe, a spinning tool for shaping wood, and painted white. Each pagoda had a hollow center, and inside each one was placed a tiny rolled-up paper strip printed with a Buddhist charm, or dharani. The project is known as the Hyakumantō Darani, which means 'one million pagoda dharani'.","Four different dharani texts were printed, each on a narrow strip of paper. Experts still argue about exactly how they were printed. Many think the printers used carved wooden blocks; others think they used plates cast from bronze or another metal. Either way, the job was printing, on an almost unbelievable scale for the time. The work took about six years, from around 764 to 770, and the pagodas were shared out among major temples, traditionally said to be ten.","Did they really make a full million? Historians are not completely sure, and some think 'one million' may have been a grand, round number. But hundreds of thousands were certainly made. Tens of thousands of the little pagodas still survive at Hōryū-ji, one of the temples that received them, which is also home to some of the oldest wooden buildings in the world. Many of the paper charms inside were lost over time, but thousands survive, nearly 4,000 at Hōryū-ji alone, and museums around the world, from Tokyo to New York and London, own a few.","The Hyakumantō Darani are among the oldest surviving printed texts whose date we know fairly well. They also show us something important about why early printing happened in East Asia. In Buddhism, copying holy words and spreading them was believed to bring blessings. The more copies, the more merit. Printing was the perfect tool for that: it made copying almost endless."]},{"heading":"The Secret Library Cave","paragraphs":["Our next stop is Dunhuang, an oasis town on the edge of the Gobi Desert in western China. For centuries it was a busy stop on the Silk Road, where camel caravans rested before crossing the deserts. Just outside the town, carved into a long cliff, are the Mogao Caves: hundreds of Buddhist cave temples filled with painted walls and statues, made between the 300s and the 1300s.","Around the year 1000, someone filled one small side-chamber of a cave with tens of thousands of old manuscripts, paintings and printed papers, and then sealed it up with a wall. Nobody is sure why. Maybe it was to protect them from invaders. Maybe it was a storeroom for worn-out holy texts, which could not just be thrown away. Then the hidden room was forgotten for about nine hundred years. The dry desert air kept the paper safe.","In 1900, a Taoist monk named Wang Yuanlu was looking after the caves and clearing away drifted sand. He noticed a crack in a painted wall, and behind it he found the sealed chamber, packed from floor to ceiling with scrolls. It held tens of thousands of items, perhaps as many as 50,000, in Chinese, Tibetan, Sanskrit, Uyghur and other languages. Today it is called the Library Cave.","In 1907 the explorer Aurel Stein, a Hungarian-born scholar working for British India, arrived at Dunhuang. He persuaded Wang Yuanlu, with payments of money, to let him take away thousands of manuscripts and paintings. Among them was one long printed scroll. Stein's way of getting these treasures is still hotly debated. Many people in China see it as taking away part of their heritage, and others later did the same, including the French scholar Paul Pelliot.","That scroll is the Diamond Sutra, now kept at the British Library in London. It is about five meters long, made of several panels of paper pasted together, and printed from carved woodblocks. At the start is a beautifully detailed picture: the Buddha sitting on a lotus throne under a canopy, speaking to an elderly disciple named Subhuti, surrounded by monks, guardian figures and even a pair of lions. It is one of the oldest dated printed illustrations in the world, and the carving is so fine that it shows woodblock printing was already a mature, skilled craft, not a new experiment.","At the very end of the scroll is a short note called a colophon. Translated, it says roughly that Wang Jie had this made, reverently, for free distribution to everyone, on behalf of his two parents. It also gives the date: the 15th day of the 4th month of the 9th year of the Xiantong reign period. In our calendar that is 11 May 868.","We know almost nothing else about Wang Jie. But because of his loving gift to his parents, his name sits on the oldest surviving printed book with an exact date. It is a book printed nearly 600 years before Gutenberg's Bible. And since the Diamond Sutra is so skillfully made, we know printing had been going on for a long time before it."]},{"heading":"A Printing Boom in China","paragraphs":["After the Diamond Sutra, the clues come thicker and faster. In the 800s, printed books were being sold in market towns, especially in Sichuan in the southwest and around the lower Yangtze River. Printers made calendars, almanacs for telling fortunes, dictionaries, Buddhist prayers and books on dreams and medicine. Printed calendars from the 870s and 880s were found in the Dunhuang cave too.","Calendars were such hot sellers that they caused trouble. In China, the calendar was official business: the emperor's astronomers decided the dates of festivals and the start of seasons. In 835, an official complained that private printers in Sichuan were selling their own calendars before the government's calendar came out, and the government ordered private calendar printing to stop. It may be one of the first attempts in history to control what people printed.","Then came a giant step. In 932, during a troubled time called the Five Dynasties, a senior minister named Feng Dao began a project to print the whole set of Confucian classics, the books every student and official had to know. Scholars carefully checked the text against the stone classics. The work took more than twenty years, until the 950s. For the first time, the most important books for government and education were printed officially, so that students across the country could own exactly the same, correct text.","Under the Song dynasty, which began in 960, printing exploded. Between 971 and 983, craftsmen in Chengdu in Sichuan carved the Kaibao Tripitaka, the complete collection of Buddhist scriptures, using about 130,000 woodblocks. Printed books of history, poetry, medicine, farming and mathematics followed. Government offices, Buddhist temples and private businesses all printed. Some Song printing houses became family firms lasting generations.","One famous writer of the Song dynasty, the poet Su Shi, noted in the 1070s that older scholars had struggled to find copies of books, but that now merchants were printing all kinds of books and producing huge numbers of pages every day. Students had more books than ever, he said, though he grumbled that they did not read them as carefully as before! Every age, it seems, has someone worrying that easy books make lazy readers."]},{"heading":"Eighty-One Thousand Blocks in the Mountains","paragraphs":["Our last stop in this chapter is Haeinsa, a Buddhist temple on the slopes of Mount Gaya in South Korea. Climb the stone steps at the top of the temple and you reach two long, plain wooden halls. Through their slatted windows you can glimpse rows and rows of wooden shelves, and on them, standing on their edges like books in a giant library, are about 81,000 carved woodblocks. Together they are called the Tripitaka Koreana, and they are the most complete set of Buddhist scriptures carved in wood that survives anywhere in the world.","The story behind them is dramatic. Korea, then ruled by the Goryeo dynasty, had carved an earlier set of Buddhist scripture blocks in the 1000s. But in 1232 Mongol armies invaded, and that first set was destroyed by fire. The Korean court had fled to Ganghwa Island to escape the Mongols. King Gojong ordered a complete new set to be carved, hoping that this huge act of devotion would win the Buddha's protection for the country.","The carving took place mostly between about 1237 and 1248. Teams of carvers prepared each block with great care. Tradition says the wood was birch, but scientific studies suggest much of it came from wild cherry and pear trees. Tradition also says the logs were soaked and boiled, then slowly dried so it would not crack or warp. Each block is about 70 centimeters long and 24 centimeters high, a few centimeters thick, carved on both sides, and weighs around 3 kilograms. Each side has 23 lines of 14 characters. The handwriting is so even that people once believed a single person carved it all, but historians now think a team of about thirty skilled carvers did the work. The whole collection holds around 52 million characters and weighs about 280 tonnes.","It was also edited very carefully. A monk named Sugi compared different versions of the scriptures and corrected errors, writing a long record of his changes. People often say the Tripitaka Koreana has no mistakes at all, but that is a myth: careful modern checks have found some missing or wrong characters. It is still astonishingly accurate for so many millions of hand-carved characters.","In 1398 the blocks were moved to Haeinsa, and they have stayed there for more than 600 years. Their storage halls, called Janggyeong Panjeon, are a marvel of science without machines. The windows on each side are different sizes, placed to let air flow through and keep the blocks dry. The floors are made of earth mixed with charcoal, lime and salt, which soak up moisture when the air is damp and give it back when it is dry. The halls survived wars and even a fire in 1817 that burned much of the temple. Experts once moved some blocks into a modern building with air conditioning, as a test, but they started to suffer, so the old halls were trusted again.","The blocks are on UNESCO's Memory of the World Register, and the halls are a UNESCO World Heritage Site. And here is the amazing thing: the blocks can still print. Because a woodblock keeps its whole page ready, it can be inked and printed again centuries later. That is one of woodblock printing's great strengths."]},{"heading":"Why Woodblocks Worked So Well","paragraphs":["Woodblock printing had some big advantages. It was cheap to start: you did not need a big machine, just wood, knives, brushes, paper and ink. It could print anything the carver could cut: complicated Chinese characters, pictures, maps, diagrams and decorations, all on the same block. And the blocks could be stored. A publisher could print fifty copies this year, put the blocks on a shelf, and print fifty more when people asked for them, without setting anything up again.","This mattered a great deal for Chinese, which uses thousands of different characters. An everyday reader might know a few thousand characters, and a scholar many more. Carving a page as one block meant the carver simply cut whatever characters were needed. There was no need to keep a huge stock of separate pieces, sorted and ready.","But there were weaknesses too. Carving was slow: a long book could need thousands of blocks and years of work. Blocks took up a great deal of space. Wood could crack, warp, rot or be eaten by insects, and fires were a constant danger. And if a carver made a mistake, fixing it meant cutting out the wrong character and fitting in a little plug of wood with the right one.","All over East Asia, woodblock printing remained the most common way to make books for well over a thousand years, right into the 1800s. In Japan, woodblocks were later used to make the famous colorful ukiyo-e prints, like Hokusai's Great Wave. Even today, artists carve and print woodblocks by hand. The idea that began with seals and stone rubbings turned out to be one of the longest-lasting technologies humans have ever made.","Still, think about that carver bent over the block, cutting character after character, day after day, and then doing it all again for the next page, and the next, for thousands of pages. A single slip of the knife could spoil a day's work. Carving a whole new block for every page was slow. And in the middle of the 1000s, a clever commoner in China looked at all those carved pages and asked a brilliant question: what if each character was its own tiny piece?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did printing start in East Asia rather than somewhere else? Historians point to a combination of conditions rather than a single genius. China had cheap paper centuries before most of the world, a long tradition of carved seals and stone inscriptions, a state that valued standard texts for its examinations and administration, and Buddhism, whose teachings promised merit for multiplying sacred words. Dating the very first print is hard, because early prints were cheap everyday items that rarely survived. The oldest survivors are religious objects that were deliberately sealed away: the Bulguksa dharani (between about 704 and 751), the Hyakumantō Darani (about 764 to 770) and the Diamond Sutra (868). There are scholarly debates around each. For the Bulguksa scroll, Korean and Chinese scholars disagree on whether it was printed in Silla or in Tang China and imported. For the Hyakumantō Darani, specialists argue whether the charms were printed from wooden blocks or metal plates, and whether a full one million were really produced. For the Diamond Sutra, the debate is ethical rather than technical: Aurel Stein's removal of Dunhuang manuscripts in 1907, partly through payments to the cave's caretaker, is regarded by many in China as a loss of national heritage, and today the International Dunhuang Project works to reunite the scattered collections digitally. The economics of woodblocks also deserve attention. Most of the cost was paid once, in carving; after that, each extra copy needed only paper, ink and a few seconds of rubbing. Because blocks could be stored and reprinted on demand, publishers did not have to guess how many copies to make in advance. That is one reason woodblock printing remained the main printing method in China, Korea and Japan until the 1800s, even after movable type was invented there. Finally, scale matters for judging impact: by the Song dynasty, official, temple and commercial printers together were producing books on a scale no hand-copying culture could match, centuries before Europe printed its first page.","funFact":"The Tripitaka Koreana woodblocks at Haeinsa are carved with about 52 million characters, and after more than 750 years they can still be inked and printed.","check":{"q":"What is the oldest surviving printed book that has an exact date printed in it?","a":"The Diamond Sutra, dated 868","wrong":["The Gutenberg Bible, printed in 1455","The Tripitaka Koreana, carved in the 1200s","The Book of Kells, made around 800"]},"sources":[{"title":"Five Things to Know About the Diamond Sutra, the World's Oldest Dated Printed Book","publisher":"Smithsonian Magazine","url":"https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/Five-things-to-know-about-diamond-sutra-worlds-oldest-dated-printed-book-180959052/"},{"title":"Diamond Sutra","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_Sutra"},{"title":"Tripitaka Koreana","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripitaka_Koreana"},{"title":"Woodblock printing","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodblock_printing"},{"title":"Movable type","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movable_type"},{"title":"Library Cave","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_Cave"},{"title":"Hyakumantō Darani","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyakumant%C5%8D_Darani"},{"title":"Seokgatap","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seokgatap"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/05-1.jpg","alt":"The woodblock-printed picture at the start of the Diamond Sutra from Dunhuang (868), showing the Buddha preaching.","file":"Diamond sutra.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diamond_sutra.jpg","author":"International Dunhuang Project","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/05-2.jpg","alt":"One of Japan's “One Million Pagodas” (about 764–770), a small wooden pagoda made to hold a printed prayer charm.","file":"百万塔・百万塔陀羅尼-One of the “One Million Pagodas” (Hyakumanto) and Invocation MET 30 47a-c 203140.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%99%BE%E4%B8%87%E5%A1%94%E3%83%BB%E7%99%BE%E4%B8%87%E5%A1%94%E9%99%80%E7%BE%85%E5%B0%BC-One_of_the_%E2%80%9COne_Million_Pagodas%E2%80%9D_(Hyakumanto)_and_Invocation_MET_30_47a-c_203140.jpg","author":"This file was donated to Wikimedia Commons as part of a project by the…","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/05-3.jpg","alt":"Rows of Tripitaka Koreana woodblocks stored on wooden shelves in the airy halls of Haeinsa Temple, South Korea.","file":"Tripitaka Koreana 02.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripitaka_Koreana_02.jpg","author":"Bernard Gagnon","license":"CC0"}],"words":3898},{"n":6,"title":"Movable Type Is Born in Asia","when":"about 1040 – 1450","summary":"A Chinese craftsman named Bi Sheng had a brilliant idea: make every character its own little piece of baked clay, so the pieces could be rearranged and used again and again. Later, people in Korea cast letters out of metal, and in 1377 they printed a book called Jikji that still survives today. A Korean king even invented a whole new, easy alphabet so more people could read.","sections":[{"heading":"A Commoner with a Big Idea","paragraphs":["Picture a busy Chinese city in the 1040s, during the Song dynasty. The streets are full of shops selling silk, tea, porcelain and printed books. In the back of one workshop, a man is pressing soft, sticky clay into small squares and carving a single character into each one with a fine blade. His name is Bi Sheng, and he is about to invent movable type.","Movable type means separate pieces, one for each character, that can be arranged to make a page, printed, and then taken apart and rearranged to make a completely different page. Compare that with a woodblock, where the whole page is carved as one piece and can only ever print that one page. With movable type, the same character can be used over and over in thousands of different books.","We know very little about Bi Sheng himself. He was a commoner, an ordinary person with no official rank, not a famous scholar or nobleman. We do not know exactly where he lived, though some historians link him to the area around Hangzhou or Hubei, and a tombstone found in Hubei province in 1990 has been suggested as his, though not everyone agrees. His life dates are often given as about 970 to 1051, but these too are uncertain.","So how do we know about him at all? Thanks to one curious, brilliant writer: Shen Kuo. Shen Kuo was a government official, astronomer, engineer, mapmaker and all-round scientist. Late in his life, around 1088, he retired to a garden estate he called Dream Brook, and wrote down hundreds of short notes on everything that fascinated him: fossils, magnetism, music, medicine, rainbows, inventions. The collection is called the Dream Pool Essays, and it is one of the great science books of medieval China. It is also where we find one of the first known written descriptions of the magnetic compass needle.","In one entry, Shen Kuo explains that during the Qingli reign period, which ran from 1041 to 1048, a commoner named Bi Sheng invented movable type. Shen Kuo describes the method so carefully that we can almost watch Bi Sheng at work. Without that single entry, Bi Sheng's name might have been lost forever. Shen Kuo also tells us that after Bi Sheng died, his type was kept by people Shen Kuo knew, who treasured it."]},{"heading":"How Bi Sheng's Clay Type Worked","paragraphs":["Here is the method, step by step, as Shen Kuo described it.","First, Bi Sheng made the type. He took sticky clay and carved one character on each small piece, with the character raised and backwards, just like on a woodblock. Each piece was thin, about as thin as the edge of a coin. Then he baked the pieces in a fire to make them hard. Baked clay is called ceramic, the same family of material as a flowerpot or a bathroom tile. So Bi Sheng's type is sometimes called ceramic type.","Second, he prepared the printing plate. He took a flat iron plate and covered it with a sticky mixture of pine resin, wax and paper ash. Then he set an iron frame on top of the plate, like the edge of a tray.","Third, he set the type. Inside the frame, he packed the characters tightly together in the right order, line after line, until the frame was full. A full frame made one page.","Fourth came the clever bit. He held the plate near a fire. The heat softened the wax and resin underneath, making them gooey. Then he pressed a smooth, flat board down on top of the type, which pushed all the pieces to exactly the same height, so the top surface was as flat as a whetstone. When the plate cooled, the wax and resin hardened again and held every character firmly in place, like tiles in cement. Now it could be inked and printed just like a woodblock.","And when the printing was finished? He warmed the plate again, the wax softened, and the type could simply be brushed or picked off with a hand, clean and ready to use again. Nothing was wasted.","Bi Sheng also thought like an efficient factory manager. He kept two formes, two trays of type, going at once. While one was being printed, the other was being filled with type for the next page. When the first was done, the second was ready, so the work never had to stop. Common characters, such as the little grammar words zhi and ye, appear many times on a page, so he made twenty or more copies of each. For rare characters that he did not have, he carved a new one on the spot and baked it in a straw fire.","To find characters quickly, he stored the type in wooden cases, sorted by how the characters sounded, using rhyme groups, with paper labels on each group. That is a little like sorting your toy bricks by color so you can find the right one fast.","Shen Kuo explained why Bi Sheng did not use wood. Wood has a grain, so it soaks up ink unevenly, and wood swells when it gets wet, so the pieces would come out uneven and stick to the wax. Baked clay did not have these problems. Shen Kuo also noted that printing just two or three copies with movable type was not quick. But for dozens, hundreds or thousands of copies, it was amazingly fast."]},{"heading":"Wang Zhen's Spinning Tables","paragraphs":["About 250 years after Bi Sheng, China was ruled by the Yuan dynasty, founded by the Mongol emperor Kublai Khan. In the town of Jingde, in what is now Anhui province, the local magistrate was a man named Wang Zhen. A magistrate was a bit like a mayor, judge and tax collector rolled into one. Wang Zhen was especially interested in farming and machines, and he was writing a giant book about agriculture.","Wang Zhen decided to try movable type, but made of wood. Remember that Bi Sheng had rejected wood because of its grain and swelling. Wang Zhen found ways around these problems. He had skilled craftsmen carve characters onto a smooth wooden board, then saw the board into separate pieces, one character each, and trim them all with a small saw and knife until they were the same height and size. To fix the type in a page, he packed the lines with thin strips of bamboo and wedged everything tight with small wooden pieces, instead of using wax.","The biggest problem was finding characters. Wang Zhen's set had tens of thousands of pieces. How could a typesetter find the right one among so many? His answer was wonderfully practical: two large round tables that could spin, a bit like a giant lazy Susan. The characters were arranged on them by rhyme group, following the official rhyme book used for examinations, and each one was numbered. One table held the main characters, and the other held the most common ones. A typesetter sat between them and turned the tables to bring the right section within reach. In Wang Zhen's own description, one person read out the numbers from a list while another picked the type. As he put it, the type comes to the person, rather than the person walking to the type.","In 1298, Wang Zhen used his wooden type to print a local history of Jingde county, a text of more than 60,000 characters. Around 100 copies were finished in about a month. That was fast work for the time. Proud of his system, he wrote a careful description of it and added it to his great farming book, the Nong Shu, which means Treatise on Agriculture, published in 1313. Because he wrote it down so clearly, his method could be copied by others.","Wooden movable type spread beyond China, too. In the Dunhuang caves that you visited in the last chapter, explorers found hundreds of wooden type pieces for the Uyghur language, a Turkic language of Central Asia, which are thought to date from around 1300. Uyghur uses an alphabet, so each piece of type could be a letter or a whole word. It is a hint that movable type was travelling along the Silk Road too.","In the late 1400s, Chinese printers in the Wuxi area, such as Hua Sui and his family, began printing books with bronze type. So by the end of the period in this chapter, China had experimented with type made of clay, wood and metal."]},{"heading":"Korea Casts Metal Letters","paragraphs":["Now we cross the Yellow Sea to Korea. In the 1200s Korea was ruled by the Goryeo dynasty, and it had some of the best metal workers in Asia. Goryeo craftsmen cast beautiful bronze bells, mirrors, Buddhist statues and coins. They also had a problem: they needed books, but many had been lost in wars, and Korea's Buddhist temples and royal court wanted copies of many different texts, each in small numbers.","Cutting a whole set of woodblocks for a book you only need twenty copies of is a big waste of effort. Movable type made more sense, and metal type would be stronger and longer-lasting than clay or wood. So Korean craftsmen began casting type from metal.","The earliest clue comes from a writer named Yi Gyu-bo. In a collection of his works, he mentions that a book of ritual rules called Sangjeong Gogeum Yemun, meaning Detailed Rites of Past and Present, was printed with metal type, in 28 copies, according to the record. That happened around 1234, while the Goryeo court was sheltering on Ganghwa Island from the Mongol invasions, the same invasions that destroyed the first Tripitaka Koreana. Sadly, no copy of that book survives, so we only know about it from the written record.","How did Korean craftsmen cast their type? Different methods were used over time. One method, described in the 1400s by the Korean scholar Seong Hyeon, went like this. First, characters were carved onto pieces of soft wood such as beech. These wooden characters were pressed into trays of fine, damp sand, leaving a character-shaped hollow. Then molten bronze was poured into channels in the sand, filling every hollow. When the metal cooled, the sand was broken away, and out came a branch of metal characters, like fruits on a stem. Each character was snapped off and filed smooth. Another method used carved beeswax shapes covered in clay, which were heated so the wax melted out, leaving a perfect mould. Experts think something like this wax method may have been used for Jikji.","Korean metal type was not quite like the type later made in Europe. The pieces were not all exactly the same height, and they were held in place with beeswax and later with bamboo and paper, so pages could sometimes come out a little uneven. And Korean printers usually rubbed the paper with a brush, just like with woodblocks, rather than using a press. Printing with metal also needed a different ink. Water-based ink tends to bead up on smooth metal, like rain on a car window, so Korean printers worked to develop inks that would stick better to their bronze type."]},{"heading":"Jikji: The Oldest Surviving Metal-Type Book","paragraphs":["Our next scene is Heungdeok Temple, just outside the city of Cheongju in central Korea, in the summer of 1377. Monks are busy in a workshop, arranging small bronze characters into pages and carefully rubbing paper across them. They are printing a book of Zen Buddhist teachings collected by a respected monk named Baegun, who had died a few years earlier. His students, the monks Seokchan and Daljam, are in charge, and the project is being paid for by a Buddhist nun named Myodeok.","The book's full title is very long, so everyone calls it by its short name: Jikji. On the last page, a printed note records that it was printed with cast metal type at Heungdeok Temple in the 7th month of 1377. That is about 78 years before the Gutenberg Bible was finished in Mainz.","If you look closely at the pages, you can see that this was not a perfect operation. Some lines lean a little. Some characters are printed more darkly than others, and a few are upside down or did not print fully. Those little wobbles are actually useful evidence: they show the pages really were made from separate pieces of type, not from a single carved block.","Jikji was printed in two volumes, but only the second survives, and it is missing its first page. Its journey since then has been a long one. In the late 1800s, a French diplomat in Seoul named Victor Collin de Plancy collected hundreds of old Korean books and took them to France, including Jikji. It was shown at the great Paris World's Fair of 1900. Later it was sold to a collector named Henri Vever, who left it to the National Library of France in his will. It entered the library's collections in 1952, where it is still kept today.","For years, few people realized how important it was. Then a Korean historian named Park Byeong-seon, who worked at the National Library of France, studied it closely and showed that it was the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type. It was displayed at an exhibition for the International Book Year in Paris in 1972, and the world took notice. In 2001, UNESCO added Jikji to its Memory of the World register. UNESCO even created a prize for protecting the world's documentary heritage called the UNESCO/Jikji Memory of the World Prize.","Many Koreans would love Jikji to come home, and there are ongoing discussions between Korea and France about it. Meanwhile, the city of Cheongju has built the Cheongju Early Printing Museum near the site where archaeologists in 1985 found remains of Heungdeok Temple, including a metal object inscribed with the temple's name, which confirmed where the temple had stood."]},{"heading":"King Sejong and an Alphabet for Everyone","paragraphs":["In 1392 a new dynasty, the Joseon, took over Korea, with its capital at Hanyang, today's Seoul. The Joseon kings believed books were essential for good government, and they took printing very seriously. In 1403, King Taejong set up an official type foundry, a workshop for casting type, and it produced a new set of bronze type known as Gyemija, named after the year in the traditional calendar. Over the following centuries the royal foundry cast many new sets of type.","Taejong's son became one of the most admired rulers in Korean history: King Sejong the Great, who reigned from 1418 to 1450. Sejong loved learning. He gathered top scholars into a research institute called the Hall of Worthies, and he supported inventions such as rain gauges, sundials and water clocks. In 1434 his foundry cast a famous, elegant set of type called Gabinja. Records say that improvements made under Sejong, such as making type that fitted together more tightly, allowed printers to produce many more pages in a day than before.","But Sejong noticed a deep problem. Koreans spoke Korean, but they wrote using Chinese characters, called Hanja in Korean. Korean and Chinese are very different languages, so writing Korean in Chinese characters was awkward. Learning thousands of characters took years of study, which only wealthy families with free time could afford. Most farmers, craftspeople and servants could not read or write at all. Printing more books did not help people who could not read them.","So Sejong did something extraordinary. He created a brand-new alphabet, designed from scratch specially for the Korean language. He presented it to his court around the end of 1443, and in 1446 he announced it to the country in a document called Hunminjeongeum, which means The Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People. Today the alphabet is called Hangul.","In his preface, Sejong explained, in words that are often translated roughly like this, that because Korean differs from Chinese, many ordinary people had things they wanted to say but could not write them down, and that he felt sorry for them and so made twenty-eight new letters for everyone to learn easily and use every day.","Hangul is brilliantly scientific. Some of the consonant letters were designed to show the shape of the mouth, tongue or throat when making the sound. For example, the letter for the 'g' or 'k' sound shows the back of the tongue lifting to touch the back of the mouth, and the letter for 'm' is a square, like closed lips. Extra strokes are added to show related sounds. The vowels are built from three simple symbols: a dot for heaven, a flat line for earth and an upright line for a person. Letters are grouped into square blocks, one block per syllable, so written Korean looks neat and even, and fits nicely into printing.","Sejong's scholar Jeong In-ji wrote in the 1446 commentary that a clever person could learn the letters in a morning, and even a slower learner could manage it in about ten days. The original alphabet had 28 letters. Four have dropped out of use, so modern Korean uses 24 basic letters: 14 consonants and 10 vowels.","Not everyone was pleased. Some scholars argued that Korea should stick with Chinese characters, the language of learning across East Asia, and many educated people kept writing in Chinese for centuries. In 1504 a cruel king, Yeonsangun, even banned the use of Hangul for a time after people used it to write criticisms of him. But Hangul survived, especially among women, merchants and ordinary readers, and today it is a great source of national pride. In South Korea, 9 October is celebrated as Hangul Day."]},{"heading":"Why Movable Type Did Not Sweep China","paragraphs":["Here is a puzzle. China invented movable type around 1040, about 400 years before Europe. Korea cast metal type by the 1200s. So why didn't movable type quickly take over all printing in East Asia, the way it later took over in Europe?","The first reason is the number of characters. A European printer needs only a few dozen letters, plus capitals, numbers and punctuation. But Chinese uses thousands of different characters, and a good printer needed many copies of the common ones. Wang Zhen's set ran to tens of thousands of pieces. Making, sorting, storing and finding so many pieces took a great deal of work and space. Even with spinning tables, it was a giant job.","The second reason is that woodblocks were good, cheap and flexible. Wood was easy to find, and carvers were skilled and not expensive to hire. A woodblock could hold text and pictures together, and it could be stored and reprinted whenever new copies were needed. With movable type, once a page was printed, the type was taken apart; if you wanted more copies a year later, you had to set the whole page all over again. For many publishers, printing small batches over many years made woodblocks the smarter choice.","The third reason is that East Asian printing was mostly done by hand-rubbing, not with a heavy press. That worked very well with wood and water-based ink, but less well with metal type, where a press and oily ink would have helped. In Korea, metal type was mainly used by the royal foundry to print official books and classics in fairly small numbers, rather than by many competing businesses selling cheap books to the public.","Movable type did not disappear, though. Chinese printers kept using wooden and bronze type for some projects. In the 1700s, the Qing dynasty emperor Qianlong ordered a huge set of more than 250,000 wooden type pieces to print a large collection of rare books, and earlier in that century a gigantic encyclopedia had been printed with bronze type. Both methods, woodblocks and movable type, existed side by side for centuries.","It is a great lesson about inventions. The best technology is not always the newest one. It is the one that fits the job, the language, the materials and the money available. In East Asia, woodblocks fitted beautifully. In a place with a small alphabet, plenty of customers and a tradition of screw presses, movable type would fit even better.","And meanwhile, far to the west in Europe, books were still being copied by hand, one letter at a time. Universities were growing, merchants were learning to read, and demand for books was exploding."]}],"deepDive":"Who invented movable type? On the evidence, the honor belongs to Bi Sheng in China in the 1040s, and the first metal movable type to Goryeo Korea, with a recorded use around 1234 and the surviving Jikji of 1377. Gutenberg's achievement in the 1450s was different: a complete system for casting large numbers of precisely uniform type with a hand mould, an oil-based ink and a screw press, built for an alphabet of a few dozen letters and a commercial market. Historians still debate whether knowledge of Asian printing reached Europe. Paper and block-printed playing cards and paper money certainly travelled west, and the Mongol Empire linked Eurasia in the 1200s and 1300s, but no document shows Gutenberg knew of Korean or Chinese type, and most scholars treat his work as an independent invention. Other debates concern the evidence itself. Bi Sheng is known almost entirely from one passage by Shen Kuo, so details of his life are uncertain. Some researchers have doubted whether fired clay type could have worked well; modern experiments and Tangut Buddhist texts from Western Xia that show features of clay type suggest it could. In Korea, a 2022 study argued that one surviving Buddhist text, Song of Enlightenment, may have been printed with metal type around 1239, which would make it older than Jikji; this claim is not universally accepted. Finally, the economics: movable type has a high fixed cost (thousands of sorts) but a low cost per new title, while woodblocks have a high cost per title but keep the page ready for reprinting. With thousands of Chinese characters and a market that bought books in small, repeated batches, woodblocks were often the rational choice. Hangul shows another route to cheap reading: reducing the number of symbols, rather than speeding up the copying of them.","funFact":"Jikji, the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, was printed in Korea in 1377 and is kept today in the National Library of France in Paris.","check":{"q":"What material did Bi Sheng use to make his movable type in the 1040s?","a":"Baked clay (ceramic)","wrong":["Cast bronze","Carved jade","Lead, tin and antimony"]},"sources":[{"title":"Bi Sheng","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bi_Sheng"},{"title":"Wang Zhen (inventor)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Zhen_(inventor)"},{"title":"Movable type","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movable_type"},{"title":"History of printing in East Asia","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_printing_in_East_Asia"},{"title":"Jikji, a treasure of the world of printing","publisher":"Bibliothèque nationale de France","url":"https://bnf.fr/en/jikji-treasure-world-printing"},{"title":"Jikji","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jikji"},{"title":"Hunminjeongeum","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunminjeongeum"},{"title":"Hangul","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hangul"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/06-1.jpg","alt":"Two pages of the Jikji (1377), the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, kept in Paris.","file":"Jikji pages.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jikji_pages.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/06-2.jpg","alt":"Wang Zhen's revolving type table from his book Nong Shu (1313): printers spun it to find wooden characters.","file":"Chinese movable type 1313-ce.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_movable_type_1313-ce.png","author":"User:Parhamr","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/06-3.jpg","alt":"Pages of the Hunminjeongeum (1446) introducing new Hangul letters like ㄱ, ㅋ and ㄷ, each explained in Chinese characters.","file":"Hunminjeongeum Haerye 03.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunminjeongeum_Haerye_03.jpg","author":"Government of Joseon","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3673},{"n":7,"title":"Europe's Hand-Copied Books","when":"about 500 – 1450","summary":"For about a thousand years, every book in Europe was written out by hand with a feather pen. Monks painted pages with gold and bright blue, and one book could take months or even years to finish. By the 1400s so many people wanted books that the scribes simply could not keep up.","sections":[{"heading":"A Room Full of Scratching Quills","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into a stone room in a monastery about 1,200 years ago. It is quiet except for a soft scratch, scratch, scratch. Along the walls, men in plain robes sit at slanted desks, each bent over a sheet of creamy animal skin. Each holds a feather pen in one hand and a small knife in the other. Some squint in the grey light from narrow windows, because candles were a fire risk near precious books. This room is a scriptorium, which simply means a place for writing. For most of the Middle Ages, rooms like this one were where Europe's books were born.","In Part 6 we saw Bi Sheng in China and the printers of Korea arranging movable type. Europe, at the very same time, had no printing of text at all. If you wanted a book, somebody had to copy it, letter by letter, from another book. That is what the word manuscript means: written by hand, from the Latin manus (hand) and scriptus (written).","Why monasteries? After the Western Roman Empire broke apart in the 400s, many of the old Roman schools and libraries faded away. Christian monasteries became some of the steadiest places of learning. Many followed the Rule of Saint Benedict, written in Italy in the 500s, which set aside hours each day for reading. Monks needed books for prayer, for church services and for study, so copying became part of holy work. A monk who copied a Bible was believed to be doing something good for his soul, and some scribes wrote little prayers at the end asking readers to remember them.","Monks did not only copy religious books. They also copied grammar books, histories, medical texts and works by Roman writers such as Virgil and Cicero. A great many ancient Latin texts that we can still read today survived only because medieval scribes kept copying them, generation after generation. Without those patient copyists, huge pieces of ancient knowledge would simply have vanished.","Let us look at a scribe's tools. His pen was a quill, made from a large flight feather, often from a goose. The feather was hardened, and the tip was cut with a penknife into a sharp, slanted nib. The nib wore down quickly, so the scribe kept his knife close by to reshape it. The same knife scraped away mistakes from the surface of the skin, and it also held the page flat while he wrote. Museums even have pictures of scribes with a quill in the right hand and a knife in the left, ready for action.","The ink was usually iron-gall ink. To make it, people collected oak galls, which are hard, round lumps that grow on oak trees when tiny wasps lay their eggs inside. The galls were crushed and soaked to release a chemical called tannic acid. Mixed with a compound of iron, the liquid turned a deep black. A sticky tree sap called gum arabic thickened it so it flowed nicely from the pen. Iron-gall ink soaks into the skin and becomes part of it, which is one reason so many medieval words are still crisp and dark today.","Before writing a single word, the scribe prepared the page. He pricked tiny holes down the edges with a sharp point, then drew faint guide lines between them using a ruler and a hard point or a lead stylus. These lines kept the writing straight and the columns tidy. If you look closely at old manuscripts, you can often still see those little pinpricks and ghostly ruled lines.","And the writing surface? Not paper, for most of this period. It was parchment, made from the skins of calves, sheep or goats. Skins were soaked in lime, scraped clean of hair and flesh, stretched tight on a frame, and dried. Very fine calfskin is called vellum. Parchment was tough and smooth, but it was costly, because each animal gave only a few large sheets. Little flaws in the skin sometimes stretched into holes, and careful scribes simply wrote around them."]},{"heading":"Gold, Blue and Dragons: Illuminated Manuscripts","paragraphs":["Some manuscripts were plain, made for everyday study. Others were works of art so dazzling that they are among the most treasured objects on Earth. These are called illuminated manuscripts. To illuminate means to light up, and that is exactly what gold and silver did when candlelight flickered across the page.","Making an illuminated page was teamwork. Usually the scribe wrote the text first, leaving blank spaces for pictures and big decorated first letters, called initials. Then an illuminator, sometimes a different person entirely, filled in the spaces. To add gold, the artist first painted a raised cushion of a chalky, glue-like paste called gesso. When it was just slightly sticky, he laid on a sheet of gold leaf so thin that a breath could blow it away. Then he rubbed it hard with a polished stone or even a dog's tooth until it gleamed like real metal, because it was real metal.","Colors came from all over nature. Blue could come from woad, a plant grown in Europe. A soft pink could come from madder roots. Green could come from verdigris, the greenish crust that forms on copper. Red often came from red lead, and a brilliant yellow came from orpiment, a mineral that contains arsenic, so painters had to be careful. The most precious blue of all was ultramarine, ground from the stone lapis lazuli, which came mainly from mines in what is now northern Afghanistan, thousands of kilometers away. Ultramarine could cost more than gold, so only the richest patrons could afford pages glowing with it.","Pigments were ground into powder and mixed with a binder such as egg white or gum so they would stick to the page. An artist might have small shells or pots of each color on the desk, and would build up a picture in thin layers. The result could be scenes from the Bible, portraits of saints, tiny animals climbing up the margins, or knights fighting snails. Yes, snails. Medieval artists loved silly and surprising little pictures in the margins, and historians still argue about what some of them mean.","The most famous illuminated manuscript of all may be the Book of Kells. It contains the four Gospels of the Bible in Latin, and it was made around the year 800. Most scholars think it was begun at the monastery on Iona, a small island off the west coast of Scotland, and it may have been finished at Kells in Ireland. After a Viking raid on Iona in 806, many monks moved to a new monastery at Kells, which is how the book got its name. Some scholars have suggested other places, such as northern England, so its exact birthplace is still debated.","The Book of Kells has about 340 leaves, called folios, of calfskin vellum, and each page today measures about 33 by 25.5 centimeters. Experts think about four main scribes copied the text and about three artists painted the great decorated pages. Its patterns are astonishing: spirals inside spirals, knots that twist without ever ending, and tiny cats, mice, birds and lions hidden among the letters. On its most famous page, the first letters of Christ's name in Greek, which look like the letters X and P, explode into a whirlwind of decoration.","Scientists at Trinity College Dublin, where the book is kept, have studied its colors with modern instruments. They found that its blue probably came from indigo or woad, not lapis lazuli as people once believed. That is a good reminder that even famous facts can change when scientists look more closely.","Interestingly, the Book of Kells contains quite a lot of copying mistakes. Experts think it was made mainly for show, to be displayed on the altar on special days like Easter, rather than read aloud every day. It was such a treasure that in about 1006, as old Irish records called the Annals of Ulster report, it was stolen for its jeweled cover. The book was found later, but its precious cover was gone forever. Today the Book of Kells is bound in four volumes, two are usually on display, and over half a million visitors a year come to see it in Dublin."]},{"heading":"Months of Work for a Single Book","paragraphs":["How long did it take to copy a book by hand? It depended on the book, the scribe and how fancy it was. A short schoolbook might take a skilled copyist a few weeks. A complete Bible, which is very long, could keep a scribe busy for many months, and a lavishly illuminated Bible could take a team years. Scribes worked only in daylight, often stopped for prayers and church services, and in winter cold could stiffen their fingers.","Copying was hard on the body, too. Scribes sometimes left complaints in the margins or at the end of a book. One old manuscript, kept today in Paris, contains a line that is often translated as 'Three fingers write, the whole body suffers.' Others grumbled about bad ink, thin parchment, the cold, or simply being hungry. Reading these notes, you can almost hear a tired scribe sighing over his desk.","At the end of a book, a scribe sometimes wrote a colophon, a short note saying who copied it, where, and for whom. For example, an Old English copy of the Gospel of Matthew from the early 1000s has a note in Latin from a scribe named Aelfric, who says he wrote it at Bath for a man named Brihtwold. Notes like this are precious to historians, because most medieval book-makers never wrote their names at all.","Books were built from bundles called quires. A scribe or workshop took several large sheets of parchment, folded them in half, and nested them together, a bit like a stack of folded birthday cards tucked inside each other. Each quire might be written by a different scribe working at the same time, which sped things up. When all the quires were done, a binder sewed them together onto strong cords or leather straps and attached them to wooden boards, which were often covered in leather. Some covers on special books were decorated with metal, carved ivory or jewels.","Now think about cost. The parchment alone for a big book could need the skins of many animals. Then there were the scribe's wages or keep, the ink, the colors, the gold, and the binding. A large, decorated book might cost as much as a house or a farm. Ordinary books were cheaper, but still far beyond what most working people could afford. Books were so valuable that many libraries chained them to the desks or shelves so that nobody could walk off with them.","Hand-copying had another problem: errors. Every time a text was copied, the scribe might skip a line, misread a word or repeat a phrase. The next scribe copied the mistakes and added new ones. After many generations, different copies of the same book could disagree in hundreds of small places. Scholars had to compare copies and guess which reading was right. This is one reason why, as we will see in later parts, printing thousands of identical copies was such a big deal for learning and science."]},{"heading":"Students, Stationers and Books for Rent","paragraphs":["Around the year 1100 and after, something new appeared in Europe: the university. Bologna in Italy is usually counted as the oldest, with roots in the late 1000s, and it became famous for law. Paris grew into a great center for theology and philosophy around 1200, and Oxford in England was teaching students by the late 1100s. Soon there were universities in Cambridge, Padua, Salamanca, Prague and many other cities.","Universities changed the book world. Monks had copied books mostly for their own monasteries. Now thousands of students and teachers in a single city all needed the same textbooks at the same time: law books, works of the Greek thinker Aristotle in Latin translation, commentaries on the Bible, and medical texts. One monk copying one book for many months could never keep up with that.","So the universities invented a clever system. It was called the pecia system, from a Latin word meaning a piece. It had grown up in Italian university towns by the early 1200s, and the University of Paris turned it into a carefully regulated procedure in the second half of the 1200s. Here is how it worked. Teachers checked a text to make sure it was correct. A bookseller, called a stationer, then had an official master copy made, called the exemplar. Instead of binding the exemplar as one book, the stationer kept it as loose pieces, each usually four folios long.","Before any renting began, officials from the university inspected the exemplar and set the price a student would pay to borrow each piece. A student, or a scribe the student paid, would rent piece number one, copy it, return it, and rent piece number two. Meanwhile someone else could be copying piece number five, and someone else piece number twelve. Because many people could copy different parts of the same book at once, new copies were made much faster than if one scribe had to wait for a whole book. It was a bit like a library that lends out one chapter at a time so that the whole class can share.","Why the strict rules? The university wanted students to study accurate texts, at fair prices. Officials checked exemplars from time to time for errors. Copies made from pecia exemplars have been identified from Paris and several Italian university towns, and sometimes you can still see little notes in the margins marking where one piece ended and the next began.","Universities also helped create a new kind of worker: the professional scribe who was not a monk. These lay scribes, meaning people who were not members of the clergy, copied books for pay. Stationers and booksellers, called librarii in Latin, bought and sold new and secondhand books, and the universities regulated them. In Paris, booksellers had to swear oaths and post money as a guarantee of good behavior. By the 1300s and 1400s, cities like Paris, Bruges and Florence had busy book workshops where scribes, illuminators and binders worked for paying customers, much like small businesses today.","The handwriting changed too. Many university books and later manuscripts were written in a compact, upright style historians call Gothic script, with tall, narrow letters packed tightly together. Narrow letters meant more words fit on each expensive page. To save even more space and time, scribes used lots of abbreviations, little marks and shortened words that readers learned to understand. When Gutenberg designed his first types, as we will see in Part 9, he copied this Gothic handwriting, abbreviations and all, so his printed pages would look like the best manuscripts."]},{"heading":"Merchants, Prayer Books and a Growing Hunger to Read","paragraphs":["You may have heard that people in the Middle Ages could not read and did not care about learning. That is a myth. It is true that most farmers and laborers could not read, and books were rare and costly. But the Middle Ages also produced universities, great poets, scientists, lawyers and libraries, and over time more and more people learned to read.","By the 1300s and 1400s, towns across Europe were growing richer. Merchants in Italy, Flanders and Germany traded wool, cloth, spices and silver over long distances. To do business, they had to write letters, keep account books, sign contracts and check bills. Many merchant families sent their sons, and sometimes daughters, to schools where they learned reading, writing and arithmetic. Some Italian cities had schools that taught calculation using the new Hindu-Arabic numerals, the 0 to 9 digits we use today.","More reading also meant more writing in everyday languages, not just Latin. In Italy, Dante Alighieri wrote his great poem, the Divine Comedy, in Italian in the early 1300s. In England, Geoffrey Chaucer wrote the Canterbury Tales in English in the late 1300s. Townspeople wanted stories, histories, songs, and practical books about health, cooking, farming and travel, written in a language they actually spoke at home.","Paper made a big difference. As we saw in Part 4, paper mills had spread through Italy, Spain and then other parts of Europe during the 1300s. Paper was much cheaper than parchment, so a book written on paper cost less, and a merchant could buy reams of it for letters and accounts. Governments, courts and churches produced mountains of documents, from tax lists to legal records, and many of them were now on paper.","The best-selling book of the late Middle Ages was not a novel or a science book. It was the book of hours, a personal prayer book for ordinary people who were not monks or priests. It contained a calendar of saints' days and short prayers to be said at different hours of the day, imitating the services monks sang in monasteries. Historians often call it a medieval bestseller: from roughly the 1300s to the mid-1500s, books of hours were made in enormous numbers, and today they are the most common kind of illuminated manuscript to survive, with tens of thousands still in existence.","Books of hours came in every price range. Kings and dukes ordered breathtaking ones painted by top artists, like the famous Very Rich Hours of the Duke of Berry, begun in the early 1400s. Merchant families bought simpler ones with just a few pictures. Many children even learned their letters by reading from the family book of hours. Workshops in Paris, Bruges and other cities turned them out in large numbers, sometimes making parts in advance and adding the owner's coat of arms or favorite saints at the end.","Even so, books were still scarce by modern standards. In about 1424, the common library of the University of Cambridge listed only 122 volumes, and more than half of them were about theology and religion. By 1473 it had grown to about 330 volumes. Before about 1450, even a large and important library in Europe might hold only a few hundred books.","All of this added up to a growing problem. More readers, more schools, more universities, more merchants, more cheap paper, but still only one way to make a copy of a book: by hand. Demand was racing ahead of supply."]},{"heading":"Pictures Carved in Wood","paragraphs":["Long before Europeans printed books with type, they began printing pictures. Once paper became common in Europe, around 1400, craftspeople began making woodcuts. A woodcut is made the same basic way as the Chinese woodblocks we met in Part 5. The artist draws a picture on a smooth plank of wood, then cuts away everything except the lines, leaving them standing up. The raised lines are inked, paper is pressed on top, and the picture appears, reversed like a mirror image.","Many of the earliest European woodcuts were religious pictures of saints, Jesus or the Virgin Mary. They were cheap enough for ordinary people to buy, perhaps at a pilgrimage site or a church festival. People pasted them on walls, inside chests, or into their prayer books. Many were colored in by hand afterwards with quick strokes of red, green and yellow. Because they were cheap and stuck up on walls, most were simply worn out and thrown away, so only a small number survive.","One of the most famous is a woodcut of Saint Christopher carrying the child Jesus across a river, with a little hermit holding a lantern, a man on a mule, and even a rabbit tucked into the scene. It bears the date 1423 written in Latin, and it was discovered in 1769, pasted inside the binding of a manuscript from the monastery library of Buxheim in southern Germany. Only one copy printed from this block survives, and it is kept at the John Rylands Library in Manchester, England.","For a long time this Saint Christopher was called the earliest dated European print. But here is a nice piece of detective work: a recent study pointed out that the style of the picture looks more like the 1440s and 1450s, and a watermark in its paper matches papers used around 1428 to 1435. So the 1423 date may not be when it was printed. It might mark something else, such as a religious occasion. The study concluded that it was probably printed in the mid-to-late 1430s.","Woodcuts were also used for playing cards. Card games had arrived in Europe in the late 1300s, and they became hugely popular. Hand-painted cards were expensive, but cards printed from woodblocks and colored with stencils could be made quickly and cheaply. In the 1400s there were card-makers in towns like Ulm and Nuremberg in Germany. Cards may have been one of the first printed products that ordinary Europeans used every day.","Then came block books. A block book is a short book in which each page, pictures and words together, was carved on a single block of wood, just like a Chinese woodblock page. Most European block books date from about the 1450s to the 1470s, so they appeared around the same time as early printing with metal type, not long before it as people once thought. They were made mainly in Germany and the Netherlands.","Popular block books included the Biblia pauperum, a picture Bible showing scenes from the life of Jesus side by side with matching stories from the Old Testament; the Ars moriendi, or Art of Dying, which gave advice on facing death with a peaceful heart; and illustrated versions of the Apocalypse, the last book of the Bible. They were heavy on pictures and light on words, because carving lots of tiny letters into wood was slow and fiddly. If you made one mistake while carving a letter, you could not easily fix it, so cutting a whole page of text into one block was risky work.","Block books show us something important. Europeans already knew how to print images on paper. What they did not yet have was a fast, flexible way to print large amounts of text. For that, they would need not a single carved block for each page, but thousands of tiny, separate letters that could be used again and again."]},{"heading":"A Continent Ready for Something New","paragraphs":["Let us step back and look at Europe around 1440. There are dozens of universities full of students who need textbooks. There are rich merchants who keep accounts and like to read. There are schools teaching children Latin grammar from little books. There are millions of Christians who want prayer books, and a church that needs huge numbers of service books, Bibles and official documents. There are lawyers, doctors, preachers and city clerks who all need texts.","On the supply side, there is cheap paper from mills in Italy, France and Germany. There are woodcut makers who know how to press ink onto paper. There are goldsmiths and metalworkers who are experts at casting small, precise objects and stamping metal with punches, like the punches used to make coins. There are wine presses and paper presses with big wooden screws, standing in vineyards and paper mills. All the pieces of a new invention are lying around, waiting for someone to put them together in the right way.","It is worth remembering what medieval scribes achieved. For roughly a thousand years they kept the knowledge of ancient Rome and Greece alive, created new learning, and produced some of the most beautiful objects ever made. Many people at the time thought hand-written books were finer than printed ones, and scribes kept working for decades after printing arrived.","But the numbers were against the scribes. A single scribe might finish a few books a year. Europe now wanted thousands. The world needed a way to copy words not one at a time, but hundreds at a time, all exactly the same.","In the German city of Mainz, a goldsmith with big debts and a bigger idea was getting ready to change all of this."]}],"deepDive":"How many books were there in medieval Europe, and how many people could read them? Historians cannot count every lost manuscript, so they work from what survives, from library catalogues and from tax and court records. Economic historians have tried to estimate total manuscript production century by century, and their work suggests output rose steeply from the 1100s onward, especially after universities appeared and paper became common. These estimates come with large error bars, because many manuscripts were destroyed by fire, war, damp, reuse as binding scraps or simple neglect. Literacy is just as tricky to measure. Being able to read was not the same as being able to write, and many people could read a little in their own language without knowing Latin. Some historians use signatures on documents as a rough guide, while others point to the huge market for books of hours as evidence that reading spread well beyond monks and nobles, including to many women. The pecia system is a good example of medieval problem-solving: by splitting an approved exemplar into rentable pieces, universities increased copying speed and controlled accuracy and price, a kind of quality control centuries before factories. Dating early prints is a science of its own. Experts compare artistic style, look at paper watermarks (each paper mill's mould left a distinctive wire design), and study how a woodblock cracked or wore down between impressions. The famous Saint Christopher dated 1423 shows why dates printed on an object do not always tell you when it was made; watermark evidence now points to the 1430s. Finally, historians stress that print did not replace manuscripts overnight. Scribes, illuminators and stationers continued working into the 1500s, often decorating printed books by hand; one of the first great printers, Peter Schoeffer, had himself been a scribe. Many early printers were former scribes who brought their skills with them.","funFact":"Scientists at Trinity College Dublin found that the blue in the Book of Kells probably came from indigo or woad plants, not from the expensive gemstone lapis lazuli that people had believed for many years.","check":{"q":"In the medieval university pecia system, what did students rent?","a":"Separate sections (pieces) of an approved master copy of a book, to copy one at a time","wrong":["Printing presses for a week at a time","Whole libraries of chained books","Rooms in a monastery scriptorium"]},"sources":[{"title":"Book of Kells","publisher":"Trinity College Dublin Library","url":"https://www.tcd.ie/library/research-collections/book-of-kells.php"},{"title":"Book of Kells","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Kells"},{"title":"Iona Abbey","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iona_Abbey"},{"title":"Making Medieval Manuscripts","publisher":"Corpus Christi College, University of Cambridge","url":"https://www.corpus.cam.ac.uk/articles/making-medieval-manuscripts"},{"title":"The Saint Christopher woodcut dated 1423","publisher":"John Rylands Research Institute and Library, University of Manchester","url":"https://rylandscollections.com/2025/08/14/the-saint-christopher-woodcut-dated-1423/"},{"title":"History of Cambridge University Library","publisher":"Cambridge University Library","url":"https://www.lib.cam.ac.uk/node/512"},{"title":"Pecia","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pecia"},{"title":"Book of hours","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_hours"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/07-1.jpg","alt":"The Chi Rho page of the Book of Kells (about 800 AD), hand-painted with swirling spirals and knotwork.","file":"KellsFol034rChiRhoMonogram.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KellsFol034rChiRhoMonogram.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/07-2.jpg","alt":"The hand-coloured Buxheim Saint Christopher woodcut, dated 1423, showing the saint carrying the child Jesus across a river.","file":"Saint Christopher 001.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Christopher_001.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/07-3.jpg","alt":"A Biblia pauperum block-book page: pictures and words carved together, with the Flight into Egypt in the middle.","file":"Biblia pauperum refugee.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biblia_pauperum_refugee.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4293},{"n":8,"title":"Gutenberg's Secret Project","when":"about 1400 – 1450","summary":"Johannes Gutenberg was a clever metalworker from the German city of Mainz who worked on a big secret for years. He borrowed huge amounts of money, made shiny mirrors for pilgrims, and got pulled into lawsuits. By 1454 his workshop was printing its first small pages, and the world was about to change.","sections":[{"heading":"A Boy from the House of Gutenberg","paragraphs":["Imagine the city of Mainz around the year 1400. It sits on the banks of the Rhine, one of the busiest rivers in Europe. Boats carry wine, wood, cloth and grain up and down the water. Above the rooftops rise the towers of a huge cathedral, because Mainz is ruled by a powerful archbishop, one of the most important churchmen in the Holy Roman Empire. Somewhere in this bustling town, a baby boy named Johannes was born.","We do not know his birthday, or even his exact birth year. No birth record survives. Historians work backwards from later documents, such as one suggesting he had come of age by about 1420, and they usually say he was born around 1400, with estimates ranging from about 1394 to 1404. In 1900, Mainz threw a big 500th birthday party for him anyway, choosing 1400 as a nice round number. According to local tradition he was baptized at the church of Saint Christoph, near his family's house, and the city still celebrates him around 24 June, the feast day of Saint John, his name saint.","His father was Friele Gensfleisch zur Laden, a wealthy merchant from one of Mainz's patrician families. Patricians were the old, rich, important families who ran much of the city's business and government. His mother, Else Wirich, was Friele's second wife and the daughter of a shopkeeper. Johannes had an older brother, also named Friele, and a sister named Else.","Wait, why isn't he called Johannes Gensfleisch? In medieval Mainz, rich families were often known by the names of the houses they owned. Gensfleisch means goose flesh in old German, which is a funny name to carry around. The family owned a large house called the Hof zum Gutenberg, the Gutenberg courtyard, and around 1427 the name Gutenberg starts appearing in records for the family. So Johannes Gensfleisch became known to history as Johannes Gutenberg, after his home.","What did young Johannes learn? Here the records are frustratingly quiet. A student named Johannes de Altavilla appears in the register of the University of Erfurt around 1418 to 1420, and some historians think this might be him, because his family had connections to the nearby town of Eltville (Alta Villa in Latin). But nobody can prove it. If he did attend, he would have learned Latin, which would help explain how he later printed Latin books so skillfully.","We do know that he became an expert metalworker. Later documents show him teaching skills like polishing gems and making metal objects, and he was linked with the goldsmiths of Strasbourg. Some historians suggest that his family had connections to the archbishop's mint in Mainz, where coins were made by striking metal with carved steel tools called dies and punches. Whether or not young Johannes ever watched coin-makers at work, his later invention depended on exactly those skills: cutting tiny shapes in hard metal, and casting soft metal into precise, identical pieces.","Mainz in those years was not a peaceful place. The patrician families and the craft guilds, who represented ordinary tradesmen like bakers, weavers and smiths, argued fiercely about taxes and power. Around 1428, the Gensfleisch family and other patricians left Mainz in protest over the city council's tax demands. Johannes's father had already died around 1419. From about 1429 to 1434, we lose track of Johannes completely. When he appears again in the records, he is living in another city."]},{"heading":"A New Life in Strasbourg","paragraphs":["In 1434, Gutenberg pops up in Strasbourg, about 150 kilometers south of Mainz along the Rhine. Strasbourg was a free imperial city, which meant it governed itself under the emperor, and it was much bigger than Mainz. It had busy markets, skilled craftsmen and a cathedral whose tall spire was being finished in those very years.","Gutenberg lived in St. Arbogast, a spot just outside the city walls, near a monastery. He was not poor. Like many patricians, he received an annuity from the city of Mainz, which was a yearly payment from money his family had invested there. His older brother Friele even transferred his own annuity to Johannes in 1434. But Gutenberg's first appearance in Strasbourg's records is a dramatic one.","Mainz owed Gutenberg 310 guilders in unpaid annuity money. A guilder, or gulden, was a gold coin, and 310 of them was a large amount. In March 1434, when a clerk from the Mainz town government happened to be in Strasbourg, Gutenberg had him arrested and held until his debt was paid. The Strasbourg council asked for the man to be released, and he was let go after promising that Mainz would pay. It tells us something about Gutenberg: he was bold, he knew the law, and he did not give up on money he was owed.","He also appears in a court case of a different kind. Around 1436 or 1437, a Strasbourg woman named Ennelin zu der Iserin Türe said Gutenberg had promised to marry her and then not kept his word. We do not know how that case ended. During the hearings, Gutenberg insulted one of the witnesses, a man named Niklaus Schott, and, according to the records, was ordered to pay him damages for the insult. There is no record that Gutenberg ever married, and historians think he probably stayed single all his life.","Records from Strasbourg's tax and customs books list Gutenberg as a half-member, or associate, of the goldsmiths' guild. That did not make him a full master goldsmith, but it shows he worked closely with metalworkers. In 1444, a list made when Strasbourg prepared for war against bands of mercenary soldiers called the Armagnacs shows that he had a fortune of over 400 pounds in Strasbourg money and had to pay for half a horse for the city's defense. He also borrowed money: in 1442 he took a loan of 80 pounds from the church foundation of St. Thomas in Strasbourg."]},{"heading":"Magic Mirrors for the Pilgrims of Aachen","paragraphs":["To understand Gutenberg's first big business, we need to visit the city of Aachen, in western Germany. Aachen's cathedral holds famous relics, objects believed to be holy, including cloths said to be connected with Jesus and the Virgin Mary. Every seven years, from 1349 onward, the relics were shown to the public in a great festival. Tens of thousands of pilgrims poured into the city to see them.","The crowds were so huge that most pilgrims could not get near the relics. Many people believed that holy objects gave off a kind of invisible blessing, like rays of light. So pilgrims bought small badges with a little convex mirror set into them, and held them up high toward the relics as they were displayed from the cathedral galleries. They believed the mirror would capture some of the holy power, which they could then carry home pinned to their hats or cloaks.","Gutenberg saw a business opportunity. In 1438 he formed a partnership with Hans Riffe, an official from the nearby town of Lichtenau, to make these pilgrim mirrors in large numbers. Soon two more partners joined: Andreas Dritzehn, who had been learning skills like gem polishing from Gutenberg, and Andreas Heilmann, a Strasbourg citizen. Each partner paid money into the business, and Gutenberg taught them his methods.","The mirrors were made by casting. The badges were made from a metal alloy of lead and tin. An alloy is a mixture of metals melted together. Molten metal was poured into moulds, cooled, and then fitted with the little mirror pieces. To make money, the partners needed to make many identical badges quickly and cheaply. Does that sound familiar? Casting large numbers of identical metal objects from moulds was exactly the skill that would later be needed to make thousands of letters of type.","But the plan hit a problem. The partners believed the next Aachen pilgrimage would happen in 1439. It actually took place in 1440, a year later than they expected. The partners were left with stocks of mirrors, debts, and a whole extra year to wait. So Gutenberg made them a new offer. According to the court records, he agreed to teach them all his arts and skills, including a secret new project. In the old German of the documents, the work is called an afentur und kunst, an adventure and art.","What was the secret art? The partners paid Gutenberg for it, and they promised to keep it hidden. Then, around the end of 1438, Andreas Dritzehn fell ill and died. Suddenly the secret partnership found itself heading to court."]},{"heading":"Clues in a Courtroom: The Lawsuit of 1439","paragraphs":["After Andreas Dritzehn died, his brothers Jörg and Claus Dritzehn wanted his share of the business. They argued that Andreas had poured a lot of money into the partnership, and they demanded that Gutenberg either pay it back or take them in as partners in his place. Gutenberg refused, saying the contract did not allow it. In 1439 the brothers took him to court in Strasbourg.","For historians, this lawsuit is a treasure chest. Many witnesses were called to describe what they had seen and heard, and the court clerks wrote down their testimony. Statements from a number of these witnesses and a copy of the court's judgment survived for more than 400 years in Strasbourg's archives. Then, in 1870, during the Franco-Prussian War, the library holding the originals burned. Luckily, scholars had already copied the documents word for word, so we can still read what the witnesses said.","What did they say? Several witnesses talked about a press. A wood-turner named Conrad Saspach had built it. A press is a machine that squeezes things flat, usually with a large wooden screw turned by a bar. Witnesses also mentioned lead and other metals, and objects they called forms. A goldsmith named Hans Dünne told the court that Gutenberg had paid him about 100 guilders, a lot of money, for making things that belonged to printing. In the old Strasbourg dialect, the word was trucken, which is related to the modern German drucken, meaning to print.","There was also a scene that sounds like something out of a spy movie. According to the testimony, when Andreas Dritzehn was dying, Gutenberg sent his servant, Lorenz Beildeck, to Andreas's house with an urgent message. The press there held four pieces, and the servant was to open it and take the pieces apart, so that nobody could look inside and figure out how it worked. When people went to look afterwards, the pieces could not be found. Clearly Gutenberg wanted his secret kept safe at any cost.","So what exactly was the adventure and art? Here we must be careful. The Strasbourg documents never clearly describe printing books with movable type, and no printed page from Strasbourg in this period has ever been found. The International Gutenberg Society in Mainz points out that there is no evidence he had yet invented his printing method or printed a book in Strasbourg. But the clues are tantalizing: a specially built press, lead and other metals, forms, a goldsmith making parts for printing, and a business owner desperate to keep it secret. Most historians believe Strasbourg was where Gutenberg began experimenting with the ideas that became his printing system.","It is also possible that some of the work was about making the mirrors themselves, or that Gutenberg was working on more than one invention. Inventors often have several projects running at once. What matters most is that by 1439, Gutenberg was already thinking about presses, metal casting and secrecy, three ingredients of his later success."]},{"heading":"The Missing Years and the Return to Mainz","paragraphs":["After the lawsuit, Gutenberg stayed in Strasbourg for a few more years. He is listed there in 1444. Then he disappears again from the records. From 1444 to 1448, historians simply do not know where he was or what he was doing. Some historians point to a Frankfurt document from 1447 that seems to mention a Henne Genssfleisch von Menze, which may mean he was already back in or near Mainz. Henne was a common nickname for Johannes.","The first sure sign of his return comes in October 1448. On that day, Gutenberg borrowed 150 guilders in Mainz, with his relative Arnold Gelthus standing as guarantor, which meant Arnold promised to pay if Gutenberg could not. The loan carried interest of 5 percent a year. Gutenberg was back in his hometown, and he needed money.","Mainz was still a busy trading city on the Rhine, close to Frankfurt, which held one of the biggest trade fairs in Europe. Merchants from all over came to Frankfurt to buy and sell, including books. If you wanted to sell a lot of copies of something, Mainz was a very good place to start.","Gutenberg set up a workshop in a building known as the Hof zum Humbrecht, which later became known as the Schöfferhof, after the printer Peter Schoeffer. There he gathered tools, metals and workers. Around 1449 or 1450, he made a deal with a wealthy Mainz businessman named Johann Fust. Fust lent him 800 guilders to build equipment for his project. A few years later, around 1452 or 1453, Fust added another 800 guilders, this time as an investment in what the documents call the work of the books. That made Fust a partner in the business.","How much was 1,600 guilders? Around 1450, a good town house in Mainz could be bought for about 500 guilders. So Fust's money together could have bought three houses, with some left over. It was far more than an ordinary craftsman could hope to earn in many years of work. Fust was taking a big risk on an unproven idea, and he expected his money back with interest.","Fust was not the only important person to join. A young man named Peter Schoeffer, born around 1425 in the town of Gernsheim, came into the workshop. He had worked as a scribe and calligrapher in Paris, copying manuscripts by hand. His knowledge of beautiful lettering was exactly what Gutenberg needed to make printed letters that looked as fine as the best handwriting. Schoeffer probably played a major role in designing the typefaces. He would later marry Fust's daughter Christina and become one of the most important printers in Europe."]},{"heading":"Why Inventing Printing Cost a Fortune","paragraphs":["Why did Gutenberg need so much money? Because he was not building one machine. He was building a whole new industry from scratch, with no instruction book and nobody nearby to copy from, and he could not sell much until all of it worked.","First, he needed letters. Each letter of the alphabet had to be carved, in reverse, on the tip of a steel rod by a highly skilled metalworker. Then those carvings had to be used to make moulds, and the moulds used to cast many thousands of small metal letters. As we will see in Part 9, getting every letter to come out exactly the same height and shape took clever tools and a lot of trial and error. Every failed experiment burned up time, metal and money.","Second, he needed metal, lots of it. Lead and tin had to be bought from merchants, and a big printing job needed many thousands of pieces of type. He also needed tools, furnaces, fuel for melting, and the presses themselves, solid wooden machines built by carpenters.","Third, he needed something to print on. Good paper came mainly from Italy and had to be bought in large amounts. For his most splendid books, Gutenberg also used vellum, made from calfskin. A single copy of a big book printed on vellum needed the skins of many dozens of calves. Buying paper and vellum for a large print run meant spending a fortune before selling a single page.","Fourth, he needed people. Typesetters, pressmen, metalworkers and helpers had to be paid, even during months when nothing was ready to sell. Historians do not know exactly how many people worked for Gutenberg, but making a big book would have taken a sizable team working for years.","Fifth, he needed ink that would stick properly to metal. The water-based inks used for woodblocks in Asia and for woodcuts in Europe did not work well on metal type; they tended to bead up instead of spreading evenly. Gutenberg had to develop a new, oily ink, more like the oil paints that artists of his time were using. More experiments, more cost.","Put all of this together and you can see the problem. An inventor might spend years and thousands of guilders before earning very much at all. That is why Gutenberg needed investors like Fust, and why the partnership became so tense. Fust wanted results and profits. Gutenberg wanted perfection. Those two goals would eventually collide, as we will see in Part 10."]},{"heading":"The First Printed Pages from Mainz","paragraphs":["Gutenberg did not wait to finish a giant book before printing anything. Like a smart inventor testing a new idea, his workshop also printed smaller things that could be sold more quickly. These early prints are rare today, and many survive only as scraps, but they tell an exciting story.","His earliest type is known to experts as the DK type. The letters stand for Donatus and Kalender, two kinds of items printed with it. The Donatus was a short Latin grammar book, named after Aelius Donatus, a Roman teacher who lived in the 300s. For more than a thousand years, European schoolchildren learned Latin from his little textbook, the Ars minor. Because every Latin school needed copies, it was a perfect product: short, always in demand, and easy to sell. Gutenberg's workshop printed several editions of the Donatus, many on tough vellum because schoolbooks got heavy use.","Almost no complete copies survive, because schoolbooks were used until they fell apart. Instead, scholars have found fragments recycled as stiffening inside the bindings of other books. Some of these fragments turned up in bindings from places far away from Mainz, which suggests that Mainz-printed schoolbooks were already being traded over long distances.","Another early print is a fragment of a German poem about the Last Judgment, the end of the world in Christian belief, sometimes linked to a work called the Sibyllenbuch, or Book of the Sibyl. It may have been printed around 1452 or 1453, or perhaps a little earlier; experts are not sure. Only a small scrap survives. There is also a short printed pamphlet in German known as the Turkish Calendar, made for the year 1455, which urged Christian rulers to fight the Ottoman Turks, who had captured Constantinople in 1453.","Then came a printing job that shows exactly why printing was useful. In 1454, a man named Paulinus Chappe came to Mainz. He was raising money for the defense of the island of Cyprus against the Ottoman Turks, and the pope had authorized the sale of indulgences for this cause. In the Catholic Church at that time, an indulgence was a document granting relief from certain punishments for sins, and people could receive one by making a donation. Each indulgence was a form with blank spaces where the name of the buyer and the date could be written in by hand.","Chappe needed thousands of identical forms, fast. Writing them all by hand would have taken an army of scribes. So in late summer 1454 he made a deal with Gutenberg to print them. Mainz printers produced a version with 31 lines and another with 30 lines, in runs of several thousand. One surviving 31-line copy was filled out in Erfurt on 22 October 1454. That handwritten date is the earliest firm date we have for European printing with movable metal type.","The 31-line indulgence was printed in a small, neat type that appears in no other book. Experts think it may have been melted down when the campaign ended, perhaps so that nobody could print fake copies. A number of these printed forms survive today, many of them, like the Donatus fragments, rescued from inside old book bindings.","Notice what these first jobs have in common. A schoolbook that every student needs. A calendar pamphlet. Official forms needed by the thousand. None of them was glamorous, but they proved the system worked and could bring in money. Behind the scenes, Gutenberg was already preparing something far bigger. Remember, too, that Gutenberg did not invent printing itself. Chinese and Korean printers had been printing with woodblocks and movable type for centuries, as we saw in Parts 5 and 6. What Gutenberg was building was a European system for producing metal type in huge quantities, and it all depended on one small, brilliant tool.","Gutenberg's real genius was not one machine but a whole system, starting with a tiny mould that could cast letters perfectly every time."]}],"deepDive":"Almost everything we know about Gutenberg comes not from his own writing but from legal and financial records: loan agreements, tax lists and lawsuits. He never signed or dated a single printed item, and no letter or diary of his survives, which makes him one of history's most important but least documented inventors. Historians piece together his story like detectives, and they often disagree. The Strasbourg lawsuit of 1439 is a good example. Its testimony mentions a press built by Conrad Saspach, lead, forms, and goldsmith Hans Dünne's work on items for printing, but it never spells out what the secret art was. Many scholars think Gutenberg was already experimenting with cast type in Strasbourg; others suspect the work may have been closer to the mirror business or to printing images, with type coming later. Because the original documents burned in 1870, scholars rely on earlier transcriptions, which adds another layer of uncertainty. Dates are slippery too. Fust's loans appear as around 1450 and 1452 in some accounts and as 1449 and 1453 in others, because they are known mainly from the Helmasperger Notarial Instrument of November 1455, which records Fust's lawsuit; his total claim, including interest, came to a little over 2,000 guilders. Experts also debate which early fragments came from Gutenberg's own shop, since none name a printer; attributions rely on type designs, paper and the places where fragments turned up. The earliest absolutely fixed date for European printing is the handwritten 22 October 1454 on a Cyprus indulgence. Money comparisons are rough guides: prices, wages and coin values varied from city to city and year to year, so a statement such as a Mainz town house cost about 500 guilders helps us imagine scale but is not an exact exchange rate. Finally, historians stress that Gutenberg combined many existing technologies, including metal casting, punch-cutting, oil-based paints and screw presses, into a new system. That combination, not any single part, was his achievement.","funFact":"In 1439 witnesses said Gutenberg sent his servant to take apart a mysterious press with four pieces so that nobody could discover its secret, and when people later went to look, the pieces could not be found.","check":{"q":"What was Gutenberg's business in Strasbourg around 1438, before the lawsuit?","a":"Making metal pilgrim mirrors for the Aachen pilgrimage","wrong":["Printing newspapers for the city council","Building ships on the Rhine","Selling paper made from wood pulp"]},"sources":[{"title":"Gutenberg in Strasbourg","publisher":"International Gutenberg Society (Gutenberg-Gesellschaft), Mainz","url":"https://www.gutenberg-gesellschaft.de/en/johannes-gutenberg/gutenberg-in-strasbourg/"},{"title":"Gutenberg's invention","publisher":"International Gutenberg Society (Gutenberg-Gesellschaft), Mainz","url":"https://www.gutenberg-gesellschaft.de/en/johannes-gutenberg/gutenbergs-invention"},{"title":"Gutenberg and his time in data","publisher":"Gutenberg Museum, Mainz","url":"https://www.gutenberg.de/en/zeit/zeitleiste_gutenberg"},{"title":"The Beginning of Printing in Mainz","publisher":"Princeton University Library","url":"https://dpul.princeton.edu/gutenberg/feature/the-beginning-of-printing-in-mainz"},{"title":"The Gutenberg Bible","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/bibles/the-gutenberg-bible.html"},{"title":"Johannes Gutenberg","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Gutenberg"},{"title":"Aachen Cathedral","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aachen_Cathedral"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/08-1.jpg","alt":"An imagined 17th-century engraving of Gutenberg with long beard and fur hat; no portrait from his lifetime survives.","file":"Johannes Gutenberg.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_Gutenberg.jpg","author":"Printmaker, Larmessin, Nicolas de, (1632-1694)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/08-2.jpg","alt":"A metal Aachen pilgrim badge (a 1979 recast of a 1330 badge), showing the holy relics held up for pilgrims.","file":"Pilgerzeichen Nachguss 1979.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pilgerzeichen_Nachguss_1979.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/08-3.jpg","alt":"A Cyprus indulgence printed in Mainz in 1454, with the buyer's name and the date written in by hand.","file":"SMO V12 D086 Letter of indulgence printed in 1454.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SMO_V12_D086_Letter_of_indulgence_printed_in_1454.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3757},{"n":9,"title":"How Gutenberg's System Worked","when":"about 1440 – 1455","summary":"Gutenberg did not build just one clever machine. He built a whole chain of tools: a steel letter, a copper dent, a little hand-held mould, a special metal, a sticky black ink and a strong press. Put them all together, and a workshop could make thousands of perfect, matching letters and print with them again and again.","sections":[{"heading":"A Puzzle Made of Thousands of Pieces","paragraphs":["Imagine you want to print a page the size of a big school atlas, packed with two tall columns of text, about 3,000 letters and spaces in all. If every letter is a separate little block, you need thousands of blocks for that one page alone. Now imagine you want to print a whole book of more than a thousand pages. Suddenly you are not thinking about a few letters. You are thinking about a mountain of them.","That was the puzzle Johannes Gutenberg faced in Mainz in the 1440s and early 1450s. As we saw in the last part, he had spent years in secret experiments, borrowing money and hiring helpers. The question was not just how to press ink onto paper. People in Europe already knew how to do that with woodcut pictures. The real question was much harder: how can you make huge numbers of small letters that are all exactly the same height, the same depth, and the same shape, cheaply and quickly, so that they fit together like perfect bricks?","Why does exact height matter so much? Think of a page of type as a flat field of tiny metal towers, each with a letter on its top. When the press pushes paper down onto them, every tower has to touch the paper with the same pressure. If one letter is a hair too short, it prints faint or not at all. If one is a hair too tall, it punches into the paper and smudges. Even a difference thinner than a sheet of paper can ruin a line. Carving letters one by one in wood would never be accurate enough, and wood swells and shrinks when it gets wet or dry.","Gutenberg had an advantage here. He had been trained as a metalworker and had worked with goldsmiths' tools. Goldsmiths knew how to cut tiny designs in hard steel, how to stamp shapes into softer metal, how to melt and cast alloys, and how to measure things very precisely. Coin makers used similar tricks to stamp the same design onto thousands of coins. Gutenberg's genius was to take these skills and turn them toward letters.","His answer was not one machine but a whole chain of steps. Each step solved one part of the problem, and each step handed its result to the next. Historians call this a system. In this part we will follow that chain from start to finish: the punch, the matrix, the hand mould, the type metal, the ink and the press. By the end, you will understand why many experts think the cleverest part of the whole invention was something small enough to hold in one hand."]},{"heading":"Step One: The Punch, a Letter Carved Backwards in Steel","paragraphs":["Everything began with the punch. A punch is a rod of steel, roughly as long as your finger, with a single letter carved on its tip. The skilled worker who made it was called a punchcutter, and this was one of the most demanding jobs in the whole workshop.","Here is the tricky part: the letter on the punch had to be carved backwards, as a mirror image. Why? Hold a rubber stamp up and look at it. The letters on the stamp are reversed, so that when you press it onto paper, they come out the right way round. Printing type works the same way. And as you will see in a moment, the punch gets flipped twice before it turns into printing type, so the punch itself had to be backwards as well.","To make a punch, the punchcutter took a bar of steel that was soft enough to cut. Using tiny files and engraving tools called gravers, he slowly shaped the end until only the letter stood up from the surface, with the metal around it filed away. Small hollow spaces inside letters, like the hole in an o or the closed part of an a, were often made with another tool called a counterpunch. The counterpunch was itself a tiny steel shape that was hammered into the end of the punch to sink those inner spaces neatly. Using the same counterpunch for several letters helped them match each other.","The punchcutter worked under good light, checking his letter again and again. He could make a smoky proof by holding the punch in a candle flame so it got coated with soot, then pressing it onto paper to see what the letter looked like. When it was finally right, the steel was hardened by heating it and then cooling it quickly in water or oil. Now it was hard enough to be hammered into another metal without bending.","Making one punch could take a skilled cutter a long time, perhaps most of a day for a complicated letter. But here was the beauty of the idea: you only needed to cut each letter once. One good punch could be the parent of thousands of printed letters. That is why, in later centuries, printers treasured their punches and matrices more than the type itself. Type wore out and was melted down; punches were the master copies."]},{"heading":"Step Two: The Matrix, a Letter-Shaped Dent in Copper","paragraphs":["Once the steel punch was hard, the next step was to make the matrix. The word matrix comes from Latin and is related to the word for mother, which is a good way to remember it: the matrix is the mother mould that gives birth to every piece of type.","The worker placed the hardened punch on a small block of copper, a much softer metal, and struck it firmly with a hammer. The punch sank into the copper and left a letter-shaped dent. Because the punch was backwards, the dent in the copper reads the right way round, just as a footprint in sand shows the shape of your shoe the right way round when you look down into it.","This sounds simple, but it needed great care. The punch had to go in perfectly straight, to exactly the right depth. If it was tilted, the letters cast from that matrix would lean or print unevenly. After striking, the copper block was usually distorted a little at its edges, so it had to be filed and trimmed into a neat rectangular block with straight sides and an even depth. This job was called justifying the matrix. A slightly crooked matrix would make every single letter cast from it crooked too, so the time spent here paid off thousands of times later.","Why copper? Copper is soft enough that a hardened steel punch can be driven into it, yet strong enough to hold its shape when hot liquid metal is poured against it again and again. It also does not melt at the temperature of molten type metal, which is far lower than copper's own melting point of about 1,085 degrees Celsius. So the copper dent could be filled, emptied and filled again for a very long time.","Gutenberg's workshop needed a separate punch and a separate matrix for every different shape of letter it wanted to print. As we will see, for his great Bible that meant not 26 shapes, and not even 52 for capitals and small letters, but around 290. That is a lot of patient steelwork before a single page could be printed."]},{"heading":"Step Three: The Hand Mould, the Heart of the Invention","paragraphs":["Now comes the part that many historians think was Gutenberg's most important idea: the hand mould. Aloys Ruppel, a famous director of the Gutenberg Museum in Mainz in the 20th century, argued that this little tool was the real key to the whole invention. The Gutenberg Museum today still describes the hand casting instrument as the most important part of the system.","Picture a small metal box, open at the top, made of two L-shaped halves that slide together. Around the metal is a wooden casing, so the caster can hold it in his hands without burning his fingers. When the two halves are pushed together, they form a narrow rectangular tunnel, a little like a square drinking straw. At the bottom end of this tunnel the copper matrix is clamped into place, held tight by a metal spring, with its letter-shaped dent facing up into the tunnel. At the top is a funnel-shaped opening for pouring.","The clever trick is that the two halves can slide sideways against each other. Pull them a little apart and the tunnel gets wider; push them closer and it gets narrower. But the height of the tunnel, the distance from the matrix to the top, never changes. This meant that the same mould could cast a skinny letter like i or l and a fat letter like m or w, yet every one of them would come out exactly the same height. That one feature solved the problem of the uneven field of tiny towers we imagined earlier.","Casting a letter went like this. The caster held the closed mould in one hand. With a small ladle in the other, he scooped up molten type metal from a little pot kept hot over a fire and poured it quickly into the opening. Many casters gave the mould a sharp upward flick as they poured, which helped push the liquid metal hard into every corner of the matrix. The metal cooled and turned solid almost at once. Then he flipped the spring, pulled the two halves apart, and out dropped a finished piece of type. He closed the mould and did it again.","Each new piece came out with an extra stub of metal attached on one end, where the metal had filled the funnel. This stub is called the jet or sprue. Workers snapped it off at a weak point built into the shape, and the leftover metal went straight back into the melting pot, so nothing was wasted. The type was then rubbed and filed smooth so it would stand straight and line up with its neighbours. A small groove called a nick was often made on one side so that a typesetter could feel, without even looking, which way round the letter was facing.","How fast was this? Later typefounders using hand moulds are believed to have cast several thousand letters in a day. Gutenberg's own speed is not recorded. Compare that with a scribe, who might write a few pages a day, or a woodcarver, who might need days to carve a single page. The hand mould turned a slow craft into something close to mass production.","Each finished piece of type is called a sort. It is a long, rectangular stick of metal with a raised, backwards letter on its top end. Line up thousands of sorts and you have a whole page waiting for ink."]},{"heading":"Step Four: Type Metal, a Recipe That Freezes Sharp","paragraphs":["What should the type be made of? This was not an easy question. The metal had to melt at a fairly low temperature, so a small fire could keep it liquid. It had to flow into the finest lines of the matrix, like the thin hairline strokes of a letter. It had to harden quickly so the caster could keep working. And it had to be tough enough to survive being squeezed in a press thousands of times.","Gutenberg's exact recipe has not survived. We know about it from later descriptions and from studying old type. The usual answer that printers settled on, and the one most experts think Gutenberg's metal was close to, is an alloy (a mixture of metals) made mostly of lead, with tin and antimony added, and tiny traces of other metals such as copper or iron. This mixture is called type metal.","Each ingredient has a job. Lead is soft, heavy and cheap, and it melts at about 327 degrees Celsius, which is hot but still easy to reach with a charcoal fire. Tin melts even lower, at about 232 degrees Celsius. When the right metals are mixed together, the alloy melts at a lower temperature than pure lead (some type metals melt at only about 240 to 250 degrees Celsius), so type metal stays runny at a comfortable working heat and freezes almost instantly when it touches the cooler mould. Tin also makes the type tougher, so it does not wear down or crack as easily.","Antimony is the most interesting ingredient. Most metals shrink a little as they cool and turn from liquid to solid. Pure lead does this, which means a pure lead letter would pull back slightly from the edges of the matrix and come out soft and blurry. Antimony does something unusual: it is one of very few substances that expand slightly as they freeze, a bit like water turning into ice. Mixed with lead and tin, it cancels out the shrinking and pushes the metal firmly into every tiny corner of the matrix. The result is a letter with crisp, sharp edges. Antimony also makes the alloy harder.","Later type metals varied a lot. Hand-set foundry type in modern times was often about 54 parts lead, 28 parts antimony and 18 parts tin, while metal for typesetting machines held much more lead, and the exact mix changed over the centuries and from foundry to foundry. Whatever Gutenberg's own proportions were, he found a recipe that worked well enough to print one of the most beautiful books ever made.","There was one serious downside, which no one at the time understood. Lead is poisonous. People who melted, cast and handled lead type every day for years could slowly build up lead in their bodies. You will read more about the health dangers of printing in a later part of this story."]},{"heading":"Step Five: An Ink That Sticks to Metal","paragraphs":["With a page of shiny metal type ready, Gutenberg met another problem. The inks used in Europe for writing and in East Asia for woodblock printing were mostly water-based. Brushed onto a wooden block, a watery ink soaks in a little and then transfers neatly to paper. But try to put a watery ink on smooth metal and it behaves like raindrops on a car window: it beads up and runs into blobs instead of spreading in a thin, even layer.","Gutenberg's solution came from the world of painting. Artists in his time, especially in the Low Countries, were experimenting with oil paints made by mixing coloured powders into oils such as linseed oil, which comes from the seeds of the flax plant. When linseed oil is boiled for a long time, it thickens into a sticky, honey-like varnish. Mix in a black powder such as soot from burning lamps or resin, called lampblack, and you get a thick, tacky ink that clings to metal like jam clings to a knife.","This oil-based ink was spread onto the type in a thin, even film, and when paper was pressed down on it, it transferred cleanly and dried into a deep, glossy black. If you look at a page of the Gutenberg Bible today, more than 570 years later, the letters still look rich and dark, as if they had been printed last year.","In the early 1980s, scientists found an amazing way to study this ink without harming a single page. At the Crocker Nuclear Laboratory at the University of California, Davis, the physicist Thomas Cahill and the historian Richard Schwab used a cyclotron, a machine that speeds up tiny particles called protons, to send a very gentle beam through pages of a Gutenberg Bible. The protons made the atoms in the ink give off X-rays, and each chemical element gives off its own pattern. This told the researchers which metals were in the ink.","They discovered that Gutenberg's ink was unusually rich in lead and copper, which helps explain its gleam. Other books from the same years did not show this metal-rich ink, so Cahill joked that it was almost as if Gutenberg had left a signature. The tests also showed small differences between batches of ink, which gave historians clues about how many presses might have been working on the Bible at the same time. Those clues are interesting but not certain, and experts still discuss what they really prove."]},{"heading":"Step Six: The Press, an Old Machine with a New Job","paragraphs":["Finally, the inked type had to meet the paper with strong, even pressure. In East Asia, printers pressed paper onto woodblocks by rubbing the back of the sheet with a brush or pad, which works well on thin, soft paper. But European paper was thicker and stiffer, and metal type needed firm, flat pressure all at once. Rubbing would never be even enough across a big page.","Gutenberg did not have to invent a pressing machine from nothing, and he did not. Europeans had been using screw presses for well over a thousand years. Since Roman times, farmers had squeezed grapes for wine and olives for oil with big wooden presses. A heavy wooden screw was turned with a bar, pushing a flat board down hard onto whatever lay underneath. Papermakers used similar presses to squeeze water out of stacks of newly made sheets, and bookbinders used them to press books flat.","Gutenberg adapted this familiar machine for printing. The flat board that pressed down was called the platen. The forme of locked-up type lay on a flat bed underneath. The paper was laid on top of the inked type, the pressman pulled the bar to turn the screw, the platen pressed down, and the ink jumped from the type to the paper. One important improvement was to stop the platen from twisting as the screw turned, because any twist would drag the paper sideways across the wet ink and smear the letters.","No press used by Gutenberg himself has survived, so we do not know exactly what it looked like. The working replicas you can see today, such as the one at the Gutenberg Museum in Mainz, are reconstructions based on the earliest pictures of print shops, which date from several decades after his death. Over the following years printers added more improvements, such as a sliding bed that could be rolled in and out from under the platen. The day-to-day work of running a press, the inking, the paper handling, and the teamwork of a busy shop, is a story of its own, which you will read in Part 11.","The key point is this: the press was an old idea given a new job. Gutenberg himself did not invent the screw press, and he never needed to. His achievement was to see that a winemaker's tool could become the final link in a chain of inventions that started with a single steel punch."]},{"heading":"Putting the System Together, and Giving Fair Credit","paragraphs":["Now we can see the whole chain at once. A punchcutter carves a backwards letter in steel. The punch is struck into copper to make a matrix. The matrix is clamped into the hand mould. Molten type metal is poured in, and out drops a sort, again and again, thousands of times. The sorts are set into lines and pages, inked with oily black ink, and pressed onto paper in an adapted screw press. After printing, the type is cleaned and sorted back into its cases, ready to spell out completely different words on the next page. Each step on its own was not completely new. Together, they made something the world had never had: a fast, reliable way to mass-produce reusable metal type in Europe.","Gutenberg also had high artistic ambitions. He wanted his printed pages to look as beautiful as the finest handwritten books. Scribes of the time used a dense, dark style of writing now called textura, because the page looks woven like a textile. They also used many joined letters, called ligatures, and many short forms of common words, called abbreviations, to fit text neatly into each line. To imitate this, Gutenberg's type for his great Bible had around 290 different characters: capitals, small letters, punctuation, many ligatures, abbreviations, and several slightly different widths of the same letter.","Why so many? Remember that every line in a column must be exactly the same width, a problem called justification. Instead of making the gaps between words very wide or very narrow, Gutenberg's typesetters could swap in a narrower version of a letter, or a ligature, or an abbreviation, to make each line fit perfectly while keeping the spacing even. The result is columns that look wonderfully even. Roughly 60 characters would have been enough just to spell the words; the other 200 or so were there for beauty.","All this meant a lot of metal. Setting several pages at once for a book as big as the Bible needed a huge stock of sorts. Some experts estimate that something like 100,000 pieces of type might have been needed for a big job like this, because the same sorts could be reused once a set of pages was printed. Across the whole Bible, the letters printed add up to millions, but they were made from that much smaller, constantly recycled stock.","Now for fair credit, which matters a great deal in this story. Gutenberg did not invent printing, and he was not the first person to print with movable type, or even with metal type. Woodblock printing had flourished in China for centuries. Bi Sheng made movable type from baked clay in the 1040s. And in Korea, printers were casting metal type by the 1200s; Jikji, printed at Heungdeok Temple in 1377, survives as the oldest known book printed with movable metal type, about 78 years before Gutenberg's Bible.","Korean metal type was made differently. Experts believe that in the Goryeo kingdom each character was usually carved in beeswax, covered in clay, and heated so the wax melted away, leaving a hollow to be filled with molten bronze. This is called lost-wax casting. Later, in the Joseon kingdom, a sand-mould method became common, where carved wooden characters were pressed into fine sand to make the mould. These methods produced beautiful type, but each mould was used only once or a few times. Gutenberg's punch, matrix and adjustable hand mould allowed the same letter to be cast over and over, very fast, with identical height. That is the true heart of his achievement: not the first movable type, but a way to make enormous amounts of it in Europe quickly and cheaply.","Historians still debate exactly how Gutenberg's earliest type was made, and whether his first experiments used the same tools that later typefounders did. But by the early 1450s, his workshop clearly had a working system. The punches were cut, the matrices struck, the moulds were busy, and the ink pots were full. With his system finally working, Gutenberg set out on the most daring project of all: a giant, beautiful Bible."]}],"deepDive":"How much of the classic punch-matrix-mould story really goes back to Gutenberg himself? For most of the 20th century, scholars followed Aloys Ruppel of the Gutenberg Museum, who argued that Gutenberg devised the adjustable hand mould and that this was the decisive invention. In the 1920s some researchers had proposed that Gutenberg used sand casting instead, and Ruppel rejected that idea. The debate reopened in 2001, when the rare-book scholar Paul Needham and the computer scientist Blaise Agüera y Arcas used digital imaging to compare thousands of individual letters in some of the earliest Mainz printing. They reported that supposedly identical letters showed more variation than expected if every one had come from a single permanent matrix, suggesting that the earliest type may have been cast with temporary moulds of some kind. Not every expert accepts their conclusions, and the study looked at early printing types rather than settling every question about the Bible type. What almost everyone agrees on is that within a few decades, European typefounding used steel punches, copper matrices and adjustable hand moulds, a system that lasted with only modest changes until the 1800s. The metallurgy also deserves a closer look. Most liquid metals shrink by a few percent as they solidify, which rounds off fine detail. Antimony and the hard antimony-tin compounds that crystallise in type metal expand slightly instead, so a well-balanced alloy freezes with almost no net shrinkage and fills the matrix sharply. Later standard alloys contained roughly 3 to 20 percent tin and 11 to 30 percent antimony, with lead making up the rest; Gutenberg's own proportions are unknown. The ink research at UC Davis in the 1980s used a technique called PIXE (particle-induced X-ray emission), a non-destructive method that identifies elements by the X-rays they give off when struck by protons. Its finding of high copper and lead in Gutenberg's ink, and differences between ink batches, has been used to argue about how many presses worked on the Bible and when, but these reconstructions remain hypotheses rather than settled history.","funFact":"Antimony, one of the metals in type metal, is one of the very few substances that expands slightly when it turns from liquid to solid, which is why it helped printing type come out of the mould with crisp, sharp edges.","check":{"q":"In Gutenberg's system, what was the matrix?","a":"A copper block with a letter-shaped dent, made by striking a steel punch into it, used as the mould for casting type","wrong":["A wooden block with a whole page carved on it","The flat board in the press that pushed the paper down","A sticky black ink made from linseed oil and soot"]},"sources":[{"title":"Just My Type: Making Letters at the Type Foundry","publisher":"Library of Congress (Bibliomania blog)","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/bibliomania/2024/12/17/just-my-type-making-letters-at-the-type-foundry/"},{"title":"The Invention","publisher":"Gutenberg Museum Mainz","url":"https://www.gutenberg.de/en/erfindung/"},{"title":"Gutenberg's Inventions – Part 3: Type and type case","publisher":"drupa / Messe Düsseldorf","url":"https://www.drupa.com/en/Media_News/drupa_blog/Print_Technologies/Pioneers_and_History_of_Printing/Gutenberg%E2%80%99s_Inventions_%E2%80%93_Part_3_Type_and_type_case"},{"title":"Antimony","publisher":"HyperPhysics, Georgia State University","url":"https://hyperphysics.gsu.edu/hbase/pertab/Sb.html"},{"title":"Cyclotron unlocks secrets surrounding Gutenberg","publisher":"The Christian Science Monitor","url":"https://csmonitor.com/1983/1020/102013.html"},{"title":"Incunabula: Dawn of Western Printing, Chapter 2","publisher":"National Diet Library, Japan","url":"https://www.ndl.go.jp/incunabula/e/chapter2"},{"title":"Art of printing","publisher":"The Korea Times","url":"https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/lifestyle/20150426/art-of-printing"},{"title":"Type metal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_metal"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/09-1.jpg","alt":"A steel punch (left) and the copper matrix struck from it (right), from an 1876 book on type-founding.","file":"De Vinne 1876 - Punch and matrix.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Vinne_1876_-_Punch_and_matrix.jpg","author":"Theodore Low De Vinne (1828-1914) (author of the book for copyright…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/09-2.jpg","alt":"A typefounder's hand mould opened into its two halves, with the matrix held on by a wire spring above.","file":"Mould (typefoundry).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mould_(typefoundry).jpg","author":"Diderot e D'Alembert, Fournier the Young, Giovanni Bodoni, Fratelli Amoretti","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/09-3.jpg","alt":"A reconstructed wooden screw press in Gutenberg's style at the Gutenberg Museum in Mainz.","file":"Gutenberg's printing press in the Gutenberg Museum, Mainz, Germany (48988292696).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gutenberg%27s_printing_press_in_the_Gutenberg_Museum,_Mainz,_Germany_(48988292696).jpg","author":"dronepicr","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4092},{"n":10,"title":"The Gutenberg Bible and a Broken Partnership","when":"about 1452 – 1468","summary":"Gutenberg's team printed a giant, gorgeous Bible in two huge volumes, so neat that a future pope said you could read it without glasses. But Gutenberg's money partner took him to court, and much of his workshop ended up in other hands. The man who changed the world of books ended his life with a modest allowance of grain, wine and clothes, never rich.","sections":[{"heading":"Why Start with a Giant Bible?","paragraphs":["By the early 1450s, Gutenberg's workshop in Mainz had a working system: punches, matrices, hand moulds, type metal, oily ink and presses. The team had already printed small things, such as Latin grammar booklets and, a little later, indulgence certificates. Now Gutenberg chose a project so big and so difficult that it seems almost crazy: a complete Latin Bible, in large format, as beautiful as the finest handwritten books in Europe.","Why the Bible? In the Europe of the 1400s, the Bible was the most important book of all. Churches, monasteries, cathedral schools and universities all needed copies for services, study and teaching. The text everyone used was the Latin translation known as the Vulgate, made mostly by Saint Jerome around the year 400. A large church Bible was expensive and took a professional scribe a very long time to copy, often a year or more. If Gutenberg could produce many copies at once, he would have customers ready and waiting, and he could prove to the world that his new art could match the best scribes.","There was also a business reason. Small items like grammar booklets earned some money, but Gutenberg had borrowed huge sums, especially from the Mainz businessman Johann Fust. A big, prestigious book that could be sold for a high price to wealthy churches and monasteries might finally pay back those debts. It was a giant bet.","Today we call the result the Gutenberg Bible. Experts also call it the 42-line Bible, or B42 for short, because most of its pages have 42 lines of text in each of their two columns. Sometimes you will also see it called the Mazarin Bible, because the first copy that scholars studied closely, in the 1700s, belonged to the library of Cardinal Mazarin in Paris."]},{"heading":"Building the Biggest Book","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into Gutenberg's workshop in Mainz around 1453. The air smells of hot metal, linseed oil and damp paper. In one corner a caster pours type metal into a hand mould. At slanted wooden cases, compositors pick out tiny backwards letters one by one. Big wooden presses creak as pressmen heave on their bars, and stacks of freshly printed sheets hang drying. Historians think the workshop may have had perhaps twenty or so workers and, at its busiest, several presses running at once, though nobody wrote down the exact numbers.","The finished Bible was enormous. It was printed in two large volumes, with roughly 640 leaves, which means about 1,280 printed pages. Each page is about 40 centimetres tall, a little bigger than a large atlas. The text is set in two columns, in a dark, dense style of letters called textura, designed to look just like the handwriting of the best scribes.","Here is a curious detail. The very first pages of the Bible do not have 42 lines per column. The first several pages have 40 lines, then one page has 41, and only after that do the pages settle at 42. Historians think the printers decided to squeeze in more lines after they had started, probably to save paper and vellum, which were very expensive. Two extra lines per column might not sound like much, but across more than a thousand pages, it saved a lot of sheets.","The printers did not set the whole book from start to finish in order. To work faster, they probably split the text into sections and set and printed several parts at the same time on different presses. Setting each page was slow work: compositors had to choose from around 290 different characters, including ligatures and abbreviations, to make every line fit perfectly. Printing all the copies probably took around two years, and the Bible appears to have been finished by about 1454 or 1455.","How many copies were made? Nobody knows for sure. Most estimates fall at about 160 to 180 copies. Of these, roughly three-quarters were printed on paper and the rest on vellum, which is fine calfskin. Some experts estimate about 135 on paper and about 35 on vellum. The paper was high-quality paper imported from Italy, and if you hold a page up to the light you can still see its watermarks, little designs left by the papermakers' moulds, which help experts trace where the paper came from. A single vellum copy needed the skins of a great many calves, by some estimates well over a hundred, so these copies were extremely costly."]},{"heading":"Hand-Painted Colour, and Pen Against Press","paragraphs":["If you could open two Gutenberg Bibles side by side, you might be surprised: they do not look the same. The black text is identical, because it was printed from the same type. But almost everything else, the colours, the decorations and the big first letters, was added by hand after printing, and that work was done differently for each copy.","In handwritten books of the time, chapter headings and important words were often written in red ink. This was called rubrication, from the Latin word for red, and the person who did it was a rubricator. Gutenberg's team at first tried to print red headings on the press, which meant inking some type red and some black and printing very carefully. It was slow and fiddly, and after a few pages they gave up. In most of the Bible, gaps were simply left in the printed text, and a rubricator filled them in by hand with red and blue ink.","Big decorated initial letters at the beginning of each book of the Bible were also left blank by the printers, so that artists called illuminators could paint them in. Wealthy buyers could pay for gold leaf, curling vines, flowers, animals and even tiny scenes in the margins. Other owners chose plain red and blue letters. Some copies were decorated in Mainz, others in different cities far away, in the local style. That is why each surviving copy is a unique blend of printing and handwork, almost a bridge between the old world of manuscripts and the new world of print.","Buyers also took their copies to bookbinders of their choice, who bound them in wooden boards covered with leather, often with metal corners and clasps. Some copies were bound in two volumes, others in three or four. So when a Gutenberg Bible left the workshop, it was really a stack of printed sheets, and its new owner finished turning it into a book.","To understand what a leap the Gutenberg Bible was, it helps to compare it with a book made the old way at almost exactly the same time and place. In the Great Hall of the Library of Congress in Washington, D.C., two glass cases face each other. One holds the library's printed Gutenberg Bible. The other holds the Giant Bible of Mainz, a huge handwritten Bible made in the Mainz area in the early 1450s, while Gutenberg's presses were busy nearby.","The Giant Bible of Mainz was written by a single scribe over a period of more than a year, from April 1452 to July 1453, letter by letter, with a quill pen. It is a magnificent work of art. But when the scribe finished, the world had exactly one new Bible. In roughly the same span of time, or a little longer, Gutenberg's workshop produced somewhere around 160 to 180 copies. Standing between those two cases, you can see the old world and the new world of books looking at each other across the room.","The two books also show how closely early printers copied the look of manuscripts. The printed Bible uses the same kind of dark textura letters, the same two-column layout, the same wide margins and the same habit of leaving room for coloured initials. Gutenberg was not trying to make something that looked new. He was trying to make something that looked exactly like the best handwritten books, only many of them at once. Readers of the time would have found the printed pages familiar, which probably helped them trust the new invention.","Speed did not mean the work was easy or cheap. Every copy still needed paper or vellum, a rubricator, perhaps an illuminator, and a binder. A finished Gutenberg Bible was still a costly object that only a church, a monastery, a university or a rich person could afford. But the most time-consuming part, writing out more than a thousand pages of text, had been done by a machine, and done far more quickly and consistently than any scribe could manage. Every copy had the same words in the same places, which meant that two readers in different cities could point to the same page and line. That kind of sameness would later become one of print's greatest gifts to scholarship.","One more difference is worth noticing. A scribe could make mistakes that appeared in only one copy. A printer's mistake appeared in every copy printed from that page of type. So printers had to check their work carefully before printing, and proofreading became an important job in print shops, as you will see in the next part."]},{"heading":"A Future Pope Sees the Pages","paragraphs":["One of the most exciting clues about the Gutenberg Bible comes from a letter. Its writer was Enea Silvio Piccolomini, an Italian churchman, poet and diplomat who later became Pope Pius II. In the autumn of 1454 he was in Frankfurt, a big trading city about 40 kilometres from Mainz, attending an important meeting of the rulers of the Holy Roman Empire. Frankfurt also had a famous market fair, where merchants came from far and wide.","On 12 March 1455, Piccolomini wrote to his friend Cardinal Juan de Carvajal in Rome. He described an amazing man he had heard about in Frankfurt who was offering Bibles for sale. Piccolomini said he had not seen a complete Bible, but he had seen some quires, which are bundles of sheets from different parts of the book. He praised the letters as extremely neat and correct, and said that Carvajal would be able to read them easily, even without his glasses.","Piccolomini also passed on numbers. Some people told him that 158 copies had been completed; others said 180. He was not sure which was right, but he had no doubt the copies existed. He added that, sadly, he probably could not buy one for his friend, because buyers had already been found for all of them before they were even finished.","This letter matters for several reasons. It is the earliest known written description of the Gutenberg Bible. It helps date the project, showing that printed sheets were already being shown around by autumn 1454. It tells us that the copies sold quickly, to eager buyers. And it gives us those numbers, 158 or 180, which match the experts' modern estimates. The one thing the letter does not tell us is the name of the amazing man in Frankfurt. It may have been Gutenberg himself, or someone working with him, but we cannot be sure."]},{"heading":"The Lawsuit of 1455","paragraphs":["Just when Gutenberg's great Bible was finally finished and selling, his partnership fell apart. To understand why, we need to remember the money. Around 1450, Johann Fust had lent Gutenberg 800 guilders, with Gutenberg's tools and equipment as security for the loan. Around 1452, Fust put in another 800 guilders, this time as an investment in what the partners called the work of the books. In total that was about 1,600 guilders, an enormous sum at the time, enough to buy several fine houses in a city.","In 1455, Fust took Gutenberg to court. He demanded his money back, plus interest, and the total he claimed was more than 2,000 guilders. Gutenberg disagreed. He argued, among other things, that the second 800 guilders had been Fust's share in a joint business, not a simple loan he had to repay, and that he had never received all of the money in the way it was described.","We know about the case from a remarkable surviving document called the Helmasperger Notarial Instrument, named after Ulrich Helmasperger, the notary who wrote it down. It records a meeting on 6 November 1455 in a large hall of a monastery in Mainz, where Fust swore an oath about how much money he had handed over. Among the witnesses on Fust's side was Peter Schoeffer, a young man who had trained as a scribe, studied in Paris, and then worked in Gutenberg's own workshop. Gutenberg himself did not attend the final hearing and sent representatives instead.","The court's decision went largely in Fust's favour, ordering Gutenberg to repay a large part of what was claimed. Gutenberg probably could not pay. Historians think that, as a result, much of his printing equipment and probably the type and remaining stock of the Bible passed to Fust. In other words, at the very moment his masterpiece was complete, Gutenberg lost control of much of the business he had spent so many years building.","It is easy to see Fust as a villain, but the true story is more complicated. Fust had risked a fortune on an untested invention and had waited years for any return. Gutenberg seems to have spent money freely on experiments and perfection. Both men were taking big risks, and when the money ran short, the partnership broke. What is clear is that Gutenberg did not get rich from his invention."]},{"heading":"Fust and Schoeffer's Masterpiece: The Mainz Psalter","paragraphs":["After the lawsuit, Fust teamed up with Peter Schoeffer, who knew Gutenberg's methods from the inside and was a gifted designer of letters. Schoeffer later married Fust's daughter Christina, so the partnership became a family business too. Together they started one of the most successful printing firms of the 1400s.","Their first great book was a Psalter, a book of psalms and other texts sung in church services. It was completed on 14 August 1457. This Mainz Psalter was a showpiece, and it broke several records at once.","First, it is the first printed book to include a printed date. Second, it names its printers: at the end of the book there is a short paragraph, called a colophon, that proudly names Johann Fust and Peter Schoeffer. The colophon also explains, in Latin, that the book was made by a clever invention of printing and forming letters, without any writing with a pen. In other words, the printers were telling the world: no scribe made this, a machine did. Gutenberg's own name never appeared in his books, which is one reason why later generations sometimes forgot his role.","Third, the Psalter is famous for its splendid printed initials in two colours, red and blue, with delicate patterns curling around the large letters. Instead of leaving gaps for a hand painter, the printers printed these decorations on the press. Experts think the initial letter and its decoration were made as separate metal pieces that fitted together like a jigsaw puzzle. Each piece was inked in its own colour, then the pieces were put back together and printed in one go along with the black text. This was one of the very first examples of colour printing in Europe.","The Psalter was printed on vellum, and only about ten copies survive today, which makes it even rarer than the Gutenberg Bible. Fust and Schoeffer went on to print many more books. Fust died in 1466, probably of plague, while on a business trip to Paris. Schoeffer kept the firm going for decades and became one of the leading printers and booksellers in Germany."]},{"heading":"Gutenberg's Quiet Last Years","paragraphs":["What happened to Gutenberg after 1455? The records are patchy, but it seems he did not give up. He probably kept a smaller workshop of his own, using some of his earlier type and equipment. Several books and smaller printed items from the late 1450s and 1460s have been linked to him or his circle, such as a big Latin dictionary and grammar called the Catholicon, dated 1460, though historians still argue about who actually printed it. None of these works carries Gutenberg's name.","In 1462, Mainz was caught up in a violent struggle between two rival archbishops. In October of that year, the forces of Adolf II of Nassau captured the city. Many citizens lost property or were forced to leave, and printers scattered to other towns, a story you will follow in Part 12. Records suggest Gutenberg was among those who lost out.","Then, in January 1465, came a surprising honour. The same Adolf II, now archbishop and elector of Mainz, named Gutenberg a member of his court. This came with a kind of pension: every year Gutenberg would receive a set of court clothing, about 2,200 litres of grain and about 2,000 litres of wine, and he would be excused from certain city taxes and duties. That is far more grain and wine than one person could use, so some historians suggest Gutenberg may have used it to feed and pay the workers in his shop. Nobody knows exactly why the archbishop rewarded him; it may have been to recognise his invention, or to make peace after the troubles of 1462.","Gutenberg died in Mainz in early 1468. The date often given is 3 February, but the Gutenberg Society notes that this may not be reliable; what we know for certain is that a document dated 26 February 1468 shows he had already died, because his printing equipment was being handed over to a Mainz official, Dr. Konrad Humery. He was probably buried in the Franciscan church in Mainz, a building that was later demolished, so his grave has been lost.","Gutenberg was not famous or wealthy at the end of his life. He had no known children, his name was not printed in his books, and for some time others received much of the credit. Yet by the time he died, printing shops were already opening in Italy and elsewhere, and the art he had developed was spreading across Europe."]},{"heading":"Who Was First? A Dutch Legend and a Priceless Book","paragraphs":["Because Gutenberg never signed his books, arguments about who invented European printing started early. The most famous rival claim comes from the Netherlands. According to a legend, a man named Laurens Janszoon Coster of Haarlem was the true inventor. The story was written down by the Dutch scholar Hadrianus Junius in his book Batavia, published in 1588, more than a century after the events it describes.","In Junius's tale, Coster was walking in the woods near Haarlem in the 1420s or 1430s and carved letters from tree bark to amuse his grandchildren. He noticed that the letters left marks, and this gave him the idea for printing. The legend even claims that a dishonest workman stole Coster's type one Christmas night and ran off to Mainz with the secret. It is a lively story, and Haarlem built a statue of Coster in its market square. But there is no solid evidence for it. A real Laurens Janszoon did live in Haarlem in the early 1400s and held town offices, but no books printed by him are known. Historians treat the Coster story as a legend, not as history.","Meanwhile, the Gutenberg Bible itself became one of the most treasured objects in the world. About 49 copies survive, some complete and some only partly complete, along with scattered single leaves. They are kept in great libraries in Germany, the United States, Britain, France, Japan and other countries. In 1930 the United States Congress approved the purchase of a large collection of early printed books from the collector Otto Vollbehr, which included a Gutenberg Bible on vellum. That copy, one of only three perfect vellum copies known, is on display in the Library of Congress in Washington, D.C.","Prices for these Bibles became legendary. In 1978 one copy was sold for about 2.2 million US dollars, and the same year the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas acquired its own complete paper copy for about 2.4 million dollars. In 1987 a single volume sold for about 5.4 million dollars, including the auction house's fee. Today, many copies have been photographed page by page and put online, so anyone with a screen can turn the pages of a book once read only by monks and scholars.","Gutenberg's Bible proved that metal type could make books as beautiful as any manuscript, and in large numbers. Fust and Schoeffer's Psalter proved that a printing business could keep going and even improve without its inventor. Gutenberg's workshop was the start, but how did a busy print shop actually turn type into pages, day after day?"]}],"deepDive":"The Gutenberg Bible raises several puzzles that historians still argue about. The first is chronology. Piccolomini's letter of 12 March 1455 shows that printed quires were circulating in Frankfurt by the autumn of 1454, so the printing must have begun by about 1452. The change from 40 to 41 to 42 lines on the earliest pages, and the early attempt at printing red headings that was soon abandoned, are read as signs of a project being adjusted on the fly to save materials and time. A second puzzle is the size of the edition. Piccolomini heard both 158 and 180; modern estimates usually say about 160 to 180, of which roughly 135 were on paper and 35 on vellum. Survival counts vary depending on what is counted: about 49 copies survive as complete or substantial copies, with further fragments and single leaves, some of which were cut up in later centuries and used to reinforce bindings. A third question is how the workshop was organised. In the 1980s, proton-beam analysis of the ink at UC Davis showed changes between batches that some researchers read as evidence that work began on two presses and expanded to six, and that the final pages used a different ink, possibly after the split with Fust. These are interesting hypotheses rather than proven facts. The Fust lawsuit is also debated. The only detailed record, the Helmasperger Notarial Instrument of 6 November 1455, presents mainly Fust's side, and it does not say outright that Gutenberg lost all his equipment; that is inferred from what Fust and Schoeffer printed next and from the type Gutenberg seems to have kept. Finally, there is the question of credit. Erasmus and others later praised Fust and Schoeffer as the inventors, and Dutch writers promoted Coster. The documentary evidence, especially the 1439 Strasbourg lawsuit and the Mainz records, strongly supports Gutenberg's central role in developing printing with movable metal type in Europe, while Asian printers had developed movable type, including metal type, long before.","funFact":"The first pages of the Gutenberg Bible have only 40 lines in each column; the printers changed to 42 lines a few pages in, most likely to save expensive paper and vellum.","check":{"q":"What was special about the Mainz Psalter printed by Fust and Schoeffer in 1457?","a":"It was the first printed book with a printed date and the printers' names, and it had printed colored initials","wrong":["It was the first book ever printed anywhere in the world","It was printed by Laurens Coster in Haarlem","It was the first book printed in English"]},"sources":[{"title":"Gutenberg and his time in data (timeline)","publisher":"Gutenberg Museum Mainz","url":"https://www.gutenberg.de/en/zeit/zeitleiste_gutenberg"},{"title":"Gutenberg's final years","publisher":"Gutenberg-Gesellschaft (International Gutenberg Society)","url":"https://www.gutenberg-gesellschaft.de/en/johannes-gutenberg/gutenbergs-final-years"},{"title":"Slice it up (review of a biography of Gutenberg)","publisher":"London Review of Books","url":"https://www.lrb.co.uk/the-paper/v47/n21/adam-smyth/slice-it-up"},{"title":"Gutenberg Bible display","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://loc.gov/loc/lcib/9621/bible.html"},{"title":"The Gutenberg Bible","publisher":"Harry Ransom Center, University of Texas at Austin","url":"https://www.hrc.utexas.edu/gutenberg-bible/"},{"title":"Gutenberg Bible","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gutenberg_Bible"},{"title":"Mainz Psalter","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainz_Psalter"},{"title":"Giant Bible of Mainz","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_Bible_of_Mainz"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/10-1.jpg","alt":"An open Gutenberg Bible at the New York Public Library: two black columns of type with red and blue painted initials.","file":"Gutenberg Bible, Lenox Copy, New York Public Library, 2009. Pic 01.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gutenberg_Bible,_Lenox_Copy,_New_York_Public_Library,_2009._Pic_01.jpg","author":"NYC Wanderer (Kevin Eng)","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/10-2.jpg","alt":"Two pages of Fust and Schoeffer's 1457 Mainz Psalter, with red and blue initials printed right in the press.","file":"1457 Mainz Psalter.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1457_Mainz_Psalter.jpg","author":"Peter Schöffer, Johannes Fust","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/10-3.jpg","alt":"The statue of Laurens Janszoon Coster on the Grote Markt in Haarlem, holding up a piece of type.","file":"Laurens Janszoon Coster statue, Haarlem, The Netherlands.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurens_Janszoon_Coster_statue,_Haarlem,_The_Netherlands.jpg","author":"Bj.schoenmakers","license":"CC0"}],"words":3731},{"n":11,"title":"Inside an Early Print Shop","when":"about 1455 – 1800","summary":"Step inside a busy old print shop! One worker picks tiny metal letters out of wooden boxes and lines them up backwards, while two others ink the type with leather balls and pull a big wooden lever to press each sheet. It was hot, noisy, inky work, and the same kind of wooden press was used for about 350 years.","sections":[{"heading":"Good Morning, Print Shop","paragraphs":["Imagine pushing open a heavy wooden door in a European town sometime around the year 1600. The first thing you notice is the smell: a mix of linseed oil, soot, damp paper and warm metal. The second thing is the noise. Somewhere a big wooden machine creaks and thumps every few seconds, as steady as a heartbeat. Workers call to each other, a boy hurries past with a stack of wet sheets, and lines of paper hang drying overhead like laundry.","This is a print shop, sometimes called a printing house. By 1600 there were hundreds of them across Europe, from small family workshops with a single press to big businesses like the one run by Christophe Plantin in Antwerp, in today's Belgium. Plantin, a Frenchman who moved to Antwerp around 1550, built one of the largest printing firms of the 1500s. At its busiest it kept more than a dozen presses running. His family business, later run by his son-in-law Jan Moretus and his descendants, kept going in the same building for about 300 years. Today that building is the Plantin-Moretus Museum, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2005, and it holds the two oldest printing presses that still survive anywhere in the world.","How do we know so much about what went on inside these shops? Partly from surviving tools, presses and account books like Plantin's, and partly from a remarkable book. Between 1683 and 1684 an English printer, mapmaker and globe maker named Joseph Moxon published a printing manual as part of his series called Mechanick Exercises. It was the first detailed printing manual in English, explaining step by step how type was cast, how pages were set and how the press was worked. Historians still use it today, though they remember that Moxon was describing how things were supposed to be done, which is not always how busy workers actually did them.","The basic workflow looked like this. First came the copy, meaning the text to be printed, usually a handwritten manuscript or an older printed book. Next the compositor turned the copy into lines of metal type. Then the pages were checked, locked up, and handed to the press crew. The pressmen inked the type and printed hundreds of sheets. Finally the type was cleaned and put back into its boxes, ready for the next page. Round and round it went, every working day, for centuries."]},{"heading":"The Compositor and the Magic Boxes","paragraphs":["The compositor was the worker who put the words together. The name comes from the Latin word for someone who places things together, and that is exactly what he did: thousands of times a day, he reached into a wooden tray and placed one tiny piece of metal next to another.","Those trays were called type cases. A type case was a shallow wooden drawer divided into many little compartments, each holding one kind of letter, number or punctuation mark. A single piece of type was called a sort. A whole set of one style and size of type, like all the letters needed for a book, was called a fount, which is where our modern word font comes from. The compositor stood in front of his cases, which rested on a slanted wooden stand, with the copy pinned up where he could read it.","The compartments were not all the same size. The letters used most often, like e, t, a, o and n in English, had bigger boxes placed close to the middle, where the compositor's hand could reach them quickest. Rare letters such as z, x or q lived in small boxes at the edges. The case was arranged by how often letters were used, not in alphabetical order. After years of practice, a compositor's hand flew to the right box without his eyes even looking, the way a good typist's fingers find keys today.","Here is where a famous pair of words comes from. In many shops, first in the Low Countries (by the 1560s) and England (by the 1580s), and later in France, the type was split between two cases. Capital letters sat in one case and small letters in another. The case of capitals rested higher up and further back on the stand, at a steeper angle, while the case of small letters sat lower down, right in front of the compositor. So printers began calling capitals 'upper case' letters and small letters 'lower case' letters. Hundreds of years later, your computer keyboard and your teacher still use those words. Not every country did this, though. In Germany and Scandinavia, printers kept using a single big case for longer, and the earliest printers of the 1400s seem to have used one large case for each typeface too.","The compositor's main tool was the composing stick, a small tray, often made of iron or brass, that he held in his left hand. One end of the stick could be adjusted and locked, setting the exact width of a line, which printers called the measure. With his right hand, the compositor picked letters from the case one at a time and placed them in the stick, working from left to right along the line.","There was a big puzzle, though. A piece of type has its letter carved backwards, like a rubber stamp, so that it prints the right way around. That meant the compositor had to set every line upside down and backwards. To help, each sort had a small groove cut into one side called the nick. By feeling for the nick with his thumb, a compositor could tell which way up a letter was without stopping to look. Lowercase letters that are mirror images of each other, such as b and d, or p and q, were especially easy to mix up, so careful compositors had to keep their wits about them. Some people say this is where the saying 'mind your p's and q's' comes from, but nobody knows for sure. It is one of several possible explanations, not a proven fact.","Skilled compositors could read backwards text almost as easily as normal text. A good compositor in the hand-press era might set something like a thousand to fifteen hundred characters in an hour, depending on the text, the language and how messy the handwriting was. That sounds fast, but a single big book page could hold two or three thousand characters, so setting one page could take a couple of hours."]},{"heading":"Making Every Line the Same Width","paragraphs":["Look at a page in almost any printed book and you will notice that the right-hand edge of the text is perfectly straight, just like the left. This neat effect is called justification, and in a hand-press shop it was done by hand, one line at a time.","Here is how. When a compositor reached the end of a line in his stick, the words almost never filled the space exactly. Maybe there was a gap the width of two letters left over. So he took out the spaces between words and swapped them for slightly wider or thinner ones. Spaces were real objects, small blocks of metal that were a little shorter than the letters so they would not touch the ink and print. They came in different widths: some as wide as the type was tall, called an em, some half that, called an en, and thinner ones still. Larger blank blocks called quads filled the empty ends of short lines, like the last line of a paragraph. The compositor tucked these spaces in until the line fitted the stick snugly, tight enough that no letter would wobble or fall out.","Early printers had a few extra tricks for making lines fit. Like the scribes they copied, they used many abbreviations, such as a little line over a vowel to show that a letter m or n had been left out. They also had special joined letters called ligatures, such as ff or fi cast as a single piece. Using a shorter or longer form of a word could help a line fit. As you read in earlier parts, Gutenberg's Bible type had about 290 different characters, partly for exactly this reason. Over the next centuries, printers slowly used fewer abbreviations, and spelling and spacing became more regular.","A composing stick held only a few lines at a time. When it was full, the compositor carefully slid the lines out onto a galley, a flat tray with raised edges on three sides. Line by line and stick by stick, a whole page built up on the galley. Then the compositor tied the page tightly around with string so the hundreds of loose pieces could be moved as one block without spilling. A dropped page of type was a disaster that printers called 'pie': a jumbled heap of letters that had to be sorted back into the cases one by one.","Pages were then taken to a large, flat stone or metal table called the imposing stone. There they were arranged inside an iron frame called a chase. The empty spaces between the pages and the frame were filled with pieces of wood called furniture, and the whole thing was squeezed tight with wedges, so that the type could be lifted and carried without a single letter falling out. A locked-up chase full of pages was called a forme. A forme for a big book could be very heavy, since every letter was a little block of lead alloy."]},{"heading":"The Folding Puzzle: Imposition","paragraphs":["Here is a brain teaser that every printer had to solve. Paper in the hand-press era came in big sheets, often around the size of a modern newspaper page opened out, or a bit smaller. Printers almost never printed one book page on one sheet. Instead, they printed several pages on each side of a big sheet, then folded it so the pages ended up in the right order. Arranging the pages in the forme so that this works is called imposition.","Try it yourself with a piece of scrap paper. Fold it in half once and you get 2 leaves, which means 4 pages, because each leaf has a front and a back. A book made this way is called a folio, from the Latin word for leaf. Big, grand books like the Gutenberg Bible were folios. Fold the sheet in half twice and you get 4 leaves, or 8 pages: a quarto, meaning 'fourth'. Fold it three times and you get 8 leaves, or 16 pages: an octavo, meaning 'eighth'. Smaller still was the duodecimo, with 12 leaves, or 24 pages, from one sheet. Book collectors still write these sizes in a kind of shorthand: 2° for folio, 4° for quarto and 8° for octavo.","Now number the pages on your folded paper and then unfold it. You will see something strange. The page numbers are not in a simple row. Some are upside down, and pages that end up next to each other in the book are far apart on the flat sheet. On one side of an octavo sheet, page 1 might sit beside page 16, with page 8 and page 9 upside down above them. The compositor had to place each page in exactly the right spot and the right way up in the forme, or the finished book would come out jumbled. Each side of the sheet needed its own forme. For an octavo, one forme held 8 pages for the outside of the sheet and another held the other 8 pages for the inside.","Printers also left little clues on the pages to help the people who folded and sewed the books. At the foot of certain pages they printed a signature mark, such as A, A2, A3 and so on, showing which sheet and which fold the page belonged to. The first sheet was A, the next B, and so on through the alphabet. Many books also had a catchword: the first word of the next page, printed alone at the bottom right of the page before. If a binder saw that the catchword did not match the top of the next page, he knew something was out of order. Many old books had no page numbers at all, so these marks were the real guide."]},{"heading":"Pulling the Bar: How the Press Crew Worked","paragraphs":["Once the forme was locked up, it was carried to the press and laid on a flat bed of stone or marble, set inside a sliding frame called the coffin. Now the press crew took over. Usually two men worked each press. One was the beater, or inker, and the other was the puller, who handled the paper and the big bar. They often swapped roles to share the hardest work.","Ink in a hand-press shop was thick and sticky, a bit like soft tar. It was made by boiling linseed oil into a kind of varnish and mixing it with lampblack, the fine black soot collected from burning oil or resin. Gutenberg had needed an oil-based ink because watery ink simply beads up on metal. The inker spread a little of this ink on a block and then used two ink balls to pick it up. An ink ball looked like a giant mushroom or a big round stamp: a wooden handle with a cup-shaped head, stuffed with wool or horsehair and covered with soft leather. The inker rocked the two balls against each other to spread the ink evenly, then dabbed them firmly over every part of the type. Too little ink made pale, patchy letters. Too much filled in the holes of letters like e and o and made blotches.","Meanwhile the puller got the paper ready. In most shops, paper was dampened the night before, often by dipping stacks of it in water and leaving them pressed together, because slightly damp paper is softer and takes the ink much better. The puller laid a sheet onto the tympan, a frame covered with parchment or cloth and hinged to the coffin. Little metal pins on the tympan, called points, poked through the sheet to hold it in exactly the same position every time. Then he folded down the frisket, a second, lighter frame covered with parchment or paper with windows cut out where the pages were. The frisket kept the margins clean, so that ink on the furniture and chase did not smudge the blank parts of the sheet. Then he folded the tympan and frisket down together over the inked type, like closing a sandwich.","Next the puller turned a handle called the rounce, which used straps and a drum to wind the coffin along rails under the press. Above it hung the platen, a flat block of wood or metal. With a mighty tug on the bar, which turned a big screw, he drove the platen down, squeezing the paper against the type. On many presses the platen was only big enough to cover half the forme at once, so the puller wound the coffin a little further and pulled a second time. Then he wound it back out, lifted the frisket and tympan, and peeled off the printed sheet. The whole cycle took something like 15 to 20 seconds.","That rhythm added up. A good two-man crew could print roughly 200 to 250 sheets on one side every hour, and printers measured their work in 'tokens' of about 250 sheets. A long working day might mean thousands of pulls on the bar, so press work was tiring and needed strong arms and backs. And remember, every sheet had two sides. After the first side was printed and the sheets had dried a little, the crew put on the second forme and printed the back, a step called perfecting or backing up. The points made small holes in the paper so that the second side could be lined up exactly with the first.","The freshly printed sheets were hung up to dry on lines or poles near the ceiling, often by a young apprentice. In many shops the youngest boy, who ran errands, fetched sheets and quickly became covered from head to toe in black ink, was known as the printer's devil."]},{"heading":"Hunting for Mistakes","paragraphs":["With so many tiny backwards letters, mistakes were certain. A compositor might grab a letter from the wrong box, especially if a previous worker had put it back in the wrong place. He might skip a line of the copy or repeat a word. Upside-down letters, swapped b's and d's, and missing spaces all sneaked in. So print shops developed careful ways of catching errors before hundreds of copies were printed.","First, a proof was pulled. A proof is a trial print, often made on a simple press or by hand, from the newly set forme. A reader or corrector, sometimes the master printer himself and in bigger shops a trained scholar, compared the proof against the original copy, often with a helper reading the copy aloud. The corrector marked mistakes in the margin, and the compositor then unlocked the forme, picked out the wrong letters with tweezers or a pointed tool, and put in the right ones.","Even so, errors slipped through. Sometimes a corrector spotted a mistake while the sheets were already being printed. The press would stop, the error would be fixed, and printing would carry on. The sheets already printed with the error were usually not thrown away, because paper was very expensive. They were bound into books along with the corrected sheets. This means that two copies of the same edition of an old book can be slightly different from each other. These are called stop-press corrections. In the 1900s, a scholar named Charlton Hinman compared dozens of copies of Shakespeare's First Folio of 1623 at the Folger Shakespeare Library in Washington and found many such small differences, helping scholars work out how the book had been printed page by page.","When printers noticed mistakes too late to fix, they often added a list of errata, Latin for 'errors', at the end of the book, telling readers how to correct them by hand. Some books had errata lists several pages long. In a way, our modern word typo, short for typographical error, is a souvenir of these battles with backwards metal letters.","Once a forme had been fully printed on both sides, there was one more job. The type had to be put back. This was called distributing the type, or distribution. The compositor took a handful of lines, read them backwards one more time, and dropped each sort quickly back into its own compartment. Because shops owned only a limited amount of type, often only enough for a few sheets at a time, distribution was essential. A shop printing a long book might set a few pages, print them, distribute the type and set the next pages with the same letters. Careless distribution, putting an a into the e box, was a hidden cause of mistakes in the next job."]},{"heading":"Masters, Apprentices and the Chapel","paragraphs":["A print shop was a small community. At the top was the master printer, who owned the presses and type, found the money to buy paper, chose what to print and dealt with customers, authors, booksellers and government officials. Below him worked journeymen, skilled workers who were paid by the day or by the amount of work they finished, and apprentices, young learners who usually lived with the master's family and trained for several years, often around seven in England, before they could become journeymen themselves.","Women were part of this world too, more often than old history books admit. Wives and daughters folded, sewed, kept accounts and sometimes set type, and when a master printer died, his widow often took over the business. In Paris in the 1500s, Charlotte Guillard ran the Soleil d'Or (Golden Sun) printing house, with five or six presses and around twenty-five to thirty workers, for about twenty years after the death of her second printer husband, publishing about two hundred editions in her career. Plantin's own daughters helped with proofreading in Antwerp.","In England, print shop workers formed a kind of club or workplace society called a chapel. Joseph Moxon described its customs in his manual of 1683. The chapel had its own rules, and workers who broke them, for example by swearing, fighting or leaving a candle burning, were fined. The fines often went to buy drinks for everyone. Even today, in some British printing and newspaper trade unions, a local workplace branch is still called a chapel, and its leader is called the father or mother of the chapel.","Print shops also gave our language some lasting words. We already met upper case and lower case, font, typo and proof. The phrase 'out of sorts', meaning grumpy or unwell, is sometimes said to come from compositors running out of particular pieces of type, but the phrase was used in other ways too, and word experts are not sure that printing is its real origin. Later printing gave us stereotype, which first meant a solid metal printing plate cast from a page of type, and cliché, the French printers' word for a similar cast plate. Both now mean a worn-out idea that gets repeated without thinking, which is a clever way of saying 'printed from the same old plate'."]},{"heading":"The Press That Barely Changed","paragraphs":["Here is one of the most surprising facts about the whole history of printing. If a printer from Gutenberg's workshop in the 1450s had been magically carried forward to a London print shop in the 1780s, he would have known almost exactly what to do. The wooden hand press, its ink balls, the composing stick and the type cases changed very little for about 350 years.","There were improvements, of course. Around the middle of the 1500s, many presses switched from a wooden screw to a stronger iron or brass one, which made each pull more precise. Printers added the rounce and the rolling carriage so the type could be wound in and out smoothly. In the early 1600s, the Dutch mapmaker and printer Willem Janszoon Blaeu of Amsterdam is credited with an improved design of the wooden press, around 1620, with a steadier screw mechanism and a more reliable way of moving the type under the platen, and versions of it spread in the Netherlands and later in England and America. Historians think that, thanks to such small changes and better organization, a press crew around 1600 could produce several times more work than Gutenberg's first pressmen.","But the basic idea stayed the same: a person inks the type by hand, lays one sheet at a time, and pulls a bar to print it, roughly one side of one sheet every 15 or 20 seconds. Benjamin Franklin, who trained as a printer in the 1720s and worked in a London print shop for a time, used a wooden press of the same general kind. A wooden common press from about 1720, which tradition says Franklin worked on at John Watts's London shop in 1726, is today kept at the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History in Washington, DC. Nobody can fully prove the story, but the press shows exactly the kind of machine he would have used.","Only around 1800 did that begin to change, when an English nobleman, Charles Stanhope, built a press made entirely of iron, and soon after that inventors began to harness steam power. That is a story for a later part of this Deep Dive. For now, picture the slow, steady rhythm of the wooden press: ink, lay the sheet, close, wind in, pull, pull, wind out, lift, and do it again. Repeated millions of times by thousands of crews in hundreds of towns, that rhythm carried new books into every corner of Europe.","And Mainz, where it had all begun, was about to lose its place at the center. Print shops did not stay in Mainz for long, and a war in 1462 helped scatter printers across Europe."]}],"deepDive":"How fast was a hand-press shop, really? Historians can do the arithmetic. A two-man crew working steadily managed roughly 200 to 250 impressions an hour, which is one side of one sheet each time. A run of 1,000 copies of one sheet therefore needed about 2,000 impressions (both sides), or eight to ten hours of hard pulling. For an octavo book of 20 sheets (320 pages), that is around 40,000 impressions: several weeks of work for one press, even before counting the time to set and correct the type. A Library of Congress description of hand-press work estimates that a 350-page book could tie up a press for about a month. This is why shops ran several presses at once, and why the compositors, who usually could not set type as fast as the press could print it, were often the real bottleneck.\n\nThere is another hidden limit: type. Metal type was expensive, so most shops owned only enough to keep a few sheets standing at a time. Books were therefore printed sheet by sheet, with the type distributed and reused as work went on. This 'setting by formes' sometimes meant compositors had to estimate in advance exactly which part of the manuscript would fall on which page, a skill called casting off. When they guessed wrong, pages near the end of a forme can look crammed with abbreviations or stretched with extra spacing.\n\nA whole academic field, called analytical or descriptive bibliography, studies these physical clues. Scholars compare damaged or unusual individual pieces of type that reappear on different pages to work out the order in which pages were set and printed. They compare spelling habits, such as one compositor preferring 'doe' and another 'do', to identify different workers. Charlton Hinman's study of Shakespeare's First Folio (published in 1963) used both methods and found that several compositors shared the work. For historians, an old book is not just a text but a record of the people, tools and decisions that produced it. Moxon's 1683 manual is a key source here, but scholars warn that it often describes ideal practice rather than what hurried workers actually did, so surviving books remain the final evidence.","funFact":"The two oldest surviving printing presses in the world are kept in the Plantin-Moretus Museum in Antwerp, in the same building where the Plantin and Moretus family printed books for about 300 years.","check":{"q":"Why are capital letters called 'upper case'?","a":"They were kept in the type case that sat higher up on the compositor's stand","wrong":["They were always printed at the top of the page","Gutenberg made capital letters bigger and heavier","Scribes wrote capitals above the line of text"]},"sources":[{"title":"Before Control+P: The Printing Process","publisher":"Library of Congress (Bibliomania blog)","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/bibliomania/2025/01/24/before-control-p-the-printing-process/"},{"title":"Letter case","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letter_case"},{"title":"Book size","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_size"},{"title":"Plantin-Moretus Museum","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plantin-Moretus_Museum"},{"title":"Joseph Moxon","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Moxon"},{"title":"Printing press","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printing_press"},{"title":"Christophe Plantin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christophe_Plantin"},{"title":"Charlotte Guillard","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotte_Guillard"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/11-1.jpg","alt":"The two oldest surviving printing presses in the world, from the 1500s, in the Plantin-Moretus Museum, Antwerp.","file":"Museum Plantin-Moretus Printing Press.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_Plantin-Moretus_Printing_Press.jpg","author":"Riopelle","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/11-2.jpg","alt":"A plate from Diderot's Encyclopédie: compositors setting type at slanted type cases, with drawings of type pieces below.","file":"Planche de l’Encyclopédie de Diderot et d’Alembert. Pl. 1. Imprimerie en Lettres, L’Opération de la casse, G.33153.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Planche_de_l%E2%80%99Encyclop%C3%A9die_de_Diderot_et_d%E2%80%99Alembert._Pl._1._Imprimerie_en_Lettres,_L%E2%80%99Op%C3%A9ration_de_la_casse,_G.33153.jpg","author":"Goussier, dessinateur","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/11-3.jpg","alt":"A steel composing stick made by Miller & Richard, holding a line of metal type ready to be printed.","file":"Miller & Richard composing stick.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miller_%26_Richard_composing_stick.jpg","author":"Edinburgh City of Print","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4343},{"n":12,"title":"Printing Spreads Like Wildfire","when":"1462 – 1500s","summary":"After a war in Mainz in 1462, printers packed up their skills and moved to new cities all over Europe. Soon there were print shops in Rome, Venice, Paris and London, making beautiful new letters, small pocket books and the very first book in English. By the year 1500, millions of printed books existed, and they were getting cheaper every year.","sections":[{"heading":"A Night of Fire in Mainz","paragraphs":["In the autumn of 1462, the city of Mainz was caught in the middle of a power struggle. Two rival churchmen both claimed to be Archbishop of Mainz, which in those days meant ruling a large territory, not just leading a church. One was Diether von Isenburg. The other was Adolph of Nassau, who had the support of the pope. Mainz had sided with Diether. On the night of 28 October 1462, Adolph's soldiers broke into the city. There was fighting in the streets, and houses were plundered and burned. Hundreds of people are thought to have died.","Afterwards, Adolph punished the townspeople who had opposed him. Many lost their property, and a large number of citizens were forced to leave the city. Among them, according to the Gutenberg Museum's history of the city, were workers from the print shops of Gutenberg and of Johann Fust and Peter Schoeffer. Printing in Mainz stopped for a while. Fust and Schoeffer, who had been busy printing beautiful books, seem to have printed almost nothing for around two or three years.","Imagine being one of those workers: a young compositor or pressman who had spent years learning the secret new craft, now suddenly without a job or a home. What did you have? Your skill. You knew how to cut punches, cast type, mix oily ink, set lines in a composing stick and work a press. In a world where hardly anyone else knew these things, that knowledge was worth a fortune. So printers did what skilled workers have always done. They went looking for cities that wanted what they could offer: rich trading towns full of merchants, university towns full of students and teachers, and cities with powerful churchmen who needed books.","Historians often tell the story of 1462 as the moment printing exploded out of Mainz, and it certainly helped. But it was not the only cause. Printing had already started to leave Mainz before the attack. In Strasbourg, Johannes Mentelin was printing a large Latin Bible by about 1460. In Bamberg, Albrecht Pfister printed books with woodcut pictures around 1460 to 1461, among the first printed books in German illustrated in this way. Some scholars think the effect of the sack has been exaggerated, since printing would probably have spread anyway once people saw how well it worked. The fairest way to put it is this: the sack of Mainz gave an extra push to a movement that was already beginning.","Whatever the exact cause, the results were astonishing. In 1460 printing was a rare skill found in only a handful of German towns. By 1500, only forty years later, printing presses had worked in more than 250 European towns and cities. Hundreds of print shops had opened and closed, and millions of printed books were in circulation. Let us follow some of those wandering printers on their journeys."]},{"heading":"Over the Alps: Subiaco and Rome","paragraphs":["The first printers to cross the Alps into Italy were two Germans, Konrad Sweynheym and Arnold Pannartz. Sweynheym had probably learned printing in Mainz, possibly in the Fust and Schoeffer workshop. Around 1464 or 1465 the pair settled not in a big city but in a quiet, beautiful spot in the hills east of Rome: the Benedictine monastery of Santa Scolastica at Subiaco. The monastery had many German monks, which may be why the two printers were welcomed there.","At Subiaco, in 1465, Sweynheym and Pannartz printed some of the first books ever made in Italy, including works by the ancient Roman writer Cicero and the early Christian author Lactantius. Their Lactantius, finished in October 1465, is the earliest printed book in Italy with an exact date. Their typeface was also interesting. Instead of the dark, pointed blackletter style of Gutenberg's Bible, they used a lighter, rounder letter inspired by the handwriting of Italian scholars. It was not yet a fully developed roman type like the letters you are reading now, but it was a step in that direction.","In 1467 the two printers moved to Rome itself, setting up in a house provided by a noble family, the Massimi. Rome was full of churchmen, scholars and visitors, and the printers turned out edition after edition of classical Latin authors and church writings. Another German, Ulrich Han from Ingolstadt, also started printing in Rome around the same time. According to the Gutenberg Museum, more than 40 print shops were set up in Rome before 1500, and about 25 of them were run by Germans.","Business was not easy, though. In 1472 Sweynheym and Pannartz sent a petition to Pope Sixtus IV asking for help. They explained that they had printed many books, but that thousands of copies were sitting unsold in their house and they were running out of money. This shows something important. Printing made books faster and cheaper to produce, but printers still had to find buyers. Printing too many copies of the same scholarly book for a small market could ruin you. Over the next decades, successful printers learned to choose titles more carefully, to work with booksellers who could ship books to faraway fairs and cities, and to print cheaper books that many more people would want."]},{"heading":"Venice: Europe's Printing Capital","paragraphs":["If you had wanted to see the busiest printing city in the world around 1490, you would have headed for Venice. This city of canals in northern Italy was one of the richest trading powers in Europe. Its merchant ships sailed all over the Mediterranean, its bankers had money to invest, paper mills operated in the region around it, and its famous university town, Padua, was close by. Venice was perfect for printing.","The first printer in Venice was another German, Johann of Speyer, sometimes called Johannes de Spira. He had been in Mainz around 1460 to 1461. In 1469 he printed an edition of Cicero's letters to his friends, the first printed book in Venice. That same year the Venetian government gave him a special privilege: for five years, nobody else was allowed to print books in Venice. This was one of the earliest printing privileges in Europe, a kind of early ancestor of patents and copyright. But Johann died in 1470, the privilege ended with him, and the city was suddenly open to anyone who wanted to try. Printers poured in.","One of the most famous was Nicolas Jenson, a Frenchman who had been master of the royal mint at Tours, where coins were made. According to a well-known account, King Charles VII of France sent him to Mainz in 1458 to learn the secrets of the new art. Jenson later settled in Venice and opened his shop in 1470. That year he printed a book by the early church historian Eusebius using a new roman typeface that printers and designers still admire today. Jenson's letters were clear, evenly spaced and beautifully balanced, with round shapes based on the handwriting of Italian humanist scholars. They made a clean break from the heavy blackletter style. More than 400 years later, the English designer William Morris praised Jenson's type and based his own Golden Type on it, and many modern typefaces have followed its example. By the late 1470s Jenson could run as many as twelve presses at once and had produced around 150 books.","Venice's printing industry grew enormous. Between 1469 and 1500, roughly 150 to 200 named printers worked in the city, and they produced around 3,500 to 3,800 known editions, more than 10 percent of all books printed in Europe in the 1400s and more than any other city. Venetian printers were especially famous for books in Greek and Hebrew, beautiful illustrated books, music printing and maps. In 1501 Ottaviano Petrucci printed there the first book of part music for several voices made with movable type. Erhard Ratdolt, a printer from Augsburg who worked in Venice, published a famous edition of the ancient Greek mathematician Euclid in 1482, one of the first printed books to include many geometry diagrams in its margins."]},{"heading":"Aldus, Griffo and the Pocket Book","paragraphs":["Aldus Manutius was born around 1450 in Bassiano, a small town south of Rome. He was not trained as a printer. He was a teacher and a scholar who loved ancient Greek, and he was worried that the great works of Greek literature, science and philosophy were scattered across rare handwritten copies that few people could ever see. His solution was to become a publisher. Around 1494, working with a businessman named Andrea Torresano, who had earlier bought Jenson's printing business, he set up a print shop in Venice. It became known as the Aldine Press.","The Aldine Press's first book appeared in 1495: a Greek grammar by Constantine Lascaris. Over the next years Aldus published a huge five-volume edition of Aristotle in Greek (1495 to 1498) and dozens of other ancient Greek texts, many printed for the very first time. Greek was very hard to print. It has its own alphabet, and its handwriting used many accents, breathing marks and joined letters, so a Greek font needed hundreds of different sorts. Aldus's Greek books helped scholars all over Europe study texts that had been almost unavailable in the West.","In 1499 the Aldine Press printed one of the most beautiful books of the whole century, a strange, dreamlike love story called the Hypnerotomachia Poliphili, with elegant woodcut pictures arranged carefully alongside the text. Book designers still study it today. But Aldus's most famous idea came two years later.","In 1501 he published the poems of the Roman poet Virgil in a new size: octavo. As you saw in the last part, an octavo is made by folding each big sheet three times, giving 8 leaves and 16 pages, so the book comes out small, roughly the size of a modern paperback. Before this, serious books of classical literature were usually large folios or quartos, often printed with long commentaries crowding around the text, and meant to sit on a desk or a lectern. Aldus's little octavos had plain text, no heavy commentary, and could be carried in a coat pocket or a saddlebag. A reader could take Virgil on a journey, read him in a garden, or study him by candlelight in a small room. Many historians see these books as distant ancestors of the modern paperback.","Aldus also printed these books in big runs. His 1501 Virgil was printed in about 1,000 copies, when 200 to 500 was more usual. Latin octavos from the Aldine Press cost about 30 soldi, a Venetian coin, which was much less than a big folio. Within a few years, the Aldine Press had published more than 100 editions. From 1502 its books carried a printer's mark showing a dolphin wrapped around an anchor, with the Latin motto festina lente, meaning 'make haste slowly'. The design became so famous that other printers copied it, and pirate editions of Aldine books, printed in Lyon in France and elsewhere, appeared within months.","Aldus's octavos needed one more ingredient: a new, space-saving kind of letter.","To fit lots of text onto a small page, Aldus needed a letter that was narrow and elegant. He turned to a brilliant punchcutter named Francesco Griffo, from Bologna. A punchcutter, as you saw in earlier parts, was the craftsman who carved each letter in reverse on the tip of a steel punch, the very first step in making type. Griffo had already cut Aldus's Greek and roman typefaces, including the roman letters used in a 1496 book by the scholar Pietro Bembo, which inspired a modern typeface still called Bembo.","For the new pocket books, Griffo cut a slanted, flowing typeface that copied the quick, joined-up handwriting used by Italian scholars of the time. It first appeared in a few words in a book of letters by Saint Catherine of Siena in 1500, and then for a whole book in the octavo Virgil of 1501. This was italic type, named after Italy. Each letter was narrow, and the font included many joined letter pairs, so more words fitted on each line, and the page looked like fine handwriting.","There are two surprises about the first italic. First, it was not used the way we use italics today, to stress a word or mark a book title. Aldus printed entire books in it. Only later did printers begin mixing italic and roman type on the same page, using italic for emphasis. Second, the first italic had no slanted capital letters. Its capitals were small upright roman capitals, and slanted italic capitals came only later.","Aldus was so proud of the new type that he asked the government of Venice for protection. The Venetian Senate gave him an exclusive privilege to use it, and popes confirmed his rights too. But type could be copied, and imitations appeared within a year or two. Aldus and Griffo also quarreled, apparently over credit and money, and Griffo left Venice. He worked for other printers and later printed small books of his own in Bologna, but much of his later life is unclear, and the last records of him date from around 1518."]},{"heading":"Paris, Lyon and Beyond","paragraphs":["While Italy raced ahead, printing was spreading in every direction. In Paris, two scholars at the Sorbonne, the city's famous college of theology, wanted good printed books for students and teachers. In 1470 they invited three German printers, Ulrich Gering, Martin Crantz and Michael Friburger, to set up a press inside the college buildings. Their first book was a collection of model letters to teach students how to write elegant Latin. Paris soon became one of Europe's great printing centers, along with Lyon, an important trading city on the Rhône River where printing began in 1473. Lyon, with its big trade fairs, became famous for books sold to merchants and ordinary readers, often in French.","Printers also reached the Low Countries and the Iberian Peninsula. Utrecht and other Dutch towns had presses by 1473. In Spain, the German printer Lambert Palmart was working in Valencia by about 1473, and in 1490 Nicolaus Spindeler printed the famous Valencian adventure story Tirant lo Blanch there. Further east, Andreas Hess printed in Buda, in Hungary, in the early 1470s, and Kasper Straube printed in Kraków, in Poland, around 1473 to 1477.","Jewish printers made important contributions too, printing Hebrew Bibles, prayer books and works of law and philosophy. Hebrew printing flourished in Italy and on the Iberian Peninsula in the 1470s and 1480s. When Jews were forced to leave Spain in 1492 and Portugal in 1497, some printers carried their skills to new homes. In Istanbul, capital of the Ottoman Empire, the brothers David and Samuel ibn Nahmias printed a Hebrew book of Jewish law, and many historians date it to 1493, though some argue for a few years later. It was the first book printed in the Ottoman Empire.","Most of these first printers were Germans. All over Europe, early print shops were run by men with names like Ulrich, Konrad, Johann and Michael, often working far from home. Because type was heavy but a press could be built by any good carpenter, a printer could set up almost anywhere he found customers and paper. If the business failed, or the local ruler became unfriendly, he could pack up his punches, matrices and some type and move on to the next town."]},{"heading":"William Caxton Brings Printing to England","paragraphs":["The man who brought printing to England did not start out as a printer at all. William Caxton was born in Kent, in south-east England, around 1422. As a young man, in 1438, he was apprenticed to a rich London mercer, a merchant who traded in fine cloth. By around 1453 he was living in Bruges, in today's Belgium, one of the busiest trading cities in northern Europe. He did well there and became governor of the community of English merchants in the Low Countries, an important and respected position. He also came to know Margaret of York, sister of the English King Edward IV, who married the powerful Duke of Burgundy in 1468.","Caxton loved books and liked translating French romances into English. He found that people kept asking for copies of his translation of a long French story about the Trojan War. Copying it by hand was exhausting; he wrote that his pen was worn, his hand weary and his eyes dimmed from so much writing. During a stay in Cologne in Germany in 1471 and 1472, where printing was already well established, he learned how the new art worked. Back in Bruges, with the help of a scribe and printer named Colard Mansion, he set up a press.","Around 1473 or 1474, Caxton printed his own translation, The Recuyell of the Historyes of Troye, meaning 'collection of the stories of Troy'. It is the first book ever printed in the English language, and it was printed not in England but in Bruges. A copy sold at auction in London in 2014 for over one million pounds.","In 1476 Caxton moved back to England and set up a press in Westminster, near Westminster Abbey and the royal palace, close to the people who could afford books: courtiers, lawyers, nobles and churchmen. It was England's first printing press. One of his first big books there was Geoffrey Chaucer's Canterbury Tales, the great collection of stories told by pilgrims on their way to Canterbury, written about 80 years earlier. In 1477 he printed The Dictes or Sayengis of the Philosophres, the earliest book printed in England with an exact printed date. Later came an English Aesop's Fables (1484) and Thomas Malory's Le Morte d'Arthur (1485), the tales of King Arthur and his knights. Altogether Caxton printed more than 100 different works, most of them in English.","Caxton also had a problem: English was not yet one language with fixed spellings. People in different parts of England spoke and wrote very differently. In the introduction to one of his later books, Caxton told a story about some merchants travelling down the River Thames who stopped at a farmhouse and asked for 'egges'. The woman of the house said she did not understand French. Another traveler asked for 'eyren', and she understood at once, because both words meant eggs in different English dialects. Caxton asked his readers which word a printer should use. By printing mostly in the English of London and the court, Caxton and the printers after him helped slowly push English toward a more standard form. When Caxton died, around 1491 or 1492, his assistant Wynkyn de Worde took over the business and later moved it to Fleet Street in London, which became famous for printing and newspapers for centuries."]},{"heading":"Cradle Books: The Incunabula","paragraphs":["Book experts have a special name for the very first printed books in Europe. Books printed before the year 1501 are called incunabula, or incunables. The word comes from Latin and originally meant swaddling clothes, the cloth wrapped around a newborn baby, or a cradle. A Dutch scholar named Hadrianus Junius used it in a book written in 1569 to describe printing's earliest days. So incunabula are 'cradle books', from the time when printing was still a baby.","The cut-off of 1501 is just a convenient round number. Libraries around the world have worked together to list every known incunabulum. The Incunabula Short Title Catalogue, kept by the British Library and its partners, records around 30,000 different editions from this period, and new discoveries still turn up. Historians estimate that all these editions together produced somewhere between about 8 and 20 million copies, depending on how many copies they think were printed of each. Typical print runs were often a few hundred copies, though some popular books were printed in a thousand or more.","Around 550,000 individual copies of incunabula are thought to survive in libraries and private collections today, a remarkable number for objects more than 500 years old. Some editions survive in hundreds of copies, but others are known only from a single battered copy or a few loose pages found inside the binding of a later book. Probably many editions have disappeared entirely, especially cheap and popular things like schoolbooks, calendars and pamphlets that were read until they fell apart.","Incunabula are full of clues to the people who made and used them. Many look a lot like manuscripts, because early printers copied the look of handwritten books their customers were used to. Big capital letters at the start of chapters were often left blank for an artist to paint in by hand, and red headings were added later with a pen. There was usually no title page at first. Instead, details about the printer, the place and the date were printed at the end, in a short note called a colophon. Title pages, page numbers and printers' marks slowly became normal by around 1500. Readers' notes in the margins, owners' names and old prices written inside the covers help historians learn who bought these books and how they read them."]},{"heading":"Cheaper Books for More Readers","paragraphs":["Did printing really make books cheaper? Yes, though not all at once. The first printed books of the 1460s often cost as much as fine manuscripts. In Venice, a study of book prices found that they began falling in the 1470s and then dropped sharply in the 1480s, a fall that continued into the 1490s. As more printers competed and print runs grew, prices came down.","We have an amazing window into this world thanks to an account book kept by a Venetian bookseller named Francesco de Madiis between 1484 and 1488. It is known as the Zornale, Venetian for 'daybook', and it records about 7,000 sales covering around 25,000 books. It shows that well over a thousand sales were for less than the daily wage of an ordinary builder, about 15 soldi. The cheapest best-seller was a 16-page reading book for children, a little psalter used to learn to read, which sold 659 copies at just 4 soldi each. A typical copy of a classical author might cost 20 to 40 soldi, roughly a master builder's pay for a day or two. At the top end, a giant 1,628-page Bible with commentary cost 1,240 soldi, the same as a handwritten prayer book. So big, fancy books were still expensive, but small books were now within reach of many working people.","Printers also discovered that they could sell many new kinds of books. Early presses produced Latin grammars for schoolchildren, especially the short grammar by Donatus, which was printed again and again. They printed calendars and almanacs telling people about holy days and the phases of the Moon. They printed law books for lawyers, medical books for doctors, prayer books for families, and dictionaries for students. They printed travel books, like Bernhard von Breydenbach's illustrated account of a pilgrimage to Jerusalem (1486), and grand illustrated histories, like the Nuremberg Chronicle of 1493, with more than 1,800 woodcut pictures printed from about 650 woodblocks. They printed stories, poems and romances in everyday languages, from Italian and French to German, Spanish, Dutch and English, not just in Latin.","Governments and churches used printing too. They printed laws, announcements, forms and the indulgence certificates you met in earlier parts. Printing could spread news of a battle, a new decree or a discovery across hundreds of miles in a few weeks. In 1493, for example, a letter by Christopher Columbus describing his first voyage was printed in Spain and then quickly reprinted in Latin and other languages in cities such as Rome, Basel, Paris and Antwerp.","By 1500, then, Europe had changed in a quiet but enormous way. Instead of a few hundred books in a great library, a single city could have thousands of copies of hundreds of titles for sale. Scholars far apart could read the same edition, with the same words on the same page. More people could own a book, and more people had a reason to learn to read. Cheap, fast printing meant new ideas could now spread faster than anyone could stop them, and an angry monk was about to prove it."]}],"deepDive":"How do historians know how many books were printed before 1501? Nobody kept a master list at the time, so the numbers are built up from the books that survive. Two great catalogues do most of the work: the Incunabula Short Title Catalogue (ISTC), now hosted by the Consortium of European Research Libraries, and the German Gesamtkatalog der Wiegendrucke ('complete catalogue of cradle prints'). Together they list around 28,000 to 30,000 editions, depending on how items are counted and on whether a few undated books really belong before 1501. Venice alone accounts for well over 3,500 of them, more than any other city.\n\nTurning editions into copies is harder. If an average print run was about 300 copies, 30,000 editions would mean roughly 9 million books; if it was closer to 500, the total rises to about 15 million. Hence the wide range, often quoted as about 8 to 20 million copies. Around 550,000 copies survive, but survival is very uneven. Big, expensive Latin folios were treasured by libraries, while cheap grammars, almanacs, broadsides and pamphlets were read to pieces. Some editions are known only from scraps recycled inside later bindings, and many have surely vanished without trace, so the real number of editions was certainly higher than the catalogues show.\n\nThe count of printing towns varies too. Different studies give figures from about 236 to 282 places by 1500, depending on whether they count a town where one wandering printer made one book, or only towns with a lasting press. What everyone agrees on is the shape of the story: a handful of German towns around 1460, more than a hundred places by the early 1480s, and well over 250 by 1500, with Italy and Germany far ahead of the rest.\n\nEconomic historians are also interested in prices and law. The Zornale of Francesco de Madiis shows books ranging from a few soldi to over a thousand, letting researchers compare book prices with wages for the first time. The Venetian privileges, such as Johann of Speyer's five-year monopoly in 1469 and Aldus's protection for his italic type, are studied as early experiments in what we now call intellectual property. They show governments trying, not always successfully, to reward inventors while still letting a new industry grow.","funFact":"The first book ever printed in English, Caxton's Recuyell of the Historyes of Troye, was printed in Bruges in today's Belgium, not in England.","check":{"q":"What special kind of letter did Francesco Griffo cut for Aldus Manutius's small books around 1500 to 1501?","a":"The first italic type","wrong":["The first capital letters","The first blackletter (Gothic) type","The first Greek alphabet"]},"sources":[{"title":"From Mainz into the World","publisher":"Gutenberg Museum Mainz / gutenberg.de","url":"https://www.gutenberg.de/en/erfindung/von_mainz_in_die_welt"},{"title":"Printing R-Evolution and Society 1450-1500: a conference reflection","publisher":"Cambridge University Library Special Collections","url":"https://specialcollections-blog.lib.cam.ac.uk/?p=16710"},{"title":"Incunable","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incunable"},{"title":"Global spread of the printing press","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_spread_of_the_printing_press"},{"title":"Aldus Manutius","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldus_Manutius"},{"title":"Italic type","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italic_type"},{"title":"Nicolas Jenson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolas_Jenson"},{"title":"William Caxton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Caxton"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/12-1.jpg","alt":"A page of Aldus Manutius's pocket-sized Virgil (Venice, 1501), the first book printed in italic type, with hand-painted decorations.","file":"Page from the edition of Virgil printed by Aldus Manutius in 1501.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Page_from_the_edition_of_Virgil_printed_by_Aldus_Manutius_in_1501.jpg","author":"Virgil","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/12-2.jpg","alt":"An old engraving from Caxton's Troy book, thought to show William Caxton kneeling to give a book to Margaret of York.","file":"Caxton-Margaret-of-York-Histoires de Troie.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caxton-Margaret-of-York-Histoires_de_Troie.jpg","author":"Raoul Lefèvre / William Caxton","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/12-3.jpg","alt":"The Aldine printer's mark, a dolphin wrapped around an anchor, in Aldus's 1509 edition of Plutarch.","file":"Plutarch's Moralia, Aldine edition (March 1509), title page (cropped).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plutarch%27s_Moralia,_Aldine_edition_(March_1509),_title_page_(cropped).jpg","author":"Aldus Manutius","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4368},{"n":13,"title":"Ideas Unleashed: Reformation and Science","when":"1517 – 1600s","summary":"In 1517 a monk named Martin Luther wrote ninety-five arguments, and printers copied them so fast that all of Germany was soon talking about them. Bibles in everyday languages, cartoons and science books with amazing pictures spread the same way. Rulers tried to ban books, but once hundreds of copies existed, ideas were very hard to stop.","sections":[{"heading":"A Monk, Ninety-five Arguments and a Printing Frenzy","paragraphs":["Picture the small university town of Wittenberg, in Saxony in eastern Germany, in the autumn of 1517. It is not a great city like Venice or Paris. It has muddy streets, a castle, a church, a young university and a few thousand people. One of its teachers is a monk and professor of Bible studies named Martin Luther, a stocky, stubborn, brilliant man in his early thirties who has been growing more and more upset about something called indulgences.","An indulgence was a church certificate that, people were told, reduced the punishment for sins after death. In 1517 a preacher named Johann Tetzel was selling indulgences near Wittenberg to raise money, partly for rebuilding St Peter's Basilica in Rome. Luther thought this was wrong: forgiveness, he believed, could not be bought. So on 31 October 1517 he wrote a list of ninety-five short arguments in Latin, called theses, inviting other scholars to debate the question. We know he sent them in a letter to Archbishop Albrecht of Mainz on that date.","You may have seen paintings of Luther angrily hammering the paper onto the wooden door of Wittenberg's Castle Church. That famous scene might have happened, because church doors were used like notice boards for university debates. But historians genuinely argue about it. The first written account of the hammering came from Luther's friend Philipp Melanchthon, who told it only after Luther died in 1546, and Melanchthon was not in Wittenberg in 1517. So the door story is possible, but not certain.","What IS certain is far more important for our story: the theses were printed, and they spread astonishingly fast. Before the end of 1517, printers in at least three cities, Leipzig, Nuremberg and Basel, had turned out their own editions. One was a large poster-style sheet that could be pinned up; another was a little four-page pamphlet you could slip into a bag. Soon they were translated into German so that people who did not read Latin could follow the argument too. Luther's friend Friedrich Myconius later wrote that within about four weeks almost all of Christendom seemed to know about them. That is an exaggeration, but it shows how it felt to people living through it.","Luther himself was surprised. In March 1518 he wrote that his theses had been printed and passed around far beyond what he had expected. He had meant to start a debate among professors. Instead, thanks to the printing press, he had started an argument with the whole of Europe.","Think about why this was new. A century earlier, other critics of the church, like Jan Hus in Bohemia, had also attracted followers. But their ideas travelled mostly by sermons and hand-copied writings, and they could be stamped out locally. In 1517 there were presses in more than 200 towns across Europe, run by businesspeople who needed to sell things. A hot argument that everyone was talking about was exactly what they wanted to print."]},{"heading":"Pamphlets, Best-sellers and Picture Wars","paragraphs":["Luther quickly discovered he was a natural writer for the new medium. Instead of long, heavy Latin books for scholars, he wrote short pamphlets in lively German, often just 8 or 16 pages. Germans called these little booklets Flugschriften, which means something like flying writings, because they flew from town to town. They were cheap to print, cheap to buy and quick to reprint. If a pamphlet sold out in Augsburg, a printer in Strasbourg could simply buy one copy, set it in type again and sell his own edition the following week.","His 1518 Sermon on Indulgence and Grace shows how this worked. It was reprinted about twenty times, by printers in Wittenberg, Leipzig, Nuremberg, Augsburg, Basel and other cities. The historian Andrew Pettegree, who has studied the numbers carefully, counts about 160 different works by Luther printed in about 828 editions by the end of 1522. Within five years of the theses, Luther had become the most published author Europe had ever seen. Historians estimate that his writings made up a very large share of everything printed in Germany in the early 1520s.","His side also simply outprinted their opponents. Between 1521 and 1525, Luther and his supporters published roughly nine times as much as the writers defending the old church. Part of the reason was business: printers printed what sold, whatever their own beliefs. Catholic replies were often long, written in Latin and harder to sell.","Germany's political map helped as well. The Holy Roman Empire was a patchwork of hundreds of separate territories and free cities, each with its own ruler. A book banned in one city could be printed just over the border in another. In 1521 Emperor Charles V outlawed Luther and banned his writings with the Edict of Worms, but in practice the ban was very hard to enforce, and Luther was hidden and protected by his own prince, Frederick the Wise of Saxony.","Many Germans could not read at all. Historians think only a small minority could read well, perhaps a few percent in the countryside and more in the towns. Yet pamphlets still reached them, because people read aloud. One literate person in a tavern, a market square or a family kitchen could share a pamphlet with dozens of listeners. Ideas also spread through songs printed on cheap sheets, which people sang to tunes they already knew.","Pictures were the other secret weapon. Woodcuts, the carved-wood pictures you met earlier in this Deep Dive, could be printed alongside text. The painter Lucas Cranach the Elder, who ran a busy workshop in Wittenberg, designed portraits of Luther and decorated the title pages of his works so that a Luther pamphlet had a recognizable look, a bit like a brand. In 1521 Cranach's workshop made the Passional Christi und Antichristi, a little book of paired pictures: on one page Jesus washes his followers' feet, and on the opposite page the pope has his own feet kissed. You did not need to read a word to understand the message.","The other side fought back with pictures too. In 1529 a woodcut published with a book by the Catholic writer Johannes Cochlaeus showed Luther as a monster with seven heads. These printed picture battles were early cousins of political cartoons. Both sides could be rude, unfair and exaggerated, which reminds us that print spreads insults and half-truths as easily as careful thinking."]},{"heading":"Bibles for Ordinary Readers","paragraphs":["In 1521, after refusing to take back his writings, Luther was secretly taken for his safety to the Wartburg, a castle on a forested hill in Thuringia. There he grew a beard, called himself Junker Jörg (Knight George) and set himself an enormous task: translating the New Testament from Greek into everyday German. He worked astonishingly fast, finishing a first version in about eleven weeks.","Luther wanted German that sounded like real people talking. He said he listened to mothers at home, children in the streets and ordinary people in the market to hear how they spoke. His translation was printed in Wittenberg in September 1522, so it is nicknamed the September Testament. It was printed in Melchior Lotter's shop with woodcut illustrations from Cranach's workshop, including dramatic pictures for the Book of Revelation. A first run of a few thousand copies, usually given as about 3,000, sold out within weeks, and a corrected edition appeared in December. Even so, it was not cheap: a full Bible was worth weeks of wages for a working person.","In 1534 the complete Luther Bible, Old Testament included, came off the press of Hans Lufft in Wittenberg, with 117 woodcuts. Over the following decades hundreds of thousands of copies of Luther's Bibles and New Testaments were printed. Because Germany had many dialects, his widely read translation also helped shape a common written German. It was not the only cause, but many language historians see it as a big step toward modern standard German.","In England, a scholar named William Tyndale had the same dream for English. At that time, translating the Bible into English without church permission was forbidden in England. Tyndale is said to have told a churchman that he would make a boy who drives the plough know more of the Scriptures than he did. Unable to get support at home, he went to Germany in 1524.","His first attempt to print, in Cologne in 1525, was discovered by an opponent and raided, and Tyndale fled up the river Rhine with whatever printed sheets he could save. In 1526 he succeeded in the city of Worms. And here is a lovely link to an earlier part of our story: the printer was Peter Schoeffer the Younger, son of the very Peter Schoeffer who had worked with Gutenberg and Fust in Mainz. The little pocket-sized New Testament, an octavo, was printed in perhaps 3,000 copies and smuggled into England hidden in bales of cloth and barrels of goods.","The Bishop of London, Cuthbert Tunstall, bought up copies and had them publicly burned. That simply gave Tyndale money to print more. Today only three copies of the 1526 Worms New Testament are known: one at the British Library, an incomplete one at St Paul's Cathedral in London, and one identified in 1996 in the State Library of Württemberg in Stuttgart, which is the most complete of the three.","Tyndale's story ended sadly. He was betrayed in Antwerp in 1535, imprisoned at Vilvoorde castle near Brussels, and executed for heresy in 1536. Yet his words lived on. Much of the wording of the famous King James Bible of 1611 comes directly from Tyndale's translation, including phrases English speakers still use, such as let there be light and the powers that be. A man who was hunted and killed for printing a book ended up shaping the English language for centuries."]},{"heading":"Trying to Put the Genie Back in the Bottle","paragraphs":["If you were a ruler or a bishop in the 1500s, the printing press could look frightening. For centuries the people in charge had been able to keep an eye on what was written, because books were few and copied slowly. Now a printer in a distant town could turn out a thousand copies of an angry pamphlet in a few days. Authorities on every side tried to get control back, and the main tools they used were censorship, licensing and lists of forbidden books.","Censorship means checking writing before or after it is published and stopping what the authorities do not like. Even before Luther, some church leaders had demanded that books be approved before printing. After 1517 the efforts grew much stronger. Lists of banned books appeared in the Catholic Netherlands in 1529, in Venice in 1543 and in Paris in 1551, where the theologians of the Sorbonne drew up their own catalogue.","In 1559 Pope Paul IV issued the first widely enforced Roman list, called the Index Librorum Prohibitorum, Latin for Index of Prohibited Books. It was extremely strict. It banned the entire works of about 550 authors, not just individual books, and it even included some Bibles printed in everyday languages. In 1564, after the great church meeting known as the Council of Trent, a revised and somewhat milder version, the Tridentine Index, set out rules that lasted for centuries. In 1571 a special group of church officials, the Congregation of the Index, was set up to examine new books and keep the list up to date.","Being on the Index meant that Catholics were forbidden to print, sell or read a book without special permission. Over the years it named works by many famous thinkers, including Galileo, Descartes, Voltaire and Kant. The list kept growing, and its last edition, in 1948, contained about 4,000 titles. It was finally abolished in 1966, when the church announced that it no longer had the force of church law. That means the Index lasted more than four centuries.","It would be unfair to suggest that only the Catholic Church censored. Protestant cities burned Catholic books and also banned the writings of other Protestant groups they disagreed with. Kings and city councils everywhere licensed printers, which meant you needed official permission to run a press at all. In England, the Stationers' Company, a guild of printers and booksellers in London, received a royal charter in 1557 that let it control most printing in the kingdom, and books had to be registered before they appeared.","Printers who broke the rules could lose everything. Their type and presses might be confiscated, their stock burned, and some were imprisoned or even executed. Many printers responded with tricks: false city names on title pages, no printer's name at all, or presses moved across the nearest border. Books printed in Geneva, Antwerp, Basel or later Amsterdam slipped into countries where they were forbidden. Censorship could slow ideas down and frighten people, but it never managed to stop print completely. A recurring lesson of this period is that once a text exists in hundreds of copies, it is very hard to make it disappear."]},{"heading":"Copies You Can Trust: Science in Print","paragraphs":["Now let us leave the religious arguments and step into the workshops of scientists, because printing changed science in a quieter but equally powerful way. To see why, imagine you are a scholar in 1400 studying an old book about the stars or the human body. Your copy was made by hand, and so was the copy it came from, and so on back for centuries. Every scribe made a few mistakes, especially with numbers and diagrams. Two scholars in different cities might be reading two quite different versions of the same book without knowing it.","Printing changed that. When a printer made 500 copies of a book, all 500 had the same words, the same numbers and the same pictures on the same pages. A professor in Padua could write to a professor in Kraków about the diagram on page 37, and both would be looking at the same diagram. If someone spotted an error, it could be listed and corrected in the next edition, and everyone could check. This sounds simple, but it made it far easier for scholars to build on each other's work and to argue about evidence.","Two famous books from the same year, 1543, show this new power. The first was by the Polish astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus. For many years he had worked out a startling idea: that the Earth and the other planets travel around the Sun, rather than everything circling a still Earth. He hesitated to publish, but a young mathematician named Georg Joachim Rheticus came to visit him, became his student, and encouraged him. The finished book, On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, was printed in Nuremberg by Johannes Petreius, one of the city's leading printers.","Copernicus died in May 1543, the same year his book was published. A traditional story says the first printed copy was placed in his hands on the very day he died. Nobody can prove that, so treat it as a touching legend. The book was not an instant best-seller. Its first print run of about 400 copies did not even sell out. But printed copies travelled, and astronomers filled the margins with notes. The historian Owen Gingerich spent decades tracking down surviving copies and found 276 copies of the first edition and 325 of the second, many covered with readers' handwriting, showing how carefully they were studied.","The second great book of 1543 was On the Fabric of the Human Body, by Andreas Vesalius, a 28-year-old professor of anatomy at the University of Padua. Unlike many teachers before him, Vesalius cut up human bodies himself to see what was really there instead of simply repeating the ancient Greek doctor Galen. He found that Galen, who had mostly dissected animals, was wrong about many details of human anatomy.","The Fabrica, as it is usually called, was a huge book printed in Basel, Switzerland, by Johannes Oporinus. Its real wonder was its pictures: more than 250 detailed woodcuts, drawn by skilled artists probably connected to the workshop of the famous Venetian painter Titian. The carved woodblocks were carried across the Alps from Italy to Basel. Its skeletons and muscle men stand in landscapes, as if posing for a portrait. Because every copy had exactly the same pictures, students across Europe could learn anatomy from the same accurate images, and other doctors could check them against real bodies. Vesalius also published a shorter, cheaper version, the Epitome, for students.","Some astronomers even became their own printers. The Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe set up a printing press and a paper mill on his island observatory of Hven in the late 1500s so he could publish his observations exactly as he wanted them. That shows how much scientists had come to rely on print."]},{"heading":"Maps, Tables and the Mathematics of the Sea","paragraphs":["Science is not only words. It is full of numbers, tables, charts and maps, and these are precisely the things that hand-copying ruins most easily. Change one word in a poem and you might still understand it. Change one number in a table of star positions or tides and a ship might end up on the rocks.","In the age of ocean voyages, printed maps became hugely important. Many of the finest were printed from engraved copper plates, where the lines were cut into metal with a sharp tool, allowing much finer detail than woodcuts. In 1569 the Flemish mapmaker Gerard Mercator printed a giant world map using a new way of drawing the round Earth on a flat sheet. On his map, a course with a constant compass direction appears as a straight line, which made it extremely useful for sailors. The price was that lands far from the equator, like Greenland, look much bigger than they really are. Versions of the Mercator projection are still used on many online maps today.","A year later, on 20 May 1570, the Antwerp mapmaker Abraham Ortelius published the Theatrum Orbis Terrarum, the Theatre of the World, often called the first modern atlas. It gathered 53 maps of the same style and size into one book, so readers could compare regions easily. Ortelius listed the mapmakers he had copied from, which helped readers judge how reliable each map was, and he updated later editions as new information arrived. A printed atlas could be improved edition after edition, like a scientific report.","Printed mathematical tables were just as important. Navigators, astronomers, merchants and surveyors used tables of positions, distances and calculations. In 1614 the Scottish mathematician John Napier published his description of logarithms, a clever trick that turns long multiplications into simpler additions, along with tables of numbers. Printed tables of logarithms spread across Europe and saved people countless hours of calculating for more than 300 years, until electronic calculators took over.","Print could carry discoveries quickly too. In March 1610 the Italian scientist Galileo Galilei published a short book called the Starry Messenger, describing what he had seen through his telescope: mountains on the Moon, many more stars than anyone had counted and four moons circling Jupiter. Its print run of about 550 copies sold out quickly, and within months scholars across Europe were arguing about it and trying to build telescopes of their own.","But fixed copies had a funny downside: mistakes were copied perfectly too. In 1631 the royal printers in London produced a Bible in which the word not was missing from one of the Ten Commandments, so it read Thou shalt commit adultery. It became known as the Wicked Bible. The printers were heavily fined and most copies were destroyed. One tiny slip in the type became hundreds of identical errors, which is why careful proofreading became so important."]},{"heading":"Did Printing Change Everything?","paragraphs":["With so much happening at once, it is tempting to say the printing press caused the Reformation and the Scientific Revolution. Historians have argued about this for a long time, and the debate is a great example of how history really works.","In 1979 an American historian named Elizabeth Eisenstein published a big two-volume book called The Printing Press as an Agent of Change. She argued that historians had badly underrated print. Printing, she said, did three big things. It spread texts far more widely, which she called dissemination. It made copies identical, so that maps, numbers, spellings and pictures became standardized. And it preserved texts, because once hundreds of copies existed in many places, a book was very unlikely to be lost forever, as many ancient works had been. These changes, she argued, helped make the Reformation, the spread of Renaissance learning and the rise of modern science possible.","Notice that her title calls print an agent of change, not the only cause. Still, other historians pushed back. Some pointed out that early printed books were often full of mistakes, that pirate printers copied each other carelessly, and that different editions of the same book could differ. The historian Adrian Johns argued that trust in printed books had to be built up slowly by people, rules and reputations; it did not come automatically with the machine. Others noted that East Asia had printing for centuries before Europe, yet its history unfolded differently, so the press alone cannot explain what happened in Europe.","Most historians today take a middle position. The Reformation also needed Luther's ideas, the support of powerful princes, anger about money and power in the church, and changes in society. The Scientific Revolution needed observatories, universities, instruments like the telescope, and bold thinkers. Printing did not make these things happen by itself. But it acted like a powerful amplifier, a loudspeaker for ideas. It let arguments reach more people, faster, in more identical copies, than ever before, and it made it much harder for anyone to silence them completely.","By 1600 Europe was a very different place from the Europe of Gutenberg's youth. Religion had split into several churches. Scientists were comparing printed observations across countries. And millions of people who would never meet each other were reading the same words. The next question was what printing would do to everyday life itself: to news, language, the law and the farthest corners of the world."]}],"deepDive":"How much did print really matter to the Reformation? Historians have tried to answer with numbers. Surveys of surviving German imprints show a dramatic jump in output after 1517, peaking in the early 1520s and then dropping steeply after 1525. Most of the growth came from short vernacular pamphlets rather than big books. Andrew Pettegree counts about 828 editions of Luther's works by the end of 1522, and estimates that in 1521–1525 evangelical writers outpublished their Catholic opponents roughly nine to one. Yet literacy was low, perhaps a few percent in rural areas and higher in towns, so print worked together with sermons, songs, reading aloud and pictures. This is why many scholars describe a 'multimedia' Reformation rather than a purely printed one. The science side raises a different question. Elizabeth Eisenstein argued in 1979 that print's 'fixity' (identical copies), together with wider dissemination and safer preservation, allowed knowledge to accumulate instead of decaying with each hand copy. Her critics, notably Adrian Johns, replied that early print was anything but fixed: pirated editions, careless compositors and rival versions were everywhere, and trust in printed books had to be built socially, through printers' reputations, licensing, learned societies and errata lists. Owen Gingerich's census of Copernicus's book offers a neat test case: about 600 surviving copies of the first two editions, many heavily annotated, show readers copying corrections and notes from one another's copies, so that knowledge travelled partly through print and partly through handwriting in the margins. Comparisons with East Asia, where printing was far older but social and political conditions differed, also warn against treating the press as a single cause. The fairest summary is that print was a powerful amplifier and preserver, whose effects depended on who controlled it, who could read, and what people wanted to argue about.","funFact":"Martin Luther, whose ideas spread thanks to printers, called the printing press \"God's highest and most extreme act of grace.\"","check":{"q":"In which year were both Copernicus's book on the Sun-centered universe and Vesalius's illustrated anatomy book, the Fabrica, published?","a":"1543","wrong":["1517","1559","1610"]},"sources":[{"title":"Medium and message: Luther's Reformation","publisher":"Acton Institute","url":"https://acton.org/medium-and-message-luthers-reformation"},{"title":"The Media Revolution and Reformation","publisher":"University of Oregon","url":"https://pages.uoregon.edu/dluebke/WesternCiv102/LutherinPrint.html"},{"title":"Tyndale (Schoeffer prints in Worms)","publisher":"City of Worms","url":"https://www.worms.de/en/web/luther/Lutherschriften/Schoefferdrucke/Tyndale.php"},{"title":"Luther Bible","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luther_Bible"},{"title":"Index Librorum Prohibitorum","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Index_Librorum_Prohibitorum"},{"title":"De revolutionibus orbium coelestium","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_revolutionibus_orbium_coelestium"},{"title":"De humani corporis fabrica","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_humani_corporis_fabrica"},{"title":"Ninety-five Theses","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninety-five_Theses"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/13-1.jpg","alt":"First page of a 1517 printed pamphlet of Martin Luther's Ninety-five Theses, made in Basel.","file":"Ninety-five Theses (Basel).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ninety-five_Theses_(Basel).jpg","author":"Martin Luther","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/13-2.jpg","alt":"The famous diagram from Copernicus's 1543 book, with the Sun (Sol) in the middle and the planets circling it.","file":"Copernican heliocentrism diagram-2.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copernican_heliocentrism_diagram-2.jpg","author":"Copernican_heliocentrism_diagram.jpg: Own work from Copernicus 1543…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/13-3.jpg","alt":"Title page of Luther's 'September Testament', Das Newe Testament Deutzsch, printed in Wittenberg in 1522.","file":"Luther Das Newe Testament Deutzsch 007.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luther_Das_Newe_Testament_Deutzsch_007.jpg","author":"Martin Luther","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3968},{"n":14,"title":"News, Spelling and Print Around the World","when":"1500s – 1700s","summary":"The first newspaper appeared in Strasbourg in 1605, and soon people everywhere could read the news every week or even every day. Young Benjamin Franklin printed a famous almanac, Samuel Johnson wrote a giant dictionary, and a new law gave authors the right to own their books. Printing presses also sailed across oceans to Mexico, India, Massachusetts and Istanbul.","sections":[{"heading":"Strasbourg, 1605: The Birth of the Newspaper","paragraphs":["How did people get news before newspapers? For most of history the answer was by word of mouth: travellers, merchants, soldiers and pilgrims carried stories from place to place, and town criers shouted announcements in the square. Rich merchants and rulers had something better. They paid for private, handwritten newsletters, sent regularly by post from busy trading cities. In Venice these handwritten news sheets were called avvisi, meaning notices. They reported battles, prices, shipwrecks, royal weddings and the arrival of ships, because for a merchant, knowing the news first could mean making or losing a fortune.","In the 1500s printers also sold one-off news pamphlets about especially exciting events, such as a great battle, a comet or a terrible flood. But these appeared only when something dramatic happened. A newspaper is different: it comes out regularly, on a schedule, with a mix of the latest news, and readers come back for the next issue.","Our first clear example comes from Strasbourg, then a German-speaking free city on the river Rhine, the same city where Gutenberg had done his secret experiments about 170 years earlier. There a bookseller named Johann Carolus had been making money by producing handwritten weekly news reports for paying customers. Copying them out by hand was slow. So Carolus bought a printing workshop, which had belonged to a printer named Thomas Jobin who had died, and installed it in his own house. Then he started printing his weekly news instead of copying it.","We know this because of a letter. In December 1605 Carolus sent a petition to the Strasbourg city council asking for protection against rivals who might copy his idea. In it he explained that he had already been printing his weekly news for some time. This petition was rediscovered in the Strasbourg city archive in the 1980s, and historians now treat it as the newspaper's birth certificate. The earliest issues that still survive date from 1609, and a volume of that year has been digitized by Heidelberg University Library, so anyone can read it online.","Carolus called his paper the Relation, short for a long German title meaning Account of all distinguished and memorable news. In 2005 the World Association of Newspapers accepted the evidence and recognized the Relation of 1605 as the world's first newspaper. Some scholars argue about definitions, because the Relation looked like a little book rather than a big folded sheet, but it was printed, it was regular and it was full of current news, so most people accept its claim."]},{"heading":"Corantos, Gazettes and the First Daily","paragraphs":["Once the idea existed, it spread quickly. A weekly paper called the Aviso was printed in Wolfenbüttel in northern Germany by 1609. Amsterdam, a busy trading port full of printers, became a great news center. From 1618 Dutch printers produced single-sheet news reports called corantos, meaning currents of news. In 1620 the first news sheets in English were actually printed in Amsterdam and shipped across the North Sea to London, because English printers were tightly controlled at home.","Early newspapers were careful. Kings did not like criticism, so papers mostly reported foreign news, which was safer than writing about your own government. Some papers were official. In England the London Gazette, first published in 1665 as the Oxford Gazette while the royal court had moved to Oxford to escape the plague, printed government announcements, and it still exists today.","Things changed when England's Licensing Act, which had required printed works to be approved in advance, was allowed to lapse in 1695. Suddenly many new papers appeared in London. On 11 March 1702 came the Daily Courant, the first daily newspaper in English. It was started by Elizabeth Mallet, who printed it near the King's Arms tavern at Fleet Bridge in London. Yes, the first English daily was launched by a woman, at a time when very few women ran businesses openly.","The Daily Courant was tiny by modern standards: a single printed sheet, with news on the front and advertisements on the back. Mallet promised readers something interesting. She said she would report only the news, mostly translated from foreign papers, and add no comments of her own, trusting readers to make up their own minds. After about forty days she sold the paper to Samuel Buckley, who ran it for many years. It lasted until 1735, when it merged with another paper.","Across the Atlantic, the first attempt at a newspaper in the English colonies, Publick Occurrences in Boston in 1690, was shut down by the government after just one issue. The Boston News-Letter, begun in 1704, lasted much longer. Newspapers, coffee houses and postal services grew together. In London, people gathered in coffee houses to read the papers aloud and argue about them, so one copy might have many readers. By the 1700s the newspaper had become part of everyday life in many European and colonial cities."]},{"heading":"Ballads, Almanacs and Benjamin Franklin's Print Shop","paragraphs":["Newspapers were not the only cheap print. Imagine walking through a busy market town in England around 1650. Pasted on walls and posts you might see broadsides: big single sheets printed on one side, used for royal proclamations, notices of executions, advertisements, warnings and news of wonders like a two-headed calf. A seller might be singing ballads, songs printed on cheap sheets with a woodcut picture at the top, telling stories of love, murder, shipwrecks and battles. For a penny you could buy one and learn the words.","Travelling sellers called chapmen walked from village to village with packs full of chapbooks, small cheap booklets of folk tales, jokes, riddles, adventure stories and advice. For many families, chapbooks and ballads were the first things they ever owned in print. These humble products were not considered great literature, but they helped millions of ordinary people get used to reading.","The best-selling printed item of all in many homes was the almanac, a yearly booklet with a calendar, the dates of fairs and holidays, times of sunrise and sunset, phases of the Moon, tides, weather predictions, astrology, recipes and advice. Farmers used almanacs to plan planting, and many families hung one by the fireplace all year.","No one shows the life of a printer in this period better than Benjamin Franklin. He was born in Boston in 1706, the fifteenth of seventeen children of a candle and soap maker. When Ben was twelve, his father apprenticed him to his older brother James, a printer. In 1721 James started a newspaper, the New-England Courant. Ben, then about sixteen, wanted to write for it but knew his brother would refuse. So he invented a character, a talkative widow named Silence Dogood, and slipped her letters under the print shop door at night. The letters were printed in 1722 and became popular before Ben admitted he had written them.","The brothers quarrelled, and in 1723 Ben ran away to Philadelphia with almost no money. He worked for other printers, travelled to London to learn more of the trade, and eventually opened his own shop. In 1729 he took over a newspaper, the Pennsylvania Gazette. In December 1732 he published the first Poor Richard's Almanack, for the year 1733, pretending it was written by a poor stargazer called Richard Saunders. He made it funny from the start, predicting the death of a real rival almanac maker, Titan Leeds, so that readers bought the next issue to see what happened.","Poor Richard was a huge success, selling about 10,000 copies a year, an enormous number for the colonies. Readers loved its short sayings, such as early to bed and early to rise makes a man healthy, wealthy and wise. Franklin admitted he collected many of these sayings from older books and polished them. He published the almanac every year until 1758, when he gathered the best proverbs into an essay later called The Way to Wealth, which was reprinted many times.","Franklin's printing business made him wealthy enough to retire from daily printing in 1748, aged about 42, and spend his time on science and public life. He experimented with electricity, helped found a library and a university, and later helped write the Declaration of Independence. Yet he always remembered his trade. As a young man he wrote a joking epitaph for himself beginning The Body of B. Franklin, Printer, and in his will, written in 1788, he described himself first of all as a printer."]},{"heading":"Fixing the Words: Spelling and Dictionaries","paragraphs":["If you could read a letter written in English in the year 1500, you would be surprised by the spelling. The same writer might spell the same word two or three different ways on one page. There were no rules to break, because there were no official rules. Spelling simply followed how a word sounded to the writer, and English was spoken very differently in different regions. Even William Shakespeare's name was spelled in several different ways during his lifetime, including in his own signatures.","Printing slowly pushed spelling toward one standard. A busy print shop could not keep stopping to decide how to spell each word, so printers developed house habits, and readers who saw thousands of printed pages got used to seeing words spelled in particular ways. London, where most English printing happened, had a strong influence. Spelling did not settle overnight, though. Early compositors sometimes changed spellings to make a line fit, adding or dropping a letter while justifying the line, and printed spelling took more than two centuries to become fairly consistent.","Dictionaries helped fix the words in place. The first English dictionary that explained words in English, Robert Cawdrey's Table Alphabeticall of 1604, listed only about 2,500 hard words. In France, the French Academy, founded in 1635, worked for decades on an official dictionary of the French language, publishing its first edition in 1694.","England had no academy to do this job, so in 1746 a group of London booksellers hired one remarkable, hard-working writer instead: Samuel Johnson. They agreed to pay him 1,500 guineas, a large sum, though he had to pay his helpers out of it. Working in his house in Gough Square in London with a few clerks to copy out quotations, Johnson read widely through English literature, marking passages that showed how words were really used.","His Dictionary of the English Language was published on 15 April 1755, in two huge folio volumes. It defined 42,773 words and included about 114,000 quotations from roughly 500 authors, including Shakespeare and Milton, to show each word in action. Johnson also had a sense of humour. He defined a lexicographer, a person who writes dictionaries, as a harmless drudge, meaning a hard-working but harmless plodder.","Johnson's Dictionary was expensive, but it became the standard English dictionary for well over a century, until the Oxford English Dictionary was published much later. It did not single-handedly decide English spelling, but it greatly strengthened the idea that there was a correct way to spell and use a word, and later dictionaries built on its spellings and definitions.","All this happened as more people learned to read. Literacy is hard to measure for the past, but one clue is how many people could sign their name on documents such as marriage records. In England, historians estimate that by around 1750 roughly 60 percent of men and perhaps 40 percent of women could sign their names, much higher than two centuries before. Cheap print, schools, religion and trade all encouraged reading, and the more people read, the more printers printed."]},{"heading":"Who Owns a Book? The Birth of Copyright","paragraphs":["Here is a puzzle that printing created. If you write a book and a printer publishes it, can another printer simply copy it and sell cheaper copies? Before printing, this hardly mattered, because copying a book by hand took so long. After printing, it became a big question, because a rival could reset a popular book and undercut the original in a few weeks.","The first answer was the privilege. Governments granted a particular printer the sole right to print a certain book for a few years. Venice gave one of the earliest in 1469, to the printer Johann of Speyer. In England, the Stationers' Company kept a register of who owned the right to print which book. Notice that these rights belonged to printers and booksellers, not to authors. A writer usually sold a manuscript once for a fee and had no further say.","England's licensing system ended in 1695, and the booksellers lobbied Parliament for new protection. The result was a law usually called the Statute of Anne, after Queen Anne. Its official name began An Act for the Encouragement of Learning, which tells you its purpose. It received royal assent on 5 April 1710 and came into force on 10 April 1710.","The Statute of Anne did two revolutionary things. First, it gave the right to copy a book to its author, who could then sell that right to a publisher. Second, the right lasted only for a limited time: 14 years from publication, which could be renewed for a second 14 years if the author was still alive. Books already in print got 21 years. After that, anyone could print the book. Copies made illegally could be destroyed, and those who made them could be fined.","That time limit was a deliberate balance. Authors and publishers got a reward for their work, which encouraged them to write and publish, while the public eventually got the work for free, so that knowledge would spread. Today we call works that nobody owns any more the public domain. Booksellers later argued that copyright should last forever, but in 1774 the House of Lords, in a case called Donaldson v Becket, ruled that it could not.","The Statute of Anne became a model for copyright laws around the world. The first United States copyright law, in 1790, used the very same scheme of 14 years plus a possible 14-year renewal. Copyright terms are much longer today, but the basic idea that creators should own their work for a limited time began in 1710."]},{"heading":"Printing Crosses the Oceans","paragraphs":["While Europeans argued about news and copyright, printing presses were travelling across the world on ships, mostly carried by missionaries, colonial governments and merchants. This spread was tied up with colonization and conversion, and the people who already lived in these lands did not ask for it. But it also produced the first printed books in many languages, some of which still survive.","The first press in the Americas came to Mexico City, the capital of the Spanish colony of New Spain. A printer from Seville named Juan Cromberger, who never crossed the ocean himself, sent an Italian-born employee named Juan Pablos, originally Giovanni Paoli from near Brescia. Pablos sailed from Seville in June 1539 and reached Mexico City that October. His first book, printed in 1539, was a short guide to Christian teaching in Nahuatl, the language of the Aztecs, and Spanish. Sadly no copy survives. Pablos printed at least 37 titles before his death around 1560, including in 1555 a Spanish and Nahuatl dictionary by Alonso de Molina, the first dictionary printed in the Americas. The building where he worked in Mexico City is now a museum.","Printing came next to Lima, in Peru, in 1584. Meanwhile, Asia received European-style presses too. In 1556 a ship carrying a press and Jesuit missionaries to Ethiopia stopped at Goa, the Portuguese colony on India's west coast. When the Ethiopian emperor no longer wanted the missionaries, the press stayed in Goa at the Jesuit College of St Paul, run by a Spanish Jesuit printer, João de Bustamante, with an Indian assistant. In 1578 a book in Tamil, printed with Tamil letters, was produced at Quilon in southern India, one of the first books printed in an Indian script.","Jesuits brought a press to Japan in 1590 and printed books in both Latin letters and Japanese. In Manila, in the Philippines, a Christian teaching book called the Doctrina Christiana was printed in 1593, using woodblocks, in Spanish and Tagalog. In China and Korea, where woodblock printing was already centuries old, the European press made little difference for a long time: the old methods worked well and were deeply rooted.","In North America, the English Puritan colony of Massachusetts got its first press in the late 1630s. A minister named Jose Glover bought a press in England and set sail with his family and a locksmith named Stephen Daye, who had agreed to run it. Glover died during the voyage, so the press belonged to his widow, Elizabeth Glover. Daye set up the press in Cambridge, Massachusetts, near the new Harvard College, with his son Matthew, who probably did much of the actual printing.","Their most famous book, printed in 1640, was The Whole Booke of Psalmes, known today as the Bay Psalm Book. It was a new translation of the Psalms into English verse for singing in church, made by local ministers. It was the first book printed in British North America. About 1,700 copies were printed, but only 11 are known today, and only five are complete. In 2013 one sold at auction for 14.2 million US dollars, a record at that time for a printed book.","The Cambridge press later printed something even more remarkable: in 1663 the whole Bible translated into Massachusett, the language of the Native people of the region, by the missionary John Eliot with Native helpers. It was the first complete Bible printed in the Americas, and it was not in English but in an Indigenous language."]},{"heading":"Istanbul's Printing Pioneer","paragraphs":["The Ottoman Empire was one of the largest and most powerful states in the world, stretching from Hungary across Turkey and the Middle East to North Africa. Its capital, Istanbul, was a great city of scholars, libraries and book markets. People sometimes say that printing was completely banned there for centuries, but the real story is more interesting.","Printing actually arrived in Istanbul quite early, through the empire's religious minorities. Jewish refugees who had been expelled from Spain set up a Hebrew press in Istanbul around 1493, or by 1504 at the latest; historians still debate the exact date. An Armenian printer, Abgar Tokhatetsi, printed books in Armenian in Istanbul in 1567, and a Greek press operated there in 1627. These communities printed books in their own languages and scripts.","For books in Ottoman Turkish and Arabic, however, the hand-copied manuscript remained king for much longer. There were several reasons, and historians still weigh them up. Thousands of skilled scribes and calligraphers earned their living copying books. Beautiful handwriting was deeply admired, and Arabic script, with letters that change shape and join up, was harder to set in metal type than the Latin alphabet. Many people also felt that holy texts, especially the Quran, should be copied by hand with great care and respect.","The man who brought Ottoman Turkish printing into being was İbrahim Müteferrika, born around 1674 in Kolozsvár, today Cluj-Napoca in Romania. He was a Hungarian who came to Istanbul as a young man, became a Muslim and rose to serve the Ottoman government as a diplomat and scholar. In 1726 he wrote an essay explaining the benefits of printing, arguing that it would make books cheaper, more accurate and more widely available, and help the empire keep up with Europe.","He gained support from powerful officials, a religious ruling approving printing for non-religious books, and permission from Sultan Ahmed III in 1727. He worked with Said Efendi, whose father had been the Ottoman ambassador in Paris. Religious books were left out, partly to reassure the scribes and scholars.","Müteferrika's press printed its first book in 1729: a big Arabic-to-Turkish dictionary known as the Vankulu dictionary, in two volumes. Over the next years he printed history books, a grammar, works on geography and the magnetic compass, and Katip Çelebi's world geography, the Cihannüma, with printed maps. By 1743 the press had printed 17 titles (some historians count 16, because two were often bound together).","That might sound small compared with Europe, but it was a real beginning, and it proved that Ottoman Turkish could be printed beautifully with Arabic letters. Printing in the Ottoman world grew much more widely in the 1800s, and today a statue of İbrahim Müteferrika stands near the old book market beside Istanbul's Grand Bazaar.","By the 1700s, then, printing had become a global technology. Presses worked in Mexico City and Lima, Goa and Manila, Cambridge and Philadelphia, Strasbourg and Istanbul. Newspapers brought weekly and even daily news, almanacs sat by fireplaces, dictionaries steadied spelling, and new laws protected authors. Yet the press itself, a wooden frame with a screw pulled by human arms, was not very different from Gutenberg's. That was about to change."]}],"deepDive":"What counts as the 'first newspaper' depends on your definition. Media historians usually require four things: mechanical reproduction (printing), regular publication, a general public that can buy it, and a mix of current news. Handwritten Venetian avvisi had regularity but no printing; earlier one-off news pamphlets had printing but no regularity. Johann Carolus's Relation is the earliest known publication to meet all four tests, but the 1605 date rests entirely on his December 1605 petition, since the oldest surviving issues are from 1609. The format argument matters too: the Relation looked like a small quarto booklet, so the typographer Stanley Morison classed it as a 'newsbook' rather than a newspaper. The World Association of Newspapers nonetheless accepted it as the first newspaper in 2005. Copyright raises another long debate. The Statute of Anne (1710) is often called the first modern copyright law, but historians note it was pushed for mainly by London booksellers who had lost their monopoly when licensing lapsed in 1695. Its limited terms (14 years, renewable once by a living author; 21 years for existing books) led to decades of court fights over whether a 'common-law' perpetual copyright also existed, settled against perpetual rights by the House of Lords in Donaldson v Becket (1774). The global spread of the press also deserves careful framing. In Mexico, Goa, Manila and Massachusetts, presses arrived with missionaries and colonial governments, and their first books were mostly religious texts aimed at converting local peoples, yet they also preserved early printed forms of Nahuatl, Tamil, Tagalog and Massachusett. For the Ottoman Empire, older accounts claiming a strict 'ban' on printing are now questioned; historians stress economic factors (a large scribal profession), aesthetic and technical difficulties of Arabic-script type, and the respect owed to hand-copied religious texts. Müteferrika's 17 titles were small in number but showed the technology could work in Ottoman Turkish.","funFact":"The first daily newspaper in English, the Daily Courant of 1702, was launched by a woman, Elizabeth Mallet, who promised to print only the facts and leave readers to make up their own minds.","check":{"q":"Which book, printed in Cambridge, Massachusetts, in 1640, was the first book printed in British North America?","a":"The Bay Psalm Book","wrong":["Poor Richard's Almanack","Johnson's Dictionary","The Daily Courant"]},"sources":[{"title":"Relation aller Fürnemmen und gedenckwürdigen Historien","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relation_aller_F%C3%BCrnemmen_und_gedenckw%C3%BCrdigen_Historien"},{"title":"Daily Courant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daily_Courant"},{"title":"Poor Richard's Almanack","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poor_Richard%27s_Almanack"},{"title":"A Dictionary of the English Language","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Dictionary_of_the_English_Language"},{"title":"Statute of Anne","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statute_of_Anne"},{"title":"Hispanic Heritage Month: Early Titles by the Americas' First Printing Press","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/loc/2018/10/hispanic-heritage-month-early-titles-by-the-americas-first-printing-press/"},{"title":"Bay Psalm Book","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay_Psalm_Book"},{"title":"Ibrahim Muteferrika","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ibrahim_Muteferrika"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/14-1.jpg","alt":"Title page of the Bay Psalm Book, the first book printed in British North America (Cambridge, Massachusetts, 1640).","file":"Bay Psalm Book title page.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bay_Psalm_Book_title_page.jpg","author":"Stephen Day (dated 1640)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/14-2.jpg","alt":"A City of London plaque marking where The Daily Courant, London's first daily newspaper, was published in 1702.","file":"The Daily Courant (8097930013).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Daily_Courant_(8097930013).jpg","author":"Simon Harriyott from Uckfield, England","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/14-3.jpg","alt":"Title page of Samuel Johnson's A Dictionary of the English Language, second edition, London, 1755.","file":"JohnsonDictionary.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohnsonDictionary.png","author":"Samuel Johnson","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3746},{"n":15,"title":"Steam, Iron and Endless Paper","when":"1800 – 1880","summary":"For 350 years, printers pulled heavy wooden presses by hand. Then came presses made of iron, then presses driven by roaring steam engines, and finally giant spinning machines fed by rolls of paper as long as a road. Paper itself got cheaper too, once people learned to make it from trees instead of old rags.","sections":[{"heading":"A Press Made of Iron","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into a London print shop in the year 1799. It would look surprisingly like Gutenberg's workshop in Mainz more than three centuries earlier. The press is a tall wooden frame, held together with pegs and bolts. A pressman grabs the long bar, leans back with his whole body and pulls, and a wooden screw lowers a flat plate called the platen onto the paper and the inked type. Then he lets go, the platen lifts, and his partner peels off the sheet. Pull, lift, peel, repeat, all day long.","The wooden hand press had served the world well, but it had real weaknesses. Wood bends and warps, so the pressure across the page was never perfectly even. Most wooden presses had a platen only about half the size of a big sheet, so the pressman had to slide the type under and pull twice to print one side of a large page. It was exhausting work, and it set a hard speed limit on how much could be printed.","Around 1800 an English nobleman decided to fix this. Charles Stanhope, the 3rd Earl Stanhope (1753–1816), was a politician who loved science and gadgets. He experimented with steamboats, canal locks, a fireproofing method and even calculating machines. His most famous invention was the Stanhope press, usually described as the first printing press made entirely of iron.","Iron changed everything about the press. A cast-iron frame did not bend, so the platen could press evenly across the whole page. Because iron is so strong, the platen could be made big enough to print a full large sheet in one pull instead of two. Stanhope also added a clever system of levers linked to the screw. As the pressman pulled the bar, the levers gave him more and more pushing force right at the end of the pull, just when the platen touched the paper. That meant crisp, dark printing without a pressman wearing himself out.","Even so, the Stanhope press was still a hand press. A crew could make roughly 200 pulls an hour, which is about one impression every 18 seconds. A newspaper printed on both sides needed two pulls per sheet, so 200 pulls produced only about 100 finished copies. For a big city paper that wanted thousands of copies each morning, that was hopeless.","Other inventors quickly joined the race. In America, George Clymer of Philadelphia designed the Columbian press in the 1810s, often called the first all-metal press made in the United States. It is easy to recognize in museums because a cast-iron eagle sits on top, acting as a counterweight that lifts the platen back up. Later came the Albion press in England and the Washington press in America. These iron hand presses were so tough that many still work today in art studios and printing museums. But to really speed things up, someone would have to take the human arm out of the job altogether."]},{"heading":"Friedrich Koenig and the Steam Press","paragraphs":["That someone was Friedrich Koenig, a German printer and inventor born in 1774. Koenig had been dreaming about a machine press since his early years in the trade. Few people in Germany wanted to pay for his experiments, so in November 1806 he moved to London, where the Industrial Revolution was in full swing and steam engines were already pumping water from mines and turning machines in cotton mills.","In London Koenig teamed up with Andreas Bauer, a skilled German precision mechanic who had trained as a watchmaker, and with a London book printer named Thomas Bensley, who helped pay for the work. Koenig received a British patent for a printing machine in 1810, and his first trial of a working machine took place in April 1812.","Koenig's great idea was to stop squeezing the paper with a flat platen. Instead, the type lay on a flat bed that slid back and forth like a drawer. The paper was wrapped around a big turning cylinder. As the bed slid underneath, the cylinder rolled over the type, pressing the paper against it one narrow strip at a time, the way you might roll a rolling pin across dough. Because only a thin line of the cylinder touched the type at any moment, it needed far less force than pressing a whole page at once.","Koenig also got rid of the leather ink balls that printers had dabbed on type for centuries. In his machine, a set of rollers automatically picked up ink and spread it evenly over the type each time the bed passed. And instead of a strong pressman, the power came from a steam engine. Workers still had to feed each blank sheet in and take each printed sheet out by hand, but the hard, repetitive labour was now done by the engine.","Among the people invited to see Koenig's machines was John Walter II, the owner of The Times of London, one of the most important newspapers in Britain. Walter had a problem: the more readers wanted his paper, the more hand presses and pressmen he needed, and still the paper came out late. He ordered two of Koenig's machines.","According to the story told afterwards, Walter worried that his pressmen would be furious, or even try to wreck the machines, because they would fear losing their jobs. So the new presses were installed quietly in a separate building. Early on the morning of 29 November 1814, the pressmen were told to wait for important news from abroad. Then Walter walked in and announced that the paper had already been printed, by steam. That day's issue proudly told readers they were holding a newspaper printed by machine, which the paper described as the biggest improvement in printing since the art was first invented.","The numbers explain the excitement. Koenig's steam press could print about 1,100 sheets an hour, several times faster than a hand press. Koenig and Bauer soon built a double-cylinder machine that printed two sheets at once, and then a perfecting machine that printed both sides of a sheet in a single pass."]},{"heading":"From London to a Monastery in Germany","paragraphs":["Success did not make Koenig's life easy. He quarrelled with Thomas Bensley, who Koenig believed wanted to control the machine for himself. In August 1817 Koenig gave up on England and returned to Germany. Together with Bauer he set up a factory in an empty old monastery at Oberzell, near the city of Würzburg in Bavaria. The firm they founded, Koenig and Bauer, still builds printing presses today, more than two hundred years later, making it one of the oldest press makers in the world.","Koenig died in 1833, but his cylinder idea lived on everywhere. In Britain, engineers such as Augustus Applegath and Edward Cowper improved the machines for The Times, adding more cylinders and faster inking. In America, a press maker named Isaac Adams built a powered platen press that became popular for printing books. By the 1830s, steam presses were humming in print shops across Europe and North America.","It is worth noticing something important here. The steam press did not appear out of nowhere. It combined the old ideas of movable type and ink with brand-new ideas from the Industrial Revolution: precise iron parts made in machine shops, steam engines, gears and belts. Inventions almost always grow this way, by joining older ideas in new combinations. Gutenberg did it with the screw press in the 1400s, and Koenig did it with the steam engine in the 1800s.","There was also a business lesson. Historians point out that Koenig's machine was a success at The Times partly because almost no other publication of the time printed enough copies to justify such an expensive machine. A small-town printer making a few hundred posters a week did not need a steam monster. A huge daily newspaper did. As cities grew and more people learned to read, more and more printers found themselves in that second group."]},{"heading":"Spinning Cylinders: Hoe's Lightning Press","paragraphs":["Koenig's press still had a slow part: the heavy bed of type sliding back and forth. Every time it reached the end, it had to stop, reverse and come back, wasting time and shaking the machine. What if the type itself could spin, round and round, without ever stopping?","In New York, the Hoe family ran a company that built presses. Robert Hoe had started the business in 1805, making wood and iron machinery. His son Richard March Hoe (1812–1886) developed a rotary press in the 1840s, received a US patent for it in 1847, and put it into commercial use that same year. Fair credit matters here: an American inventor named Josiah Warren had shown a rotary press design back in 1832 but chose not to patent it. Hoe turned the idea into a practical, money-making machine.","In Hoe's press, the type for a page was locked onto a huge central cylinder that turned continuously. The pieces of type were held in special wedge-shaped frames so they would not fly off as the cylinder spun. Around this big drum stood several smaller impression cylinders, sometimes as many as ten, each with its own worker feeding in sheets of paper. With every turn of the giant drum, each small cylinder printed a sheet. People called it the Lightning Press, and the name fit. Each feeder could produce about 2,000 one-sided sheets an hour, so a ten-feeder machine could make around 20,000.","These monsters were big, noisy and expensive, often filling a whole room. But a newspaper with a large circulation could finally print its morning edition in a few hours instead of all night.","There was one more clever trick that made rotary printing practical. Instead of putting precious, fragile type on the cylinder, printers learned to make a copy of the whole page called a stereotype. Workers pressed a thick sheet of papier-mâché-like material onto the locked-up page of type to make a mould, bent the mould into a curve, and poured molten metal into it. Out came a solid, curved metal plate of the whole page that fitted neatly onto a cylinder. Stereotyping developed during the 1840s and became widespread in the 1860s. It protected the type from wear, freed the type for the next job, and let a newspaper cast several identical plates so that several presses could print the same page at once. Hoe's company began using stereotype plates in its presses in 1861, and newspapers kept using them until the 1970s."]},{"heading":"The Endless Web, and the Price Bullock Paid","paragraphs":["Even the Lightning Press needed people to feed in one sheet of paper at a time. The next leap was to stop using sheets altogether and feed the press from a giant roll of paper, which printers call a web.","William Bullock was born in 1813 in Greenville, New York. He had worked as a machinist and iron-founder, run his own machine shop, and even edited a newspaper in Philadelphia, and he had been tinkering with self-feeding presses since the 1850s. On 14 April 1863 he received US patent number 38,200 for a printing press that used a continuous web of paper. Working with papermakers, he had the paper made in long rolls, and by around 1865 his presses were running on them.","Here is how a web press works. A huge roll of paper sits at one end, mounted on an axle. The paper unrolls and threads through the machine like a ribbon. It passes between cylinders that print one side, then between more cylinders that print the other side, so the sheet is printed on both sides in one pass. Printers call this perfecting. Finally, a sharp knife on a turning cylinder cuts the ribbon into separate sheets. Nobody has to feed paper by hand. The press just keeps eating paper as fast as the roll can turn.","Bullock's press could print up to about 12,000 sheets an hour, and later improvements pushed it to as many as 30,000. The New-York Tribune reported that early Bullock presses were turning out roughly 8,000 to 10,000 copies an hour.","Sadly, Bullock's own invention led to his death. On 3 April 1867 he was adjusting one of his new presses being installed at the Philadelphia Public Ledger newspaper. He tried to kick a driving belt back onto a pulley, and his leg was caught and crushed in the machinery. The wound became badly infected, and on 12 April 1867 he died during an operation to remove the leg. In those days before modern antibiotics, infections from injuries were often deadly. His story is a reminder that the early factories and print rooms were dangerous places, full of fast belts and gears with no safety guards.","Others carried the web press forward. Hoe's company built a web perfecting press that the New-York Tribune used from 1875, turning out about 14,000 complete copies an hour. Machines were added that folded the newspapers automatically as they came off the press. By the late 1800s, the biggest newspaper presses could print tens of thousands of sheets an hour. Remember the Stanhope press, at about 200 pulls an hour? In less than a hundred years, printing speed had multiplied by more than a hundred."]},{"heading":"A Machine That Makes Endless Paper","paragraphs":["All these fast presses had a very hungry appetite. A press that printed tens of thousands of sheets an hour needed mountains of paper, and a web press needed paper in long, unbroken rolls. But for most of history, paper had been made one sheet at a time by hand.","In a traditional paper mill, a worker called a vatman dipped a wire mould into a tub of watery pulp, lifted it out, and shook it gently so the fibers spread and tangled. Another worker flipped the wet sheet onto a piece of felt. Stacks of sheets were squeezed in a press and hung up to dry. Skilled teams worked fast, but each sheet could never be bigger than the mould, and there was a limit to how many sheets people could make in a day.","The solution came from France. Louis-Nicolas Robert (1761–1828) worked at a paper mill at Essonnes, south of Paris, owned by the Didot family. In 1799 Robert was granted a French patent for a machine that made paper in one continuous strip. In his machine, pulp flowed onto an endless belt of woven wire mesh that moved along like a conveyor belt. Water drained through the mesh, and the fibers knitted into a long wet sheet.","Robert and his employer, Saint-Léger Didot, argued over who owned the invention. France was in turmoil after the Revolution, so Didot sent his English brother-in-law, John Gamble, to Britain to find support. Gamble met two London stationers, the brothers Henry and Sealy Fourdrinier, who agreed to pay for the development. They hired a talented engineer named Bryan Donkin. In 1803 an improved machine was installed at Frogmore Mill in Apsley, Hertfordshire, and another followed in 1804.","The Fourdrinier brothers spent huge sums on the project and ended up going bankrupt, but their name stuck. Today a paper machine of this type is still called a Fourdrinier machine. Modern ones follow the same basic plan as the early ones, in much bigger form. At the wet end, a watery mixture of fibers that is almost all water sprays onto a fast-moving wire mesh. Water drains away and the fibers lock together. Next, the wet sheet passes through presses that squeeze out more water. Then it winds around dozens of steam-heated drying cylinders. Finally, polished rollers called calenders smooth it, and the finished paper is wound onto a giant reel.","The science is the same as in the paper Cai Lun's workers made in China nearly 1,700 years earlier: tangled plant fibers, held together by hydrogen bonds as they dry. The difference is speed and size. Modern paper machines can be longer than a football field and produce a sheet of paper several meters wide, moving faster than a car on a city street."]},{"heading":"Paper from Trees: Friedrich Gottlob Keller","paragraphs":["Machines solved the problem of making paper fast, but not the problem of what to make it from. European paper had been made mostly from old linen and cotton rags. Rag collectors went from door to door buying old clothes and cloth scraps. As printing exploded in the 1800s, there simply were not enough rags in the world to feed all the paper machines. Papermakers searched for something else: straw, grasses, even other plants.","The answer was standing all around them in forests. More than a century earlier, in 1719, the French scientist René-Antoine Ferchault de Réaumur had noticed that wasps build their papery nests by chewing wood into a pulp, and he suggested humans might make paper from wood too. For a long time, nobody managed to do it on a practical scale.","Friedrich Gottlob Keller was born on 27 June 1816 in Hainichen, in the Kingdom of Saxony in Germany. His father was a weaver, and young Friedrich worked in the family trade, though he much preferred machines. In 1841 he wrote an idea in his notebook, which he called his idea book: a machine to grind wood into fibers. Inspired partly by Réaumur's suggestion, he spent the next few years working on it. By 1844 he had built a grinder that held a piece of wood against a turning, wet grindstone. The stone tore the wood into tiny fibers, which mixed with water into a pulp. Keller made paper from this ground wood pulp.","In the summer of 1844 Keller sent a sample to the government of Saxony, hoping for money to build a better grinder. He got none. He did not have the money to develop the idea himself, so he sold his invention to a paper manufacturer named Heinrich Voelter for a small sum. A patent was granted in August 1845 in both their names, but in 1852 Keller could not pay his share to renew it, and Voelter became the only owner. Voelter and the engineer Johann Matthäus Voith turned Keller's idea into an industry. Keller himself earned no royalties. Later in life, German paper makers collected money to thank him, and he lived out his years in the town of Krippen, where he died in 1895.","Fair credit, again: in the same years, a man named Charles Fenerty in Nova Scotia, Canada, also made paper from wood pulp and announced his discovery in 1844, apparently without knowing about Keller. Like many inventions, wood pulp paper was found by more than one person at nearly the same time.","Ground wood pulp made weak paper on its own, so it was often mixed with stronger fibers. Later in the 1800s, chemists found ways to cook wood chips in chemicals to free the fibers more completely, making stronger paper. Together, these methods made paper astonishingly cheap. Trees, unlike rags, could be grown in enormous numbers. Cheap wood-pulp paper had a hidden flaw, though: much of it was acidic and slowly turned yellow and brittle. That problem, and what libraries do about it, is a story for later in this Deep Dive."]},{"heading":"Penny Papers, Pickwick and the Reading Explosion","paragraphs":["Put fast steam presses, endless paper and cheap wood pulp together, and something remarkable happens: reading becomes affordable for almost everyone.","In the early 1800s, an American newspaper typically cost about six cents, a lot of money for a working family. In September 1833, a young New York printer named Benjamin Day launched The Sun and sold it for just one cent. His paper promised to give everyone all the news of the day at a price anyone could pay. Day hired boys to sell papers on street corners, shouting the headlines, and the newsboy became a famous sight in cities. Two years later James Gordon Bennett started the New York Herald, packed with local news, crime reports, interviews and a page about money. These cheap, lively papers became known as the penny press, and their huge sales only made sense because steam presses could print so many copies so quickly.","In Britain, magazines took off too. Charles Knight's Penny Magazine, started in 1832, sold more than 200,000 copies in its first year, offering cheap articles about science, history and art to working people.","Novels changed shape as well. In April 1836 a young writer named Charles Dickens began publishing The Pickwick Papers not as a single expensive book but in monthly parts, each costing a shilling. Sales started small, but once Dickens introduced the funny servant Sam Weller, they soared. By the final double issue in November 1837, the parts were selling about 40,000 copies, and the publishers had to keep reprinting the early parts. Dickens published most of his later novels as serials in parts or in magazines too. Readers waited eagerly for each new installment, a bit like waiting for the next episode of a TV series today.","Cheap printing also transformed schools. Textbooks, readers and spelling books could be printed by the hundreds of thousands. In the United States, the McGuffey Readers, first published in the 1830s, taught generations of children to read and sold many millions of copies. As more countries introduced public schooling for all children, such as Britain's Education Act of 1870, millions of new readers wanted newspapers, magazines and books. Railways, another steam-age invention, carried bundles of newspapers from city presses to towns far away, often by the next morning.","So by 1880 the world of print looked completely different from 1800. Presses were made of iron and steel, driven by steam, fed by rolls of paper made by machines from trees. A single big-city newspaper could print more copies in one night than a fifteenth-century printer might make in years.","But there was still a bottleneck hiding in plain sight. Before any page reached those roaring machines, a compositor had to stand at a type case and set it, picking up every single letter with his fingers, just as in Gutenberg's day. And pictures were still a struggle: a photograph could not simply be dropped onto a printed page. Presses were now lightning fast, but setting the type was still done by hand, one letter at a time, and pictures were hard to print. The next great inventors would set out to solve both problems."]}],"deepDive":"Why did printing speed jump so dramatically in the 1800s? Engineers often explain it with two ideas: reciprocating versus rotary motion, and batch versus continuous feeding. A flat-bed press, even Koenig's, is reciprocating: the bed must accelerate, travel, stop and reverse. Every reversal wastes time and puts heavy stress on the machine, so there is a practical speed limit. A rotary press removes the stop-and-reverse cycle entirely, because a cylinder can turn at a steady speed forever. Contact between a cylinder and the paper is also a narrow line rather than a whole page, so much less total force is needed for a sharp impression. The second idea, continuous feeding, removed the human from the loop. Even a very skilled feeder can only place so many sheets per minute onto a cylinder. Bullock's web, and Hoe's later web perfecting presses, replaced that human rate limit with the speed at which a reel of paper can unwind, which is limited mainly by how strong wet ink and paper are and how fast the cutting and folding can keep up. The numbers tell the story: roughly 200 pulls an hour for the Stanhope press around 1800; about 1,100 sheets an hour for Koenig's steam press in 1814; around 2,000 sheets per feeder per hour on Hoe's type-revolving press in the 1840s; and about 14,000 complete copies an hour on a web perfecting press at the New-York Tribune by 1875. Historians also debate cause and effect. Did faster presses create mass readership, or did growing literacy, cities and advertising create a demand that pushed inventors to build faster presses? Most historians answer: both, feeding each other. Paper is a good example of a hidden bottleneck. Without the Fourdrinier machine and wood pulp, fast presses would have been starved of material, and the price of a newspaper could not have fallen to a penny. Notice too how many credits are shared or disputed in this period: Warren and Hoe for the rotary press, Robert and the Fourdriniers for the paper machine, Keller and Fenerty for wood pulp. Industrial inventions were usually the work of networks of mechanics, investors and users, not single lone geniuses.","funFact":"Koenig and Bauer, the company Friedrich Koenig founded with Andreas Bauer in an old monastery near Würzburg in 1817, is still building printing presses more than 200 years later.","check":{"q":"Which newspaper was the first to be printed on Friedrich Koenig's steam-powered press, on 29 November 1814?","a":"The Times of London","wrong":["The New York Sun","The Philadelphia Public Ledger","The New-York Tribune"]},"sources":[{"title":"Printing Newspapers 1400-1900: A Brief Survey of the Evolution of the Newspaper Printing Press","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/headlinesandheroes/2022/04/printing-newspapers-1400-1900/"},{"title":"Friedrich Koenig","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Koenig"},{"title":"Richard March Hoe","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_March_Hoe"},{"title":"William Bullock (inventor)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Bullock_(inventor)"},{"title":"Fourdrinier machine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourdrinier_machine"},{"title":"Friedrich Gottlob Keller","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Gottlob_Keller"},{"title":"Penny press","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penny_press"},{"title":"Koenig & Bauer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koenig_%26_Bauer"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/15-1.jpg","alt":"Friedrich Koenig's double-cylinder steam-powered press of 1814, the kind first used to print The Times.","file":"Koenig's steam press - 1814.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koenig%27s_steam_press_-_1814.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/15-2.jpg","alt":"An iron Stanhope hand press built around 1830, at the National Waterfront Museum in Swansea, Wales.","file":"Stanhope printing press - 2022-08-22.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanhope_printing_press_-_2022-08-22.jpg","author":"The wub","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/15-3.jpg","alt":"An engraving of R. Hoe & Co.'s eight-cylinder type-revolving press, with workers feeding paper all around it.","file":"Eight cylinder type-revolving printing machine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eight_cylinder_type-revolving_printing_machine.jpg","author":"Bolles, Albert Sidney, 1846-1939","license":"No restrictions"}],"words":4041},{"n":16,"title":"Typesetting Machines and Printed Pictures","when":"1796 – 1950","summary":"A struggling actor found he could print from a flat stone using grease and water, and colorful posters soon covered city walls. Then clever machines learned to set type from a keyboard, casting whole lines of hot metal in seconds. And by turning photos into tiny dots, newspapers could finally print real photographs.","sections":[{"heading":"An Actor in Debt and a Slab of Stone","paragraphs":["Our story begins not with an engineer but with an actor. Alois Senefelder was born on 6 November 1771 in Prague, where his father, also an actor, was performing at the time. The family lived in Munich, in Bavaria, and young Alois studied law before following his father onto the stage. He also wrote plays, and one of them was a success.","His next play was not. Printing it cost money, and Senefelder ended up in debt. Copying plays by hand was slow, and paying a printer was expensive, so he wondered whether he could print his own writing cheaply. He tried etching letters into copper plates, the way artists made engravings. But copper was costly, and writing backwards into it took a lot of practice.","For practice, he used slabs of a fine, smooth limestone that came from quarries near the Bavarian village of Solnhofen. This stone is so fine-grained that, in the same quarries, workers have found some of the most perfect fossils ever discovered, including the feathered dinosaur-bird Archaeopteryx. Senefelder found the stone easy to polish and easy to write on.","Senefelder later wrote his own account of how the breakthrough happened, and it has become a famous tale. As he told it, his mother asked him to write down a list of clothes for the washerwoman, and with no paper handy, he scribbled it on a freshly polished stone using his homemade greasy ink, made from wax, soap and lampblack. Afterwards he wondered what would happen if he etched the rest of the stone away with acid, leaving his writing raised up like type. That is the story he told, and historians usually repeat it as his own memory rather than as checked fact.","His early experiments, starting around 1796, were with raised writing, a kind of relief printing from stone. But over the next couple of years, roughly by 1798, he discovered something much stranger and more powerful. He did not need to carve or etch the stone deeply at all. A completely flat stone could print, using nothing but chemistry. In 1796 he also started a small publishing business with the composer Franz Gleissner, printing sheet music, which turned out to be one of the first big uses of the new method."]},{"heading":"The Oil-and-Water Trick","paragraphs":["Every printing method before Senefelder worked by shape. In woodblock printing and in Gutenberg's type, the parts that print stand up higher than the rest, so the ink roller only touches them. That is called relief printing. In engraving, the opposite happens: lines are cut into a metal plate, ink is pushed into the grooves, the surface is wiped clean, and paper pressed hard against it pulls the ink out. That is called intaglio printing. Senefelder's method was something new: the printing surface was completely flat. Printers call this planographic printing, meaning printing from a plane, or flat, surface.","How can a flat stone print a picture? The secret is a fact you can see in your own kitchen: oil and water do not mix. Pour a little cooking oil into a glass of water and it floats in blobs, refusing to blend. Water molecules are strongly attracted to each other and pull away from oily molecules, so the two stay separate.","Here is how a lithographer uses this. First, an artist draws on the smooth stone with a greasy crayon or a greasy liquid ink called tusche. The grease soaks a little way into the slightly porous limestone. Next, the printer coats the stone with a solution of gum arabic, a sticky substance from acacia trees, mixed with a weak acid. This treatment makes all the bare areas of the stone love water and hold onto it, while the greasy drawing stays water-repelling.","Now the printing begins. A worker wipes the stone with a wet sponge. Water clings to the bare stone but beads up and runs off the greasy drawing. Then he rolls oily printing ink over the whole surface. The ink sticks to the greasy drawing but is pushed away by the wet stone around it. A sheet of paper is laid on top and the stone and paper are run through a press with a scraping bar. Lift the paper, and there is the drawing, printed. Wet the stone again, ink again, print again: hundreds of copies can be made from one drawing.","Senefelder first called his invention stone printing or chemical printing. The French name, lithographie, won out, and in English it became lithography. The word comes from two Greek words: lithos, meaning stone, and graphein, meaning to write. Lithography literally means stone writing.","Senefelder took out patents in several countries and in 1818 published a thick instruction book on the whole art of stone printing, which was translated into French and English the following year. He died in Munich in 1834. His process had one huge advantage over engraving: an artist could draw directly on the stone in a natural way, with all the soft shading of a crayon drawing, and the printer could reproduce it faithfully. Great artists noticed. Francisco Goya made lithographs of bullfights in the 1820s, Eugène Delacroix illustrated Goethe's Faust, and the French artist Honoré Daumier drew thousands of lithographed cartoons poking fun at politicians for Paris newspapers."]},{"heading":"A Rainbow of Stones: Chromolithography","paragraphs":["If one stone could print one color, why not use several stones to print many colors? That idea became chromolithography, from the Greek word chroma, meaning color. In July 1837 the French printer Godefroy Engelmann of Mulhouse received a patent for a color lithography method, though some historians argue that colored lithographs were being made a little earlier.","Chromolithography was slow, careful, skilled work. For each color, the artist had to draw a separate stone showing only where that color should go. A yellow stone, a red stone, a blue stone, a stone for skin tones, a stone for shadows, and so on. Each sheet of paper went through the press once for each stone. To make the colors land in exactly the right places, printers used small marks or pins to line up the paper precisely every time, called registration. Using twelve colors was not unusual, and some prints needed many more. One example in the New York Public Library's collection was made with 19 stones.","The results were dazzling. In the 1800s, before color photography, chromolithographs brought bright color to ordinary homes for the first time. Families hung cheap copies of famous paintings, nicknamed chromos, on their walls. Shops sold colorful labels for fruit crates, cigar boxes and tin cans. Children collected trade cards, little advertising pictures given away by stores. Scientists used chromolithographs to show birds, flowers, rocks and the human body in accurate color.","In the United States, a German-born printer named Louis Prang became famous for his chromolithographs. He published color copies of popular paintings and printed the first American Christmas cards, helping start a tradition that still fills mailboxes every December.","Lithography also gave birth to the modern poster. In Paris in the late 1800s, artists such as Jules Chéret and Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec designed huge, brilliantly colored posters for theaters, cabarets, bicycles and drinks. Printed by color lithography, they turned city walls into an open-air art gallery. People even peeled them off walls to collect them.","Heavy stones were eventually replaced by thin, flexible metal plates that could wrap around a cylinder, and chromolithography was replaced by cheaper and faster color printing methods by the late 1930s. But the oil-and-water trick at its heart never went away. Keep that in mind for later in this Deep Dive."]},{"heading":"The Slowest Job and Mergenthaler's Answer","paragraphs":["By the 1880s, as we saw in the last part, steam-driven rotary presses could print tens of thousands of newspapers an hour. Yet every single letter on every page still had to be picked out of a type case by a human hand. Picture a big-city newspaper composing room at night: rows of men standing at slanted wooden cases, their fingers darting from compartment to compartment, clicking tiny metal letters into composing sticks. A skilled compositor was fast, but even the best could set only a few thousand letters in an hour, and a large newspaper page holds tens of thousands. Then, after printing, every letter had to be sorted back into its little box.","Newspapers grew bigger and bigger, and they needed armies of compositors. Their speed and accuracy often decided how much they were paid. Publishers dreamed of a machine that could set type the way a typist types a letter.","Many inventors tried and failed. Some built machines that picked up ready-made pieces of type and lined them up, but sorting the used type back into place proved terribly difficult. The most famous failure was the Paige Compositor, a marvelously complicated machine with thousands of parts. The author Mark Twain invested a fortune in it over many years and lost it when the machine proved too fragile and fussy to compete. The winning idea came from a different direction: instead of handling old type at all, why not cast fresh type every time?","Ottmar Mergenthaler was born in 1854 in the kingdom of Württemberg in southern Germany. He trained as a watchmaker and clockmaker, a perfect apprenticeship for someone who would one day build a machine with thousands of tiny moving parts. In 1872, at the age of eighteen, he emigrated to the United States and went to work in a relative's instrument workshop, which soon settled in Baltimore, Maryland, making scientific instruments and models for inventors' patents.","In 1876 Mergenthaler was approached by James O. Clephane, a court stenographer (a person who records everything said in a courtroom), and his partners, who wanted a faster way to produce printed copies of legal papers. That project led him to the problem of typesetting. For years he built and abandoned prototypes. By 1884 he had settled on the key idea: the machine would assemble a row of small moulds for an entire line of text, then pour molten metal into them to cast the whole line as one solid bar.","In 1886 he had a working machine and founded a company to make it. A group of newspaper publishers had helped pay for the development, among them Whitelaw Reid, the publisher of the New-York Tribune. So it was in the Tribune's composing room in July 1886 that the first Linotype went to work on a real daily newspaper. The same year, the Tribune used it to set a book, The Tribune Book of Open-Air Sports, the first book ever set on a Linotype.","The name explained the trick: the machine produced a line of type all at once. You will often read that someone in the Tribune office exclaimed that it made a line o' type, giving the machine its name. That is a popular story, but there is no firm record of who said it first, so it is best treated as a legend.","The Tribune was delighted. By 1889 it had 42 Linotypes working at once. Other papers rushed to buy them, and soon the clatter of Linotypes filled composing rooms around the world. Mergenthaler, sadly, did not enjoy his success for long. He clashed with the newspaper investors running the company and was pushed aside. He fell ill with tuberculosis and died in Baltimore in 1899, aged only 45."]},{"heading":"Inside a Linotype","paragraphs":["A Linotype looks like a cross between a giant typewriter, a pipe organ and a small factory. It stands taller than an adult and has thousands of parts. One early model, the Square Base Linotype, had about 5,000 parts and cost about 1,000 dollars, a large sum at the time. Here is what happens when an operator sits down at it.","The operator faces a keyboard of 90 keys. Above it sits a slanted metal box called the magazine, divided into long channels. Each channel holds a stack of matrices, small flat pieces of brass with the shape of a letter cut into one edge. Remember the matrices from Gutenberg's hand mould? These are the same idea, a mould for a letter, but now hundreds of them are stored inside a machine.","When the operator presses a key, say the letter e, a little gate opens at the bottom of the e channel and one brass matrix drops out. It slides down onto a moving belt and lines up next to the previous letter in a holder called the assembler. When the operator presses the space key, the machine drops in a spaceband instead: two thin wedges of steel sliding against each other.","When the line is almost full, the operator pulls a lever and the machine takes over. The spacebands are pushed upward. Because they are wedge-shaped, they get wider as they rise, spreading the words apart until the line fills exactly the right width. That is justification, the job hand compositors used to do by slipping in tiny spaces one by one, now done in a second.","Next comes the hot part. Behind the line of matrices sits a pot of molten type metal, an alloy mostly of lead with some antimony and tin, kept at about 280 degrees Celsius. A plunger squirts the liquid metal into a mould pressed against the line of matrices. In moments it cools and hardens into a single metal bar with a whole line of raised, backwards letters along its top edge. This bar is called a slug. The machine trims it and drops it onto a tray, next to the slug before it. Line by line, a column of slugs grows into a page.","The cleverest part of all happens last. An arm lifts the used matrices to the top of the machine, onto a long bar called the distributor bar. Every matrix has a pattern of notched teeth along its top, a different pattern for each letter, like a key. As the matrices slide along the bar, each one stays hanging until it reaches the spot where the bar's ridges no longer match its teeth. There it drops neatly into its own channel in the magazine, ready to be used again. The machine sorts its own letters, solving the problem that defeated so many earlier inventors.","A Linotype let one operator set type roughly three to five times faster than a hand compositor. And because every slug was brand new, the letters were always sharp. If the operator made a mistake, though, there was a catch: the whole line had to be cast again. After printing, the slugs were simply melted down and the metal reused."]},{"heading":"Monotype: One Letter at a Time, by Paper Tape","paragraphs":["At almost the same time, another American inventor had a different idea. Tolbert Lanston was born in 1844 in Troy, Ohio, and later lived in Washington, D.C. In June 1887 he received patents for a typesetting system that came to be called the Monotype, meaning single type.","The Monotype split the job between two separate machines. The first was a keyboard. As the operator typed, the keyboard did not cast anything. Instead, it punched patterns of holes into a long paper tape, a bit like the rolls that play tunes on a player piano. The operator could work in a quiet office far from the hot metal.","The paper tape was then fed into the second machine, the caster. Jets of compressed air passing through the holes told the caster which letter to make next. The caster moved a frame full of matrices so that the right one sat above a mould, squirted in molten metal, and cast a single, separate letter. One by one, the letters were pushed into lines, with exactly the right spaces calculated in advance so every line came out justified.","Why build it this way? Because individual letters are easier to correct. If a word was wrong, a worker could pluck out a single letter with tweezers instead of recasting a whole line. That made the Monotype especially popular for books, scientific works, timetables and complicated tables of numbers, where accuracy and beautiful spacing mattered more than speed. Linotypes ruled newspapers; Monotypes were loved by book printers.","Lanston's company succeeded thanks to many helpers, especially the engineer John Sellers Bancroft, who greatly improved the machine. In Britain, the Monotype Corporation also became famous for typefaces. In 1932 it produced Times New Roman, designed for The Times of London, the same newspaper that had first used Koenig's steam press back in 1814. Some people call that punched paper tape an early step toward digital typesetting, since it stored text as a code of holes that a machine could read."]},{"heading":"Pictures Made of Dots","paragraphs":["Text was now fast. Pictures were another matter. Photography had been invented in the 1830s, but for decades newspapers could not print photographs at all. The problem is that a printing press puts down ink or no ink, black or white. A photograph is full of smooth grays, from pale silver to deep shadow. How can a press that only prints solid black make gray?","Until the 1880s, the answer was to copy photos by hand. Artists redrew a photograph onto a block of wood, and skilled engravers cut it into a printing block, using clusters of fine lines to suggest shading. Magazines such as Harper's Weekly employed teams of these artists. It worked, but it took time and the engraver's hand always changed the picture a little.","The solution is called the halftone. As early as 1852, the English photography pioneer William Henry Fox Talbot suggested using a fine screen, like a veil, to break an image into tiny pieces for printing. Over the next few decades many inventors experimented with the idea.","On 4 March 1880, the New York Daily Graphic printed a picture called A Scene in Shantytown, New York, showing simple wooden shacks on the edge of the city. It is generally described as the first halftone photograph printed in a newspaper. The Daily Graphic, founded in 1873, was the first American daily newspaper with illustrations every day, and its work on photo printing was led by Stephen Henry Horgan. The 1880 screen was crude and the picture looked rough, but it proved the idea worked.","Better methods quickly followed. In 1882 the German inventor Georg Meisenbach patented a process he called autotype and became the first to make relief halftones a commercial success. In the United States, Frederic Ives and the brothers Louis and Max Levy improved the process by producing high-quality glass screens ruled with crisscrossing lines.","Here is how a halftone works. The photograph is rephotographed through a screen covered with a fine grid of lines. The screen breaks the image into a pattern of dots, all evenly spaced. Where the picture is dark, the dots come out big and almost touch. Where it is light, the dots are tiny specks surrounded by white paper. The press only ever prints solid black dots, but your eye cannot see dots that small from normal reading distance, so it blends them together and sees smooth shades of gray. That is why the result is called a halftone.","You can test this yourself. Take a magnifying glass and look closely at a photograph in a printed newspaper or magazine. You will see the dots. Newspaper screens are fairly coarse, often around 85 lines of dots per inch, because cheap, absorbent newsprint makes ink spread. Glossy magazine paper can hold much finer screens with far more dots, which is why magazine photos look sharper."]},{"heading":"Newspapers Explode, and Jobs Change","paragraphs":["Put Linotypes, rotary presses, cheap wood-pulp paper and halftone photographs together, and you get the giant newspaper of around 1900. Big-city papers grew to dozens of pages, with special Sunday editions much thicker still. Some printed hundreds of thousands of copies a day. In 1890 the reformer Jacob Riis published How the Other Half Lives, a book about the crowded tenements of New York, and some of his photographs were reproduced as halftones, helping readers see poverty with their own eyes. Photojournalism, telling news with photographs, was born.","Advertising boomed alongside. Department stores bought whole pages to show their goods. Mail-order catalogues full of pictures let families on distant farms shop by post. Color lithography printed eye-catching posters and labels, and cheaper color printing on newspaper presses made something new possible: the color comic strip. In 1895 Richard Outcault's character known as the Yellow Kid began appearing in color in New York's Sunday papers, one of the ancestors of today's comics.","For workers, the new machines brought both loss and opportunity. Hand compositors feared that Linotypes would put them out of work, and in some shops jobs were cut. But newspapers were growing so fast that many compositors retrained as Linotype operators, and printers' unions bargained for better conditions. The productivity of the machines helped compositors win shorter working days, including a nine-hour day. Linotype operating also became a skilled trade that some women and deaf workers entered, since the work depended on sharp eyes and quick fingers rather than hearing.","Hot-metal typesetting ruled for nearly a century. Then computers arrived. In July 1978, The New York Times stopped setting its paper in hot metal and switched to a computer-based system, retiring its last 61 Linotypes. A few working Linotypes still clatter away in museums and small print shops, kept alive by enthusiasts who love the smell of hot metal.","But one invention from this story was about to become more important than ever. Senefelder's oil-and-water trick was about to become the backbone of almost all modern printing."]}],"deepDive":"Halftones are a beautiful example of science and perception working together. A printing press is essentially binary: at any point on the paper there is ink or there is not. A halftone fakes continuous tone using a property of human vision called spatial integration. The eye's lens and retina cannot resolve detail finer than about one arcminute at normal viewing distances, so when dots are smaller than that, the brain averages the light reflected from a small area. If 30 percent of a patch is covered with ink, it looks like a 30 percent gray. Halftone screens are described in lines per inch: coarse newsprint often uses around 85, while glossy magazine and book printing commonly use much finer screens. Finer screens give smoother pictures, but tiny dots on absorbent paper spread out and blur, an effect printers call dot gain, so each paper has a practical limit. In the early process, the photograph was rephotographed through a glass screen ruled with crossed lines a short distance in front of the film; optical effects of the screen turned bright areas into large dots and dark areas into small ones on the negative, which became the reverse on the printing plate. Today software does this mathematically. Typesetting machines raise a different debate: who should get credit, and was the Linotype really revolutionary? Mergenthaler's machine succeeded where dozens of earlier composing machines failed largely because it avoided distributing used type: it cast fresh type and melted the old. Yet it relied on the matrix idea that goes back to Gutenberg's hand mould, and its commercial success depended on investors like Whitelaw Reid and the huge market of daily newspapers. Labour historians also argue about its effects. Some compositors lost jobs, but newspaper growth absorbed many of them, and unions negotiated training and hours. Finally, Lanston's Monotype keyboard stored text as punched holes on paper tape, separate from the casting machine. Separating the input of text from its output is the same basic idea used in later phototypesetting and in digital publishing, which is why some historians see the Monotype as an ancestor of computer typesetting.","funFact":"The fine-grained limestone from Solnhofen in Bavaria, prized for lithography stones, is the same rock in which fossils of the feathered Archaeopteryx were found, which is why one species is named Archaeopteryx lithographica.","check":{"q":"In a Linotype machine, what is a slug?","a":"A solid metal bar with a whole line of type cast on it","wrong":["A single brass letter mould","A greasy crayon used to draw on stone","A roll of paper punched with holes"]},"sources":[{"title":"Alois Senefelder","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alois_Senefelder"},{"title":"Chromolithography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromolithography"},{"title":"The Linotype: The Machine that Revolutionized Movable Type","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/headlinesandheroes/2022/06/the-linotype-the-machine-that-revolutionized-movable-type/"},{"title":"Ottmar Mergenthaler's Square Base Linotype","publisher":"ASME","url":"https://www.asme.org/about-asme/engineering-history/landmarks/235-ottmar-mergenthalers-square-base-linotype"},{"title":"Linotype machine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linotype_machine"},{"title":"Tolbert Lanston","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tolbert_Lanston"},{"title":"The Daily Graphic","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Daily_Graphic"},{"title":"Halftone","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halftone"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/16-1.jpg","alt":"Engraved portrait of Alois Senefelder (1771–1834), the German inventor of lithography.","file":"Alois Senefelder 1.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alois_Senefelder_1.jpg","author":"Ephraïm Conquy","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/16-2.jpg","alt":"A Mergenthaler Linotype Model 8 typesetting machine in a museum, with its keyboard in front and the sloping type magazine above.","file":"Mergenthaler Linotype Model 8 Lino compose machine on display at NMST.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mergenthaler_Linotype_Model_8_Lino_compose_machine_on_display_at_NMST.jpg","author":"Tausheef Hassan Auntu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/16-3.jpg","alt":"\"A Scene in Shantytown, New York\" from the Daily Graphic, 4 March 1880 — one of the first halftone photos printed in a newspaper.","file":"A Scene in Shantytown, New York (1880).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Scene_in_Shantytown,_New_York_(1880).png","author":"The Daily Graphic","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3921},{"n":17,"title":"How Modern Printing Works","when":"1900 – today","summary":"A printer in New Jersey made a mistake that turned out to be brilliant: ink bounced onto a rubber sheet and printed better than before. Soon four inks were mixing into every color you can imagine. Then an inventor used static electricity to copy pages, and lasers and tiny ink drops brought printing right onto your desk.","sections":[{"heading":"The Mistake That Made Offset","paragraphs":["Imagine a small print shop in New Jersey in the early 1900s. The machine is thumping along, sheet after sheet, when the person feeding paper misses one. Nobody notices for a moment. The press keeps turning, and with no paper in the way, the inked plate presses its picture straight onto the soft rubber that was supposed to sit behind the paper. Then the next sheet goes through and gets printed on both sides at once: by the plate on the front, and by the rubber on the back.","That is the story usually told about Ira Washington Rubel, an American printer who made lithographic prints on paper. When he looked at the accidental sheet, he saw something surprising. The picture that came from the rubber was sharper and cleaner than the one that came directly from the metal plate. Rubber is soft and squishy, so it pressed the ink into every little hollow of the paper's surface, while the hard plate only touched the high bumps.","Rubel understood what this meant. Instead of printing straight from the plate, he could print on purpose in two steps: plate to rubber, rubber to paper. The ink is 'set off' (passed along) from one surface to the next, which is why this way of printing is called offset printing. Most books describe his discovery as happening around 1903 or 1904, although different sources give slightly different years, so it is safest to say 'about 1904'. With a partner he built a press with three cylinders, and by about 1907 Rubel offset presses were working in San Francisco.","As with so many inventions in this story, Rubel was not completely alone. Back in 1875 an Englishman named Robert Barclay had patented an offset press for printing on tin, the shiny metal used for biscuit boxes and tea cans. You cannot easily press a hard metal plate against a hard tin sheet, so Barclay used a softer cylinder covered in cardboard to carry the picture across. In Europe, a printer named Kašpar Hermann developed offset presses for paper at about the same time as Rubel and started printing on one in Germany in 1907. So the fair way to tell it is that offset printing on paper grew up on both sides of the Atlantic around 1904, with Rubel's happy accident as its most famous moment.","Why did this matter so much? Remember Alois Senefelder from the last chapter, who discovered in the 1790s that you could print from a flat stone using the fact that oil and water do not mix. Lithography was wonderful for pictures, but heavy stones were slow and awkward. Offset printing took Senefelder's oil-and-water trick, put it on a thin metal plate wrapped around a fast spinning cylinder, and added the rubber 'blanket' in the middle. The result was a way of printing that was fast, cheap and gentle on the plate. It soon became the most important printing method in the world, and it still prints most of the books, magazines, posters and leaflets you have ever held."]},{"heading":"Oil, Water and a Rubber Blanket","paragraphs":["It starts with the plate. A modern offset plate is a thin, flexible sheet, usually of aluminum, about as thick as a few sheets of paper. It is completely flat. There are no raised letters like Gutenberg's type and no carved hollows. Instead, the plate's surface is treated so that it has two kinds of areas. The image areas, where the letters and pictures will be, attract oily ink. The non-image areas, which should stay blank, love water instead.","Here is the clever chemistry. Ink is greasy, a bit like thick paint mixed with oil. Water and oil repel each other: think of drops of salad oil floating on vinegar and refusing to mix. On the press, the plate cylinder first passes under dampening rollers that spread a very thin film of water (printers call it fountain solution, water with a few additives) across the plate. The water sticks only to the blank areas. Then the plate passes under the inking rollers. The ink tries to stick everywhere, but where there is already a film of water it is pushed away. It can only grab hold of the image areas. In a fraction of a second, the flat plate is carrying a perfect inked picture of the page, with clean wet spaces in between.","Next, the plate cylinder rolls against the blanket cylinder, which is wrapped in a rubber blanket. The inked picture transfers onto the rubber. Notice something fun: on the plate the words read the right way round, on the blanket they are reversed like writing seen in a mirror, and when the blanket presses onto the paper they are turned the right way round again. That double flip means plate makers do not have to think backwards the way Gutenberg's punch cutters did.","Finally the paper passes between the blanket cylinder and a hard impression cylinder that squeezes it against the rubber. The ink jumps from the blanket to the paper, and out comes a printed sheet. Because the plate never touches the paper, it wears out slowly, and the soft blanket can print well even on slightly rough paper.","In the old days, plates were made photographically: a film negative of the page was laid on a light-sensitive plate and exposed to strong light, which changed which parts would accept ink. Today most print shops use computer-to-plate machines, known as CTP. A laser draws the page directly onto the plate from a computer file, with no film at all.","There are two main families of offset press. Sheet-fed presses swallow single sheets of paper, one after another, and are used for shorter jobs such as brochures, posters, art books and letterheads. Web-fed presses, sometimes called web offset, print on huge rolls of paper that unwind in a continuous ribbon, like Bullock's web press from the 1860s but much faster. They are used for newspapers and big magazine runs. The biggest web presses can race paper through at more than 900 meters per minute and turn out tens of thousands of newspapers in an hour; some are rated at up to about 80,000 cut copies an hour. For newspapers the ink simply soaks into the soft newsprint. For glossy magazines, the paper rushes through hot ovens to dry the ink before it is cut and folded."]},{"heading":"Four Inks, Every Color","paragraphs":["Pick up a colorful comic, cereal box or magazine and look closely at a photograph with a magnifying glass. The smooth picture will dissolve into a crowd of tiny dots in just four colors: cyan (a bright sky blue), magenta (a hot pinkish purple), yellow and black. Printers call this CMYK. The K stands for 'key', the old printers' name for the black plate that carried the fine details and outlines that the other colors were lined up against.","How can four inks make thousands of colors, including greens, oranges, browns and skin tones? The answer is a kind of science called subtractive color. White paper reflects all the colors of light back to your eye. Each ink works like a filter that soaks up, or subtracts, part of that light. Cyan ink absorbs red light, magenta absorbs green light, and yellow absorbs blue light. Print yellow on top of cyan and between them they soak up both blue and red, so the only light bouncing back to your eye is green. Magenta plus yellow leaves red. All three together soak up almost everything and give a very dark color.","This is the opposite of how a screen works. Your phone or computer makes colors by adding light from tiny red, green and blue glowing dots, which is called additive color. On a screen, red plus green light makes yellow, which surprises most people the first time they hear it. Paper cannot glow, so printers must work by taking light away instead.","So why add black at all, if cyan, magenta and yellow together make a dark color? Real inks are not perfect filters. Stacked up, the three colored inks usually make a muddy dark brown rather than a crisp black. They also pile a lot of wet ink onto one spot, which takes longer to dry and can make thin paper wrinkle or tear. Black ink is cheaper, darker and sharper, which is especially important for small text. Designers sometimes print a 'rich black' by putting some colored ink underneath the black, to make large dark areas look deep and velvety.","The second trick is the halftone, which you met in the last chapter. Each of the four colors is printed as a grid of dots that are bigger in some places and smaller in others. Where the magenta dots are tiny and spread apart, you see mostly white paper with a hint of pink. Where they are big and nearly touching, you see strong magenta. Without halftones, four inks could only make eight solid colors (each ink on or off, in every combination, plus white paper). With dots of different sizes, they can fool your eye into seeing a smooth rainbow.","To make this work, each color gets its own plate, and the paper passes through four printing units one after another, getting each layer of dots in turn. The grids of dots for each color are tilted at different angles so that they do not line up in an ugly way and create wavy patterns. Under a magnifying glass, the overlapping dots form little flower-like clusters that printers call rosettes. Everything has to be lined up, or 'in register', to a fraction of a millimeter. If you have ever seen a cheap comic with a blurry ghost of color sticking out beside a face, you were looking at a picture that slipped out of register."]},{"heading":"Flexo, Gravure and Screens","paragraphs":["Offset is king for books and magazines, but it is not the only kind of modern printing. Look around a supermarket and you will see the work of several other methods, each good at a different job.","Flexography, or flexo for short, is the champion of packaging. It is really a modern, speedy cousin of Gutenberg's letterpress: the image stands up as raised parts on a plate, which is inked and pressed onto the material. The difference is that flexo plates are flexible, made of rubber or a kind of plastic called photopolymer, so they can be wrapped around spinning cylinders and print on bumpy or slippery surfaces such as plastic bags, foil wrappers, cardboard boxes, labels and milk cartons. A special roller called an anilox roller, covered in thousands of tiny engraved cells, delivers exactly the right amount of ink to the plate, and a thin metal 'doctor blade' scrapes off any extra. The first patented press of this kind was built in Liverpool in 1890, but its ink smeared so badly that people nicknamed the machine 'Bibby's Folly'. Early versions were called aniline printing after the dyes in their ink, and the friendlier name 'flexography' was chosen in 1951.","Gravure works the opposite way. Instead of raised letters, the picture is cut into the surface of a copper-plated cylinder as millions of tiny hollow cells. The cylinder spins through a bath of runny ink, a doctor blade wipes the smooth surface clean, and ink stays only inside the cells. When paper is pressed against the cylinder, it pulls the ink out of the cells. Deeper or wider cells hold more ink and print darker tones, so gravure can make very rich, smooth pictures. Making the engraved cylinders is expensive, with a separate cylinder for every color, so gravure only makes sense for enormous jobs: catalogues, magazines printed in hundreds of thousands or millions of copies, wallpaper, wrapping films and some postage stamps. Modern rotary gravure grew out of work in the 1890s by the Czech inventor Karel Klíč and his partners in Lancaster, England.","Screen printing is the method behind most printed T-shirts, as well as many posters and signs. A fine mesh is stretched tight across a frame, and a stencil blocks some of the holes. Ink is poured on top and a rubber squeegee drags it across the screen, pushing ink through the open holes onto the fabric below. One screen is needed for each color. The mesh used to be made of silk, which is why people still sometimes call it silkscreen printing. Stencil printing has ancient roots in Asia, and in the 1960s artists such as Andy Warhol made screen printing famous in the art world with bright, repeated images of soup cans and film stars."]},{"heading":"10-22-38 Astoria","paragraphs":["Now we leave the giant presses and meet a quiet man with sore hands. Chester Carlson was born in Seattle in 1906 and grew up poor. As a boy he loved printing so much that he made his own little newspaper. He studied physics at the California Institute of Technology, graduating in 1930, and then worked at Bell Laboratories and later in the patent department of an electronics company in New York.","Patent work meant copying. Every patent application needed several copies of long documents and drawings, and in the 1930s the choices were poor: retype everything, send it away to be photographed (slow and expensive), or use carbon paper, which only made a few blurry copies at a time. Carlson was also studying law at night, copying out pages of law books by hand, and he suffered from arthritis that made his hands ache. He began to dream of a machine that could make a copy of a page in seconds.","He read about a property of some materials called photoconductivity. A photoconductor acts like an insulator in the dark, holding onto static electricity, but becomes a conductor when light shines on it, letting the charge leak away. Carlson realized he could use this to make a picture out of static electricity. He started experimenting in his kitchen in Jackson Heights, Queens, which reportedly filled with the rotten-egg smell of burnt sulfur, and then rented a small room above a bar in Astoria, Queens, for a lab. In 1938 he hired a helper, Otto Kornei, an out-of-work Austrian engineer and physicist.","On 22 October 1938, about two weeks after Kornei joined him, they tried it. Kornei wrote '10-22-38 Astoria' in ink on a glass microscope slide. They coated a zinc plate with a thin layer of sulfur, a photoconductor, and rubbed it with a handkerchief to give it a static charge, the same way you can charge a balloon by rubbing it on your hair. They laid the slide on the plate and shone a bright lamp on it for a few seconds. Where light passed through the clear glass, the charge leaked away. Where the inked letters blocked the light, the charge stayed. Then they dusted the plate with fine powder, which clung only to the charged areas, and suddenly the words appeared. Carlson pressed a sheet of waxed paper on top, warmed it so the wax melted around the powder, and peeled away the world's first xerographic copy.","Carlson called his process electrophotography. He was granted a key United States patent in 1942. But almost nobody wanted it. Over several years, many large companies, including some of the biggest names in American business, turned him down; it looked like a messy curiosity, and carbon paper seemed good enough. Finally, in 1944, a nonprofit research organization called the Battelle Memorial Institute agreed to help develop it, and in the late 1940s a small photographic paper company in Rochester, New York, called Haloid, took it on. A professor of classical languages suggested a new name from Greek: xerography, from 'xeros' meaning dry and 'graphein' meaning to write, because unlike photography it needed no wet chemicals.","Haloid's first copier, the Model A of 1949, was clumsy. Making one copy took many separate manual steps, so it was used mostly for making printing plates. The real breakthrough was the Xerox 914, unveiled in 1959 and delivered to customers from 1960. It was the first fully automatic office copier that used ordinary paper: you put your original on the glass, pressed a button, and a copy slid out. It weighed about 650 pounds (nearly 300 kilograms) and, famously, came with a small fire extinguisher, because the hot parts that fused the toner could set paper alight. Offices loved it anyway. It sold far beyond the company's hopes, Haloid renamed itself Xerox, and 'to xerox' became an everyday verb meaning to photocopy.","In a modern copier the same steps happen on a spinning drum or belt coated with a photoconductor. A thin wire with a high voltage, called a corona wire, sprays an even electric charge over the drum. Light reflected from the original page hits the drum; white parts of the page reflect light and wipe out the charge, while dark letters leave charged areas behind. Toner, a powder of tiny plastic and pigment particles, is given the opposite charge so it clings only to the charged picture. A sheet of paper is charged to pull the toner across, then squeezed between hot rollers that melt the toner into the paper. That is why copies and laser prints feel warm when they come out. Carlson became very wealthy from his invention and gave most of his fortune away to charity before he died in 1968."]},{"heading":"Laser Beams and Flying Drops","paragraphs":["Xerography copied pages that already existed. But by the late 1960s, computers were creating text that had never been on paper at all. How could a computer draw a page onto a copier drum? A young optics scientist at Xerox named Gary Starkweather had an idea: use a laser beam as a super-fast pencil of light.","Starkweather built a rough prototype at Xerox's research lab in Webster, New York, around 1969, but his managers were not convinced. In 1971 he moved to Xerox's new Palo Alto Research Center in California, known as Xerox PARC, a lab bursting with ideas about the computers of the future. There, using a Xerox copier as the starting point, he built a working machine he called SLOT, the scanning laser output terminal. Over the next few years his improved designs became the basis of Xerox's first laser printer for businesses.","Here is how a laser printer draws. Inside, a tiny laser blinks on and off millions of times a second as the computer sends it the page, dot by dot. The beam hits a mirror with several flat sides spinning at high speed, which sweeps it across the charged photoconductor drum in a straight line, like a flashlight beam swept across a wall. Each sweep draws one thin line of dots. The drum turns a tiny step and the next line is drawn. Wherever the laser hits, the electric charge changes, so the drum ends up carrying an invisible static picture of the page. From there the steps are just like Carlson's copier: toner sticks to the pattern, is pulled onto the paper and is melted in place by hot rollers. A laser printer is really a photocopier that copies a page from a computer's memory instead of from a piece of paper.","Xerox launched the 9700, a big, fast laser printer for offices and businesses, in 1977, and it became one of the most profitable products the company ever made. Then laser printers shrank. In May 1984, Hewlett-Packard introduced the LaserJet, a desktop laser printer that cost 3,495 US dollars and printed 8 pages a minute at 300 dots per inch. That price sounds high, but it was cheap compared with earlier office machines. HP went on to sell more than 100 million LaserJets by 2006. A year later, the Apple LaserWriter, using a similar engine made by the Japanese company Canon, helped start the desktop publishing revolution you will read about in Chapter 19. Starkweather was inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2012 and died in 2019.","Meanwhile, other engineers were working on a completely different idea: why not just squirt the ink? Inkjet printers do exactly that, firing drops of liquid ink straight onto the paper with no drum, no toner and no plate. Most home inkjets use thermal inkjet technology. Behind each tiny nozzle is a microscopic heater. When the printer sends a pulse of electricity, the heater warms a little ink for a few millionths of a second, so fast that it boils into a tiny bubble. The bubble swells and shoots a droplet out of the nozzle; when it collapses, fresh ink is sucked in to refill the chamber, ready to fire again. Canon calls its version Bubble Jet. According to a story the company tells, a Canon engineer, Ichiro Endo, noticed in 1977 that ink spurted out of a syringe that had accidentally been touched by a hot soldering iron. At almost the same time, a team at Hewlett-Packard was developing the same idea, and HP sold an early thermal inkjet printer, the ThinkJet, in 1984.","Other inkjets, such as many made by Epson, use piezoelectric crystals instead of heat. These special crystals bend slightly when electricity is applied, squeezing the ink chamber like a tiny hand squeezing a water pistol. Because they do not boil the ink, they can use a wider range of inks, which is why piezo heads are popular in factories that print on fabric, tiles or packaging.","The numbers in an inkjet are astonishingly small. A single droplet may hold just one to five picoliters. A picoliter is one trillionth of a liter; a single raindrop could hold tens of thousands of them. A print head can have hundreds or thousands of nozzles, each firing thousands of times per second. Color inkjets use the same CMYK idea as printing presses, often with extra light cyan and light magenta inks for smoother photos, and they arrange their drops in patterns that work like halftone dots."]},{"heading":"Printing Is Everywhere","paragraphs":["Take a moment to look around the room you are in. The book on the shelf was probably printed by offset lithography, its pages rolling off a big press as part of a large sheet folded into sections, just like the folios and octavos of the old hand press. The cereal box and the crisp packet were probably printed by flexography. A glossy catalogue might be gravure. The band T-shirt is likely screen printed. The homework sheet or the boarding pass came from a laser printer or an inkjet. Even the price label at the supermarket may have come from yet another kind of printer that uses heat to darken special paper.","Each of these methods connects back to ideas we have already met. Offset uses Senefelder's oil-and-water discovery. Flexo is a descendant of raised type and woodblocks. Gravure grew out of engraving, where artists cut lines into copper plates. CMYK color depends on the halftone dots invented in the 1800s. And laser printers and copiers are built on Chester Carlson's static electricity, controlled by computers. Modern printing is not one invention but a whole toolbox, built by many people in many countries over more than a century.","By the end of the 1900s, printing could do things Gutenberg could never have imagined: millions of full-color copies in a night, a copy of any page in seconds, and a printer on almost every office desk and in many homes. Newspapers could reach millions of readers before breakfast. Anyone with a computer could print a party invitation, a school report or a homemade comic.","But every powerful tool can be used well or badly, and printing's power had a shadow side. The same presses that spread knowledge could also spread lies and hate. Governments could burn books as well as print them. Printers breathed poisonous fumes, forests were cut down for paper, and some of the books in the world's libraries were quietly crumbling on their shelves. Printing had become fast, colorful and everywhere, but all that power also brought serious problems."]}],"deepDive":"Offset lithography rests on surface chemistry rather than shape. A modern aluminum plate is grained and anodized so that the bare metal oxide is hydrophilic (it attracts water), while the image areas carry a hydrophobic coating that attracts greasy ink. The dampening system lays down a water film only a micrometer or so thick; too much water and the ink emulsifies and looks washed out, too little and ink creeps into the blank areas, a fault printers call scumming. Balancing ink and water is still a core skill of a press operator, even with computer controls. The rubber blanket matters because it is compressible: it conforms to the paper surface, which is why offset prints well on textured or cheap paper and why the plate itself lasts for tens or even hundreds of thousands of impressions. CMYK printing is a practical compromise. Ideal subtractive primaries would each absorb exactly one third of the visible spectrum, but real pigments leak and overlap, so the full range of printable colors (the gamut) is smaller than what a good screen can show. Bright greens, deep blues and neon colors are especially hard, which is why packaging often adds spot inks and why some high-end printers use six or more process colors. Halftone screens are rotated, traditionally by about 30 degrees between the strongest colors, to avoid moiré, the shimmering interference pattern produced when two regular grids overlap. Newer stochastic or frequency-modulated screening scatters tiny dots of equal size randomly and avoids the problem altogether. On the history side, the invention of offset is a classic case of simultaneous discovery: Barclay's tin-printing offset press of 1875 came first, and Rubel in the United States and Hermann in Europe both brought offset to paper around 1904, so historians usually avoid naming a single inventor. Xerography likewise built on earlier physics, including the Hungarian physicist Pál Selényi's experiments with electrostatic images, but Carlson combined photoconductivity, electrostatics and dry powder into the first practical dry copying process.","funFact":"The Xerox 914, the first automatic plain-paper office copier, was shipped with a small fire extinguisher because its hot fusing parts could set paper on fire.","check":{"q":"In offset printing, where does the ink go between the metal plate and the paper?","a":"Onto a rubber blanket, which then prints onto the paper","wrong":["Through a fine silk screen","Into tiny cells engraved in a copper cylinder","Into a bubble heated by a tiny electric heater"]},"sources":[{"title":"Offset printing","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Offset_printing"},{"title":"Light and Static: The Origins of the Xerox Copy","publisher":"The Henry Ford","url":"https://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/articles/xerox"},{"title":"Gary K. Starkweather","publisher":"National Inventors Hall of Fame","url":"https://invent.org/inductees/gary-k-starkweather"},{"title":"Xerography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xerography"},{"title":"Flexography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flexography"},{"title":"Rotogravure","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotogravure"},{"title":"Inkjet printing","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inkjet_printing"},{"title":"Chester Carlson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chester_Carlson"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/17-1.jpg","alt":"How offset printing works (labels in Japanese): the plate cylinder inks the rubber blanket cylinder, which presses the image onto paper over the impression cylinder.","file":"Offset print ja.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Offset_print_ja.png","author":"Zeichner und Fotograf: Lothar Spurzem The original uploader was Spurzem at…","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 de"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/17-2.jpg","alt":"A cat picture made of cyan, magenta, yellow and black halftone dots; the zoomed box shows the tiny overlapping dots.","file":"Cmyk AM screen.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cmyk_AM_screen.png","author":"Yamo74","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/17-3.jpg","alt":"A Xerox 914, the first plain-paper office copier (1959), on display at the Museum of Business History and Technology in Delaware.","file":"Museumofbusinesshistoryandtechnology (32000833342).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museumofbusinesshistoryandtechnology_(32000833342).jpg","author":"Marcin Wichary from San Francisco, U.S.A.","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4307},{"n":18,"title":"The Dark Side of Print","when":"1500s – today","summary":"Printing spread wonderful ideas, but it could also spread lies, and some rulers answered books with bonfires. Printers breathed poison from their lead letters, forests became paper, and some old library books are slowly crumbling to dust. This chapter looks at the problems printing caused and how people have fought to fix them.","sections":[{"heading":"Bonfires and Banned Books","paragraphs":["So far, most of our story has been about printing as a hero: spreading Bibles, science, news and stories to millions of people. But a fair history has to look at the shadows too. The very thing that made the press so powerful, its ability to put the same words in front of thousands of readers, was exactly what made people in charge nervous. A handwritten pamphlet might reach a few friends. A printed one could reach a whole country before anyone could stop it.","Almost as soon as printing spread, rulers and church leaders tried to control it. You met the Catholic Church's Index of Prohibited Books in Chapter 13. Protestant rulers, kings and city councils made their own rules too. In many countries, printers needed a licence from the government, and books had to be checked by an official censor before they could be sold. Printing a forbidden book could mean losing your press, paying a huge fine, going to prison or worse.","Some printers paid with their lives. Étienne Dolet was a French scholar and printer in Lyon who published works that the authorities considered heretical. He was arrested several times and in 1546 he was executed in Paris, and his books were burned with him. In 1889 a statue of him was put up in Paris on the spot where he died, honoring him as a martyr for free thought, although it was melted down during the Second World War. In England, the translator William Tyndale, whose English New Testament had to be printed abroad and smuggled in, was executed in 1536.","In colonial New York, a German-born printer named John Peter Zenger was thrown in jail in 1734 because his newspaper criticized the colony's governor. At his trial in 1735, his lawyer argued that printing something true should not be a crime, and the jury agreed and set him free. The case became a famous step on the road to freedom of the press in America.","Burning books is one of the oldest ways of trying to destroy ideas. One of the most notorious book burnings in history happened in Germany on 10 May 1933, a few months after the Nazi Party came to power. It was organized mainly by the German Student Association, as the high point of a campaign it called 'Against the Un-German Spirit'. Students raided libraries and bookshops for books by Jewish writers, political opponents and anyone the Nazis disliked. Bonfires were lit in more than 20 university towns that night. In Berlin, on a square called the Opernplatz (today Bebelplatz), about 20,000 books were burned in front of a crowd of about 40,000 people, and Propaganda Minister Joseph Goebbels gave a speech that was broadcast on the radio. The books thrown into the flames included works by the psychologist Sigmund Freud, the novelists Thomas Mann, Stefan Zweig and Erich Maria Remarque, the poet Bertolt Brecht, and even the American writers Helen Keller, Jack London and Ernest Hemingway.","More than a century earlier, the German poet Heinrich Heine, who was himself Jewish-born, had written a line in his 1821 play 'Almansor' that is often quoted about that night: 'Where they burn books, they will in the end also burn people.' Sadly, his warning came true in the Holocaust. Today, in the middle of Bebelplatz in Berlin, there is a memorial by the artist Micha Ullman: a glass window in the ground through which you look down into an underground room of empty white bookshelves, with space for about the number of books that were burned. Heine's words are on a plaque nearby.","Censorship did not end in 1945. In many countries today, writers and journalists are still jailed for what they print, and books are still banned from libraries and schools. That is why so many people argue that the freedom to print and to read is worth protecting carefully."]},{"heading":"Printed Lies and the Moon Hoax","paragraphs":["A printing press does not know whether the words in it are true. It will print a lie just as neatly as a fact, and often a lie spreads faster, because it is more exciting. From the very beginning of print, people used it for propaganda: one-sided messages designed to change what people think, often by stirring up fear or anger.","During the Reformation, both sides printed woodcut cartoons showing their enemies as monsters or devils. During wars, governments printed posters to make soldiers and citizens hate the other side. Some printed material was far worse than unfair. In the 1400s and 1500s, printed pamphlets spread false stories accusing Jewish communities of terrible crimes, and these lies fed violence against real people. In the early 1900s, a forged document called 'The Protocols of the Elders of Zion' was printed in Russia, claiming to reveal a secret Jewish plan to rule the world. In 1921 the London newspaper The Times showed that large parts of it had been copied from an older French satire that had nothing to do with Jewish people at all. Even after it was proved to be a fake, it was printed again and again in many languages, and the Nazis used it in their propaganda. It is one of the clearest examples of how a printed lie can do terrible harm long after it has been exposed.","Not every printed lie is so dark. Some were hoaxes, made to fool readers and sell papers. The most famous is the Great Moon Hoax. In the summer of 1835, New York's 'The Sun', one of the new cheap 'penny papers' that sold for a single cent, began a series of six articles starting on 25 August. They claimed to be reprinted from a respected Scottish science journal and reported that the real British astronomer Sir John Herschel, working in South Africa, had used an enormous new telescope to see life on the Moon.","The details were wonderfully vivid. The telescope's lens was said to be 24 feet (over 7 meters) across and to weigh 7 tons. Through it, the articles said, Herschel had seen forests, seas and beaches, herds of bison-like animals, goats with a single horn, small zebra-like creatures, beavers that walked on two legs and built huts, and finally furry winged people, the 'man-bats', who built temples. The series ended by claiming that the observations had stopped when sunlight was focused through the giant lens and set the observatory on fire.","None of it was true. Herschel was really in South Africa studying the southern sky, but he knew nothing about the articles. The author was almost certainly a Sun reporter named Richard Adams Locke, who admitted it in 1840. Many readers believed the story, at least for a while, and newspapers across the United States and Europe reprinted it. The Sun admitted in September 1835 that the articles were made up, but never printed a proper apology. Herschel was amused at first, and later rather annoyed at having to answer questions about his supposed man-bats.","Historians still argue about exactly how many people were fooled and how much the paper's sales rose; some popular retellings probably exaggerate both. But the hoax shows something important. In 1835, ordinary people were hungry for science news, and most could not check the facts for themselves. A cheap, fast newspaper with an exciting story could reach thousands of readers in a day. The same is true today, only much faster, which is why journalists, scientists and readers all have to ask: who wrote this, and how do they know?"]},{"heading":"Poison in the Letters","paragraphs":["Printers were often clever, well-read people, but their workshops could be dangerous places to spend a life. The biggest hidden danger was the type itself. Remember that type metal is mostly lead, mixed with tin and antimony. Lead is a poison. It does not make you sick all at once. Instead, it builds up in the body over months and years, causing stomach cramps (an old name was 'lead colic'), tiredness, headaches, weak muscles and, in serious cases, damage to the nerves and brain. Children are especially sensitive to it.","People noticed the danger early. The Italian doctor Bernardino Ramazzini, who wrote one of the first books about diseases caused by work, published around 1700, discussed the health problems of many trades. In 1786 Benjamin Franklin, who had started out as a printer, wrote a letter to his friend Benjamin Vaughan about lead. He remembered from his youth as a compositor in London in the 1720s that some printers who heated their type, for example by warming the cases near a fire, ended up with weak or painful hands, and he wrote about how long it can take for people to accept that a common substance is harmful.","Compositors picked up thousands of pieces of lead type every day with their bare fingers, and lead dust from the cases got onto their hands, their food and their clothes. Many ate and drank at their workbenches, which made it worse. Workers in type foundries, where type was cast, breathed fumes from pots of molten metal. After the Linotype arrived in the 1880s, every machine had its own little pot of hot metal bubbling beside the operator, and newspapers also melted down huge amounts of lead to cast curved printing plates for their rotary presses, a process called stereotyping.","In 1917 the American doctor Alice Hamilton, a pioneer of workplace health, and her colleague Charles Verrill published a major study called 'Hygiene of the Printing Trades' for the US government. They found lead poisoning among typesetters, metal melters and stereotypers, and called it one of the most serious problems in the industry. Their recommendations sound simple now: good ventilation, hoods over the metal pots, washrooms, separate places to eat and never eating with dirty hands. Slowly, safety rules and better habits made print shops healthier, and when hot metal type disappeared in the 1970s and 1980s, so did most of the lead.","Lead was not the only danger. Inks once contained poisonous pigments, including lead-based whites and yellows. Printers used powerful solvents to clean ink off rollers and plates, and breathing their vapors day after day could harm the liver, kidneys and nerves. As recently as the early 2010s, doctors in Japan discovered that an unusual number of young workers in the proof-printing department of a small printing company in Osaka had developed a rare cancer of the bile ducts. Investigators linked it to a cleaning solvent called 1,2-dichloropropane that the workers had breathed in heavy doses for years in a poorly ventilated room. The discovery led to new safety rules in Japan, and it reminded the world that the printing industry still has to watch the chemicals it uses very carefully."]},{"heading":"Forests, Water and Paper","paragraphs":["In Chapter 15 you saw how Friedrich Gottlob Keller's method of grinding wood into pulp made paper cheap enough for everyone. That was a triumph for readers, but it also meant that the world's appetite for paper would be fed by trees, in enormous numbers. Newspapers, magazines, books, catalogues, office paper, cardboard boxes and packaging all come from the same basic recipe of plant fibers and water.","Making paper from wood uses a lot of resources. Logs have to be cut, transported and either ground up or cooked with chemicals to separate the cellulose fibers from the lignin, the natural glue that holds wood together. Paper mills use huge amounts of water to carry the fibers, and a great deal of energy to run machines and dry the paper on rows of hot cylinders. The pulp and paper industry is one of the biggest industrial users of energy in many countries.","Then there is whiteness. Wood pulp is naturally brownish, the color of a paper grocery bag. To make bright white paper, mills bleach it. For much of the 1900s, many mills used chlorine gas for bleaching, and the waste water flowing into rivers contained harmful chemicals, including dioxins, which can build up in fish and animals. Scientists and campaigners raised the alarm in the 1980s, and since the 1990s most mills have switched to gentler methods such as chlorine dioxide or oxygen-based bleaches, and treat their waste water before releasing it. The rivers near many old mill towns are much cleaner now than they were fifty years ago.","Forests are the other big question. Cutting down old natural forests for pulp can destroy the homes of animals and plants. Today a large share of the wood used for paper comes from trees grown on purpose in managed plantations, or from leftover wood chips and sawdust from sawmills, and certification schemes such as the Forest Stewardship Council, founded in 1993, put labels on paper that comes from responsibly managed forests. Problems remain, but readers can help by choosing certified and recycled paper.","The best answer of all is to use paper again. Paper is one of the most recycled materials in the world. In recycling, old paper is soaked and churned in water until it falls apart back into pulp. Ink is removed by a process called deinking, often by blowing air bubbles through the pulp so that ink particles stick to the bubbles and float to the top as a gray foam that can be skimmed away. Then the clean fibers are made into new paper. Each time, the fibers get a little shorter and weaker, so they can usually be recycled only a handful of times (often quoted as about five to seven), after which they are too short to hold a sheet together. That is why some fresh fiber is usually mixed in.","The numbers are encouraging. In the United States in 2018, about 67 million tons of paper and cardboard were thrown away, and about 68 percent of it was recycled, the highest rate of any material in household trash. Corrugated cardboard boxes were recycled at a rate of over 96 percent. In Europe, the paper recycling rate reached about 79 percent in 2023. Recycling paper saves energy, water and trees, and it keeps paper out of landfills, where it can rot and release methane, a powerful greenhouse gas."]},{"heading":"The Slow Fire in the Library","paragraphs":["Here is a mystery that librarians discovered in the 1900s. In a great library, a book printed on rag paper in the 1500s might still have bright white, flexible pages. But a book printed in 1900, four hundred years younger, might have pages so yellow and brittle that a corner snaps off like a cracker when you turn it. What was going on?","The answer was acid. From about the middle of the 1800s until the 1980s, most paper was acidic. There were two main reasons. First, cheap paper made from ground wood still contained lignin and other substances that break down into acids, especially when exposed to light and heat. (This is why old newspapers left in the sun turn yellow so quickly.) Second, papermakers 'sized' paper, treating it so ink would not spread like on a paper towel, with a mixture of rosin and a chemical called alum. Alum is acidic, and over the years it helps create acid inside the paper.","Acid attacks the long cellulose molecules that give paper its strength, chopping them into shorter and shorter pieces. It works slowly and silently, which is why librarians call it the 'slow fire'. You cannot see flames, but year by year the paper loses its strength until it crumbles. Depending on how acidic the paper is and how warm and damp the shelves are, books can become too fragile to use in as little as 50 to 70 years. By the 1980s, a large share of the books at the Library of Congress in Washington, D.C., one of the biggest libraries in the world, were thought to be too brittle for normal use.","In the 1930s and later in the 1950s, an American document restorer named William Barrow carried out careful studies showing just how badly acidic book papers were decaying. His work helped convince papermakers to change. In the 1950s chemists developed new 'alkaline' sizing that did not need alum, and by the 1980s and 1990s acid-free paper had become normal for books. Good acid-free paper is expected to last for many centuries: around 500 years for average grades and perhaps more than 1,000 years for the best. Standards were written to define 'permanent paper', including an American standard in 1984 and the international standard ISO 9706 in 1994. Books printed on such paper may carry a tiny infinity symbol inside a circle, often on the copyright page. See if you can find one in a book near you.","But what about the millions of acidic books already on library shelves? Libraries fight back in several ways. They keep books in cool, dry, dark storerooms, because heat and damp speed up the decay. They copy the most fragile books onto microfilm or scan them into digital files so that the words survive even if the paper does not. And they use mass deacidification. The Library of Congress, for example, has treated millions of books with a process in which the books are placed in large cylinders and soaked in a liquid carrying fine particles of magnesium oxide. The particles neutralize the acid and leave behind an alkaline reserve, a sort of chemical shield that keeps neutralizing new acid for years to come. Treated paper is tested to make sure it lasts several times longer than untreated paper. Deacidification cannot make brittle pages strong again, so it works best before books start to crumble."]},{"heading":"When Machines Took the Jobs","paragraphs":["Every big change in printing helped millions of readers, but it also hurt the workers whose skills suddenly were not needed. This has happened again and again throughout the story.","The first to feel it were the scribes. In the late 1400s, some scribes and scholars complained loudly that the new presses were churning out careless, ugly, error-filled books for anyone with money. A Venetian friar, Filippo de Strata, wrote a protest to the city's ruler in the 1470s asking for printing to be restrained. The German abbot Johannes Trithemius wrote a work called 'In Praise of Scribes' in 1492, arguing that monks should keep copying by hand because copying was good for the soul and parchment would last longer than paper. With a touch of irony, his book was then printed so that more people could read it. Copying by hand did not vanish overnight; scribes kept making fine manuscripts for wealthy customers and documents for governments for many years. But as an everyday way of making books, hand copying slowly faded away.","Four hundred years later, it was the hand compositors' turn. When the Linotype and Monotype machines arrived in the 1880s and 1890s, one operator could set type several times faster than a person picking letters from a case. Many typesetters feared for their jobs, and some did lose them. But printing was growing so fast, with bigger newspapers and more magazines, that many compositors retrained as machine operators, and printers' unions became some of the strongest trade unions in the world.","Then, in the 1960s to the 1980s, phototypesetting and computers made hot metal type itself unnecessary. Now journalists could type their stories straight into a computer, and pages could be made without anyone setting metal at all. This led to some of the bitterest labor battles in printing history. The most famous was the Wapping dispute in London. In January 1986, Rupert Murdoch's company News International moved the printing of newspapers including The Times and The Sun to a new plant at Wapping in East London, using new computer technology. About 6,000 print workers went on strike and were dismissed. The dispute, with angry picket lines outside the new plant, lasted about a year before the strike collapsed in February 1987. The new plant needed only a fraction of the old number of printers. A whole craft, with centuries of skill and tradition behind it, had almost disappeared.","Historians and economists still argue about how to weigh up these changes. New technology usually made printed things cheaper and more plentiful, and created new kinds of jobs, from machine engineers to graphic designers. But for the particular people whose skills were replaced, especially older workers, the change could be very hard. The same questions are being asked today about computers and artificial intelligence."]},{"heading":"Pirates and Copycats","paragraphs":["If you can print a book, you can also print someone else's book without asking. From the earliest days of print, 'pirate' printers copied successful books and sold them cheaply, without paying the author or the original publisher anything. Soon after Aldus Manutius in Venice began his popular pocket-size books in italic type, printers in Lyon in France were turning out imitations. Martin Luther complained bitterly that pirate printers rushed out sloppy, error-filled copies of his writings, sometimes even before his own printer had finished. Readers did not always know which copy was the real one.","At first, the only protection was a 'privilege', special permission from a ruler that gave one printer the right to print a certain book for a few years. Later, countries began to write copyright laws. Britain's Statute of Anne of 1710, which you met in Chapter 14, gave the right to the author for a limited time. But laws stopped at the border. For much of the 1800s, American publishers freely reprinted books by British authors without paying them. Charles Dickens, whose novels were hugely popular in the United States, toured America in 1842 and spoke out for international copyright, to the annoyance of some American newspapers. Change came slowly: the Berne Convention, an international agreement on copyright, was signed in 1886, and the United States passed a law in 1891 that began to protect foreign authors.","Copyright is a balancing act. Authors, artists and publishers need to be paid, or they cannot afford to keep creating. But readers, students and scientists also need to be able to read, quote and build on what others have written, and copyright eventually ends so that old works become free for everyone. Arguments about where the line should be have never stopped. Photocopiers raised them in the 1960s and 1970s, and the internet has made copying easier than ever before.","Looking back over this chapter, the dark side of print is mostly the dark side of people. Printing is a tool. It can spread truth or lies, free thinkers or punish them, poison workers or be made safe, destroy forests or recycle them. Many of the problems we have met were eventually tackled by doctors, scientists, librarians, campaigners and ordinary readers who refused to accept them. And just as people were learning these lessons, a new tool arrived. Then computers arrived, and printing began to leave paper behind altogether."]}],"deepDive":"Paper decay is a chemistry problem called acid hydrolysis. Cellulose is a long chain of glucose units; in the presence of acid and moisture, the bonds linking those units break, shortening the chains and reducing fiber strength. Conservators measure this with fold-endurance tests, folding a strip back and forth until it breaks, and with pH tests. The two big sources of acid in industrial-era paper were alum-rosin sizing, introduced in the early 1800s, and lignin-rich groundwood pulp, whose breakdown products are acidic and which also yellows by oxidation in light. Mass deacidification systems, such as the magnesium oxide treatment used by the Library of Congress, aim to raise pH to slightly alkaline and leave an alkaline reserve of a few percent of calcium or magnesium carbonate or oxide; accelerated-aging tests suggest treated paper can last several times longer. Critics note that treatment cannot restore lost strength and can cause slight color changes, so it is a preventive measure rather than a cure. Lead poisoning in printing is a case study in occupational medicine: the harm was described anecdotally for centuries, from Ramazzini in 1700 to Franklin in 1786, but systematic statistical study came only with investigators such as Alice Hamilton in the early 1900s, and real reform followed only once evidence was gathered. Debates about printed misinformation are also old. Historians caution against assuming that readers of the past were simply gullible; the 1835 Moon Hoax, for instance, was read by some as entertainment, and claims about its effect on the Sun's circulation are probably inflated in popular retellings. Similarly, economists studying technological unemployment in printing, from Linotype to computer typesetting, generally find that output and total employment in publishing often grew while specific crafts shrank sharply, which is why both optimistic and pessimistic accounts of automation can point to printing for evidence.","funFact":"Many books printed on long-lasting acid-free paper carry a tiny infinity symbol inside a circle to show that the paper meets a permanence standard.","check":{"q":"Why are many books printed between about 1850 and 1980 turning yellow and brittle?","a":"Their paper is acidic, and the acid slowly breaks down the paper fibers","wrong":["They were printed with too much black ink","Bookworms eat the glue in the bindings","The pages were bleached with sunlight"]},"sources":[{"title":"Book Burning","publisher":"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, Holocaust Encyclopedia","url":"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning"},{"title":"Great Moon Hoax","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Moon_Hoax"},{"title":"Mass Deacidification","publisher":"Library of Congress","url":"https://loc.gov/preservation/scientists/projects/mass_deacid.html"},{"title":"Acid-free paper","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acid-free_paper"},{"title":"Paper and Paperboard: Material-Specific Data","publisher":"U.S. Environmental Protection Agency","url":"https://www.epa.gov/facts-and-figures-about-materials-waste-and-recycling/paper-and-paperboard-material-specific-data"},{"title":"Paper recycling","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paper_recycling"},{"title":"Wapping dispute","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wapping_dispute"},{"title":"Étienne Dolet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89tienne_Dolet"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/18-1.jpg","alt":"Nazi book burning on the Opernplatz in Berlin, 10 May 1933: crowds watch as banned books burn in a huge pile.","file":"Bundesarchiv Bild 102-14597, Berlin, Opernplatz, Bücherverbrennung.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597,_Berlin,_Opernplatz,_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg","author":"Pahl, Georg","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/18-2.jpg","alt":"\"Lunar Animals and Other Objects,\" an 1835 New York Sun print for the Great Moon Hoax, showing made-up winged man-bats and unicorns.","file":"Lunar animals and other objects LCCN2003665049.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lunar_animals_and_other_objects_LCCN2003665049.jpg","author":"Popular Graphic Arts","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/18-3.jpg","alt":"An old album whose pages have turned brown from acid in the paper — the same acid damage that makes old books yellow and brittle.","file":"Acid Migration on to book page.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acid_Migration_on_to_book_page.jpg","author":"Heritage Preservation Department - MNHS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":4081},{"n":19,"title":"From Desktop to Digital Publishing","when":"1950s – today","summary":"Computers changed printing forever. Metal letters were swapped for flashes of light, then for numbers inside a computer, and suddenly anyone with a desk could design pages. Then the World Wide Web let people publish to the whole planet in a second, and e-readers put whole libraries in a pocket.","sections":[{"heading":"Goodbye Hot Metal, Hello Light","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into a big newspaper building in the 1950s. Down on the composing room floor, rows of Linotype machines clatter like a thunderstorm. Each one has a pot of melted lead bubbling inside it, kept at around 300 degrees Celsius, and the air smells of hot metal and oil. Every line of every story is cast in metal, used once, and then melted down again. This was called hot metal typesetting, and it had ruled printing for about seventy years. But a quiet new idea was about to make all that heat and noise disappear.","The idea was simple to say and hard to build: instead of casting letters in metal, why not make them out of light? This is called phototypesetting. In France, two engineers named René Higonnet and Louis Moyroud, working in Lyon, built a machine they called the Lumitype. They first showed it in 1946. French companies were not very interested, so the two inventors found backers in the United States, where the machine was developed further and renamed the Photon.","Here is how a machine like the Photon worked. Inside was a spinning glass disc. Around its edge were tiny photographic negatives of every letter, a bit like little stencils made of film: the letter was clear and everything around it was dark. When the operator typed a letter, the machine waited for exactly the right moment as the disc spun, then fired a very short flash of light, like a camera flash. The light shone through the clear letter shape, through a lens, and onto light-sensitive photographic film or paper. Move the film a tiny bit, flash the next letter, and line by line a whole page of text appeared, sharp and black, without a single drop of molten metal.","The lens was a clever bonus. By changing the lens, the same letter on the disc could be made bigger or smaller. A hot metal print shop needed a different set of metal type for every size of every font, which meant tons of lead and huge cabinets. A phototypesetter could make many sizes from one disc.","In 1953 the Lumitype-Photon set its first complete book, a science book called The Wonderful World of Insects by Albro T. Gaul. Other companies soon raced in. Mergenthaler, the same company that had built the Linotype, made a photo machine called the Linofilm, and the Monotype company made one called the Monophoto. Engineers had even tried the idea earlier: in Japan, Nobuo Morisawa and a partner named Ishii had applied for a patent in 1924 for a phototypesetting machine for Japanese characters. As so often in this story, good ideas popped up in more than one place.","Why did phototypesetting win? The answer connects back to Senefelder's oil-and-water trick from Part 16. By the 1950s and 1960s more and more printers were switching to offset lithography, which prints from a thin, flat plate rather than raised metal letters. Offset printers did not need metal type at all. They needed a clean, sharp picture of the page that could be photographed onto a plate. Phototypesetting produced exactly that. People called it cold type, because no hot metal was involved, and it could even be done in an ordinary office.","During the 1960s and 1970s computers made phototypesetting faster still. Instead of a spinning disc, later machines drew letters on a screen, called a cathode ray tube, or later with a laser, and computer programs worked out where to break lines and how much space to put between words. Workers cut out the printed columns of text and glued them onto big boards with hot wax, a job called paste-up, and those boards were photographed to make the printing plates.","The old hot metal world slowly closed down. One famous farewell came at The New York Times, which printed its last edition set in hot metal in July 1978 and switched to computer typesetting. Many skilled Linotype operators, who could set type at amazing speeds, had to learn completely new jobs or retire. It was the same story we saw with scribes and hand compositors: a new machine made words cheaper to produce, and the people who had mastered the old way had to adapt."]},{"heading":"A Library Typed by Hand: Project Gutenberg","paragraphs":["Now let's jump to the University of Illinois in the summer of 1971. A student named Michael Hart had been given an account on a big computer called a Xerox Sigma V, a mainframe that filled a room in the university's Materials Research Lab. Computers back then were rare and expensive, and an account like his came with a lot of computer time. Hart wondered: what is the most valuable thing I could do with this?","His answer was surprising. He decided the most valuable thing a computer could store was not numbers or calculations, but books, because a book typed into a computer could be copied endlessly for free. The first text he chose was a copy of the United States Declaration of Independence. He typed it in himself and finished on 4 July 1971, Independence Day in the United States. That date is counted as the start of Project Gutenberg, the first free library of electronic books, or e-books.","The name was a tribute to Johannes Gutenberg, whom you met in Parts 8 to 10. Hart saw the computer as a new kind of printing press. Gutenberg's press had made copies cheap; a computer could make copies cost almost nothing at all. Once a book was typed in correctly, a million copies could be made as easily as one.","Hart's computer was connected to an early network called ARPANET, which later grew into the internet. At first he wanted to send the Declaration to everyone on the network as a message, but a colleague warned him that such a big message might jam the system. So instead he stored the file and told people where they could find it and copy it. It was a tiny file by today's standards, only about 5 kilobytes, less than a single small photo on a modern phone.","Project Gutenberg grew slowly at first. In 1972 Hart typed in the U.S. Bill of Rights, and in 1973 a volunteer typed in the U.S. Constitution. Volunteers typed in books of the Bible and works of Shakespeare. The rule was to use what Hart called Plain Vanilla ASCII, the simplest possible kind of computer text, so that any computer, old or new, cheap or expensive, could read the files. Where a printed book used italics or bold letters, the e-book used capital letters instead.","Project Gutenberg only shares books that are in the public domain. That means books whose copyright has run out, usually because they are old, so anyone is allowed to copy them. This links back to Part 14 and the Statute of Anne, which first gave authors rights for a limited time. Hart cared a lot about the public domain and worried when copyright terms were made longer, because that meant fewer old books could be shared freely.","Here is how the library grew, according to the project's own history. It reached 1,000 e-books in August 1997, 10,000 in October 2003, 20,000 in December 2006, and about 32,500 by early 2009. A big boost came in October 2000, when a volunteer named Charles Franks started Distributed Proofreaders, a website where thousands of people could each check a few scanned pages of a book against the typed text. Splitting the work into small pieces made it go much faster, a bit like the medieval pecia system from Part 7, where students copied sections of a book one piece at a time.","Today Project Gutenberg holds tens of thousands of free e-books, well over 70,000, from Alice's Adventures in Wonderland to Frankenstein, and anyone with an internet connection can read them. Michael Hart died in 2011, but the project he started by typing one famous document is still growing, run largely by volunteers."]},{"heading":"Desktop Publishing: A Print Shop on Your Desk","paragraphs":["Even with phototypesetting, making a professional-looking magazine or leaflet in the early 1980s still needed expensive machines and trained specialists. If your school club wanted a newsletter, you probably used a typewriter and a photocopier, and it looked like it. Then, in just a couple of years, three inventions came together and changed that completely. Paul Brainerd, one of the people involved, later described them as a three-legged stool: a computer, a printer language and a page-design program.","The first leg was the computer. In January 1984 Apple launched the Macintosh. Unlike most computers of the time, which showed only rows of green or white letters, the Mac had a graphical screen, a mouse and a choice of fonts. What you saw on the screen looked a lot like what would come out on paper. People called this WYSIWYG, pronounced 'wizzy-wig', short for 'what you see is what you get'.","The second leg was a language for describing pages, and its story starts at Xerox's famous research center, Xerox PARC, in California. In 1978 Charles Geschke set up a lab there to study imaging and hired John Warnock. Between 1979 and 1981 they and other researchers designed a printing language called Interpress. Xerox was slow to share it with the world, so in December 1982 Warnock and Geschke left and started their own company, Adobe. In 1984 Adobe released PostScript.","PostScript is a page description language. Instead of telling a printer 'put a black dot here, and here, and here', it describes the page in mathematical shapes: draw this letter at this size, at this angle, in this place; draw this line; fill this circle with gray. The printer then works out the dots for itself. Because everything is described with math, a PostScript page can be printed on a cheap office printer or on a top-quality machine with far more dots per inch, and it simply looks sharper on the better one.","The secret ingredient was how Adobe described the letters themselves. Each letter's outline was stored as a set of smooth curves called Bezier curves. A Bezier curve is defined by a few points: two end points and some control points that pull the curve into shape, a bit like bending a flexible ruler. With a handful of these curves you can describe the graceful shape of a letter 'g' or 'S', and then make it any size without it going jagged. Adobe also used clever tricks, called hinting, to make small letters look neat even on printers with fewer dots. Warnock kept parts of this secret for many years.","The third leg was the page-design program. Paul Brainerd, who had worked in newspapers, founded a company in 1984 named Aldus, after Aldus Manutius, the Venetian printer of pocket books and italic type you met in Part 12. In July 1985 Aldus released PageMaker. With PageMaker you could place columns of text, headlines and pictures on a page by dragging them around with a mouse. Brainerd is usually credited with coining the phrase desktop publishing.","The final piece was the Apple LaserWriter printer, announced on 23 January 1985, the same day Aldus announced PageMaker, and shipped that March. It had PostScript built in. It cost 6,995 US dollars, which was a lot of money, but it was a tiny fraction of the cost of professional typesetting equipment, and several Macs on a network could share one LaserWriter. Its laser printing, which you learned about in Part 17, put down about 300 dots per inch. For finer work, the same PostScript file could be sent to a professional imagesetter, a high-quality machine that made film for offset printing at far higher resolutions.","Suddenly small businesses, schools, churches, clubs and even kids could design pages that looked professionally printed. An Adobe engineer, Doug Brotz, later said PostScript 'democratized the print world', meaning it put printing power into the hands of ordinary people. In the 1990s Warnock pushed for another invention built on PostScript ideas: the PDF, or Portable Document Format, which lets a page look the same on any computer. Today people create PDFs in enormous numbers every year. If you have ever downloaded a worksheet or a ticket, you have probably used one."]},{"heading":"A Web Where Anyone Can Publish","paragraphs":["Desktop publishing let anyone design a page. The next invention let anyone share that page with the whole world, instantly, without paper at all. It was born at CERN, a huge physics laboratory on the border of Switzerland and France, where thousands of scientists from many countries study the tiniest particles in the universe.","In the late 1980s a British computer scientist at CERN named Tim Berners-Lee had a problem. Scientists came and went, used different kinds of computers, and stored their information in different ways. Finding a document, or knowing who had written what, was frustrating. In March 1989 Berners-Lee wrote a proposal for a system that would link documents on different computers together using hypertext. Hypertext is text with links in it: you click on a word and jump to another document. His boss did not say yes right away, but he let Berners-Lee keep working on the idea.","In 1990, using a powerful black NeXT computer, Berners-Lee wrote three key inventions that still run the web today. HTML, or HyperText Markup Language, is the way a web page is written, with little tags that say 'this is a heading' or 'this is a link'. HTTP, or HyperText Transfer Protocol, is the set of rules computers use to ask for pages and send them. And the URL, the web address, gives every page its own unique name, like a street address for a house. He also wrote the first web browser, to read pages, and the first web server, to send them out.","On 6 August 1991 the first website became available to people outside CERN. Its address was info.cern.ch, and its pages explained what the World Wide Web was, how to use a browser and how to set up your own web server. In other words, the very first website was a guide to making more websites. You can still visit a copy of it at the same address today, plain blue links on a white page.","Berners-Lee made a decision that mattered enormously: he did not patent the web. He wanted it to be open and free so that anyone could build on it without paying. In 1993 CERN made the web's software freely available for anyone to use. The same year, a team at the University of Illinois, the same university where Project Gutenberg began, released a browser called Mosaic that showed pictures and text together and was easy for ordinary people to use. The web exploded. In 1994 Berners-Lee founded the World Wide Web Consortium, called W3C, to look after the web's shared rules.","It is worth pausing to notice how big a change this was. In Gutenberg's day, to publish you needed a press, type, paper, ink, skilled workers and a lot of money, and printing was often controlled by licenses from rulers or churches, as you saw in Part 13. Even in 1985 you needed a computer and a printer and a way to hand out copies. On the web, one person with a computer could publish a page that could be read the same day in Tokyo, Lagos, Lima and Oslo. By 2004 there were more than 51 million websites.","New kinds of publishing grew from this. In the late 1990s people began keeping web logs, soon shortened to blogs, which were like public diaries or magazines written by one person. In 2001 Wikipedia started, an encyclopedia written by volunteers, a bit like a giant, never-ending version of the Distributed Proofreaders idea. Newspapers put their stories online. Scientists shared research papers in hours instead of months. Of course, as Part 18 showed, the same speed that spreads good information also spreads rumors and lies, which is why checking sources matters more than ever."]},{"heading":"Electronic Paper and E-Readers","paragraphs":["Reading a long book on a glowing computer screen can be tiring, and computers are not something you can curl up with in a hammock. So inventors asked: could we make a screen that behaves like paper? This idea is called electronic paper, or e-paper, and researchers had been working on it since the 1970s.","A big step came in 1997, when Joseph Jacobson, a physicist at the MIT Media Lab in Massachusetts, started a company called E Ink with several colleagues, including two students, J.D. Albert and Barrett Comiskey. As one E Ink spokesperson later put it, the goal was 'one book that could be all books', a single object whose pages could change into any text.","Here is how E Ink works. Imagine millions of tiny bubbles, called microcapsules, each roughly as wide as a human hair, spread in a thin layer on plastic. Inside each bubble is a clear liquid with tiny white particles that carry a positive electric charge and tiny black particles that carry a negative charge. Behind the layer are electrodes, which can make a small electric field. Opposite charges attract and like charges repel, so if the field pulls the white particles to the top, that spot looks white; flip the field and the black particles rise, and that spot looks black. Millions of these spots together make letters and pictures.","E-paper has two big advantages. First, it does not shine light at you. Like real paper, it reflects the light around it, so it is easy to read in bright sunshine, though you need a lamp or a built-in front light in the dark. Second, once the particles have moved, they stay put without needing more power. The screen only uses energy when the page changes, which is why an e-reader's battery can last for weeks.","Sony released an e-paper reader called the Librie in Japan in 2004, and its Sony Reader came to the United States in 2006. Then, on 19 November 2007, the online store Amazon launched the Kindle at a price of 399 US dollars. It used an E Ink screen about 6 inches across, and its big trick was a built-in wireless connection: you could buy a book and download it in about a minute, without a computer. At launch, roughly 90,000 books were available, and the first batch reportedly sold out within hours. Later, phones and tablets with bright color screens also became popular ways to read e-books.","E-books changed publishing in other ways too. Authors who could not find a traditional publisher could now publish their own books online, called self-publishing, and sell them directly to readers. And a related idea, print-on-demand, used fast digital printers to print a paper book only when someone ordered it, one copy at a time. In Gutenberg's day a printer had to guess how many copies to make and risked being stuck with piles of unsold books. With print-on-demand, a book never has to go out of print.","Did e-books kill paper books? Not at all. Printed books are still sold in huge numbers, hundreds of millions of copies a year in the United States alone, and many readers use both: an e-reader for travel, a paper book for the bedside table. Instead of replacing the printed page, digital publishing joined it, just as printing once joined, rather than instantly ended, handwriting."]},{"heading":"Will Our Digital Words Last?","paragraphs":["Remember the clay tablets from Ashurbanipal's library in Part 1? Some have survived about 2,600 years, and older ones even longer, because clay is tough, and a fire actually baked many of them harder. Now think about a file on a computer. Could you still read it in 2,600 years? Could you read it in 50? This is one of the biggest puzzles of digital publishing.","Digital files face dangers that clay and paper do not. Storage itself can fail: floppy disks, CDs and hard drives can wear out, and over time stored bits can flip or fade, a problem nicknamed bit rot. Even if the file survives perfectly, the machine to read it may vanish. Who still has a computer that can read an 8-inch floppy disk from the 1970s? And even with the right machine, you may need the right program, because file formats change. A document saved by a word processor in 1990 can be hard to open today.","A famous warning came from Britain. In 1986, to mark 900 years since the Domesday Book of 1086, the BBC created a modern Domesday Project, in which about a million people, many of them schoolchildren, recorded what life in Britain was like. It was stored on special laser discs that needed a particular computer setup. Within about fifteen years that equipment had become so rare that experts worried the whole project might become unreadable, while the original Domesday Book, written on parchment more than 900 years earlier, was still perfectly readable. Rescue teams eventually managed to copy the data into modern formats.","To fight this, people build digital libraries and archives. One of the largest is the Internet Archive, a nonprofit digital library founded in San Francisco in 1996 by a computer engineer named Brewster Kahle. Kahle's goal was to save a copy of the web itself, because web pages change and disappear all the time. In 2001 the Archive opened the Wayback Machine, which let the public look at old versions of websites going back to the mid-1990s; at the start it held over 10 billion web pages, and today it holds hundreds of billions. The Archive also scans books and keeps old films, music and software.","National libraries are working on the problem too, including the Library of Congress in the United States and the British Library, which keeps the Diamond Sutra from Part 5. Archivists use several strategies. Migration means regularly copying files onto new storage and converting them into new formats before the old ones die out. Emulation means writing programs that make a modern computer pretend to be an old one, so old software can still run. And many archives keep several copies in different places, so one fire or flood cannot destroy everything. There is an old idea behind this: the best way to save a text is to make many copies, which is exactly what printing did for books.","Simple, open formats help as well. Project Gutenberg's choice of Plain Vanilla ASCII back in 1971 looks very wise today: files from the 1970s can still be read on almost any device. PDF has a special archive version designed to be readable far into the future. The lesson is that publishing is not finished when words appear on a screen. Someone has to care for them, as monks once cared for manuscripts and librarians cared for printed books, or they can be lost.","From hot metal to flashes of light, from a typed Declaration to a web of billions of pages, the 1900s and 2000s turned printing into something that mostly happens inside computers. Printing now often means words on screens. But while some inventors were taking ink off the page, others were wondering something even stranger: what if a printer could build not letters, but solid objects?"]}],"deepDive":"Historians of technology like to point out that each new publishing technology cuts one cost almost to zero and moves the bottleneck somewhere else. Gutenberg's system made copying cheap but setting type was still slow; the Linotype sped up setting; phototypesetting removed the metal; desktop publishing removed the expensive specialist equipment; and the web removed the cost of distribution. What remains scarce today is attention and trust: anyone can publish, so readers must decide what is reliable. A technical heart of this story is the page description language. Before PostScript, a printer usually had to receive a bitmap, a grid saying which dots to ink, which tied a document to one machine's resolution. PostScript, which is actually a full programming language, describes shapes as mathematical paths, often built from cubic Bezier curves, each defined by two end points and two control points. The printer's interpreter converts these paths into dots at whatever resolution it has, which is why one file could print at about 300 dots per inch on a 1985 LaserWriter and at much higher resolution on a professional imagesetter. Font hinting adds instructions that nudge outlines to fit the pixel grid, so small letters stay even. PDF, introduced in the early 1990s, kept this imaging model but removed most of the programming, making files more predictable. Digital preservation raises a genuine debate. Some experts, including internet pioneer Vint Cerf, have warned of a possible 'digital dark age' in which future historians find our era poorly recorded because formats and devices became obsolete. Others argue the risk is manageable through migration, emulation, open standards and many distributed copies, a principle libraries call 'lots of copies keep stuff safe' (LOCKSS, also the name of a Stanford-based preservation program). There is also the question of what to save: the Internet Archive's Wayback Machine captures a huge but incomplete sample of the web, and pages behind logins or built by complicated programs are hard to archive. Finally, historians note that print did not vanish when screens arrived. Just as manuscripts were still made for decades after Gutenberg, printed books, newspapers and packaging continue alongside digital text, and many of the most popular e-book titles are also bestselling paper books.","funFact":"The world's first website, written by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, can still be visited today at the address info.cern.ch.","check":{"q":"What was the very first e-book that Michael Hart typed in to start Project Gutenberg in 1971?","a":"The United States Declaration of Independence","wrong":["The Gutenberg Bible","A Shakespeare play","The Diamond Sutra"]},"sources":[{"title":"History and Philosophy of Project Gutenberg","publisher":"Project Gutenberg","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/about/background/history_and_philosophy.html"},{"title":"Project Gutenberg (1971-2009)","publisher":"Project Gutenberg","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/31632/pg31632.html"},{"title":"PostScript: A Digital Printing Press","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://computerhistory.org/blog/postscript-a-digital-printing-press/"},{"title":"Oral History of Paul Brainerd","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/collections/catalog/102657986"},{"title":"Tim Berners-Lee","publisher":"World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)","url":"https://www.w3.org/People/Berners-Lee/"},{"title":"The World's First Web Site","publisher":"History.com","url":"https://www.history.com/articles/the-worlds-first-web-site"},{"title":"Electronic paper turns a page","publisher":"Science News Explores","url":"https://www.snexplores.org/article/electronic-paper-turns-page"},{"title":"Wayback Machine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/19-1.jpg","alt":"The NeXT computer Tim Berners-Lee used as the first web server at CERN; its label warns \"Do not power it down!!\"","file":"First Web Server.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Web_Server.jpg","author":"User:Coolcaesar at en.wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/19-2.jpg","alt":"An Apple LaserWriter II (1988), a later model of the laser printer Apple launched in 1985 for desktop publishing.","file":"Apple Laserwriter II.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apple_Laserwriter_II.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/19-3.jpg","alt":"The first Amazon Kindle (2007), with its E Ink screen showing a Wikipedia page and a keyboard below.","file":"Amazon Kindle - Wikipedia.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amazon_Kindle_-_Wikipedia.jpg","author":"ShakataGaNai","license":"CC BY 3.0"}],"words":4217},{"n":20,"title":"The Future of Printing — and the Big Picture","when":"today and tomorrow","summary":"Today some printers do not print words at all: they build solid things, layer by layer, from toys to rocket parts. Scientists are even printing with living cells. And every page you read, on paper or on a screen, is the newest step in a 5,000-year adventure that started with marks in clay.","sections":[{"heading":"A Printer That Builds Things","paragraphs":["Picture a small workshop in California in 1983. An engineer named Chuck Hull works for a company called UVP that uses ultraviolet light, the invisible light that gives you sunburn, to harden special coatings on things like tabletops. These coatings are made of a liquid plastic called a photopolymer, which stays runny in the dark but turns solid in a fraction of a second when ultraviolet light hits it. Hull had a bold thought: if light can harden one thin layer of this liquid, why not harden another layer on top of it, and another, and another, until you have built a whole solid object?","Think about how a printing press works. It puts one thin layer of ink on a flat sheet. Hull's idea was to print thousands of thin layers on top of each other, each one shaped like a slice of the finished object. It is a bit like making a loaf of bread by stacking slices instead of baking it whole. That year Hull used his process to make his first printed part: a small eye wash cup, the kind used to rinse an eye. It was not glamorous, but it proved the idea worked.","Hull called his process stereolithography, from Greek words meaning roughly 'solid stone writing', a cousin of the word lithography that you met in Part 16. Here is how it works. A computer holds a 3D design of the object and slices it into very thin horizontal layers. A platform sits just below the surface of a vat of liquid photopolymer. An ultraviolet laser beam, steered by mirrors, traces the shape of the first slice on the liquid's surface, and wherever the beam touches, the liquid hardens. Then the platform moves down by the thickness of one layer, fresh liquid flows over the top, and the laser traces the next slice, which sticks to the one below. Layer by layer, the object grows out of the liquid like something rising out of a pond.","Hull filed for a patent on 8 August 1984, and it was granted on 11 March 1986. In 1986 he co-founded a company called 3D Systems to sell the machines, and soon afterward, around 1987 to 1988, it began selling the SLA-1, the first commercial 3D printer. Hull also helped create a computer file format called STL that describes the surface of a 3D object as lots of tiny triangles. STL is still used by most 3D printing software today, so in a way, almost every 3D print carries a trace of his work.","Fair credit matters, just as it did with Bi Sheng, Korea's metal type and Gutenberg. Hull was not the only person thinking about building objects in layers. In Japan, Hideo Kodama published a description of a similar light-hardening method in 1981. In France, three researchers, Alain Le Mehaute, Olivier de Witte and Jean Claude Andre, filed a patent for a stereolithography process on 16 July 1984, about three weeks before Hull, though their project was not developed into a business. Hull is widely honored as the inventor of stereolithography because he built a working system, patented it and launched an industry. He was inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2014 and received the U.S. National Medal of Technology and Innovation in 2023.","People often call this whole family of methods additive manufacturing, which means making things by adding material rather than taking it away. Most traditional manufacturing is subtractive: you start with a block of metal or wood and cut, drill or carve away what you don't want, like the woodblock carvers of Part 5 cutting away everything except the letters. Additive manufacturing does the opposite and adds material only where it is needed, so it can waste much less."]},{"heading":"How 3D Printers Work Today","paragraphs":["Since Hull's first machine, inventors have found many ways to print in layers. The most common kind in homes and schools is called fused deposition modeling, or FDM, developed by Scott Crump, who co-founded a company called Stratasys and developed the method in the late 1980s. An FDM printer works a bit like a very precise, robot-controlled hot glue gun. A thin plastic thread called filament is pushed into a heated nozzle, where it melts. Motors move the nozzle across the build plate, drawing one layer of melted plastic, which cools and hardens within moments. Then the nozzle rises a tiny bit, or the plate drops, and it draws the next layer on top.","Those layers are very thin. A typical home printer uses layers about 0.1 to 0.3 millimeters thick, and some machines can go below 0.05 millimeters. That means a toy only 3 centimeters tall can be made of a hundred or more layers. If you look closely at a 3D-printed object, you can often see the tiny ridges where one layer meets the next, like the rings on a tree or the lines of a page of text.","Before printing, a program called a slicer takes the 3D design and cuts it into layers, then works out the exact path the nozzle must follow for each one, writing out a long list of instructions. This is surprisingly similar to the work of the compositor from Part 11 arranging letters line by line, except the computer does it in seconds. Because a printer cannot put plastic on thin air, the slicer also adds temporary support structures under overhanging parts, which are snapped off afterward.","Other methods use powders. In powder bed fusion, a thin layer of fine plastic or metal powder is spread across a bed, and a powerful laser melts or fuses the powder only where the object should be. Then a fresh layer of powder is spread on top, and the process repeats. When it is finished, the solid part is dug out of the loose powder, which can be reused. This is how many strong metal parts are made. The U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology groups additive manufacturing into several main families, including powder bed fusion, material extrusion such as FDM, and vat photopolymerization, the family that includes Hull's stereolithography.","The list of printable materials keeps growing. Besides everyday plastics such as PLA, which is made from plant starches like corn, printers can use nylon, rubbery plastics, resins, ceramics, sand, concrete, wax, and metals including steel, titanium, aluminum and copper. Some experimental printers even squeeze out chocolate or dough. Each material needs its own settings: the right temperature, the right speed and the right layer thickness. Getting those right is a bit like the old printers learning exactly how damp to make their paper and how much ink to put on the type.","For a long time 3D printers were expensive machines for factories and laboratories. That began to change in the mid-2000s, when Adrian Bowyer, an engineer at the University of Bath in England, started the RepRap project to design an open, low-cost printer that could print many of its own parts. A few years later, in 2009, key patents on FDM expired, and companies began selling printers cheap enough for schools, libraries and families. Today some public libraries lend time on 3D printers, much as they lend books.","The uses are amazingly varied. Designers print prototypes, early test versions of a product, to check how it looks and fits before making thousands. Dentists print models of teeth and the clear plastic trays used to straighten them. Doctors print models of a patient's heart or skull to plan tricky surgeries. Engineers have printed custom medical implants shaped to match a person's own bones, such as a replacement section of jawbone. Hobbyists print toy figures, chess sets, replacement knobs and cookie cutters. People have even built small houses by printing layers of concrete from giant nozzles. And charities and volunteers have printed low-cost prosthetic hands for children."]},{"heading":"Printing Parts for Rockets","paragraphs":["Some of the most exciting 3D printing happens in space programs, where every part must survive extreme heat, pressure and shaking. A rocket engine is fiendishly complicated. Its combustion chamber, where fuel burns, must be cooled by liquid flowing through hundreds of tiny channels in its walls, or it would melt. Making those channels the old way meant machining and joining many separate pieces, sometimes more than a thousand, and every joint was a place where something could leak or crack.","With 3D printing, engineers can print the chamber wall and its cooling channels as a single piece. NASA's Marshall Space Flight Center in Huntsville, Alabama, leads a project called RAMPT, short for Rapid Analysis and Manufacturing Propulsion Technology, which develops 3D-printed rocket engine parts and the computer tools to test them. Since it began, the project has carried out about 500 test firings of 3D-printed injectors, nozzles and combustion chambers, adding up to more than 16,000 seconds of burning.","The materials are special too. NASA scientists developed copper alloys, such as one called GRCop-42, that can carry heat away quickly and be printed from metal powder. Some printed chambers are wrapped in carbon fiber, which makes them about 40 percent lighter than traditional designs. NASA says printing could cut the cost of some engine parts by up to 70 percent and make them in half the time, and that test parts can be delivered in weeks instead of years. Some printed engine components are now about 3 meters (10 feet) tall.","Printing metal parts that must never fail is not as simple as pressing a button. Tiny gaps or bubbles can hide inside a layer if the powder does not melt completely, and a hidden flaw in a rocket engine could be dangerous. So engineers inspect printed parts carefully, sometimes using X-ray scans that look right through the metal, and they fire test engines again and again on test stands before trusting a design. That is why projects like RAMPT spend so much time testing, and why they also build computer simulations to predict how printed metal will behave under extreme heat and pressure.","There is a lovely echo of history here. When Gutenberg combined punches, moulds, type metal, oil-based ink and a screw press, he did not just make printing faster; he made a whole new kind of production possible. Rocket engineers say something similar about 3D printing: it lets them design shapes that simply could not be made any other way, like curving internal channels inside solid metal. Astronauts have also tested 3D printers aboard the International Space Station, imagining a future where crews on long missions print spare parts and tools instead of carrying every possible piece from Earth."]},{"heading":"Printing With Living Cells and Glowing Circuits","paragraphs":["If a printer can lay down plastic and metal, could it lay down living cells? Scientists around the world are trying exactly that. It is called bioprinting. Instead of ink, a bioprinter uses a bioink: a soft, jelly-like material called a hydrogel, mostly made of water, mixed with living cells. The hydrogel acts like scaffolding, holding the cells in place and giving them a comfortable, wet home while they grow.","Bioprinters use methods you have already met in this story, adapted for delicate living things. Extrusion bioprinters squeeze bioink out of a nozzle in thin threads, like the FDM printers in the last section but without heat, which would kill the cells. Droplet bioprinters work like the inkjet printers from Part 17, shooting tiny droplets of bioink. Laser-based bioprinters use light, some of them hardening bioink layer by layer much like Hull's stereolithography.","It is far harder than printing plastic. Cells are fragile. Being squeezed through a narrow nozzle puts them under stress, and some types, especially certain stem cells, often do not survive. The biggest challenge of all is blood supply. Every cell in your body needs oxygen and food, and most cells are close to a tiny blood vessel. A thick printed tissue has no blood vessels, so cells deep inside it starve. Scientists are working hard on ways to print networks of channels that could act like blood vessels, but it remains one of the field's toughest problems.","So what can bioprinting do today? Mostly, it helps research. Scientists print small pieces of tissue to test new medicines, which can tell them how real human cells might react and may reduce some animal testing. They print models of diseases to study how illnesses develop. Researchers have printed cartilage, the bendy tissue in your nose and ears, and scaffolds to help bone heal, and they have tested printed tissues such as a windpipe graft in animals. Relatively simple structures are the furthest along. Printing a whole working heart, kidney or liver that could be transplanted into a person is not yet possible. It is a long-term dream, and some researchers, including teams working with Chuck Hull's company, are pursuing it, but it may take many years.","Printers are also making electronics. In printed electronics, special inks containing tiny particles of metal or other conducting materials are printed onto plastic, paper or fabric to make wires, antennas and simple circuits. The thin antennas inside some shop security tags and contactless cards can be made this way, and researchers are printing flexible sensors and even thin solar cells. Because printing can cover large areas cheaply, it might one day let us print electronics the way we print newspapers.","Screens are becoming more paper-like too. The E Ink technology you met in Part 19 now comes in color versions and is used for electronic shelf labels in shops and signs at bus stops that change without using much power. Engineers have built bendable and foldable screens, and some phones now fold in half like a booklet. The line between a printed page and a screen is getting blurrier every year."]},{"heading":"Will Paper Survive?","paragraphs":["With e-readers, phones, websites and computers that can write text by themselves, you might expect paper books to be fading away. Yet printed books are still sold in enormous numbers. In the United States alone, hundreds of millions of printed books are sold every year, and children's picture books, cookbooks and bestselling novels are still mostly bought on paper. Libraries remain busy, and many have added e-book lending, computers and even 3D printers without throwing out their shelves.","Why does paper hold on? Part of the answer is that a book is a very good piece of technology. It needs no battery, never crashes, can be read in sunshine, survives being dropped, and can be lent to a friend with no password. A well-made book on acid-free paper, as Part 18 explained, can last for centuries. Some research also suggests that many people find it easier to remember and understand long texts they read on paper, though scientists still debate how big that effect is and why it happens.","Packaging, labels, posters, money, tickets and medicine leaflets are printed too. The world's printing presses are still running, even if newspapers print fewer copies than they used to. What has changed is that paper is now one choice among many, rather than the only way to share words.","Then there is a brand-new twist: artificial intelligence, or AI, programs that can produce text, pictures and even whole articles in seconds. In a way, this is the next chapter of the oldest story in this Deep Dive. Woodblocks made copying faster; Gutenberg's type made it faster still; computers made copies almost free. AI tools can now make new text almost free as well. That is powerful and useful, but it also means there is more text in the world than ever, and not all of it is true. As in the age of pamphlets and the Great Moon Hoax, readers need to ask: who made this, how do they know, and can I check it?","So the honest answer to 'Will paper survive?' is: probably yes, but alongside screens, not instead of them. Just as scribes kept writing for decades after printing arrived, and handwriting is still taught today, old and new ways of sharing words tend to live side by side, each doing what it does best."]},{"heading":"Big Lessons From a 5,000-Year Story","paragraphs":["Let's step back and look at the whole journey. One of the clearest lessons is that inventions are built on earlier inventions. Gutenberg did not appear out of nowhere. He needed paper, which came from China through the Islamic world and Spain and Italy. He needed the idea of pressing an image again and again, which goes back to cylinder seals in Mesopotamia and woodblocks in Asia. He adapted a screw press that winemakers and papermakers already used. Later, the steam press needed the steam engine, the laser printer needed lasers and static electricity, and the web needed computers, networks and the alphabet itself.","The second lesson is that credit is shared across many cultures. Sumerians gave us writing in clay, Egyptians papyrus and hieroglyphs, Phoenicians and Greeks the alphabet. China gave the world paper, woodblock printing and Bi Sheng's movable type. Korea cast the oldest surviving book printed with metal type, the Jikji of 1377, and King Sejong created Hangul. The Islamic world spread papermaking and built huge libraries. Europe combined these threads into Gutenberg's fast system and later the industrial press. America, Britain, France, Germany, Switzerland, Japan and many other places added the inventions of the last two centuries. No single country or person owns the story of printing.","The third lesson is that each big step made words cheaper and more plentiful, and that changed who could read and who could speak. When books cost as much as a house, only rulers, churches and the wealthy owned them. When printing made them cheaper, more people learned to read, argued about religion, checked scientific results, followed the news and even questioned their governments. Historians like Elizabeth Eisenstein argued that print was an 'agent of change'. Others point out that print was one powerful helper among many causes, not a magic switch. Both views remind us that technology and people shape each other.","That is why so many countries protect freedom of speech and freedom of the press. Being able to copy and share words freely lets people learn, share discoveries, remember the past and hold powerful people to account. But Part 18 showed the other side: print has also carried propaganda, hoaxes and hatred, and rulers have burned books to silence ideas. The power to publish comes with a responsibility to publish truthfully, to give fair credit, to check facts and to treat other people with respect. That responsibility now belongs to everyone, because today almost anyone can publish."]},{"heading":"From Clay Tablets to Your Screen","paragraphs":["Let's take one last journey, very fast, through everything we have seen. More than 5,000 years ago, a scribe in Uruk pressed a reed into wet clay to record sacks of grain. Egyptians wrote on papyrus, Greeks and Romans used alphabets and scrolls, and the codex gave us pages we could turn. In China, paper made writing light and cheap, and the Diamond Sutra of 868 showed that whole books could be printed from carved wood. About 1,000 years ago Bi Sheng arranged clay characters into pages, and Korean printers cast metal type and printed Jikji in 1377.","Then, more than 570 years ago, in Mainz, Gutenberg's system of punches, moulds, type metal, oily ink and press began turning out Bibles, and printing spread to more than 230 European towns by 1500. Luther's pamphlets flew across Germany, Copernicus and Vesalius printed their discoveries, newspapers and dictionaries appeared, and printers set up shop from Mexico City to Goa. Steam, iron and wood-pulp paper made printing an industry. Linotypes, halftones, offset presses, photocopiers and laser printers followed. Then computers, PostScript, the web and e-readers carried words onto screens, and 3D printers began to print objects, and even living cells.","And now here you are, at the newest point in that 5,000-year line, reading these words. Whether you are holding paper or looking at a screen, you are using the work of thousands of people across many centuries and many lands: the papermakers of China and Baghdad, the punchcutters of Mainz, the compositors with ink on their fingers, the engineers at CERN and the volunteers of Project Gutenberg.","Here is the most exciting part. For almost all of history, publishing was something only a few people could do. Today, with a computer, a tablet, or even a pencil and a photocopier, you can be a publisher too. You can write a story, draw a comic, design a poster for a school event, start a club newsletter, record facts about your town or build a website about something you love. If you do, remember the lessons of this journey: check your facts, give credit to the people whose ideas you build on, and use the power of words to help others understand the world.","Every page you read, paper or screen, is the latest step in a 5,000-year story that began with marks in clay. The next page has not been written yet. Maybe you will be the one to write it."]}],"deepDive":"Two debates run underneath this chapter. The first concerns who invented 3D printing. Patent records show that Alain Le Mehaute, Olivier de Witte and Jean Claude Andre filed a French patent for stereolithography on 16 July 1984, about three weeks before Chuck Hull's U.S. filing on 8 August 1984, and Hideo Kodama in Japan had published a layer-by-layer photopolymer method in 1981. Hull's patent, granted in 1986, and his company 3D Systems turned the idea into a working industry, which is why he is usually honored as the inventor. Historians of technology see the same pattern as with printing itself: several people reach similar ideas when the enabling pieces, here ultraviolet-curing resins, lasers and computer-aided design, become available, and the person who builds a practical system gets most of the fame. The second debate is about how revolutionary printing really was. Elizabeth Eisenstein's 1979 book The Printing Press as an Agent of Change argued that print's ability to make standardized, permanent, widely shared copies transformed European thought. Critics such as Adrian Johns replied that early printed books were often full of errors and piracy, and that trust in print had to be built through social institutions, not just machines. Most historians now take a middle view: print was a powerful amplifier whose effects depended on religion, politics, trade and literacy. Bioprinting shows why careful language matters in science writing. Oxygen can only diffuse a short distance through living tissue, roughly 100 to 200 micrometers, so thick printed tissues without blood vessels cannot keep their inner cells alive. Researchers have printed small tissue models for drug testing, cartilage constructs and bone scaffolds, and have tested some printed grafts in animals, but no bioprinted solid organ is in routine use in human transplant patients. Claims of 'printed organs' in headlines usually refer to small, simplified models or early research. The same caution applies to AI-generated text, which can be fluent yet wrong: as with every earlier copying technology, making words cheaper puts more weight on checking where they came from.","funFact":"The first object Chuck Hull ever 3D printed, in 1983, was a small eye wash cup.","check":{"q":"What did Chuck Hull's stereolithography use to harden each layer of liquid plastic?","a":"Ultraviolet light from a laser","wrong":["A hot iron press","Melted lead from a Linotype","Static electricity and toner"]},"sources":[{"title":"3D Systems Co-Founder and CTO Chuck Hull Awarded National Medal of Technology and Innovation","publisher":"3D Systems","url":"https://www.3dsystems.com/press-releases/3d-systems-co-founder-chief-technology-officer-chuck-hull-awarded-national-medal"},{"title":"Charles W. Hull","publisher":"National Science and Technology Medals Foundation","url":"https://nationalmedals.org/laureate/charles-w-hull/"},{"title":"What Is Additive Manufacturing?","publisher":"National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)","url":"https://www.nist.gov/additive-manufacturing/what-additive-manufacturing"},{"title":"NASA Additive Manufacturing Project Shapes Future for Agency, Industry Rocket Makers","publisher":"NASA","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/marshall/nasa-additive-manufacturing-project-shapes-future-for-agency-industry-rocket-makers/"},{"title":"3D bioprinting of cells, tissues and organs","publisher":"NIH PubMed Central (PMC)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7434768"},{"title":"Electronic paper turns a page","publisher":"Science News Explores","url":"https://www.snexplores.org/article/electronic-paper-turns-page"},{"title":"3D printing","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3D_printing"},{"title":"Chuck Hull","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Hull"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/20-1.jpg","alt":"Stereolithography diagram: a laser (a) shines through a tank of liquid resin (b), hardening it layer by layer into an object (d) that a platform (e) lifts.","file":"Schematic representation of Stereolithography.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schematic_representation_of_Stereolithography.png","author":"Paolo Cignoni","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/20-2.jpg","alt":"A desktop 3D printer whose nozzle has built a blue plastic vase layer by layer; more printed models sit on top.","file":"3D Printing.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3D_Printing.jpg","author":"MukhtaromST","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/printing-press/20-3.jpg","alt":"A 3D-printed rocket engine part blazes during a NASA hot-fire test at Marshall Space Flight Center.","file":"3-D Printed Rocket Part Blazes to Life.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3-D_Printed_Rocket_Part_Blazes_to_Life.jpg","author":"NASA/MSFC/David Olive","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3818}],"words":79882};
